window.QR_INDEX = {"py":[{"sid":"A1.01","title":"Installing Python with Anaconda","cmd":"","terms":["Anaconda","installing Python"],"tags":["setup","tools"],"task":["install Python","get Python","set up Python on a new computer","download Anaconda"],"examinable":true,"search":"installing python with anaconda  setup tools anaconda installing python install python get python set up python on a new computer download anaconda installing anaconda allows us to get python, jupyterlab and the most common packages in one download. it is useful when python is being set up on a new computer for the first time. alternatively, python can be installed on its own from python.org, which is smaller but leaves packages to be installed later."},{"sid":"A1.02","title":"Checking the installed Python version","cmd":"python --version","terms":["python --version","version of Python"],"tags":["setup","tools"],"task":["which Python version","check Python is installed","python version"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking the installed python version python --version setup tools python --version version of python which python version check python is installed python version the `python --version` command allows us to check that python is installed and which version it is. it is useful when an installation has finished, or when code behaves differently on two computers. alternatively, `import sys` then `print(sys.version)` shows the same information from inside a program."},{"sid":"A1.03","title":"Opening a project folder in VS Code","cmd":"","terms":["VS Code","project folder"],"tags":["setup","tools"],"task":["open a folder in VS Code","open my project","start a project in VS Code"],"examinable":true,"search":"opening a project folder in vs code  setup tools vs code project folder open a folder in vs code open my project start a project in vs code opening a folder in vs code allows us to keep all the files for one piece of work together and see them in the explorer panel. it is useful when a program uses more than one file. alternatively, single files can be opened on their own, but then vs code does not know which folder the program runs in."},{"sid":"A1.04","title":"Choosing the Python interpreter in VS Code","cmd":"","terms":["interpreter","Python extension"],"tags":["setup","tools"],"task":["choose the Python interpreter","VS Code uses the wrong Python","select kernel","pick the environment"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing the python interpreter in vs code  setup tools interpreter python extension choose the python interpreter vs code uses the wrong python select kernel pick the environment choosing the interpreter allows us to tell vs code which installation of python runs our programs. it is useful when vs code cannot find python, or when packages installed with anaconda are reported as missing. alternatively, the interpreter can be set once for the whole folder by choosing a virtual environment in it."},{"sid":"A1.05","title":"Writing and running a first script","cmd":"print(\"Hello, world!\")","terms":["print()","script",".py file"],"tags":["setup","core","printing"],"task":["write my first program","hello world","create a script","run a script in VS Code"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing and running a first script print(\"hello, world!\") setup core printing print() script .py file write my first program hello world create a script run a script in vs code the `print()` function allows us to show text and values on the screen when a script runs. it is useful when a program needs to report a result, and when checking that python is set up correctly. alternatively, a notebook displays the value of the last line of a cell without `print()`."},{"sid":"A1.06","title":"Running a script from a terminal","cmd":"python first_program.py","terms":["terminal","running a script"],"tags":["setup","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["run a script from the terminal","run python file","execute a .py file","python command"],"examinable":false,"search":"running a script from a terminal python first_program.py setup tools non-examinable terminal running a script run a script from the terminal run python file execute a .py file python command the `python` command allows us to run a script by typing its file name in a terminal. it is useful when a program is run without vs code, or when extra options must be typed after the command. alternatively, the ▶ button in vs code types this command for us."},{"sid":"A1.07","title":"Opening a terminal","cmd":"Terminal (macOS), Command Prompt or PowerShell (Windows)","terms":["terminal","command line","opening a terminal"],"tags":["terminal","tools","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["open the terminal","command prompt","PowerShell","shell window"],"examinable":false,"search":"opening a terminal terminal (macos), command prompt or powershell (windows) terminal tools setup non-examinable terminal command line opening a terminal open the terminal command prompt powershell shell window a terminal is a window in which commands are typed instead of clicked. it is useful because many programming tools, such as `python` and `pip`, are run this way. alternatively, vs code and anaconda navigator hide some commands behind buttons, but the terminal is the same everywhere."},{"sid":"A1.08","title":"Finding the current folder and listing its files","cmd":"pwd, ls (macOS and Linux)   cd, dir (Windows)","terms":["pwd","ls","dir","current folder"],"tags":["terminal","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["where am I","current folder","list files","pwd and ls","dir"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the current folder and listing its files pwd, ls (macos and linux)   cd, dir (windows) terminal tools non-examinable pwd ls dir current folder where am i current folder list files pwd and ls dir the commands `pwd` and `ls` on macos and linux, and `cd` and `dir` on windows, allow us to see which folder the terminal is in and what it contains. it is useful before running a script, because `python script.py` works only in the folder that holds the script. alternatively, the file browser of the computer shows the same folder, but the terminal shows the one that commands will use."},{"sid":"A1.09","title":"Moving between folders with `cd`","cmd":"cd shop_project   cd ..","terms":["cd","change directory","moving between folders"],"tags":["terminal","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["change folder","go into a folder","go up a folder","cd"],"examinable":false,"search":"moving between folders with cd cd shop_project   cd .. terminal tools non-examinable cd change directory moving between folders change folder go into a folder go up a folder cd the command `cd` allows us to move the terminal into another folder. it is useful for reaching the folder of a project before running its scripts. alternatively, a terminal opened from vs code already starts in the project folder."},{"sid":"A1.10","title":"Making a new folder","cmd":"mkdir shop_project","terms":["mkdir","making a folder"],"tags":["terminal","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["make a folder","create a directory","mkdir"],"examinable":false,"search":"making a new folder mkdir shop_project terminal tools non-examinable mkdir making a folder make a folder create a directory mkdir the command `mkdir` allows us to create a new folder from the terminal. it is useful for starting a project, because each project is kept in its own folder. alternatively, the file browser creates a folder with a right-click, but the command can be repeated and recorded."},{"sid":"A1.11","title":"Repeating, completing and stopping commands","cmd":"Up arrow, Tab, Ctrl+C","terms":["Ctrl+C","up arrow","Tab completion","stopping a program"],"tags":["terminal","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["stop a running program","repeat the last command","autocomplete in the terminal","Ctrl+C"],"examinable":false,"search":"repeating, completing and stopping commands up arrow, tab, ctrl+c terminal tools non-examinable ctrl+c up arrow tab completion stopping a program stop a running program repeat the last command autocomplete in the terminal ctrl+c three keys allow a terminal to be used quickly: the up arrow brings back the last command, tab completes a name, and `ctrl+c` stops a running program. it is useful because typing is the slow part of the terminal, and a program stuck in a loop can be stopped at once."},{"sid":"A1.12","title":"Trying code in the Python prompt","cmd":"python  →  >>>","terms":["Python prompt","interactive mode","REPL",">>>"],"tags":["tools","interpreter","non-examinable"],"task":["try code interactively","Python prompt","REPL","type Python commands one at a time"],"examinable":false,"search":"trying code in the python prompt python  →  >>> tools interpreter non-examinable python prompt interactive mode repl >>> try code interactively python prompt repl type python commands one at a time typing `python` on its own allows us to open the python prompt, where each line is run as soon as it is typed. it is useful for testing a single idea, such as how a string method behaves, without saving a file. alternatively, a notebook cell does the same and keeps the work."},{"sid":"A1.13","title":"Finding out what a function does with `help()`","cmd":"help(len)","terms":["help()","documentation in Python"],"tags":["tools","interpreter","non-examinable"],"task":["what does this function do","help on a function","documentation in Python","help"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding out what a function does with help() help(len) tools interpreter non-examinable help() documentation in python what does this function do help on a function documentation in python help the function `help()` allows us to read what a function or method does, from within python. it is useful when the name of a function is known but its arguments are not. alternatively, the python documentation on the web gives longer explanations and examples."},{"sid":"A1.14","title":"Listing what an object can do with `dir()`","cmd":"dir(obj)","terms":["dir()","listing methods"],"tags":["tools","interpreter","non-examinable"],"task":["what can an object do","list the methods","dir","find methods of a type"],"examinable":false,"search":"listing what an object can do with dir() dir(obj) tools interpreter non-examinable dir() listing methods what can an object do list the methods dir find methods of a type the function `dir()` allows us to list the names of the methods and attributes of a value. it is useful when a method is half remembered. alternatively, the documentation lists them all with explanations, but `dir()` is quicker."},{"sid":"A1.15","title":"Reading the Python documentation","cmd":"docs.python.org","terms":["documentation","docs.python.org","reading the docs"],"tags":["tools","interpreter","non-examinable"],"task":["read the documentation","look up a function online","docs.python.org"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading the python documentation docs.python.org tools interpreter non-examinable documentation docs.python.org reading the docs read the documentation look up a function online docs.python.org the official documentation at docs.python.org allows us to look up any function, module or error in the language. it is useful when a question needs more than `help()` can give. alternatively, a search engine finds explanations, but the official pages are correct for the version of python that is installed."},{"sid":"A1.16","title":"Reading the underlines and the Problems panel","cmd":"View > Problems","terms":["Problems panel","red underline","Pylance","warnings in VS Code"],"tags":["tools","vscode","non-examinable"],"task":["red underline","what do the squiggles mean","Problems panel","find errors in VS Code"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading the underlines and the problems panel view > problems tools vscode non-examinable problems panel red underline pylance warnings in vs code red underline what do the squiggles mean problems panel find errors in vs code vs code underlines code that it thinks is wrong, and the problems panel lists every underline with its line number. it is useful because many mistakes, such as a misspelt name, are shown before the program is run. alternatively, running the program shows the first error only, after the fault has already happened."},{"sid":"A1.17","title":"Running selected lines in the terminal","cmd":"select lines → Shift+Enter","terms":["run selection","Shift+Enter in VS Code","Python terminal"],"tags":["tools","vscode","non-examinable"],"task":["run selected lines","run part of a file","send code to the terminal"],"examinable":false,"search":"running selected lines in the terminal select lines → shift+enter tools vscode non-examinable run selection shift+enter in vs code python terminal run selected lines run part of a file send code to the terminal the command *run selection/line in python terminal* allows a few lines of a script to be run without running the whole file. it is useful for trying out a line, as at the python prompt, while keeping the code in the file. alternatively, the ▶ button runs the whole file."},{"sid":"A1.18","title":"Formatting the code automatically","cmd":"Format Document","terms":["formatting code","format on save","Black"],"tags":["tools","vscode","style","non-examinable"],"task":["tidy the code","format code automatically","fix indentation","Format Document"],"examinable":false,"search":"formatting the code automatically format document tools vscode style non-examinable formatting code format on save black tidy the code format code automatically fix indentation format document the command *format document* allows vs code to correct spacing, line breaks and quotation marks to a standard style. it is useful because consistent layout is clearer to read, and the style rules need not be remembered."},{"sid":"A1.19","title":"Moving around a file quickly","cmd":"Ctrl+P, Ctrl+F, F2, Ctrl+/","terms":["keyboard shortcuts","find","rename symbol","comment toggle"],"tags":["tools","vscode","non-examinable"],"task":["keyboard shortcuts in VS Code","jump to a file","search a file","comment out lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"moving around a file quickly ctrl+p, ctrl+f, f2, ctrl+/ tools vscode non-examinable keyboard shortcuts find rename symbol comment toggle keyboard shortcuts in vs code jump to a file search a file comment out lines a few keyboard shortcuts allow code to be found, renamed and commented without using the mouse. it is useful because these four jobs are repeated all day when programs grow."},{"sid":"A1.20","title":"Installing Python from python.org instead of Anaconda","cmd":"python.org → Downloads → installer","terms":["python.org","installing Python without Anaconda","Windows macOS Linux"],"tags":["setup","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["install Python without Anaconda","python.org installer","download Python from python.org"],"examinable":false,"search":"installing python from python.org instead of anaconda python.org → downloads → installer setup tools non-examinable python.org installing python without anaconda windows macos linux install python without anaconda python.org installer download python from python.org the installer from python.org allows python to be installed on its own, without anaconda. it is useful on a computer where anaconda is not wanted, or for a smaller installation. alternatively, anaconda also installs many packages and jupyter, which suits the course."},{"sid":"A1.21","title":"Fixing \"python is not recognised\" or \"command not found\"","cmd":"","terms":["python not recognised","command not found","PATH"],"tags":["setup","tools","errors","non-examinable"],"task":["python is not recognised","command not found","Python not on the path","python does not run"],"examinable":false,"search":"fixing \"python is not recognised\" or \"command not found\"  setup tools errors non-examinable python not recognised command not found path python is not recognised command not found python not on the path python does not run the messages \"python is not recognised\" (windows) and \"python: command not found\" (macos and linux) tell us that the terminal cannot find python."},{"sid":"A1.22","title":"What happens when a program is run","cmd":"","terms":["interpreter","how Python runs a file","syntax highlighting"],"tags":["setup","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["how does Python run my program","what happens when a script runs","interpreter","line by line"],"examinable":false,"search":"what happens when a program is run  setup tools non-examinable interpreter how python runs a file syntax highlighting how does python run my program what happens when a script runs interpreter line by line when a file is run, the python interpreter reads it, translates each statement into instructions for the computer, and carries them out in order."},{"sid":"A2.01","title":"Starting JupyterLab","cmd":"jupyter lab","terms":["JupyterLab","jupyter lab"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["start Jupyter","open JupyterLab","launch a notebook","jupyter lab command"],"examinable":false,"search":"starting jupyterlab jupyter lab notebooks tools non-examinable jupyterlab jupyter lab start jupyter open jupyterlab launch a notebook jupyter lab command the `jupyter lab` command allows us to open jupyterlab, where notebooks are created and run, in a web browser. it is useful when code and notes are to be kept together, or when results should appear step by step. alternatively, jupyterlab can be started with the *launch* button in anaconda navigator."},{"sid":"A2.07","title":"Starting JupyterLab from Anaconda Navigator","cmd":"Anaconda Navigator → Home → JupyterLab → Launch","terms":["Anaconda Navigator","launching JupyterLab"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["start JupyterLab from Anaconda","Anaconda Navigator","launch Jupyter from Navigator"],"examinable":false,"search":"starting jupyterlab from anaconda navigator anaconda navigator → home → jupyterlab → launch notebooks tools setup non-examinable anaconda navigator launching jupyterlab start jupyterlab from anaconda anaconda navigator launch jupyter from navigator the *launch* button on the jupyterlab tile of anaconda navigator allows us to start jupyterlab without typing a command. it is useful for anyone who installed python with anaconda and prefers to click, because navigator already knows which python to use. alternatively, the `jupyter lab` command in a terminal starts it in a chosen folder."},{"sid":"A2.08","title":"Starting JupyterLab Desktop","cmd":"JupyterLab Desktop → Open folder / New notebook","terms":["JupyterLab Desktop","desktop application"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["JupyterLab Desktop","open a notebook app","start Jupyter without the terminal"],"examinable":false,"search":"starting jupyterlab desktop jupyterlab desktop → open folder / new notebook notebooks tools setup non-examinable jupyterlab desktop desktop application jupyterlab desktop open a notebook app start jupyter without the terminal jupyterlab desktop is an ordinary application that runs jupyterlab in its own window, with no web browser and no terminal. it is useful for beginners who want notebooks without installing anaconda or typing commands. alternatively, anaconda navigator or the `jupyter lab` command run the same jupyterlab in a browser."},{"sid":"A2.09","title":"Choosing between JupyterLab and Jupyter Notebook","cmd":"","terms":["JupyterLab or Jupyter Notebook","difference between JupyterLab and Jupyter Notebook","jupyter notebook"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["JupyterLab or Jupyter Notebook","which Jupyter","classic notebook versus lab"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing between jupyterlab and jupyter notebook  notebooks tools non-examinable jupyterlab or jupyter notebook difference between jupyterlab and jupyter notebook jupyter notebook jupyterlab or jupyter notebook which jupyter classic notebook versus lab jupyterlab and jupyter notebook are two programs that open and run the same `.ipynb` files, and the difference is the layout around the notebook. it is useful to know which one is open, because the menus and the way of opening several files differ. alternatively, a notebook can also be opened inside vs code, which has its own layout again."},{"sid":"A2.10","title":"Finding the parts of the JupyterLab window","cmd":"","terms":["Launcher","file browser","sidebar","JupyterLab layout"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["parts of the JupyterLab window","sidebar","file browser","launcher","menu bar"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the parts of the jupyterlab window  notebooks tools non-examinable launcher file browser sidebar jupyterlab layout parts of the jupyterlab window sidebar file browser launcher menu bar jupyterlab has a menu bar, a left sidebar, a main work area and a status bar, and each part has a fixed job."},{"sid":"A2.11","title":"Working with several documents side by side","cmd":"drag a tab to the side of the work area","terms":["multiple documents","split view","side by side","JupyterLab tabs"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["two notebooks side by side","split the work area","arrange tabs"],"examinable":false,"search":"working with several documents side by side drag a tab to the side of the work area notebooks tools non-examinable multiple documents split view side by side jupyterlab tabs two notebooks side by side split the work area arrange tabs dragging a document's tab to the edge of the work area allows jupyterlab to show two or more documents at once. it is useful for comparing two notebooks, or for reading a data file or a script beside the notebook that uses it."},{"sid":"A2.12","title":"Opening two views of one notebook","cmd":"right-click the tab → New View for Notebook","terms":["new view for notebook","two views of one notebook"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["two views of one notebook","see two parts of a notebook","new view"],"examinable":false,"search":"opening two views of one notebook right-click the tab → new view for notebook notebooks tools non-examinable new view for notebook two views of one notebook two views of one notebook see two parts of a notebook new view the command *new view for notebook* allows one notebook to be shown in two panels at once, each scrolled to a different place. it is useful for a long notebook."},{"sid":"A2.13","title":"Running code in a console beside a notebook","cmd":"right-click the tab → New Console for Notebook","terms":["console","new console for notebook","shared kernel"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["console beside a notebook","try code without a cell","scratch console"],"examinable":false,"search":"running code in a console beside a notebook right-click the tab → new console for notebook notebooks tools non-examinable console new console for notebook shared kernel console beside a notebook try code without a cell scratch console a console attached to a notebook allows a line of code to be tried out using the notebook's variables, without adding a cell to the notebook. it is useful for quick checks, such as printing the type of a variable, that do not belong in the saved notebook."},{"sid":"A2.14","title":"Stopping JupyterLab and its kernels","cmd":"File → Shut Down","terms":["shut down","stopping JupyterLab","running kernels"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["stop Jupyter","shut down JupyterLab","close kernels","quit a notebook server"],"examinable":false,"search":"stopping jupyterlab and its kernels file → shut down notebooks tools non-examinable shut down stopping jupyterlab running kernels stop jupyter shut down jupyterlab close kernels quit a notebook server the command *shut down* in the file menu allows us to stop jupyterlab and every kernel that is running. it is useful because closing the browser tab leaves the program running, and each running notebook still uses memory. alternatively, the *running terminals and kernels* panel shuts down single notebooks while jupyterlab stays open."},{"sid":"A2.02","title":"Running a cell with Shift+Enter","cmd":"Shift+Enter","terms":["Shift+Enter","running cells","code cell"],"tags":["notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["run a cell","execute a cell","Shift Enter","run and move on"],"examinable":false,"search":"running a cell with shift+enter shift+enter notebooks non-examinable shift+enter running cells code cell run a cell execute a cell shift enter run and move on pressing `shift+enter` allows us to run the code in the current cell and move to the next one. it is useful when a program is built up and checked one small step at a time. alternatively, the ▶ button in the toolbar runs the current cell."},{"sid":"A2.03","title":"Displaying a value without print","cmd":"","terms":["last line of a cell","print() in notebooks"],"tags":["notebooks","printing","non-examinable"],"task":["show a value without print","last line of a cell","display output","show a variable"],"examinable":false,"search":"displaying a value without print  notebooks printing non-examinable last line of a cell print() in notebooks show a value without print last line of a cell display output show a variable a notebook displays the value of the last line of a cell automatically. it is useful when a value is checked quickly while working. alternatively, `print()` shows every value that is printed, in notebooks and scripts alike."},{"sid":"A2.04","title":"Writing notes in a Markdown cell","cmd":"","terms":["Markdown cell","notes in a notebook"],"tags":["notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["write notes in a notebook","Markdown cell","headings in a notebook","text cell"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing notes in a markdown cell  notebooks non-examinable markdown cell notes in a notebook write notes in a notebook markdown cell headings in a notebook text cell a markdown cell allows us to write headings, notes and lists between the code cells of a notebook. it is useful when a notebook explains what each step does. alternatively, `#` comments explain code inside a code cell."},{"sid":"A2.05","title":"Restarting the kernel and running all cells","cmd":"Kernel > Restart Kernel and Run All Cells","terms":["restart kernel","Run All","hidden state"],"tags":["notebooks","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["restart the kernel","run all cells","clear all variables","start fresh","NameError after editing"],"examinable":false,"search":"restarting the kernel and running all cells kernel > restart kernel and run all cells notebooks debugging non-examinable restart kernel run all hidden state restart the kernel run all cells clear all variables start fresh nameerror after editing restarting the kernel and running all cells allows us to check that a notebook gives the same results from top to bottom with a clean memory."},{"sid":"A2.06","title":"Choosing between a notebook and a script","cmd":"","terms":["notebook or script",".ipynb",".py"],"tags":["notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["notebook or script","when to use a notebook","choose between a notebook and a program"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing between a notebook and a script  notebooks tools non-examinable notebook or script .ipynb .py notebook or script when to use a notebook choose between a notebook and a program notebooks and scripts both run python code, but suit different jobs. it is useful to choose deliberately, because each has strengths that the other lacks. alternatively, a project can use both: a notebook to explore, and scripts for the finished program."},{"sid":"A3.01","title":"Installing a package with `pip`","cmd":"pip install pytest","terms":["pip install","installing packages","package"],"tags":["packages","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["install a package","pip install","add a library","get a package"],"examinable":false,"search":"installing a package with pip pip install pytest packages tools non-examinable pip install installing packages package install a package pip install add a library get a package the `pip install` command allows us to download a package and add it to the python installation that `pip` belongs to. it is useful when a program needs code that does not come with python. alternatively, `conda install` installs packages into an anaconda installation."},{"sid":"A3.02","title":"Installing a package from a notebook with `%pip`","cmd":"%pip install pytest","terms":["%pip install","installing from a notebook"],"tags":["packages","notebooks","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["install in a notebook","%pip install","install from Jupyter","pip in a cell"],"examinable":false,"search":"installing a package from a notebook with %pip %pip install pytest packages notebooks tools non-examinable %pip install installing from a notebook install in a notebook %pip install install from jupyter pip in a cell the `%pip install` command allows us to install a package from inside a notebook cell, into the same python that the notebook uses. it is useful when a notebook reports `modulenotfounderror` and the package is missing. alternatively, `pip install` in a terminal works if the terminal uses the same python as the notebook."},{"sid":"A3.03","title":"Installing a package with `conda`","cmd":"conda install pytest","terms":["conda install"],"tags":["packages","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["install with conda","conda install","Anaconda packages"],"examinable":false,"search":"installing a package with conda conda install pytest packages tools non-examinable conda install install with conda conda install anaconda packages the `conda install` command allows us to install a package into an anaconda installation. it is useful when a package needs other, non-python software that conda can also install. alternatively, `pip install` works inside anaconda too, and is the default in this module."},{"sid":"A3.04","title":"Importing a package and checking it works","cmd":"import pytest","terms":["import","__version__"],"tags":["packages","core","non-examinable"],"task":["check a package works","import a package","is it installed"],"examinable":false,"search":"importing a package and checking it works import pytest packages core non-examinable import __version__ check a package works import a package is it installed the `import` statement allows us to load a package so that its code can be used in a program. it is useful when checking that an installation worked, before writing code that depends on the package. alternatively, `pip show pytest` in a terminal reports whether, and where, a package is installed."},{"sid":"A3.05","title":"ModuleNotFoundError: no module named","cmd":"","terms":["ModuleNotFoundError","wrong environment"],"tags":["packages","errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["no module named","ModuleNotFoundError","import fails","package not found"],"examinable":false,"search":"modulenotfounderror: no module named  packages errors debugging non-examinable modulenotfounderror wrong environment no module named modulenotfounderror import fails package not found a `modulenotfounderror` tells us that python could not find a package with the name given after `import`."},{"sid":"A3.06","title":"Checking which Python is running","cmd":"sys.executable","terms":["sys.executable","which Python"],"tags":["packages","tools","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["which Python is running","sys.executable","wrong environment","where is Python"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking which python is running sys.executable packages tools debugging non-examinable sys.executable which python which python is running sys.executable wrong environment where is python the `sys.executable` value allows us to see the full path of the python program that is running our code. it is useful when a package is installed but still cannot be imported, because it shows which installation is in use. alternatively, the interpreter name in the bottom-right corner of vs code shows the same thing."},{"sid":"A3.07","title":"Updating `pip`","cmd":"python -m pip install --upgrade pip","terms":["upgrading pip","pip version"],"tags":["packages","tools","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["update pip","upgrade pip","pip is out of date"],"examinable":false,"search":"updating pip python -m pip install --upgrade pip packages tools setup non-examinable upgrading pip pip version update pip upgrade pip pip is out of date the command `python -m pip install --upgrade pip` allows the installer to be updated to its latest version. it is useful when `pip` prints a notice that a new version is available, or when an install fails with an old version. alternatively, an old `pip` often works, so the update is not needed unless a problem appears."},{"sid":"A4.01","title":"Virtual environments","cmd":"","terms":["virtual environment","environment"],"tags":["environments","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["what is a virtual environment","separate packages per project","why use environments"],"examinable":false,"search":"virtual environments  environments tools non-examinable virtual environment environment what is a virtual environment separate packages per project why use environments a virtual environment is a separate copy of python, with its own installed packages, kept inside one project folder."},{"sid":"A4.02","title":"Creating a virtual environment with `venv`","cmd":"python -m venv .venv","terms":["venv","python -m venv"],"tags":["environments","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["create a virtual environment","venv","new environment for a project","python -m venv"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating a virtual environment with venv python -m venv .venv environments tools non-examinable venv python -m venv create a virtual environment venv new environment for a project python -m venv the `python -m venv` command allows us to create a virtual environment in a folder of the project. it is useful when a new project starts. alternatively, `conda create` makes an anaconda environment instead."},{"sid":"A4.03","title":"Activating a virtual environment","cmd":"source .venv/bin/activate","terms":["activate","deactivate"],"tags":["environments","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["activate an environment","turn on the venv","source activate","Scripts activate"],"examinable":false,"search":"activating a virtual environment source .venv/bin/activate environments tools non-examinable activate deactivate activate an environment turn on the venv source activate scripts activate activating an environment allows us to make the terminal use that environment's python and pip. it is useful before installing packages for the project or running its scripts from the terminal. alternatively, vs code activates the environment automatically once it is chosen as the interpreter."},{"sid":"A4.04","title":"Recording and reinstalling packages with `requirements.txt`","cmd":"pip freeze > requirements.txt","terms":["requirements.txt","pip freeze","pip install -r"],"tags":["environments","packages","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["requirements.txt","save the list of packages","pip freeze","reinstall packages","share dependencies"],"examinable":false,"search":"recording and reinstalling packages with requirements.txt pip freeze > requirements.txt environments packages tools non-examinable requirements.txt pip freeze pip install -r requirements.txt save the list of packages pip freeze reinstall packages share dependencies a `requirements.txt` file allows us to record the packages a project needs, so that the environment can be rebuilt on another computer. it is useful when work is shared, submitted or moved."},{"sid":"A4.05","title":"Using the environment in VS Code and Jupyter","cmd":"","terms":["kernel","ipykernel"],"tags":["environments","tools","notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["use the environment in VS Code","select the venv in Jupyter","set the kernel for an environment"],"examinable":false,"search":"using the environment in vs code and jupyter  environments tools notebooks non-examinable kernel ipykernel use the environment in vs code select the venv in jupyter set the kernel for an environment choosing the environment as the interpreter or kernel allows us to make vs code and jupyterlab run code with the project's packages. it is useful when a package is installed in the environment but an editor still reports it as missing. alternatively, scripts can always be run from a terminal in which the environment is active."},{"sid":"A4.06","title":"Using a conda environment instead","cmd":"conda create --name env_name python=3.12","terms":["conda create","conda activate"],"tags":["environments","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["conda environment","create a conda env","conda create","environment with a Python version"],"examinable":false,"search":"using a conda environment instead conda create --name env_name python=3.12 environments tools non-examinable conda create conda activate conda environment create a conda env conda create environment with a python version the `conda create` command allows us to make a separate environment with anaconda, stored centrally rather than in the project folder. it is useful when anaconda is already in use and a project needs a particular python version. alternatively, newer tools such as `uv` create and fill a `.venv` folder very quickly with one command (`uv venv`, `uv pip install`)."},{"sid":"A5.01","title":"Keeping a history of a project with Git","cmd":"","terms":["version control","Git","repository","commit"],"tags":["git","tools","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["what is Git","version control","keep a history of changes","why use Git"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping a history of a project with git  git tools setup non-examinable version control git repository commit what is git version control keep a history of changes why use git git is a tool that records snapshots of a project, called commits, so that earlier versions can be seen and restored. it is useful because a working program can be changed safely, and a change that breaks it can be undone. alternatively, copies named `final`, `final2` and `final_really` hold versions too, but cannot say what changed."},{"sid":"A5.02","title":"Starting a repository","cmd":"git init","terms":["git init","git config","starting a repository"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["start a repository","git init","begin tracking a project"],"examinable":false,"search":"starting a repository git init git tools non-examinable git init git config starting a repository start a repository git init begin tracking a project the command `git init` allows git to start watching the folder that the terminal is in. it is useful at the start of a project, before the first commit. alternatively, `git clone` copies a repository that already exists."},{"sid":"A5.03","title":"Saving a snapshot with `add` and `commit`","cmd":"git add .   git commit -m \"message\"","terms":["git add","git commit","staging"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["save a snapshot","commit changes","git add and git commit","save my work in Git"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving a snapshot with add and commit git add .   git commit -m \"message\" git tools non-examinable git add git commit staging save a snapshot commit changes git add and git commit save my work in git the commands `git add` and `git commit` allow a snapshot of the chosen files to be saved with a message. it is useful after each small piece of work that runs."},{"sid":"A5.04","title":"Seeing what has changed","cmd":"git status   git diff","terms":["git status","git diff","changes"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["what has changed","git status","git diff","see modified files"],"examinable":false,"search":"seeing what has changed git status   git diff git tools non-examinable git status git diff changes what has changed git status git diff see modified files the commands `git status` and `git diff` allow us to see which files have changed since the last commit, and what the changes are. it is useful before a commit, to check that only the intended changes are saved."},{"sid":"A5.05","title":"Looking at the history of commits","cmd":"git log --oneline","terms":["git log","history","commit hash"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["history of commits","git log","look at past versions"],"examinable":false,"search":"looking at the history of commits git log --oneline git tools non-examinable git log history commit hash history of commits git log look at past versions the command `git log --oneline` allows the list of commits to be seen, with the newest first. it is useful for finding when a change was made, or which version to return to. alternatively, the history can be browsed in vs code, which shows the same list with buttons."},{"sid":"A5.06","title":"Leaving files out of a repository with `.gitignore`","cmd":".gitignore","terms":[".gitignore","ignoring files","virtual environment"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["ignore files",".gitignore","leave out data files","do not track a folder"],"examinable":false,"search":"leaving files out of a repository with .gitignore .gitignore git tools non-examinable .gitignore ignoring files virtual environment ignore files .gitignore leave out data files do not track a folder a file called `.gitignore` allows a list of names and patterns to be kept out of the repository. it is useful for files that should not be saved, such as a virtual environment, caches or private data. alternatively, careful use of `git add` avoids them, but `.gitignore` also protects against `git add .`."},{"sid":"A5.07","title":"Copying a project to GitHub and back","cmd":"git remote add origin ...   git push   git clone","terms":["GitHub","git push","git clone","remote"],"tags":["git","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["put a project on GitHub","push","clone a repository","copy a project from GitHub"],"examinable":false,"search":"copying a project to github and back git remote add origin ...   git push   git clone git tools non-examinable github git push git clone remote put a project on github push clone a repository copy a project from github github is a web service that stores repositories, and the commands `git push` and `git clone` copy a project to it and from it. it is useful for a backup, for working on more than one computer, and for sharing a project."},{"sid":"B1.01","title":"Creating a variable","cmd":"name = value","terms":["variable","assignment (=)"],"tags":["variables","core"],"task":["make a variable","assign a value","name a value","store a number","create a variable"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a variable name = value variables core variable assignment (=) make a variable assign a value name a value store a number create a variable the `=` sign allows us to store a value under a name, so that it can be used later in the program. it is useful when the same value is needed more than once, or when a value has a meaning that a name makes clear."},{"sid":"B1.02","title":"Changing the value of a variable","cmd":"count = count - n","terms":["changing a variable","updating a variable"],"tags":["variables","core"],"task":["change a variable","update a value","add one to a count","reassign"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing the value of a variable count = count - n variables core changing a variable updating a variable change a variable update a value add one to a count reassign assigning to an existing name allows us to replace the value that it stores. it is useful when something changes while the program runs. alternatively, a new name can be used for the new value when the old value is still needed."},{"sid":"B1.03","title":"Rules for variable names","cmd":"","terms":["variable names","naming rules","snake_case"],"tags":["variables","core","style"],"task":["valid variable names","naming rules","what can I call a variable","snake_case"],"examinable":true,"search":"rules for variable names  variables core style variable names naming rules snake_case valid variable names naming rules what can i call a variable snake_case python's naming rules allow us to choose names that python accepts and that people can read."},{"sid":"B1.04","title":"NameError: name is not defined","cmd":"","terms":["NameError","misspelt variable"],"tags":["variables","errors","debugging"],"task":["name not defined","NameError","variable does not exist","typo in a variable name"],"examinable":true,"search":"nameerror: name is not defined  variables errors debugging nameerror misspelt variable name not defined nameerror variable does not exist typo in a variable name a `nameerror` tells us that the program used a name that has not been given a value."},{"sid":"B1.05","title":"Variables as labels","cmd":"","terms":["labels","variables as labels"],"tags":["variables","core","objects"],"task":["what is a variable","variable as a label","two names for one value","assignment"],"examinable":true,"search":"variables as labels  variables core objects labels variables as labels what is a variable variable as a label two names for one value assignment a variable is a label attached to a value, not a box that holds it."},{"sid":"B1.06","title":"Assigning several variables at once","cmd":"a, b = value_1, value_2","terms":["multiple assignment"],"tags":["variables","core"],"task":["assign two variables at once","swap two values","multiple assignment"],"examinable":true,"search":"assigning several variables at once a, b = value_1, value_2 variables core multiple assignment assign two variables at once swap two values multiple assignment multiple assignment allows us to give values to several variables in one line. it is useful when a few values belong together. alternatively, one variable per line is clearer when the values are unrelated."},{"sid":"B1.07","title":"Using a constant for a fixed value","cmd":"VAT_RATE = 0.2","terms":["constants","UPPER_CASE names"],"tags":["variables","core","style"],"task":["constant","fixed value","named constant","VAT rate","uppercase name"],"examinable":true,"search":"using a constant for a fixed value vat_rate = 0.2 variables core style constants upper_case names constant fixed value named constant vat rate uppercase name a constant is a variable whose value is meant to stay the same for the whole program, written in capital letters. it is useful when a fixed value such as a tax rate is used in several places, so that it is changed in one place only."},{"sid":"B2.01","title":"Arithmetic with numbers","cmd":"total = a * b + c","terms":["arithmetic","+ - * /","operators"],"tags":["numbers","core"],"task":["do arithmetic","add subtract multiply divide","calculate in Python","Python as a calculator"],"examinable":true,"search":"arithmetic with numbers total = a * b + c numbers core arithmetic + - * / operators do arithmetic add subtract multiply divide calculate in python python as a calculator the operators `+`, `-`, `*` and `/` allow us to add, subtract, multiply and divide numbers. it is useful when a program calculates totals, differences or averages. alternatively, the built-in function `sum()` adds up a whole list of numbers."},{"sid":"B2.02","title":"Powers with `**`","cmd":"result = x ** 2","terms":["** (power)","exponent"],"tags":["numbers","core"],"task":["square a number","power","exponent","raise to a power","**"],"examinable":true,"search":"powers with ** result = x ** 2 numbers core ** (power) exponent square a number power exponent raise to a power ** the `**` operator allows us to raise a number to a power. it is useful when a formula squares a length. alternatively, `side * side` gives the same result for a square."},{"sid":"B2.03","title":"Mixing integers and floats","cmd":"","terms":["int and float","integers","floats"],"tags":["numbers","core","types"],"task":["integers and floats","why do I get a decimal","int versus float","mixed arithmetic"],"examinable":true,"search":"mixing integers and floats  numbers core types int and float integers floats integers and floats why do i get a decimal int versus float mixed arithmetic python stores whole numbers as integers (`int`) and numbers with a decimal point as floats (`float`)."},{"sid":"B2.04","title":"Writing large numbers with underscores","cmd":"number = 1_250_000","terms":["underscores in numbers","large numbers"],"tags":["numbers","core","style"],"task":["write a big number","underscore in a number","readable large numbers"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing large numbers with underscores number = 1_250_000 numbers core style underscores in numbers large numbers write a big number underscore in a number readable large numbers underscores inside a number allow us to group its digits so that a large value is clearer to read. it is useful when typing values such as populations or file sizes. alternatively, `f\"{visitors:,}\"` adds commas when a large number is printed."},{"sid":"B2.05","title":"Finding the remainder with `%`","cmd":"remainder = a % b","terms":["% (modulo)","remainder"],"tags":["numbers","core"],"task":["remainder","modulo","what is left over","%"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding the remainder with % remainder = a % b numbers core % (modulo) remainder remainder modulo what is left over % the modulo operator `%` allows us to find the remainder when one whole number is divided by another. it is useful when checking whether items share out exactly. alternatively, `//` gives the number of whole boxes, and the two are often used together."},{"sid":"B2.09","title":"Testing whether a number is even or odd","cmd":"number % 2 == 0","terms":["even and odd","remainder test","divisible"],"tags":["numbers","core"],"task":["is a number even","odd or even","test divisibility","check evenness"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing whether a number is even or odd number % 2 == 0 numbers core even and odd remainder test divisible is a number even odd or even test divisibility check evenness if we have a whole number, then the remainder after dividing by 2 shows whether it is even or odd, and the same test works for any divisor. it is useful for checks such as whether a pack of items can be shared equally between two shelves. alternatively, a comparison with a list of known values works for a few numbers, but not for any number."},{"sid":"B2.06","title":"Dividing and rounding down with `//`","cmd":"full_boxes = eggs // box_size","terms":["// (floor division)","whole-number division"],"tags":["numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["integer division","divide and round down","how many whole boxes","//"],"examinable":false,"search":"dividing and rounding down with // full_boxes = eggs // box_size numbers core non-examinable // (floor division) whole-number division integer division divide and round down how many whole boxes // the `//` operator allows us to divide and keep only the whole-number part of the answer. it is useful when counting how many complete groups can be made. alternatively, `/` gives the exact answer as a float."},{"sid":"B2.07","title":"Rounding a number with `round()`","cmd":"round(number, 1)","terms":["round()","decimal places"],"tags":["numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["round a number","decimal places","round to 2 places","round()"],"examinable":false,"search":"rounding a number with round() round(number, 1) numbers core non-examinable round() decimal places round a number decimal places round to 2 places round() the `round()` function allows us to round a number to a chosen number of decimal places. it is useful when a calculated value has more decimal places than are meaningful. alternatively, an f-string such as `f\"{average:.1f}\"` rounds only the printed value and leaves the stored value unchanged."},{"sid":"B2.08","title":"Inexact floats","cmd":"","terms":["float rounding","0.1 + 0.2"],"tags":["numbers","core","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["0.1 + 0.2","floating point error","inexact decimals","why does Python give 0.30000000000000004"],"examinable":false,"search":"inexact floats  numbers core debugging non-examinable float rounding 0.1 + 0.2 0.1 + 0.2 floating point error inexact decimals why does python give 0.30000000000000004 floats are stored in binary, so most decimal fractions are stored very slightly inaccurately."},{"sid":"B3.01","title":"Objects and types","cmd":"type(value)","terms":["object","type","what is an object","int float str bool"],"tags":["objects","types","core"],"task":["what type is this","objects and types","type of a value","everything is an object"],"examinable":true,"search":"objects and types type(value) objects types core object type what is an object int float str bool what type is this objects and types type of a value everything is an object every value in python is an object, and every object has a type that decides what it can do. whole numbers are `int` objects, decimal numbers are `float` objects, text is `str` objects, and `true` and `false` are `bool` objects. lists, dictionaries and the instances of classes are objects too."},{"sid":"B3.02","title":"Calling a method with a dot","cmd":"text.upper()   len(text)","terms":["method","dot notation","method call","function or method"],"tags":["objects","methods","core"],"task":["call a method","dot notation","text.upper()","use a method"],"examinable":true,"search":"calling a method with a dot text.upper()   len(text) objects methods core method dot notation method call function or method call a method dot notation text.upper() use a method a method is an action that belongs to an object, and it is called by writing the object, a dot, the method name and brackets. a function such as `len()` is written first and is given the value in brackets. both give back a result, and the difference is where the action belongs: `upper()` is part of what a string can do, so it is called on a string. it is useful to know the difference, because it decides how the code is written."},{"sid":"B3.03","title":"Methods and new values","cmd":"text = text.upper()","terms":["strings cannot be changed","method returns a new value","immutable string"],"tags":["objects","methods","strings"],"task":["method does not change the variable","save the result of a method","strings are unchangeable","assign the result"],"examinable":true,"search":"methods and new values text = text.upper() objects methods strings strings cannot be changed method returns a new value immutable string method does not change the variable save the result of a method strings are unchangeable assign the result a string cannot be changed after it is made, so a string method gives back a new string and leaves the original alone. a line such as `item.upper()` on its own makes the new string and then loses it, so the result is stored in a name to keep it."},{"sid":"B3.04","title":"Finding the methods of a type with `dir` and `help`","cmd":"dir(str)   help(str.upper)","terms":["dir","help","list the methods of a type","documentation in the program"],"tags":["objects","methods","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["find the methods of a type","what can a string do","dir and help","list of methods"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the methods of a type with dir and help dir(str)   help(str.upper) objects methods tools non-examinable dir help list the methods of a type documentation in the program find the methods of a type what can a string do dir and help list of methods the function `dir()` lists the names that belong to a type, including its methods, and `help()` prints the documentation of one of them. it is useful for finding what an object can do without leaving the program. editors and notebooks also show the methods after the dot, and the tab key completes them."},{"sid":"B3.05","title":"Numbers as objects","cmd":"(2.5).is_integer()   (5).bit_length()","terms":["methods of int","methods of float","numbers are objects"],"tags":["objects","numbers","types"],"task":["methods on numbers","number as an object","is_integer"],"examinable":true,"search":"numbers as objects (2.5).is_integer()   (5).bit_length() objects numbers types methods of int methods of float numbers are objects methods on numbers number as an object is_integer whole numbers and decimal numbers are objects as well, but they have only a few methods, so number work mostly uses operators and functions such as `+`, `abs()` and `round()`. the brackets around a number are needed so that python does not read the dot as part of a decimal point."},{"sid":"B3.06","title":"Chaining methods one after another","cmd":"text.strip().lower().replace(old, new)","terms":["method chaining","chain methods","call methods in a row"],"tags":["objects","methods","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["chain methods","several methods in a row","strip then lower","method chaining"],"examinable":false,"search":"chaining methods one after another text.strip().lower().replace(old, new) objects methods strings non-examinable method chaining chain methods call methods in a row chain methods several methods in a row strip then lower method chaining because each method returns a new object, another method can be called on the result straight away, and a row of calls reads from left to right. it is useful when a value passes through several small changes."},{"sid":"B3.07","title":"Types and their methods","cmd":"str   int   float   list   dict","terms":["types and their methods","which methods","summary of types"],"tags":["objects","types","methods"],"task":["types and their methods","str int float list dict","which methods belong to which type"],"examinable":true,"search":"types and their methods str   int   float   list   dict objects types methods types and their methods which methods summary of types types and their methods str int float list dict which methods belong to which type each type has its own methods, so the type of a value decides what can be called on it. a method that belongs to one type gives an `attributeerror` when called on another."},{"sid":"B4.01","title":"Creating a string","cmd":"text = \"some text\"","terms":["string","quotes"],"tags":["strings","core"],"task":["make a string","text in quotes","single or double quotes","create text"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a string text = \"some text\" strings core string quotes make a string text in quotes single or double quotes create text quotation marks allow us to store text, called a string, in a variable. it is useful whenever a program handles names, labels or messages. alternatively, single quotes work in the same way; double quotes are used in this guide."},{"sid":"B4.02","title":"Changing the case of a string","cmd":"text.title()   text.upper()   text.lower()","terms":["title()","upper()","lower()","method"],"tags":["strings","core"],"task":["change the case","make uppercase","make lowercase","capitalise each word","title case"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing the case of a string text.title()   text.upper()   text.lower() strings core title() upper() lower() method change the case make uppercase make lowercase capitalise each word title case the string methods `title()`, `upper()` and `lower()` allow us to change the capital letters in a piece of text. it is useful when text typed in different ways must look the same, or be compared. alternatively, `capitalize()` makes only the first letter a capital."},{"sid":"B4.03","title":"Putting values into a string with an f-string","cmd":"f\"{value} text {number:.2f}\"","terms":["f-string","formatting numbers","{}"],"tags":["strings","core","printing"],"task":["put a variable in text","f-string","format a number in text","insert values in a string"],"examinable":true,"search":"putting values into a string with an f-string f\"{value} text {number:.2f}\" strings core printing f-string formatting numbers {} put a variable in text f-string format a number in text insert values in a string an f-string allows us to place the values of variables inside a piece of text. it is useful when printing results with labels and units. alternatively, strings can be joined with `+`, but numbers must then be converted with `str()` first."},{"sid":"B4.04","title":"Adding tabs and newlines to a string","cmd":"\"\\n\"   \"\\t\"","terms":["\\n (newline)","\\t (tab)","whitespace"],"tags":["strings","core","printing"],"task":["new line in a string","tab in a string","special characters","backslash n"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding tabs and newlines to a string \"\\n\"   \"\\t\" strings core printing \\n (newline) \\t (tab) whitespace new line in a string tab in a string special characters backslash n the codes `\\n` and `\\t` allow us to start a new line and add a tab inside a string. it is useful when printing a layout. alternatively, several `print()` calls each start their own line."},{"sid":"B4.05","title":"Stripping whitespace from a string","cmd":"text.strip()   text.lstrip()   text.rstrip()","terms":["strip()","lstrip()","rstrip()"],"tags":["strings","core"],"task":["remove spaces","trim a string","strip whitespace","clean input"],"examinable":true,"search":"stripping whitespace from a string text.strip()   text.lstrip()   text.rstrip() strings core strip() lstrip() rstrip() remove spaces trim a string strip whitespace clean input the `strip()` method allows us to remove spaces, tabs and newlines from both ends of a string. it is useful when text has been typed or read from a file and has stray spaces that would make comparisons fail. alternatively, `lstrip()` and `rstrip()` remove whitespace from the left or right end only."},{"sid":"B4.06","title":"Removing a prefix from a string","cmd":"text.removeprefix(prefix)   text.removesuffix(suffix)","terms":["removeprefix()","removesuffix()"],"tags":["strings","core"],"task":["remove a prefix","remove a suffix","cut off the start of a string","strip a file extension"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing a prefix from a string text.removeprefix(prefix)   text.removesuffix(suffix) strings core removeprefix() removesuffix() remove a prefix remove a suffix cut off the start of a string strip a file extension the `removeprefix()` method allows us to remove a known piece of text from the start of a string. it is useful when codes share a fixed start. alternatively, `removesuffix()` removes text from the end, such as `.txt` from a file name."},{"sid":"B4.07","title":"SyntaxError: unterminated string literal","cmd":"","terms":["SyntaxError","unterminated string","apostrophe"],"tags":["strings","errors","debugging"],"task":["unterminated string","SyntaxError with quotes","missing closing quote"],"examinable":true,"search":"syntaxerror: unterminated string literal  strings errors debugging syntaxerror unterminated string apostrophe unterminated string syntaxerror with quotes missing closing quote a `syntaxerror` tells us that python cannot read the program, so nothing in it runs."},{"sid":"B4.08","title":"Joining strings with `+`","cmd":"text = text_1 + text_2","terms":["+ (joining strings)","concatenation"],"tags":["strings","core"],"task":["join two strings","concatenate","add strings together","plus with text"],"examinable":true,"search":"joining strings with + text = text_1 + text_2 strings core + (joining strings) concatenation join two strings concatenate add strings together plus with text the `+` operator allows us to join strings end to end. it is useful when building a short label from two or three pieces of text. alternatively, an f-string is clearer to read and converts numbers automatically."},{"sid":"B4.09","title":"Finding the length of a string with `len()`","cmd":"len(text)","terms":["len() of a string"],"tags":["strings","core","non-examinable"],"task":["length of a string","how many characters","len of text","count characters"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the length of a string with len() len(text) strings core non-examinable len() of a string length of a string how many characters len of text count characters the `len()` function allows us to count the characters in a string, including spaces. it is useful when checking that a code has the expected number of characters. alternatively, `len()` also counts the items in a list."},{"sid":"B4.10","title":"Splitting a string into a list with `split()`","cmd":"text.split(separator)","terms":["split()","splitting text"],"tags":["strings","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["split a string","break text into words","split on commas","text to list"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting a string into a list with split() text.split(separator) strings lists non-examinable split() splitting text split a string break text into words split on commas text to list the `split()` method allows us to break a string into a list of smaller strings at a chosen separator. it is useful for text that holds several values. alternatively, slicing takes fixed positions, but `split()` works whatever the length of each piece."},{"sid":"B4.11","title":"Joining a list into a string with `join()`","cmd":"separator.join(items)","terms":["join()","joining text"],"tags":["strings","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["join a list into a string","put words together with commas","join","list to text"],"examinable":false,"search":"joining a list into a string with join() separator.join(items) strings lists non-examinable join() joining text join a list into a string put words together with commas join list to text the `join()` method allows us to combine the items of a list into one string, with a chosen separator between them. it is useful for turning a list into a readable line."},{"sid":"B4.12","title":"Replacing part of a string with `replace()`","cmd":"text.replace(old, new)","terms":["replace()","replacing text"],"tags":["strings","non-examinable"],"task":["replace text","find and replace in a string","swap characters","change part of a string"],"examinable":false,"search":"replacing part of a string with replace() text.replace(old, new) strings non-examinable replace() replacing text replace text find and replace in a string swap characters change part of a string the `replace()` method allows us to swap every copy of one piece of text for another. it is useful for tidying text, such as changing separators or correcting a repeated typing mistake. alternatively, a loop could rebuild the string, but `replace()` is a single call."},{"sid":"B4.13","title":"Testing whether text contains other text with `in`","cmd":"part in text","terms":["in for strings","text contains","not in"],"tags":["strings","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["does text contain","is a word in a string","substring test","in operator for text"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing whether text contains other text with in part in text strings conditions non-examinable in for strings text contains not in does text contain is a word in a string substring test in operator for text the words `in` and `not in` allow us to test whether one piece of text appears inside another. it is useful for checking a code or a word in a message. alternatively, `find()` also locates the text, but gives its position and not `true` or `false`."},{"sid":"B4.14","title":"Finding the position of text with `find()`","cmd":"text.find(part)","terms":["find()","position of text"],"tags":["strings","non-examinable"],"task":["position of text","find where a word starts","index of a substring","find returns minus one"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the position of text with find() text.find(part) strings non-examinable find() position of text position of text find where a word starts index of a substring find returns minus one the `find()` method allows us to ask where a piece of text starts, counting from 0. it is useful for splitting a string at a position that is not fixed. alternatively, `in` only says whether the text is present."},{"sid":"B4.15","title":"Reading part of a string by index and slice","cmd":"text[start:stop], text[-1]","terms":["string index","string slice"],"tags":["strings","slices","non-examinable"],"task":["get part of a string","slice a string","first character","last character","substring"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading part of a string by index and slice text[start:stop], text[-1] strings slices non-examinable string index string slice get part of a string slice a string first character last character substring an index or a slice in square brackets allows us to read one character or a part of a string, as for a list. it is useful for codes with a fixed layout. alternatively, `split()` suits text with separators, and a slice suits text with fixed positions."},{"sid":"B4.16","title":"Testing whether text is all digits with `isdigit()`","cmd":"answer.isdigit()","terms":["isdigit()","checking digits","isalpha()"],"tags":["strings","input","non-examinable"],"task":["is the answer a number","check input is digits","isdigit","validate numeric text"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing whether text is all digits with isdigit() answer.isdigit() strings input non-examinable isdigit() checking digits isalpha() is the answer a number check input is digits isdigit validate numeric text the `isdigit()` method allows us to ask whether a string contains only the digits 0 to 9. it is useful before `int()`, so that text typed by a user is checked instead of causing a `valueerror`. alternatively, a `try` block catches the error after it happens, so it also accepts negative numbers, and decimals when `float()` is used."},{"sid":"B5.01","title":"Checking a value's type with `type()`","cmd":"type(value)","terms":["type()","int","float","str","bool"],"tags":["types","core","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["what type is this value","check a type","is it a string or a number","type()"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a value's type with type() type(value) types core debugging non-examinable type() int float str bool what type is this value check a type is it a string or a number type() the `type()` function allows us to find out what kind of value a variable holds. it is useful when a calculation behaves strangely. alternatively, printing the value inside square brackets also reveals text that looks like a number."},{"sid":"B5.02","title":"Using `True` and `False` values","cmd":"","terms":["True","False","boolean"],"tags":["types","core","conditions"],"task":["True and False","booleans","yes no values","truth values"],"examinable":true,"search":"using true and false values  types core conditions true false boolean true and false booleans yes no values truth values a boolean value is either `true` or `false`, and is the result of every comparison."},{"sid":"B5.03","title":"Converting text to a number with `int()`","cmd":"int(text)","terms":["int()","converting text to numbers"],"tags":["types","core","input"],"task":["text to integer","convert string to int","turn input into a number","int()"],"examinable":true,"search":"converting text to a number with int() int(text) types core input int() converting text to numbers text to integer convert string to int turn input into a number int() the `int()` function allows us to turn text that contains a whole number into an integer that can be used in calculations. it is useful because `input()` always gives text, even when a number is typed. alternatively, `float()` converts text that contains a decimal point."},{"sid":"B5.04","title":"Converting with `float()` and `str()`","cmd":"float(text)","terms":["float()","str()"],"tags":["types","core","non-examinable"],"task":["text to a decimal number","number to text","float","str","convert types"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting with float() and str() float(text) types core non-examinable float() str() text to a decimal number number to text float str convert types the `float()` and `str()` functions allow us to convert text to a decimal number, and a number to text. it is useful when a price arrives as text, or when a number must be joined to a string with `+`. alternatively, an f-string turns numbers into text automatically."},{"sid":"B5.05","title":"ValueError: invalid literal for int()","cmd":"","terms":["ValueError","invalid literal"],"tags":["types","errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["invalid literal for int","ValueError","cannot convert text to number","int of a decimal string"],"examinable":false,"search":"valueerror: invalid literal for int()  types errors debugging non-examinable valueerror invalid literal invalid literal for int valueerror cannot convert text to number int of a decimal string a `valueerror` from `int()` tells us that the text does not contain a whole number."},{"sid":"B5.06","title":"Using `None` for a missing value","cmd":"reading = None","terms":["None","missing value"],"tags":["types","core","non-examinable"],"task":["missing value","None","nothing","no value yet","empty value"],"examinable":false,"search":"using none for a missing value reading = none types core non-examinable none missing value missing value none nothing no value yet empty value the value `none` means \"no value\", and has a type of its own (`nonetype`). it is useful when something is not known yet. alternatively, a number such as 0 could be used, but then it cannot be told apart from a real reading of 0."},{"sid":"C1.01","title":"Creating a list","cmd":"items = [item_1, item_2, item_3]","terms":["list","square brackets"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["make a list","store several values","list of items","square brackets"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a list items = [item_1, item_2, item_3] lists core list square brackets make a list store several values list of items square brackets square brackets allow us to store several values, in order, under one name. it is useful when a program handles a group of similar things."},{"sid":"C1.02","title":"Reading an item from a list by index","cmd":"items[0]","terms":["index","shelf[0]","negative index","[-1]"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["get an item from a list","first item","index of a list","item by position"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading an item from a list by index items[0] lists core index shelf[0] negative index [-1] get an item from a list first item index of a list item by position an index in square brackets allows us to read one item from a list. it is useful when a program needs the first item, the last item, or an item at a known position."},{"sid":"C1.03","title":"Using a list item in a message","cmd":"f\"text {items[0]}\"","terms":["list item in an f-string"],"tags":["lists","core","printing"],"task":["use a list item in a message","item in an f-string","print an item from a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"using a list item in a message f\"text {items[0]}\" lists core printing list item in an f-string use a list item in a message item in an f-string print an item from a list placing an indexed item inside an f-string allows us to build a message from a list. it is useful for labels, receipts and reports that quote one item. alternatively, the item can be stored in a variable first, which is clearer when it is used several times."},{"sid":"C1.04","title":"Changing an item in a list","cmd":"items[1] = value","terms":["changing a list item","assignment by index"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["change an item in a list","replace a list element","update by index"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing an item in a list items[1] = value lists core changing a list item assignment by index change an item in a list replace a list element update by index assigning to an index allows us to replace one item of a list. it is useful when a value changes while the program runs."},{"sid":"C1.05","title":"Adding an item to the end of a list","cmd":"items.append(value)","terms":["append()","adding to a list"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["add to a list","append","add at the end","grow a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding an item to the end of a list items.append(value) lists core append() adding to a list add to a list append add at the end grow a list the `append()` method allows us to add one item to the end of a list. it is useful when a list grows while the program runs. alternatively, `insert()` adds an item at a chosen position instead of the end."},{"sid":"C1.06","title":"Inserting an item at a position","cmd":"items.insert(index, value)","terms":["insert()"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["insert in a list","add at a position","put an item at the start"],"examinable":true,"search":"inserting an item at a position items.insert(index, value) lists core insert() insert in a list add at a position put an item at the start the `insert()` method allows us to place an item at a chosen position, moving the later items along. it is useful when the order of the items matters. alternatively, `append()` is simpler when the item belongs at the end."},{"sid":"C1.17","title":"Adding several items with `extend()`","cmd":"items.extend(more_items)","terms":["extend()","+= on a list","adding several items"],"tags":["lists","core","non-examinable"],"task":["add several items","extend a list","add a list to a list","join lists in place"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding several items with extend() items.extend(more_items) lists core non-examinable extend() += on a list adding several items add several items extend a list add a list to a list join lists in place the `extend()` method allows us to add every item of another list to the end of a list. it is useful when a group of items arrives at once, such as a second bag of shopping. alternatively, `append()` adds one item; given a list, it adds the whole list as a single item."},{"sid":"C1.07","title":"Removing an item by position with `del`","cmd":"del items[index]","terms":["del","deleting a list item"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["delete from a list","remove by position","del","remove the nth item"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing an item by position with del del items[index] lists core del deleting a list item delete from a list remove by position del remove the nth item the `del` statement allows us to delete the item at a given index. it is useful when the position of the unwanted item is known and the item itself is not needed again. alternatively, `pop()` removes an item and also gives it back."},{"sid":"C1.08","title":"Removing and using the last item with `pop()`","cmd":"last = items.pop()","terms":["pop()","pop(index)"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["remove and use the last item","pop","take the last element","stack"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing and using the last item with pop() last = items.pop() lists core pop() pop(index) remove and use the last item pop take the last element stack the `pop()` method allows us to remove an item from a list and keep it in a variable. it is useful when the removed item is still needed. alternatively, `del` removes an item without giving it back."},{"sid":"C1.09","title":"Removing an item by value with `remove()`","cmd":"items.remove(value)","terms":["remove()"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["remove a value from a list","remove by value","delete a matching item"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing an item by value with remove() items.remove(value) lists core remove() remove a value from a list remove by value delete a matching item the `remove()` method allows us to delete an item by its value instead of its position. it is useful when the item is known but its position is not. alternatively, `del` and `pop()` work from the position."},{"sid":"C1.10","title":"Sorting a list","cmd":"sorted(items), items.sort()","terms":["sort()","sorted()","sorting"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["sort a list","order from smallest","sorted versus sort","alphabetical order","keep original order"],"examinable":true,"search":"sorting a list sorted(items), items.sort() lists core sort() sorted() sorting sort a list order from smallest sorted versus sort alphabetical order keep original order the `sort()` method puts a list in order permanently, while the `sorted()` function gives back an ordered copy and leaves the list alone. it is useful for ordered reports such as lowest to highest price. alternatively, `max()` and `min()` find only the largest or smallest item."},{"sid":"C1.11","title":"Reversing a list","cmd":"items.reverse()","terms":["reverse()"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["reverse a list","backwards","flip the order"],"examinable":true,"search":"reversing a list items.reverse() lists core reverse() reverse a list backwards flip the order the `reverse()` method allows us to turn a list around, so that the last item comes first. it is useful when items were recorded oldest first but are wanted newest first. alternatively, `sort(reverse=true)` orders the items by value instead of turning them around."},{"sid":"C1.12","title":"Finding the number of items in a list","cmd":"len(items)","terms":["len()","length of a list"],"tags":["lists","core"],"task":["how many items in a list","length of a list","count the items","len"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding the number of items in a list len(items) lists core len() length of a list how many items in a list length of a list count the items len the `len()` function allows us to count the items in a list. it is useful for reports such as \"3 items in the basket\", and for working out the last index."},{"sid":"C1.13","title":"IndexError: list index out of range","cmd":"","terms":["IndexError","list index out of range"],"tags":["lists","errors","debugging"],"task":["list index out of range","IndexError","too large an index","off by one"],"examinable":true,"search":"indexerror: list index out of range  lists errors debugging indexerror list index out of range list index out of range indexerror too large an index off by one an `indexerror` tells us that the program asked for a position that the list does not have."},{"sid":"C1.14","title":"Sorting by a rule with key","cmd":"sorted(items, key=len)","terms":["sort key","key=","sorting by length"],"tags":["lists","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["sort by length","sort with a key","custom sort order","sort by a rule"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting by a rule with key sorted(items, key=len) lists sorting non-examinable sort key key= sorting by length sort by length sort with a key custom sort order sort by a rule the argument `key=` allows `sorted()` and `sort()` to order items by a rule instead of by their own value. it is useful for orders such as shortest name first. alternatively, the items could be changed before sorting, but `key=` leaves the list as it is."},{"sid":"C1.15","title":"Sorting records by one of their fields","cmd":"sorted(items, key=func)","terms":["sorting a list of dictionaries","key function"],"tags":["lists","sorting","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["sort records","sort by a field","sort a list of tuples by one item","sort by second item"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting records by one of their fields sorted(items, key=func) lists sorting dictionaries non-examinable sorting a list of dictionaries key function sort records sort by a field sort a list of tuples by one item sort by second item a common way to sort a list of dictionaries is a small function that returns the field to sort by, given as the `key`. it is useful for records such as the lines of a receipt, ordered by price or by name. alternatively, a `lambda` expression writes the function inline, but a named function is clearer at this level."},{"sid":"C1.18","title":"Writing a sort key with `lambda`","cmd":"sorted(items, key=lambda item: item[\"price\"])","terms":["lambda","sort key","anonymous function"],"tags":["lists","functions","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["lambda","sort by a field in one line","short function","anonymous function","sort key"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing a sort key with lambda sorted(items, key=lambda item: item[\"price\"]) lists functions sorting non-examinable lambda sort key anonymous function lambda sort by a field in one line short function anonymous function sort key a `lambda` expression allows a short function to be written inside a call, with no `def` and no name. it is useful for a sort key that is used once, such as the price field of each record. alternatively, a named function written with `def` is clearer when the rule needs more than one expression or is used in several places."},{"sid":"C1.16","title":"Finding the largest or smallest record with `max()` and `min()`","cmd":"max(items, key=func)   min(items, key=func)","terms":["max with key","min with key"],"tags":["lists","sorting","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["largest record","find the maximum by a field","min and max with key","item with the biggest value"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the largest or smallest record with max() and min() max(items, key=func)   min(items, key=func) lists sorting dictionaries non-examinable max with key min with key largest record find the maximum by a field min and max with key item with the biggest value the functions `max()` and `min()` also accept `key=`, so they can pick the record with the largest or smallest field. it is useful for questions such as which line of a receipt costs most. alternatively, a loop that keeps the largest value does the same, but `max()` is one call."},{"sid":"C2.01","title":"Making a sequence of numbers with `range()`","cmd":"list(range(n))","terms":["range()","list(range())"],"tags":["ranges","lists","core"],"task":["sequence of numbers","range","count from 0","list of numbers"],"examinable":true,"search":"making a sequence of numbers with range() list(range(n)) ranges lists core range() list(range()) sequence of numbers range count from 0 list of numbers the `range()` function allows us to produce a sequence of whole numbers without typing them out. it is useful for counting, such as the days of a week or the numbers on a shelf label."},{"sid":"C2.02","title":"Choosing where `range()` starts and stops","cmd":"range(start, stop)","terms":["range(start, stop)"],"tags":["ranges","lists","core"],"task":["start and stop of range","numbers from a to b","range between two numbers","exclusive end"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing where range() starts and stops range(start, stop) ranges lists core range(start, stop) start and stop of range numbers from a to b range between two numbers exclusive end two arguments to `range()` allow us to choose the first number and the number to stop before. it is useful when counting starts at 1. alternatively, `range()` with one argument always starts at 0."},{"sid":"C2.03","title":"Counting in steps with `range()`","cmd":"range(start, stop, step)","terms":["range(start, stop, step)","step"],"tags":["ranges","lists","core"],"task":["count in steps","every second number","step in range","counting by tens"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting in steps with range() range(start, stop, step) ranges lists core range(start, stop, step) step count in steps every second number step in range counting by tens a third argument to `range()` allows us to count in steps larger than 1. it is useful for regular intervals, such as the minutes of each quarter hour."},{"sid":"C2.04","title":"Finding the smallest, largest and total of a list","cmd":"min(numbers), max(numbers), sum(numbers)","terms":["min()","max()","sum()"],"tags":["lists","numbers","core"],"task":["smallest and largest","total of a list","sum of numbers","min max sum"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding the smallest, largest and total of a list min(numbers), max(numbers), sum(numbers) lists numbers core min() max() sum() smallest and largest total of a list sum of numbers min max sum the functions `min()`, `max()` and `sum()` allow us to summarise a list of numbers in one step. it is useful for questions such as the coldest day, the warmest day and the total rainfall."},{"sid":"C2.05","title":"Calculating an average from a list","cmd":"sum(numbers) / len(numbers)","terms":["average","mean of a list"],"tags":["lists","numbers","core"],"task":["average of a list","mean","calculate the mean","sum divided by length"],"examinable":true,"search":"calculating an average from a list sum(numbers) / len(numbers) lists numbers core average mean of a list average of a list mean calculate the mean sum divided by length if we have a list of numbers, then dividing the total by the number of items gives the average. it is useful for summaries such as the mean temperature of a week. alternatively, a library such as pandas has a `mean()` function, but this works with no extra packages."},{"sid":"C2.06","title":"Taking part of a list with a slice","cmd":"items[start:stop]","terms":["slice","slicing","[start:stop]"],"tags":["lists","slices","core"],"task":["part of a list","slice a list","first three items","sublist","sub list"],"examinable":true,"search":"taking part of a list with a slice items[start:stop] lists slices core slice slicing [start:stop] part of a list slice a list first three items sublist sub list a slice allows us to take a section of a list by giving a start and a stop index. it is useful for working with part of the data. alternatively, a loop can pick out the same items, but a slice is shorter."},{"sid":"C2.07","title":"Copying a list with a slice","cmd":"copy = items[:]","terms":["copying a list","[:]"],"tags":["lists","slices","core","copying"],"task":["copy a list","independent copy","copy with a slice","avoid changing the original"],"examinable":true,"search":"copying a list with a slice copy = items[:] lists slices core copying copying a list [:] copy a list independent copy copy with a slice avoid changing the original a slice with no start and no stop allows us to make an independent copy of a list. it is useful when a list must be changed but the original is still needed. alternatively, the `copy()` method does the same, but a slice is the form the course uses."},{"sid":"C2.08","title":"Taking every second item with a step","cmd":"items[::step]","terms":["slice step","[::2]","reversing a list"],"tags":["lists","slices","non-examinable"],"task":["every second item","step in a slice","reverse a list with a slice","skip items"],"examinable":false,"search":"taking every second item with a step items[::step] lists slices non-examinable slice step [::2] reversing a list every second item step in a slice reverse a list with a slice skip items a third number in a slice allows us to take every second, third or later item. it is useful for picking regular readings out of a longer list. alternatively, `range()` with a step gives the positions, but a slice step is shorter."},{"sid":"C3.01","title":"Creating a tuple","cmd":"name = (value_1, value_2)","terms":["tuple","round brackets"],"tags":["tuples","core"],"task":["make a tuple","pair of values","fixed collection","round brackets"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a tuple name = (value_1, value_2) tuples core tuple round brackets make a tuple pair of values fixed collection round brackets round brackets allow us to store values in a tuple, which is like a list but cannot be changed. it is useful for values that are fixed. alternatively, a list holds the same values, but it would allow them to be changed by mistake."},{"sid":"C3.02","title":"Unchangeable tuples","cmd":"","terms":["immutable","tuple assignment error"],"tags":["tuples","core","errors"],"task":["tuples cannot change","immutable","item assignment error","why is my tuple fixed"],"examinable":true,"search":"unchangeable tuples  tuples core errors immutable tuple assignment error tuples cannot change immutable item assignment error why is my tuple fixed a tuple cannot be changed after it is created, so any attempt to assign to one of its items stops the program."},{"sid":"C3.03","title":"Replacing a whole tuple","cmd":"name = (new_1, new_2)","terms":["writing over a tuple"],"tags":["tuples","core"],"task":["change a tuple","replace a tuple","update a tuple","make a new tuple"],"examinable":true,"search":"replacing a whole tuple name = (new_1, new_2) tuples core writing over a tuple change a tuple replace a tuple update a tuple make a new tuple assigning a new tuple to the same name allows us to change the values that the name refers to. it is useful when the fixed values do change."},{"sid":"C3.04","title":"Choosing between a list and a tuple","cmd":"","terms":["list or tuple","when to use a tuple"],"tags":["tuples","lists","core"],"task":["list or tuple","when to use a tuple","changeable or fixed"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between a list and a tuple  tuples lists core list or tuple when to use a tuple list or tuple when to use a tuple changeable or fixed a list suits values that grow, shrink or change, and a tuple suits values that are fixed as a group. it is useful to choose deliberately, because the choice tells the reader (and python) what is allowed to change."},{"sid":"C3.05","title":"Unpack a tuple into variables","cmd":"latitude, longitude = station_position","terms":["unpacking","multiple assignment from a tuple"],"tags":["tuples","non-examinable"],"task":["unpack a tuple","split a tuple into variables","assign from a pair","latitude and longitude"],"examinable":false,"search":"unpack a tuple into variables latitude, longitude = station_position tuples non-examinable unpacking multiple assignment from a tuple unpack a tuple split a tuple into variables assign from a pair latitude and longitude unpacking allows us to give each value of a tuple its own variable in one line. it is useful when the values have different meanings. alternatively, each value can be read by index, but names make the meaning clear."},{"sid":"C3.06","title":"Removing duplicates with a set","cmd":"unique_items = set(items)","terms":["set","duplicates","set()"],"tags":["sets","non-examinable"],"task":["remove duplicates","unique items","set","distinct values in a list"],"examinable":false,"search":"removing duplicates with a set unique_items = set(items) sets non-examinable set duplicates set() remove duplicates unique items set distinct values in a list a set allows us to store values in which each one appears only once. it is useful for removing repeats, such as the different items that were sold in a day. alternatively, a list can be searched for repeats with a loop, but a set does it in one step."},{"sid":"C4.01","title":"Creating a dictionary","cmd":"d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2}","terms":["dictionary","curly brackets","key-value pair"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["make a dictionary","key and value","labelled data","curly brackets"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a dictionary d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2} dictionaries core dictionary curly brackets key-value pair make a dictionary key and value labelled data curly brackets curly brackets allow us to store information as pairs, each made of a key and a value. it is useful when values are looked up by a label instead of a position. alternatively, two lists could be kept side by side, but they can fall out of step."},{"sid":"C4.02","title":"Reading a value from a dictionary","cmd":"d[key]","terms":["dictionary key","reading a dictionary value"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["look up a value","get by key","read from a dictionary","find a value from its name"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a value from a dictionary d[key] dictionaries core dictionary key reading a dictionary value look up a value get by key read from a dictionary find a value from its name a key in square brackets allows us to read the value that is stored with it. it is useful for looking something up by name. alternatively, `get()` does the same without an error when the key is missing."},{"sid":"C4.03","title":"Reading a value safely with `get()`","cmd":"d.get(key, default)","terms":["get()","default value"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["avoid KeyError","get with a default","safe lookup","value if the key is missing"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a value safely with get() d.get(key, default) dictionaries core get() default value avoid keyerror get with a default safe lookup value if the key is missing the `get()` method allows us to read a value and supply a default for a key that is missing. it is useful when a key may not be present. alternatively, square brackets are simpler when the key is certain to exist."},{"sid":"C4.04","title":"Adding a new pair to a dictionary","cmd":"d[key] = value","terms":["adding to a dictionary"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["add to a dictionary","new key","insert a pair","store a new value"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a new pair to a dictionary d[key] = value dictionaries core adding to a dictionary add to a dictionary new key insert a pair store a new value assigning to a new key allows us to add a pair to a dictionary. it is useful when new information arrives. alternatively, a dictionary can be built in one statement, but adding pairs one at a time suits data that arrives while the program runs."},{"sid":"C4.05","title":"Starting with an empty dictionary","cmd":"d = {}","terms":["empty dictionary"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["empty dictionary","start with nothing","build a dictionary in a loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"starting with an empty dictionary d = {} dictionaries core empty dictionary empty dictionary start with nothing build a dictionary in a loop a common way to build a dictionary is to start with empty curly brackets and add one pair at a time. it is useful when the contents are not known until the program runs."},{"sid":"C4.06","title":"Changing a value in a dictionary","cmd":"d[key] = new_value","terms":["changing a dictionary value"],"tags":["dictionaries","core"],"task":["change a value","update a dictionary entry","replace a value"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing a value in a dictionary d[key] = new_value dictionaries core changing a dictionary value change a value update a dictionary entry replace a value assigning to an existing key allows us to replace its value. it is useful when a stored number changes."},{"sid":"C4.09","title":"Removing a pair with `del`","cmd":"del d[key]","terms":["del","removing a dictionary pair"],"tags":["dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["delete from a dictionary","remove a key","del d[key]","remove a pair"],"examinable":false,"search":"removing a pair with del del d[key] dictionaries non-examinable del removing a dictionary pair delete from a dictionary remove a key del d[key] remove a pair the `del` statement allows us to delete a key and its value from a dictionary. it is useful when an entry is no longer needed. alternatively, `pop()` removes the pair and gives back the value."},{"sid":"C4.07","title":"Looping through the keys and values of a dictionary","cmd":"for key, value in d.items():","terms":["items()","looping through a dictionary"],"tags":["dictionaries","core","loops"],"task":["loop over a dictionary","keys and values","items","for each pair"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through the keys and values of a dictionary for key, value in d.items(): dictionaries core loops items() looping through a dictionary loop over a dictionary keys and values items for each pair the `items()` method allows a loop to take each key and its value together. it is useful for reports that need both. alternatively, looping over the keys alone is enough when the values are not needed."},{"sid":"C4.08","title":"Looping through only the keys or only the values","cmd":"for key in d: ;; d.values()","terms":["keys()","values()","looping through keys"],"tags":["dictionaries","core","loops"],"task":["loop over keys","loop over values","only the keys","only the values"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through only the keys or only the values for key in d: ;; d.values() dictionaries core loops keys() values() looping through keys loop over keys loop over values only the keys only the values looping over a dictionary directly gives the keys, and the `values()` method gives the values. it is useful when only one half of each pair is needed. alternatively, `items()` gives both halves together."},{"sid":"C4.10","title":"Looping through a dictionary in sorted order","cmd":"for key in sorted(d):","terms":["sorted keys"],"tags":["dictionaries","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["dictionary in sorted order","sorted keys","alphabetical order of keys"],"examinable":false,"search":"looping through a dictionary in sorted order for key in sorted(d): dictionaries loops non-examinable sorted keys dictionary in sorted order sorted keys alphabetical order of keys the `sorted()` function allows a loop to visit the keys of a dictionary in alphabetical order. it is useful for reports that must be quick to scan."},{"sid":"C4.11","title":"Storing similar items in one dictionary","cmd":"d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2}","terms":["dictionary of similar values"],"tags":["dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["store similar items","dictionary of dictionaries","table of records","many objects in one dictionary"],"examinable":false,"search":"storing similar items in one dictionary d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2} dictionaries non-examinable dictionary of similar values store similar items dictionary of dictionaries table of records many objects in one dictionary if many values mean the same kind of thing, then one dictionary can hold them all, with a label as the key. it is useful for data such as one temperature per day. alternatively, a list holds the same values, but the days would then be known only by position."},{"sid":"C4.12","title":"Counting items with a dictionary","cmd":"counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1","terms":["counting","frequency count","tally"],"tags":["dictionaries","loops","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["count items","tally occurrences","word frequency","count with a dictionary"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting items with a dictionary counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1 dictionaries loops patterns non-examinable counting frequency count tally count items tally occurrences word frequency count with a dictionary a common way to count how often each item appears is a dictionary that holds one count for each item. it is useful for tallies, such as how many times each item was sold in a day. alternatively, `count()` counts one value at a time, so it needs a separate call for every item."},{"sid":"C4.13","title":"Counting one value in a list with `count()`","cmd":"items.count(value)","terms":["count()","counting in a list"],"tags":["lists","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["count one value in a list","how many times does a value appear","count method"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting one value in a list with count() items.count(value) lists patterns non-examinable count() counting in a list count one value in a list how many times does a value appear count method the `count()` method allows us to ask how many times one value appears in a list. it is useful when only one value matters. alternatively, a dictionary counts every value in one pass."},{"sid":"C4.14","title":"Finding the item with the highest count","cmd":"for key, count in counts.items():","terms":["most common item","largest count"],"tags":["dictionaries","loops","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["most common item","highest count","find the maximum count","mode"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the item with the highest count for key, count in counts.items(): dictionaries loops patterns non-examinable most common item largest count most common item highest count find the maximum count mode if we have a dictionary of counts, then a loop that keeps the largest count seen so far finds the most common item. it is useful for reports such as the best-selling item. alternatively, sorting the counts puts the largest first, which also shows the order of the others."},{"sid":"C4.15","title":"Sorting a dictionary by its values","cmd":"sorted(d.items(), key=lambda pair: pair[1], reverse=True)","terms":["sorting a dictionary by value","ranking counts"],"tags":["dictionaries","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["sort a dictionary by value","rank the counts","largest first","order a tally"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting a dictionary by its values sorted(d.items(), key=lambda pair: pair[1], reverse=true) dictionaries sorting non-examinable sorting a dictionary by value ranking counts sort a dictionary by value rank the counts largest first order a tally if we have a dictionary of counts or totals, then sorting its `items()` by the second part of each pair lists the keys from largest value to smallest. it is useful for a ranked report, such as the best-selling items first. alternatively, `counter(...).most_common()` gives the same ranking for counts."},{"sid":"C5.01","title":"Storing a list in a dictionary","cmd":"d = {key: [item_1, item_2]}","terms":["list in a dictionary","nesting"],"tags":["nesting","dictionaries","lists","core"],"task":["list inside a dictionary","dictionary with a list value","several values per key"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing a list in a dictionary d = {key: [item_1, item_2]} nesting dictionaries lists core list in a dictionary nesting list inside a dictionary dictionary with a list value several values per key a list as the value of a key allows one dictionary entry to hold several items. it is useful when each label has a group of values. alternatively, a separate dictionary key could be used for each item, but then the group would have no name."},{"sid":"C5.02","title":"Storing a dictionary in a dictionary","cmd":"d = {key: {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2}}","terms":["dictionary in a dictionary"],"tags":["nesting","dictionaries","core"],"task":["dictionary of dictionaries","nested dictionary","records keyed by name"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing a dictionary in a dictionary d = {key: {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2}} nesting dictionaries core dictionary in a dictionary dictionary of dictionaries nested dictionary records keyed by name a dictionary as the value of a key allows each entry to carry several named facts. it is useful when every item has the same kinds of detail. alternatively, two separate dictionaries could hold the prices and the counts, but they would have to be kept in step."},{"sid":"C5.03","title":"Storing a table as a list of lists","cmd":"table = [[12.5, 14.0], [11.5, 9.0]]","terms":["list of lists","table","two-dimensional list"],"tags":["nesting","lists","core"],"task":["table as a list of lists","2D list","grid of numbers","rows and columns in Python"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing a table as a list of lists table = [[12.5, 14.0], [11.5, 9.0]] nesting lists core list of lists table two-dimensional list table as a list of lists 2d list grid of numbers rows and columns in python a list whose items are themselves lists allows us to store a table of rows and columns. it is useful for readings taken in a regular pattern. alternatively, one list per row could be kept under separate names, but a single table can be looped through."},{"sid":"C5.04","title":"Looping through the rows of a table","cmd":"for row in table:","terms":["looping through a table","nested loop"],"tags":["nesting","lists","core","loops"],"task":["loop over rows of a table","for each row","go through a table"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through the rows of a table for row in table: nesting lists core loops looping through a table nested loop loop over rows of a table for each row go through a table a loop over a list of lists allows us to work through a table one row at a time. it is useful for summaries such as the average of each day."},{"sid":"C5.05","title":"Changing one value in a table","cmd":"table[row][column] = value","terms":["changing a table value"],"tags":["nesting","lists","core"],"task":["change one cell in a table","update a value in a list of lists","table row column"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing one value in a table table[row][column] = value nesting lists core changing a table value change one cell in a table update a value in a list of lists table row column two indexes on the left of `=` allow us to replace a single value inside a table. it is useful for correcting one reading without touching the others."},{"sid":"C5.06","title":"Storing records as a list of dictionaries","cmd":"receipt = [{\"item\": \"tea\", \"price\": 2.50}]","terms":["list of dictionaries","records"],"tags":["nesting","dictionaries","lists","core"],"task":["list of dictionaries","records","rows with named fields","receipt as data"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing records as a list of dictionaries receipt = [{\"item\": \"tea\", \"price\": 2.50}] nesting dictionaries lists core list of dictionaries records list of dictionaries records rows with named fields receipt as data a list of dictionaries allows us to store many records that each have the same named fields. it is useful for data such as the lines of a receipt, where every line has an item, a price and a quantity. alternatively, a list of lists holds the same values, but the fields are then known only by position."},{"sid":"C5.07","title":"Looping through a list of dictionaries","cmd":"for row in table:","terms":["looping through records"],"tags":["nesting","dictionaries","lists","core","loops"],"task":["loop over a list of dictionaries","go through records","read fields from each record"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through a list of dictionaries for row in table: nesting dictionaries lists core loops looping through records loop over a list of dictionaries go through records read fields from each record a common way to process records is to loop over the list and read the fields of each dictionary. it is useful for totals and reports."},{"sid":"C6.01","title":"Two names for one list","cmd":"","terms":["alias","two names for one list","shared list"],"tags":["identity","lists","core","objects"],"task":["two names for one list","alias","why did my other list change","copying by assignment"],"examinable":true,"search":"two names for one list  identity lists core objects alias two names for one list shared list two names for one list alias why did my other list change copying by assignment assigning one list variable to another allows both names to refer to the same list, so a change made through one name shows through the other."},{"sid":"C6.02","title":"Making an independent copy of a list","cmd":"copy = items.copy()","terms":["copy()","list()","independent copy"],"tags":["identity","lists","core","copying"],"task":["copy a list","independent copy","copy method","change one without the other"],"examinable":true,"search":"making an independent copy of a list copy = items.copy() identity lists core copying copy() list() independent copy copy a list independent copy copy method change one without the other the `copy()` method, or a slice with `[:]`, allows us to make a new list with the same items as the original. it is useful when one list must be changed while the original is kept. alternatively, `list(readings)` also makes a copy, but `copy()` says what it does."},{"sid":"C6.03","title":"Comparing values with `==` and objects with `is`","cmd":"","terms":["==","is","same value or same object"],"tags":["identity","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["== versus is","same value or same object","equality or identity"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing values with == and objects with is  identity lists non-examinable == is same value or same object == versus is same value or same object equality or identity the operator `==` asks whether two names have the same value, and `is` asks whether they are the same object."},{"sid":"C6.04","title":"Checking for the same object with `id()`","cmd":"id(a) == id(b)","terms":["id()"],"tags":["identity","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["same object","id function","memory identity","are these the same list"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking for the same object with id() id(a) == id(b) identity lists non-examinable id() same object id function memory identity are these the same list the `id()` function allows us to ask which object a name refers to, because every object has its own identity number. it is useful for showing whether two names share one object. alternatively, the `is` operator asks the same question more directly."},{"sid":"C6.05","title":"Using `is` only to test for `None`","cmd":"if latest is None:","terms":["is None","testing for None"],"tags":["identity","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["test for None","is None","do not use == None"],"examinable":false,"search":"using is only to test for none if latest is none: identity objects non-examinable is none testing for none test for none is none do not use == none a common way to test for the missing-value object `none` is the operator `is`. it is useful because `none` exists once only, so the test is always reliable. alternatively, `== none` gives the same answer here, but `is none` is the form that other programmers expect."},{"sid":"C6.06","title":"Mutable and immutable objects","cmd":"","terms":["mutable","immutable"],"tags":["identity","lists","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["mutable versus immutable","which types can change","lists change strings do not"],"examinable":false,"search":"mutable and immutable objects  identity lists objects non-examinable mutable immutable mutable versus immutable which types can change lists change strings do not a mutable object can be changed in place, and an immutable object cannot."},{"sid":"C6.07","title":"Adding to a list and to a string with `+=`","cmd":"","terms":["+= on a list","+= on a string"],"tags":["identity","lists","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["+= on a list","+= on a string","add in place","list changes but string makes a new one"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding to a list and to a string with +=  identity lists strings non-examinable += on a list += on a string += on a list += on a string add in place list changes but string makes a new one the operator `+=` changes a list in place but makes a new string, so a second name is affected differently."},{"sid":"C6.08","title":"Shared inner lists in a copy","cmd":"","terms":["shallow copy","deepcopy","copying a list of lists"],"tags":["identity","lists","nesting","copying","non-examinable"],"task":["copy of a list of lists","shallow copy","inner lists shared","nested copy problem"],"examinable":false,"search":"shared inner lists in a copy  identity lists nesting copying non-examinable shallow copy deepcopy copying a list of lists copy of a list of lists shallow copy inner lists shared nested copy problem a copy of a list of lists makes a new outer list, but the inner lists are still shared with the original."},{"sid":"D1.01","title":"Testing how a string starts or ends","cmd":"text.startswith(prefix), text.endswith(suffix)","terms":["startswith()","endswith()","prefix","suffix"],"tags":["strings","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["string starts with","string ends with","check a prefix","file extension test"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing how a string starts or ends text.startswith(prefix), text.endswith(suffix) strings conditions non-examinable startswith() endswith() prefix suffix string starts with string ends with check a prefix file extension test the methods `startswith()` and `endswith()` allow us to ask whether text begins or ends with a given piece. it is useful for checking a code prefix or a file type. alternatively, `in` finds the text anywhere, and a slice compares fixed positions."},{"sid":"D1.02","title":"Counting how often text appears","cmd":"text.count(part)","terms":["count() for strings","counting text"],"tags":["strings","non-examinable"],"task":["count occurrences in a string","how many times does text appear","count a letter"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting how often text appears text.count(part) strings non-examinable count() for strings counting text count occurrences in a string how many times does text appear count a letter the method `count()` allows us to find how many times a piece of text appears in a string. it is useful for counting separators or repeated words. alternatively, `split()` makes a list whose length gives a similar count."},{"sid":"D1.03","title":"Checking what kind of characters a string holds","cmd":"text.isalpha()   text.isalnum()   text.isupper()   text.islower()   text.isspace()","terms":["isalpha()","isalnum()","isupper()","islower()","isspace()"],"tags":["strings","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["is it all letters","is alphanumeric","character types","isalpha isdigit"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking what kind of characters a string holds text.isalpha()   text.isalnum()   text.isupper()   text.islower()   text.isspace() strings conditions non-examinable isalpha() isalnum() isupper() islower() isspace() is it all letters is alphanumeric character types isalpha isdigit the methods `isalpha()`, `isalnum()`, `isupper()`, `islower()` and `isspace()` allow us to ask what the characters of a string are. it is useful for checking typed text. alternatively, `isdigit()` checks digits, and a loop can test the characters one by one."},{"sid":"D1.04","title":"Comparing strings alphabetically","cmd":"","terms":["comparing strings","alphabetical order","sorting text"],"tags":["strings","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["compare strings","alphabetical order","which word comes first","string comparison"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing strings alphabetically  strings conditions non-examinable comparing strings alphabetical order sorting text compare strings alphabetical order which word comes first string comparison strings can be compared with ` ` and `==`, and python compares them character by character, with capital letters before small ones."},{"sid":"D1.05","title":"Looping through the characters of a string","cmd":"for character in text:","terms":["looping through a string","characters"],"tags":["strings","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["loop over characters","each letter in a string","iterate a string"],"examinable":false,"search":"looping through the characters of a string for character in text: strings loops non-examinable looping through a string characters loop over characters each letter in a string iterate a string a `for` loop over a string allows us to take its characters one at a time. it is useful for checking or counting characters. alternatively, a slice picks out characters at known positions, and `count()` counts a single character."},{"sid":"D1.06","title":"Building a string in a loop","cmd":"result = result + character","terms":["building a string","accumulating text"],"tags":["strings","loops","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["build a string in a loop","add characters one by one","accumulate text"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a string in a loop result = result + character strings loops patterns non-examinable building a string accumulating text build a string in a loop add characters one by one accumulate text a common way to build text is to start with an empty string and add to it inside a loop. it is useful for changing text one character at a time. alternatively, `join()` combines a list of pieces in one call."},{"sid":"D1.07","title":"Checking whether a word reads the same backwards","cmd":"text == text[::-1]","terms":["reversing a string","palindrome"],"tags":["strings","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["palindrome","reads the same backwards","reverse a string","compare with reversed text"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking whether a word reads the same backwards text == text[::-1] strings conditions non-examinable reversing a string palindrome palindrome reads the same backwards reverse a string compare with reversed text if we have a string, then the slice `[::-1]` gives it backwards, and comparing the two shows whether it reads the same in both directions. it is useful for checking codes that include a mirrored part."},{"sid":"D1.08","title":"Cleaning typed text before using it","cmd":"answer.strip().lower()","terms":["cleaning input","strip and lower","normalising text"],"tags":["strings","input","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["clean user input","strip and lowercase","normalise text","ignore case and spaces"],"examinable":false,"search":"cleaning typed text before using it answer.strip().lower() strings input patterns non-examinable cleaning input strip and lower normalising text clean user input strip and lowercase normalise text ignore case and spaces a common way to prepare typed text is to remove spaces from the ends with `strip()` and change it to lower case, before comparing it. it is useful because people type `\" tea \"`, `\"tea\"` and `\"tea\"` for the same item. alternatively, a list of every spelling could be checked, but it would miss some."},{"sid":"D1.09","title":"Formatting numbers in an f-string","cmd":"f\"{value:,.2f}\"  f\"{share:.1%}\"","terms":["format specifiers","thousands separator","percentage format","width"],"tags":["strings","printing","non-examinable"],"task":["format numbers","thousands separator","percentage","two decimal places in an f-string"],"examinable":false,"search":"formatting numbers in an f-string f\"{value:,.2f}\"  f\"{share:.1%}\" strings printing non-examinable format specifiers thousands separator percentage format width format numbers thousands separator percentage two decimal places in an f-string a format after the colon inside an f-string allows us to control how a number is shown: decimals, thousands separators, percentages and width. it is useful for reports and receipts, where numbers must be clear to read. alternatively, `round()` changes the value itself, and a format changes only how it is displayed."},{"sid":"D1.10","title":"Aligning text in columns","cmd":"f\"{text:<10}{number:>8.2f}\"","terms":["alignment","columns","ljust","rjust","centre"],"tags":["strings","printing","non-examinable"],"task":["align text in columns","pad with spaces","tidy table output","left and right align"],"examinable":false,"search":"aligning text in columns f\"{text:<10}{number:>8.2f}\" strings printing non-examinable alignment columns ljust rjust centre align text in columns pad with spaces tidy table output left and right align if we have values to print in rows, then a width and an alignment inside the f-string line them up in columns. it is useful for receipts and tables, so that numbers line up on the right. alternatively, `ljust()`, `rjust()` and `center()` do the same on a string."},{"sid":"D1.11","title":"Writing text over several lines","cmd":"\"\"\"line 1 line 2\"\"\"   text.splitlines()","terms":["triple quotes","multi-line string","splitlines()"],"tags":["strings","non-examinable"],"task":["multi-line string","triple quotes","text over several lines","splitlines"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing text over several lines \"\"\"line 1 line 2\"\"\"   text.splitlines() strings non-examinable triple quotes multi-line string splitlines() multi-line string triple quotes text over several lines splitlines three quotation marks in a row allow a string to run over several lines, with the line breaks kept. it is useful for a block of text."},{"sid":"D1.12","title":"Showing quotation marks and backslashes in a string","cmd":"\"She said \\\"open\\\"\"   r\"C:\\new\\readings\"","terms":["escape character","backslash","raw string"],"tags":["strings","non-examinable"],"task":["quote marks in a string","backslash in a string","raw string","Windows path"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing quotation marks and backslashes in a string \"she said \\\"open\\\"\"   r\"c:\\new\\readings\" strings non-examinable escape character backslash raw string quote marks in a string backslash in a string raw string windows path a backslash allows a special character, such as a quotation mark or a newline, to be written inside a string, and an `r` before the string switches this off. it is useful for text that holds quotation marks, and for file paths on windows. alternatively, single quotes around the string let it hold double quotes without a backslash."},{"sid":"D2.01","title":"Finding the Python for each calculator key","cmd":"","terms":["calculator keys","scientific calculator","math module"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["calculator keys in Python","which function is square root","sin cos log in Python","calculator equivalents"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the python for each calculator key  maths numbers core non-examinable calculator keys scientific calculator math module calculator keys in python which function is square root sin cos log in python calculator equivalents every key of a scientific calculator has a matching piece of python, either an operator or a function of the `math` module."},{"sid":"D2.02","title":"Finding the absolute value of a number","cmd":"abs(number)","terms":["abs()","absolute value","modulus"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["absolute value","remove the minus sign","size of a number","abs"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the absolute value of a number abs(number) maths numbers core non-examinable abs() absolute value modulus absolute value remove the minus sign size of a number abs the function `abs()` allows us to remove the sign of a number, giving its distance from zero. it is useful for sizes of differences, such as how far a reading is from a target, whichever side it is on. alternatively, `max(x, -x)` gives the same result, but `abs()` says what is meant."},{"sid":"D2.03","title":"Square roots","cmd":"math.sqrt(x)","terms":["sqrt()","square root"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["square root","sqrt","root of a number","math.sqrt"],"examinable":false,"search":"square roots math.sqrt(x) maths numbers core non-examinable sqrt() square root square root sqrt root of a number math.sqrt the function `math.sqrt()` allows us to find the square root of a number. it is useful for finding the side of a square from its area. alternatively, `x ** 0.5` gives the same result, but `math.sqrt()` is easier to read."},{"sid":"D2.04","title":"Cube roots and other roots","cmd":"number ** (1 / 3)","terms":["cube root","nth root","fractional power"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["cube root","nth root","fractional power","root other than square"],"examinable":false,"search":"cube roots and other roots number ** (1 / 3) maths numbers non-examinable cube root nth root fractional power cube root nth root fractional power root other than square a fractional power allows any root to be found, because the nth root of a number is the same as raising it to the power 1/n. it is useful for roots other than the square root. alternatively, `math.cbrt()` gives the cube root directly in recent versions of python."},{"sid":"D2.05","title":"Powers with `pow()`","cmd":"pow(x, y)   math.pow(x, y)","terms":["pow()","power function"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["power function","pow","x to the y","exponent"],"examinable":false,"search":"powers with pow() pow(x, y)   math.pow(x, y) maths numbers non-examinable pow() power function power function pow x to the y exponent the functions `pow()` and `math.pow()` allow a power to be written as a function call, as on a calculator's power key. it is useful when the base and the power are long expressions that read better as arguments. alternatively, the operator `**` is shorter and is the usual choice."},{"sid":"D2.06","title":"Finding e to a power with `math.exp()`","cmd":"math.exp(x)","terms":["exp()","e to the power","exponential"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["e to the power","exponential","exp","natural exponent"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding e to a power with math.exp() math.exp(x) maths numbers core non-examinable exp() e to the power exponential e to the power exponential exp natural exponent the function `math.exp()` allows us to calculate e raised to a power, where e is about 2.718. it is useful for growth and decay that happen continuously. alternatively, `math.e ** x` gives the same result, but `math.exp()` is more accurate."},{"sid":"D2.07","title":"Finding the natural logarithm with `math.log()`","cmd":"math.log(x)","terms":["log()","ln","natural logarithm"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["natural logarithm","ln","log of a number","math.log"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the natural logarithm with math.log() math.log(x) maths numbers core non-examinable log() ln natural logarithm natural logarithm ln log of a number math.log the function `math.log()` with one argument allows us to find the natural logarithm of a number, the power to which e must be raised to give it. it is useful for undoing `math.exp()`, and for finding how long growth takes. alternatively, `math.log10()` gives the logarithm to base 10."},{"sid":"D2.08","title":"Finding logarithms to base 10 and base 2","cmd":"math.log10(x)   math.log2(x)   math.log(x, base)","terms":["log10()","log2()","logarithm to a base"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["log base 10","log base 2","logarithm with a base","log10"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding logarithms to base 10 and base 2 math.log10(x)   math.log2(x)   math.log(x, base) maths numbers core non-examinable log10() log2() logarithm to a base log base 10 log base 2 logarithm with a base log10 the functions `math.log10()` and `math.log2()` allow us to find the power of 10 or of 2 that gives a number, and `math.log(x, base)` allows any base to be used. it is useful for numbers over a wide range, such as the number of digits of a count, or the number of times that a value can be halved. alternatively, `math.log(x) / math.log(base)` gives the same result."},{"sid":"D2.09","title":"Finding the value of pi and e","cmd":"math.pi   math.e","terms":["pi","math.pi","math.e","constants"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["value of pi","value of e","math constants","pi"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the value of pi and e math.pi   math.e maths numbers core non-examinable pi math.pi math.e constants value of pi value of e math constants pi the constants `math.pi` and `math.e` allow us to use the numbers π and e in a calculation, to the accuracy of the computer. it is useful for circles, such as the area of a round table top, and for exponential growth. alternatively, typing 3.14 gives a less accurate value, and the error grows in a long calculation."},{"sid":"D2.10","title":"Converting between degrees and radians","cmd":"math.radians(degrees)   math.degrees(radians)","terms":["radians()","degrees()","deg to rad","angle units"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["degrees to radians","radians to degrees","convert angles","radians"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting between degrees and radians math.radians(degrees)   math.degrees(radians) maths numbers core non-examinable radians() degrees() deg to rad angle units degrees to radians radians to degrees convert angles radians the functions `math.radians()` and `math.degrees()` allow an angle to be changed between degrees and radians. it is useful because the trigonometric functions of python work in radians, while angles are usually measured in degrees. alternatively, an angle in degrees can be multiplied by `math.pi / 180`, which is what `math.radians()` does."},{"sid":"D2.11","title":"Finding sine, cosine and tangent","cmd":"math.sin(angle)   math.cos(angle)   math.tan(angle)","terms":["sin()","cos()","tan()","trigonometry"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["sine","cosine","tangent","trigonometry","sin cos tan"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding sine, cosine and tangent math.sin(angle)   math.cos(angle)   math.tan(angle) maths numbers core non-examinable sin() cos() tan() trigonometry sine cosine tangent trigonometry sin cos tan the functions `math.sin()`, `math.cos()` and `math.tan()` allow us to find the sine, cosine and tangent of an angle. it is useful for slopes and distances. alternatively, a table of values can be used, but a function gives any angle."},{"sid":"D2.12","title":"Passing degrees to a trig function by mistake","cmd":"","terms":["degrees and radians mistake","wrong angle unit"],"tags":["maths","numbers","mistakes","non-examinable"],"task":["wrong trig answer","degrees in sin","angle in the wrong unit","trig gives odd values"],"examinable":false,"search":"passing degrees to a trig function by mistake  maths numbers mistakes non-examinable degrees and radians mistake wrong angle unit wrong trig answer degrees in sin angle in the wrong unit trig gives odd values a trigonometric function that is given degrees treats the number as radians, and returns a result that is wrong with no error."},{"sid":"D2.13","title":"Finding an angle with the inverse functions","cmd":"math.asin(x)   math.acos(x)   math.atan(x)","terms":["asin()","acos()","atan()","inverse trigonometric functions","arcsin"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["inverse trig","arcsin","arccos","arctan","find an angle"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding an angle with the inverse functions math.asin(x)   math.acos(x)   math.atan(x) maths numbers core non-examinable asin() acos() atan() inverse trigonometric functions arcsin inverse trig arcsin arccos arctan find an angle the functions `math.asin()`, `math.acos()` and `math.atan()` allow an angle to be found from a ratio. it is useful for finding an angle from two lengths. alternatively, `math.atan2()` finds an angle from two lengths and handles every direction."},{"sid":"D2.14","title":"Finding the angle of a direction with `atan2`","cmd":"math.atan2(rise, run)","terms":["atan2()","direction angle","bearing"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["angle of a direction","atan2","bearing from rise and run","angle of a vector"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the angle of a direction with atan2 math.atan2(rise, run) maths numbers non-examinable atan2() direction angle bearing angle of a direction atan2 bearing from rise and run angle of a vector the function `math.atan2(y, x)` allows us to find the angle of a direction from its two parts, whichever way the direction points. it is useful for the angle of a path or a ramp, where `atan()` cannot tell a rise to the left from one to the right. alternatively, `math.atan(y / x)` also works, but fails when `x` is 0."},{"sid":"D2.15","title":"Finding the length of the longest side","cmd":"math.hypot(x, y)","terms":["hypot()","hypotenuse","Pythagoras","distance"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["hypotenuse","length of the longest side","Pythagoras","distance from components"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the length of the longest side math.hypot(x, y) maths numbers core non-examinable hypot() hypotenuse pythagoras distance hypotenuse length of the longest side pythagoras distance from components the function `math.hypot()` allows us to find the length of the longest side of a right-angled triangle from the other two sides. it is useful for the distance between two points."},{"sid":"D2.16","title":"Finding a factorial","cmd":"math.factorial(n)","terms":["factorial","n!","arrangements"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["factorial","n factorial","product of 1 to n"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding a factorial math.factorial(n) maths numbers non-examinable factorial n! arrangements factorial n factorial product of 1 to n the function `math.factorial()` allows us to find n!, the product of every whole number from 1 to n. it is useful for counting the orders in which items can be arranged."},{"sid":"D2.17","title":"Counting combinations with `math.comb` and `math.perm`","cmd":"math.comb(n, k)   math.perm(n, k)","terms":["comb()","perm()","nCr","nPr","combinations"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["combinations","permutations","n choose k","how many ways to choose"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting combinations with math.comb and math.perm math.comb(n, k)   math.perm(n, k) maths numbers non-examinable comb() perm() ncr npr combinations combinations permutations n choose k how many ways to choose the functions `math.comb(n, k)` and `math.perm(n, k)` allow us to count the ways to choose k items from n, when the order does not matter (`ncr`) or when it does (`npr`). it is useful for counting selections, such as the teams of 3 that can be picked from 10 people."},{"sid":"D2.18","title":"Rounding down, rounding up and cutting off decimals","cmd":"math.floor(x)   math.ceil(x)   math.trunc(x)","terms":["floor()","ceil()","trunc()","rounding down","rounding up"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["round down","round up","truncate","floor and ceiling","cut off decimals"],"examinable":false,"search":"rounding down, rounding up and cutting off decimals math.floor(x)   math.ceil(x)   math.trunc(x) maths numbers core non-examinable floor() ceil() trunc() rounding down rounding up round down round up truncate floor and ceiling cut off decimals the functions `math.floor()`, `math.ceil()` and `math.trunc()` allow a decimal number to be rounded down, rounded up or cut off at the decimal point. it is useful for whole units, such as the number of boxes needed for a number of items. alternatively, `round()` goes to the nearest whole number."},{"sid":"D2.19","title":"Finding the quotient and remainder together with `divmod`","cmd":"divmod(a, b)","terms":["divmod()","quotient and remainder"],"tags":["maths","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["quotient and remainder","divide with remainder","divmod","hours and minutes from minutes"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the quotient and remainder together with divmod divmod(a, b) maths numbers non-examinable divmod() quotient and remainder quotient and remainder divide with remainder divmod hours and minutes from minutes the function `divmod()` allows us to find the whole number of times that one number fits into another, and the remainder, in one call. it is useful for splitting a total into parts. alternatively, `//` and `%` give the two parts separately."},{"sid":"D2.20","title":"Writing large and small numbers in scientific notation","cmd":"6.02e23   1.5e-3   f\"{x:.2e}\"","terms":["scientific notation","e notation","EXP key","standard form"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["scientific notation","very large number","very small number","1e-3"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing large and small numbers in scientific notation 6.02e23   1.5e-3   f\"{x:.2e}\" maths numbers core non-examinable scientific notation e notation exp key standard form scientific notation very large number very small number 1e-3 a number followed by `e` and a power of ten allows very large and very small numbers to be typed, as with the exp key of a calculator. it is useful for measurements across many orders of size. alternatively, underscores make a large whole number clearer, but `e` notation is shorter."},{"sid":"D2.21","title":"Comparing decimal numbers with `math.isclose`","cmd":"math.isclose(a, b)","terms":["isclose()","comparing floats","tolerance"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["compare floats","are two decimals equal","isclose","tolerance"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing decimal numbers with math.isclose math.isclose(a, b) maths numbers core non-examinable isclose() comparing floats tolerance compare floats are two decimals equal isclose tolerance the function `math.isclose()` allows two decimal numbers to be compared with a small allowed difference. it is useful because calculations such as sines and logarithms give results with a tiny error, so `==` often gives `false` for numbers that are equal on paper. alternatively, `round()` on both sides also works, but `isclose()` says what is meant."},{"sid":"D2.22","title":"Combining calculator functions in one expression","cmd":"2 * math.pi * math.sqrt(length / 9.81)","terms":["long expression","brackets","order of operations","scientific formula"],"tags":["maths","numbers","core","non-examinable"],"task":["long formula","combine functions","pendulum period","write a physics equation in Python"],"examinable":false,"search":"combining calculator functions in one expression 2 * math.pi * math.sqrt(length / 9.81) maths numbers core non-examinable long expression brackets order of operations scientific formula long formula combine functions pendulum period write a physics equation in python if we have a formula from a textbook, then its parts map to python operators and `math` functions, with brackets to keep the order correct. it is useful for checking a calculation that is long. alternatively, each part can be calculated and stored in a variable first, which is easier to check."},{"sid":"D3.01","title":"Finding the median of a list","cmd":"sorted(numbers)[len(numbers) // 2]","terms":["median","middle value","sorted list"],"tags":["lists","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["median by hand","middle value","sort and pick the middle"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the median of a list sorted(numbers)[len(numbers) // 2] lists numbers statistics non-examinable median middle value sorted list median by hand middle value sort and pick the middle the median is the middle value of a sorted list, so a very high or very low reading changes it less than it changes the mean. it is useful for readings that include an odd value. with an even number of values the median is the mean of the two middle values."},{"sid":"D3.02","title":"Finding the mean and median with statistics","cmd":"statistics.mean(numbers)   statistics.median(numbers)","terms":["statistics","mean","median","standard deviation"],"tags":["modules","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["mean and median","average","statistics module","mean"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the mean and median with statistics statistics.mean(numbers)   statistics.median(numbers) modules numbers statistics non-examinable statistics mean median standard deviation mean and median average statistics module mean the `statistics` module allows the mean, median and spread of a list of numbers to be found with one call each, and `median()` handles odd and even lengths. it is useful for summaries of readings, where the mean alone can mislead. alternatively, `sum() / len()` gives the mean, but needs the sorting steps for the median."},{"sid":"D3.03","title":"Finding the most common value","cmd":"statistics.mode(data)   statistics.multimode(data)","terms":["mode","most common value","multimode"],"tags":["modules","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["most common value","mode","which value appears most"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the most common value statistics.mode(data)   statistics.multimode(data) modules numbers statistics non-examinable mode most common value multimode most common value mode which value appears most the function `statistics.mode()` gives the most common value in a list, and `statistics.multimode()` gives every value when there is a tie. it is useful for the typical value of a category."},{"sid":"D3.04","title":"Finding the range of a list","cmd":"max(numbers) - min(numbers)","terms":["range of a list","spread","max minus min"],"tags":["lists","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["range of a list","largest minus smallest","spread","max minus min"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the range of a list max(numbers) - min(numbers) lists numbers statistics non-examinable range of a list spread max minus min range of a list largest minus smallest spread max minus min the largest value minus the smallest gives the range, the simplest measure of how spread out the values are. it is useful for a quick sense of variation. a single extreme value changes it a lot."},{"sid":"D3.05","title":"Finding the standard deviation and variance","cmd":"statistics.stdev(data)   statistics.variance(data)","terms":["standard deviation","variance","spread","stdev","pstdev"],"tags":["modules","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["standard deviation","variance","spread of data","stdev"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the standard deviation and variance statistics.stdev(data)   statistics.variance(data) modules numbers statistics non-examinable standard deviation variance spread stdev pstdev standard deviation variance spread of data stdev the standard deviation says how far values typically lie from the mean, in the same units as the values, and the variance is its square. a small standard deviation means the values sit close together. it is useful for comparing how steady two sets of readings are."},{"sid":"D3.06","title":"Finding the quartiles and percentiles","cmd":"statistics.quantiles(data, n=4)","terms":["quartiles","quantiles","percentiles","interquartile range"],"tags":["modules","numbers","statistics","non-examinable"],"task":["quartiles","percentiles","quantiles","25th and 75th percentile"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the quartiles and percentiles statistics.quantiles(data, n=4) modules numbers statistics non-examinable quartiles quantiles percentiles interquartile range quartiles percentiles quantiles 25th and 75th percentile the function `statistics.quantiles()` cuts a sorted list into equal parts. with `n=4` it gives the three quartile points, with the median in the middle. it is useful for describing where most of the values lie, and for spotting values that fall far outside them."},{"sid":"D3.08","title":"Counting the values and the different values in a list","cmd":"numbers.count(value)   len(set(numbers))","terms":["frequency","how often a value appears","unique values","distinct values"],"tags":["lists","numbers","statistics","sets","non-examinable"],"task":["count values","count distinct values","how many different values","unique count"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting the values and the different values in a list numbers.count(value)   len(set(numbers)) lists numbers statistics sets non-examinable frequency how often a value appears unique values distinct values count values count distinct values how many different values unique count the method `count()` gives how many times one value appears in a list, and `len(set(...))` gives how many different values there are. it is useful whenever a question asks \"how many\", about one value or about the variety of values."},{"sid":"D3.07","title":"Summarising a list of numbers in one report","cmd":"def summarise(values):","terms":["summary of a list","report"],"tags":["lists","numbers","statistics","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["summary report of a list","function that summarises numbers","descriptive statistics"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising a list of numbers in one report def summarise(values): lists numbers statistics functions non-examinable summary of a list report summary report of a list function that summarises numbers descriptive statistics a function that returns the mean, median, range and standard deviation allows the same summary to be used for any list of readings. it is useful when several lists must be compared in the same way."},{"sid":"D4.01","title":"Creating an array from a list","cmd":"np.array(numbers)","terms":["NumPy","np.array","ndarray","import numpy as np"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["make an array","numpy array from a list","create a numpy array"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating an array from a list np.array(numbers) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable numpy np.array ndarray import numpy as np make an array numpy array from a list create a numpy array the function `np.array()` allows us to turn a list of numbers into an array, which is numpy's type for a whole row of numbers. it is useful once a program does arithmetic on many numbers at once. alternatively, a list is enough for a handful of values, but it cannot do arithmetic on all of its items in one step."},{"sid":"D4.02","title":"Making arrays with `arange`, `linspace`, `zeros` and `ones`","cmd":"np.arange(start, stop, step)   np.linspace(start, stop, n)","terms":["np.arange","np.linspace","np.zeros","np.ones"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["array of zeros","array of ones","evenly spaced numbers","sequence of numbers","arange","linspace"],"examinable":false,"search":"making arrays with arange, linspace, zeros and ones np.arange(start, stop, step)   np.linspace(start, stop, n) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable np.arange np.linspace np.zeros np.ones array of zeros array of ones evenly spaced numbers sequence of numbers arange linspace the functions `np.arange()` and `np.linspace()` allow us to build an array of evenly spaced numbers without typing them. the function `np.arange()` steps by an amount and stops before the end, like `range()`, and `np.linspace()` gives a chosen number of values with both ends included. it is useful for the x values of a plot or a table of inputs."},{"sid":"D4.03","title":"Arithmetic on a whole array","cmd":"array * number","terms":["vectorised arithmetic","array maths","element-wise"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["arithmetic on a whole array","add a number to every item","vectorised maths","no loop needed"],"examinable":false,"search":"arithmetic on a whole array array * number numpy arrays libraries non-examinable vectorised arithmetic array maths element-wise arithmetic on a whole array add a number to every item vectorised maths no loop needed an arithmetic operator between an array and a number allows us to change every item with one expression and no loop. this is called vectorised arithmetic, and it is how numpy does calculations on many values quickly. it is useful for conversions and formulas applied to a whole column of readings."},{"sid":"D4.04","title":"Using maths functions on a whole array","cmd":"np.sqrt(a)   np.exp(a)   np.log(a)   np.sin(a)","terms":["np.sqrt","np.exp","np.log","np.sin","np.pi"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["sqrt of an array","log of an array","exp of every value","numpy maths functions"],"examinable":false,"search":"using maths functions on a whole array np.sqrt(a)   np.exp(a)   np.log(a)   np.sin(a) numpy arrays libraries numbers non-examinable np.sqrt np.exp np.log np.sin np.pi sqrt of an array log of an array exp of every value numpy maths functions numpy has its own versions of the calculator functions, such as `np.sqrt()`, `np.exp()`, `np.log()` and `np.sin()`, and each one works on every item of an array at once. it is useful for applying a formula to many values. the `math` module works on one number at a time, so a list would need a loop."},{"sid":"D4.05","title":"Selecting items with a condition","cmd":"a[a > 20]","terms":["boolean array","boolean mask","filter an array"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["filter an array","keep values above a threshold","boolean indexing","select by condition"],"examinable":false,"search":"selecting items with a condition a[a > 20] numpy arrays libraries conditions non-examinable boolean array boolean mask filter an array filter an array keep values above a threshold boolean indexing select by condition comparing an array with a number gives an array of `true` and `false` values, and putting that inside square brackets keeps only the items where it is `true`. it is useful for picking out readings above a limit without a loop. the conditions can be joined with `&` for and, `|` for or, and each comparison needs its own brackets."},{"sid":"D4.06","title":"Reading items and slices from an array","cmd":"a[0]   a[1:4]   a[-1]","terms":["array index","array slice"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["index an array","slice an array","first and last element","part of an array"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading items and slices from an array a[0]   a[1:4]   a[-1] numpy arrays libraries non-examinable array index array slice index an array slice an array first and last element part of an array indexes and slices on an array work as they do on a list, with positions counted from 0. one difference is that a slice of an array is a view of the same data, so changing the slice changes the original array. it is useful to know this before changing part of an array."},{"sid":"D4.07","title":"Summarising an array with `mean`, `std`, `min` and `max`","cmd":"a.mean()   a.std()   a.min()   a.max()   np.median(a)","terms":["np.mean","np.median","standard deviation","np.std","summary statistics"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["mean of an array","standard deviation","minimum","maximum","summarise an array"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising an array with mean, std, min and max a.mean()   a.std()   a.min()   a.max()   np.median(a) numpy arrays libraries numbers non-examinable np.mean np.median standard deviation np.std summary statistics mean of an array standard deviation minimum maximum summarise an array array methods and functions such as `mean()`, `median()`, `std()`, `min()`, `max()` and `sum()` allow us to summarise a whole array in one call. it is useful for readings, since the mean, the spread and the extremes tell most of the story. the `np.median()` and `np.percentile()` functions are functions, not methods."},{"sid":"D4.08","title":"Building a table with a two-dimensional array","cmd":"np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])","terms":["2D array","matrix","shape","rows and columns"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["2D array","table of numbers","matrix","array of arrays"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a table with a two-dimensional array np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]]) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable 2d array matrix shape rows and columns 2d array table of numbers matrix array of arrays a list of lists of equal length makes a two-dimensional array, with rows and columns. its `shape` is the number of rows followed by the number of columns, and an item is read with `[row, column]`."},{"sid":"D4.09","title":"Summarising an array by row or column","cmd":"a.mean(axis=0)   a.sum(axis=1)","terms":["axis","column means","row sums"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["mean of each column","sum of each row","axis 0 and 1","summarise by row or column"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising an array by row or column a.mean(axis=0)   a.sum(axis=1) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable axis column means row sums mean of each column sum of each row axis 0 and 1 summarise by row or column the `axis` argument allows us to summarise down the columns (`axis=0`) or along the rows (`axis=1`) of a two-dimensional array. it is useful for a table of readings."},{"sid":"D4.10","title":"Changing the shape of an array with `reshape`","cmd":"a.reshape(2, 3)","terms":["reshape","flatten","ravel","transpose"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["reshape an array","change dimensions","rows and columns from a flat array"],"examinable":false,"search":"changing the shape of an array with reshape a.reshape(2, 3) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable reshape flatten ravel transpose reshape an array change dimensions rows and columns from a flat array the method `reshape()` allows us to lay the same numbers out in different rows and columns, as long as the total count stays the same. a `-1` lets numpy work out one of the sizes. it is useful for turning a long row of readings into a table, and the attribute `.t` swaps rows and columns."},{"sid":"D4.11","title":"Sorting an array and finding positions","cmd":"np.sort(a)   np.argsort(a)   np.argmax(a)   np.where(a > 20)","terms":["np.sort","np.argsort","np.argmax","np.where"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["sort an array","index of the maximum","argsort","position of the largest"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting an array and finding positions np.sort(a)   np.argsort(a)   np.argmax(a)   np.where(a > 20) numpy arrays libraries non-examinable np.sort np.argsort np.argmax np.where sort an array index of the maximum argsort position of the largest the function `np.sort()` gives a sorted copy, `np.argmax()` gives the position of the largest item, and `np.where()` gives the positions where a condition is true. it is useful for finding which day was hottest, not only how hot it was."},{"sid":"D4.12","title":"Rounding and limiting values with `round` and `clip`","cmd":"a.round(decimals)   np.clip(a, low, high)","terms":["np.round","np.clip","limit values"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["round an array","clip values","limit to a range","cap outliers"],"examinable":false,"search":"rounding and limiting values with round and clip a.round(decimals)   np.clip(a, low, high) numpy arrays libraries numbers non-examinable np.round np.clip limit values round an array clip values limit to a range cap outliers the method `round()` rounds every item of an array, and `np.clip()` limits every item to a range, so values below the minimum become the minimum and values above the maximum become the maximum. it is useful for tidying readings that fall outside a possible range."},{"sid":"D4.13","title":"Combining arrays of different shapes with broadcasting","cmd":"table - row_means[:, None]","terms":["broadcasting","array shapes"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","non-examinable"],"task":["subtract row means","broadcasting","array of different shapes","centre each column"],"examinable":false,"search":"combining arrays of different shapes with broadcasting table - row_means[:, none] numpy arrays libraries non-examinable broadcasting array shapes subtract row means broadcasting array of different shapes centre each column when two arrays have different shapes, numpy stretches the smaller one to fit, as long as the shapes match from the right, or one of them is 1. this is called broadcasting. it allows a single value, or a row, to be applied to a whole table without a loop."},{"sid":"D4.14","title":"Generating random numbers with NumPy","cmd":"rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)   rng.integers(1, 7, size=10)","terms":["np.random","default_rng","random arrays","seed"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","random","non-examinable"],"task":["random numbers","random generator","simulate data","reproducible random numbers","seed"],"examinable":false,"search":"generating random numbers with numpy rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)   rng.integers(1, 7, size=10) numpy arrays libraries random non-examinable np.random default_rng random arrays seed random numbers random generator simulate data reproducible random numbers seed a random number generator made with `np.random.default_rng()` allows us to draw many random numbers at once, as an array. it is useful for simulations, such as ten thousand dice rolls. a number in the call, the seed, makes the sequence repeat, which is useful for tests and for sharing results."},{"sid":"D4.15","title":"Measuring how two arrays vary together","cmd":"np.corrcoef(x, y)   np.polyfit(x, y, 1)","terms":["correlation","np.corrcoef","np.polyfit","line of best fit"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["correlation of two arrays","line of best fit","polyfit","corrcoef","relationship between variables"],"examinable":false,"search":"measuring how two arrays vary together np.corrcoef(x, y)   np.polyfit(x, y, 1) numpy arrays libraries numbers non-examinable correlation np.corrcoef np.polyfit line of best fit correlation of two arrays line of best fit polyfit corrcoef relationship between variables the function `np.corrcoef()` gives the correlation between two arrays, a number from -1 to 1, and `np.polyfit()` fits a line through them. it is useful for questions such as whether warmer days sell more ice cream."},{"sid":"D4.16","title":"Plotting a NumPy array","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y)   np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)","terms":["plot an array","matplotlib with NumPy"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","plots","non-examinable"],"task":["plot an array","plot a sine curve","line plot of numpy data","graph of a function"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting a numpy array plt.plot(x, y)   np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100) numpy arrays libraries plots non-examinable plot an array matplotlib with numpy plot an array plot a sine curve line plot of numpy data graph of a function matplotlib accepts numpy arrays directly, and `np.linspace()` gives smooth x values for a formula. a formula is applied to the whole array, then the two arrays are plotted. it is useful for drawing a function, where a loop would be needed with lists."},{"sid":"D4.17","title":"Saving and loading an array","cmd":"np.savetxt(path, a, delimiter=\",\")   np.loadtxt(path, delimiter=\",\")","terms":["np.savetxt","np.loadtxt","np.save","np.load"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","libraries","files","non-examinable"],"task":["save an array to a file","load an array from a file","write numbers to csv","savetxt"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving and loading an array np.savetxt(path, a, delimiter=\",\")   np.loadtxt(path, delimiter=\",\") numpy arrays libraries files non-examinable np.savetxt np.loadtxt np.save np.load save an array to a file load an array from a file write numbers to csv savetxt the functions `np.savetxt()` and `np.loadtxt()` write an array to a text file and read it back. it is useful for numeric tables, and for a table with column names and mixed types, pandas is a better choice."},{"sid":"D5.01","title":"Creating a date with the datetime module","cmd":"date(year, month, day)","terms":["datetime","date","creating a date"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["make a date","create a date","datetime.date","year month day"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating a date with the datetime module date(year, month, day) modules dates datetime non-examinable datetime date creating a date make a date create a date datetime.date year month day the `date` class of the `datetime` module allows a calendar date to be stored as a single value, with its parts available separately. it is useful for records such as the day a book was borrowed. alternatively, a date can be kept as text, but text cannot be compared or counted from."},{"sid":"D5.02","title":"Finding the number of days between two dates","cmd":"(returned - borrowed).days","terms":["date difference","timedelta","days between"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["days between two dates","how long between","subtract dates","elapsed days"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the number of days between two dates (returned - borrowed).days modules dates datetime non-examinable date difference timedelta days between days between two dates how long between subtract dates elapsed days subtracting one date from another allows us to find the time between them, as an object with a `days` attribute. it is useful for questions such as how many days a book was kept."},{"sid":"D5.03","title":"Adding days to a date with timedelta","cmd":"date_1 + timedelta(days=n)","terms":["timedelta","adding days","due date"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["add days to a date","date plus a week","timedelta","due date"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding days to a date with timedelta date_1 + timedelta(days=n) modules dates datetime non-examinable timedelta adding days due date add days to a date date plus a week timedelta due date a `timedelta` allows a number of days to be added to a date or taken from it. it is useful for dates that depend on another."},{"sid":"D5.04","title":"Showing a date as text and reading a date from text","cmd":"date_1.strftime(format)   datetime.strptime(text, format)","terms":["strftime","strptime","date format"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["date to text","text to date","strftime","strptime","format a date","parse a date"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing a date as text and reading a date from text date_1.strftime(format)   datetime.strptime(text, format) modules dates datetime non-examinable strftime strptime date format date to text text to date strftime strptime format a date parse a date the method `strftime()` allows a date to be written as text in a chosen layout, and `strptime()` allows text to be read as a date. it is useful for reports and for dates typed by a user, which arrive as text. alternatively, `str(date)` gives the year-month-day form only."},{"sid":"D5.05","title":"Making a date and time with datetime","cmd":"datetime(year, month, day, hour, minute)   datetime.now()","terms":["datetime","time of day","now","hour minute"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["date and time","datetime object","timestamp","hours and minutes"],"examinable":false,"search":"making a date and time with datetime datetime(year, month, day, hour, minute)   datetime.now() modules dates datetime non-examinable datetime time of day now hour minute date and time datetime object timestamp hours and minutes the `datetime` class stores a date and a time of day together, from a year, month, day, hour and minute (and optionally seconds). the call `datetime.now()` gives the current moment on the computer's clock. it is useful when the time of day matters."},{"sid":"D5.06","title":"Finding the time between two datetimes","cmd":"(arrived - sent).total_seconds()","terms":["total_seconds","hours between","time difference"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["time between two datetimes","seconds elapsed","duration","total_seconds"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the time between two datetimes (arrived - sent).total_seconds() modules dates datetime non-examinable total_seconds hours between time difference time between two datetimes seconds elapsed duration total_seconds subtracting two `datetime` values gives a `timedelta`, and its `total_seconds()` gives the whole difference in seconds, which can be divided to give minutes or hours. the `days` attribute gives only the whole days, so it misses part of a day. it is useful for durations that are not whole days."},{"sid":"D5.07","title":"Comparing and sorting dates","cmd":"sorted(dates)   max(dates)   d1 < d2","terms":["sort dates","compare dates","latest date","earliest date"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["compare dates","sort dates","earliest date","latest date"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing and sorting dates sorted(dates)   max(dates)   d1 < d2 modules dates datetime sorting non-examinable sort dates compare dates latest date earliest date compare dates sort dates earliest date latest date dates can be compared with ` `, and a list of dates can be given to `sorted()`, `min()` and `max()`, because the order of dates is the order of time. it is useful for finding the earliest or latest record."},{"sid":"D5.08","title":"Finding the day of the week","cmd":"date_1.weekday()   date_1.strftime(\"%A\")","terms":["weekday","day of the week","isoweekday"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["day of the week","weekday name","is it a weekend","which day was it"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the day of the week date_1.weekday()   date_1.strftime(\"%a\") modules dates datetime non-examinable weekday day of the week isoweekday day of the week weekday name is it a weekend which day was it the method `weekday()` gives the day of the week as a number, with monday as 0, and `strftime(\"%a\")` gives its name. the method `isoweekday()` numbers the days from 1 for monday to 7 for sunday. it is useful when a program behaves differently on some days of the week."},{"sid":"D5.09","title":"Date and time format codes","cmd":"%d   %m   %B   %Y   %H   %M","terms":["strftime codes","date format codes","%Y %m %d"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["date format codes","%Y %m %d","what does %B mean","strftime codes"],"examinable":false,"search":"date and time format codes %d   %m   %b   %y   %h   %m modules dates datetime non-examinable strftime codes date format codes %y %m %d date format codes %y %m %d what does %b mean strftime codes the codes used by `strftime()` and `strptime()` start with `%`, and each stands for one part of a date or time. letters in the format that are not codes, such as `/`, `-` and `:`, are copied as they are."},{"sid":"D5.10","title":"Keeping dates as dates, not text","cmd":"datetime.strptime(text, format)","terms":["dates as text","sort text dates","wrong date order"],"tags":["modules","dates","datetime","non-examinable"],"task":["dates as text problem","sort dates correctly","convert text to dates","keep dates as dates"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping dates as dates, not text datetime.strptime(text, format) modules dates datetime non-examinable dates as text sort text dates wrong date order dates as text problem sort dates correctly convert text to dates keep dates as dates dates stored as text sort and compare by their characters, not by time, so the order is wrong without an error message. converting the text to dates with `strptime()` first makes the order, the differences and the comparisons correct."},{"sid":"E1.01","title":"Comparing values with `==` and `!=`","cmd":"value_1 == value_2","terms":["==","!=","equality","inequality"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["equal to","not equal to","compare two values","== versus ="],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing values with == and != value_1 == value_2 conditions core == != equality inequality equal to not equal to compare two values == versus = the operators `==` and `!=` allow us to ask whether two values are equal or different. it is useful for checks such as whether a parcel has been delivered. alternatively, a single `=` stores a value and does not compare, so `==` is needed for a test."},{"sid":"E1.02","title":"Comparing numbers with `<`, `>`, `<=` and `>=`","cmd":"number <= limit","terms":["<",">","<=",">=","numerical comparison"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["greater than","less than","compare numbers","at least","at most"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing numbers with <, >, <= and >= number <= limit conditions core < > <= >= numerical comparison greater than less than compare numbers at least at most the operators ` `, ` =` allow us to ask which of two numbers is larger. it is useful for limits, such as whether a parcel is under the weight limit for a price band. alternatively, `==` checks only for an exact match."},{"sid":"E1.03","title":"Storing a comparison in a variable","cmd":"result = number > limit","terms":["boolean expression","boolean variable"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["store a comparison","boolean variable","True or False result"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing a comparison in a variable result = number > limit conditions core boolean expression boolean variable store a comparison boolean variable true or false result a common way to keep the result of a comparison is to store it in a variable with a name that reads like a question. it is useful when the same test is used in several places, or when a name explains the test."},{"sid":"E1.04","title":"Running code only when a condition is true","cmd":"if condition:","terms":["if","indentation after if"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["if statement","do something only when","conditional code","run only if"],"examinable":true,"search":"running code only when a condition is true if condition: conditions core if indentation after if if statement do something only when conditional code run only if an `if` statement allows us to run code only when a condition is true. it is useful for actions that depend on the data."},{"sid":"E1.05","title":"Choosing between two outcomes with `if`-`else`","cmd":"if ...: else:","terms":["else"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["if else","two outcomes","choose between two actions","either or"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between two outcomes with if-else if ...: else: conditions core else if else two outcomes choose between two actions either or an `else` block allows us to run different code when the condition is false. it is useful when there are exactly two outcomes."},{"sid":"E1.06","title":"Choosing between several outcomes with `if`-`elif`-`else`","cmd":"if ...: elif ...: else:","terms":["elif","if-elif-else chain"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["elif","several outcomes","multiple conditions","grade boundaries","more than two choices"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between several outcomes with if-elif-else if ...: elif ...: else: conditions core elif if-elif-else chain elif several outcomes multiple conditions grade boundaries more than two choices an `if-elif-else` chain allows us to choose one outcome from three or more. it is useful for price bands, such as postage that depends on weight. alternatively, nested `if` statements give the same result, but a chain is clearer to read."},{"sid":"E1.07","title":"Combining tests with `and` and `or`","cmd":"if is_signed and is_paid:","terms":["and","or","combining conditions"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["and or","both conditions","either condition","combine tests"],"examinable":true,"search":"combining tests with and and or if is_signed and is_paid: conditions core and or combining conditions and or both conditions either condition combine tests the words `and` and `or` allow us to combine several tests into one condition. it is useful when a decision depends on more than one fact. alternatively, `if` statements can be placed inside one another, but a single combined condition is shorter."},{"sid":"E1.08","title":"Running code only when a list has items","cmd":"if items:","terms":["empty list","testing a list"],"tags":["conditions","lists","core"],"task":["is the list empty","run only if the list has items","if items","non-empty list"],"examinable":true,"search":"running code only when a list has items if items: conditions lists core empty list testing a list is the list empty run only if the list has items if items non-empty list a list used as a condition allows us to test whether it has any items, because an empty list counts as false. it is useful before processing a list. alternatively, `len(parcels) > 0` gives the same answer, but a list alone is shorter."},{"sid":"E1.09","title":"Using `if` without `else`","cmd":"","terms":["if without else","optional else"],"tags":["conditions","core"],"task":["if without else","do nothing otherwise","optional step"],"examinable":true,"search":"using if without else  conditions core if without else optional else if without else do nothing otherwise optional step an `if` statement with no `else` allows a program to act only in the case that matters and do nothing otherwise. it is useful for actions such as a warning that is needed only sometimes. alternatively, an `else` block is needed when something must happen in both cases."},{"sid":"E1.16","title":"Checking for special items in a list","cmd":"for item in items: if item == value:","terms":["special items","if inside a loop","checking a list"],"tags":["conditions","lists","loops","core"],"task":["check for special items in a list","loop with if","find an item in a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking for special items in a list for item in items: if item == value: conditions lists loops core special items if inside a loop checking a list check for special items in a list loop with if find an item in a list an `if` inside a `for` loop allows each item of a list to be checked, and the special ones to be handled differently. it is useful for lists in which a few items need extra care. alternatively, `in` tests whether a special item is present at all, but not which ones."},{"sid":"E1.10","title":"Ignoring capital letters when comparing text","cmd":"if text.lower() == value:","terms":["case-insensitive comparison"],"tags":["conditions","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["ignore capital letters","case insensitive comparison","lower() when comparing"],"examinable":false,"search":"ignoring capital letters when comparing text if text.lower() == value: conditions strings non-examinable case-insensitive comparison ignore capital letters case insensitive comparison lower() when comparing a common way to compare text without caring about capital letters is to change the case first with `lower()`. it is useful when text is typed by different people. alternatively, the comparison can list every spelling, but that is long and can miss one."},{"sid":"E1.11","title":"Testing whether a value is in a list","cmd":"if value in items:","terms":["in","not in"],"tags":["conditions","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["is a value in a list","membership","in and not in"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing whether a value is in a list if value in items: conditions lists non-examinable in not in is a value in a list membership in and not in the words `in` and `not in` allow us to test whether a value is in a list. it is useful for checks such as whether a tracking code has already been recorded. alternatively, a loop could search the list, but `in` needs one line."},{"sid":"E1.17","title":"Checking the items of one list against another","cmd":"if key in d:","terms":["using multiple lists","checking one list against another"],"tags":["conditions","lists","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["check key in a dictionary","items of one list against another","membership in a dictionary"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking the items of one list against another if key in d: conditions lists loops non-examinable using multiple lists checking one list against another check key in a dictionary items of one list against another membership in a dictionary a loop over one list with an `in` test against a second list allows every requested item to be checked against what is available. it is useful for orders and stock. alternatively, a dictionary of stock counts also records how many are left."},{"sid":"E1.12","title":"Using several if statements when more than one can be true","cmd":"","terms":["several if statements","elif or if"],"tags":["conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["several ifs","more than one can be true","separate if statements versus elif"],"examinable":false,"search":"using several if statements when more than one can be true  conditions non-examinable several if statements elif or if several ifs more than one can be true separate if statements versus elif separate `if` statements allow every test to run, while an `if-elif-else` chain stops at the first test that is true. it is useful to choose separate statements when several things can apply at once. alternatively, a chain is correct when only one outcome is allowed."},{"sid":"E1.13","title":"Chaining comparisons to test a range","cmd":"low < number <= high","terms":["chained comparison","range test"],"tags":["conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["range test","number between two limits","chained comparison","low < x < high"],"examinable":false,"search":"chaining comparisons to test a range low < number <= high conditions non-examinable chained comparison range test range test number between two limits chained comparison low < x < high a chained comparison allows us to test that a value lies between two limits in one expression. it is useful for ranges such as a weight band, written as it would be in mathematics."},{"sid":"E1.14","title":"Values that count as false","cmd":"","terms":["truthiness","truthy and falsy","empty values"],"tags":["conditions","core","non-examinable"],"task":["what counts as False","empty list is false","zero is false","truthy and falsy"],"examinable":false,"search":"values that count as false  conditions core non-examinable truthiness truthy and falsy empty values what counts as false empty list is false zero is false truthy and falsy in a condition, the values `0`, `0.0`, the empty string, the empty list and `none` count as false, and almost everything else counts as true."},{"sid":"E1.15","title":"Testing one value against several with `in`","cmd":"if value in (a, b):","terms":["in with several values","membership test"],"tags":["conditions","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["compare with several values","value in a tuple","either of two values"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing one value against several with in if value in (a, b): conditions lists non-examinable in with several values membership test compare with several values value in a tuple either of two values a common way to test a value against several possibilities is to ask whether it is `in` a tuple or list of them. it is useful for statuses and categories."},{"sid":"E2.01","title":"Repeating code for every item in a list","cmd":"for item in items:","terms":["for loop","looping through a list"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["for loop","repeat for every item","loop over a list","go through each item"],"examinable":true,"search":"repeating code for every item in a list for item in items: loops core for loop looping through a list for loop repeat for every item loop over a list go through each item a `for` loop allows us to run the same code once for every item in a list. it is useful when a list has many items that all need the same treatment."},{"sid":"E2.02","title":"Several actions for each item","cmd":"for item in items:    print(...)    print(...)","terms":["indented block","code inside a loop"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["several actions in a loop","indent the loop body","more than one line per item"],"examinable":true,"search":"several actions for each item for item in items:    print(...)    print(...) loops core indented block code inside a loop several actions in a loop indent the loop body more than one line per item every indented line below the `for` line is part of the loop, so more than one step can run for each item. it is useful when an item needs several actions."},{"sid":"E2.03","title":"One action after a loop","cmd":"for item in items:    ...  print(\"Done\")","terms":["code after a loop","indentation ends the loop"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["after the loop","one action at the end","unindent after a loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"one action after a loop for item in items:    ...  print(\"done\") loops core code after a loop indentation ends the loop after the loop one action at the end unindent after a loop a line that is not indented allows us to run code once, after the loop has finished. it is useful for a summary, such as a message at the end of a stocktake."},{"sid":"E2.04","title":"Repeating code a set number of times with `range()`","cmd":"for n in range(start, stop):","terms":["for with range","counting loop"],"tags":["loops","ranges","core"],"task":["repeat n times","loop with range","count loop","for n in range"],"examinable":true,"search":"repeating code a set number of times with range() for n in range(start, stop): loops ranges core for with range counting loop repeat n times loop with range count loop for n in range a `for` loop over `range()` allows us to repeat code for a chosen run of numbers. it is useful for numbers that are not stored in a list. alternatively, a list of the numbers could be written out, but `range()` is shorter and cannot contain a typing mistake."},{"sid":"E2.05","title":"Adding up values with a loop","cmd":"total = total + value","terms":["running total","accumulator"],"tags":["loops","core","patterns"],"task":["add up values in a loop","running total","accumulate","sum with a loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding up values with a loop total = total + value loops core patterns running total accumulator add up values in a loop running total accumulate sum with a loop a common way to add up values is to start a total at zero and add each value inside a loop. it is useful when something must be done to each value as well as adding it up. alternatively, `sum()` adds a list in one step when nothing else is needed."},{"sid":"E2.06","title":"Looping through part of a list","cmd":"for item in items[:n]:","terms":["looping through a slice"],"tags":["loops","slices","core"],"task":["loop over part of a list","first n items","slice in a loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through part of a list for item in items[:n]: loops slices core looping through a slice loop over part of a list first n items slice in a loop a slice in a `for` loop allows us to repeat code for only part of a list. it is useful when only some items are wanted. alternatively, `range()` could be used with indexes, but a slice needs no counting."},{"sid":"E2.07","title":"Looping through a tuple","cmd":"for item in items:","terms":["looping through a tuple"],"tags":["loops","tuples","core"],"task":["loop over a tuple","iterate a tuple","each item of a tuple"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through a tuple for item in items: loops tuples core looping through a tuple loop over a tuple iterate a tuple each item of a tuple a `for` loop over a tuple allows us to use each fixed value in turn. it is useful for values that must not change."},{"sid":"E2.08","title":"Looping through a dictionary","cmd":"for key, value in d.items():","terms":["looping through a dictionary"],"tags":["loops","dictionaries","core"],"task":["loop over a dictionary","keys and values","for key value in items"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping through a dictionary for key, value in d.items(): loops dictionaries core looping through a dictionary loop over a dictionary keys and values for key value in items a `for` loop over `items()` allows us to use every key and value of a dictionary. it is useful for reports, such as an item and its price on each line. alternatively, a loop over the keys alone is enough when the values are not needed."},{"sid":"E2.09","title":"IndentationError: expected an indented block","cmd":"","terms":["IndentationError","expected an indented block"],"tags":["loops","errors","debugging"],"task":["expected an indented block","IndentationError after for","empty loop body"],"examinable":true,"search":"indentationerror: expected an indented block  loops errors debugging indentationerror expected an indented block expected an indented block indentationerror after for empty loop body an `indentationerror` of this kind tells us that a line that must be indented was not."},{"sid":"E2.10","title":"IndentationError: unexpected indent","cmd":"","terms":["IndentationError","unexpected indent"],"tags":["loops","errors","debugging"],"task":["unexpected indent","extra spaces","IndentationError","stray indentation"],"examinable":true,"search":"indentationerror: unexpected indent  loops errors debugging indentationerror unexpected indent unexpected indent extra spaces indentationerror stray indentation an `indentationerror` of this kind tells us that a line was indented without a reason."},{"sid":"E2.11","title":"SyntaxError: expected ':'","cmd":"","terms":["SyntaxError","missing colon"],"tags":["loops","errors","debugging"],"task":["expected ':'","missing colon","SyntaxError after for or if"],"examinable":true,"search":"syntaxerror: expected ':'  loops errors debugging syntaxerror missing colon expected ':' missing colon syntaxerror after for or if a `syntaxerror` that expects a colon tells us that the `for` line does not end with `:`."},{"sid":"E2.12","title":"A line indented under the loop by mistake","cmd":"","terms":["line inside the loop","line outside the loop"],"tags":["loops","debugging","mistakes"],"task":["line indented under the loop by mistake","repeated when it should not be","indentation changes behaviour"],"examinable":true,"search":"a line indented under the loop by mistake  loops debugging mistakes line inside the loop line outside the loop line indented under the loop by mistake repeated when it should not be indentation changes behaviour a line indented one level too far runs once per item instead of once, and a line indented too little runs only once."},{"sid":"E2.16","title":"A second line not indented under the loop","cmd":"","terms":["forgetting to indent an additional line","logic error"],"tags":["loops","debugging","mistakes"],"task":["line not indented under the loop","only runs once","missing indentation","logic error"],"examinable":true,"search":"a second line not indented under the loop  loops debugging mistakes forgetting to indent an additional line logic error line not indented under the loop only runs once missing indentation logic error a line that belongs inside the loop but is not indented runs once, after the loop, and not once for each item."},{"sid":"E2.13","title":"Numbering the items of a list with `enumerate()`","cmd":"for number, item in enumerate(items, start=1):","terms":["enumerate()","numbering items","position in a loop"],"tags":["loops","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["number the items","enumerate","index and item together","start numbering at 1"],"examinable":false,"search":"numbering the items of a list with enumerate() for number, item in enumerate(items, start=1): loops lists non-examinable enumerate() numbering items position in a loop number the items enumerate index and item together start numbering at 1 the function `enumerate()` allows a loop to take each item together with its position. it is useful for numbered lists, such as the lines of a receipt. alternatively, a counter variable or `range(len(shelf))` gives the position, but `enumerate()` needs no extra lines."},{"sid":"E2.14","title":"Looping through two lists together with `zip()`","cmd":"for a, b in zip(items_1, items_2):","terms":["zip()","looping over two lists","pairs"],"tags":["loops","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["loop over two lists","zip","pair up lists","go through two lists together"],"examinable":false,"search":"looping through two lists together with zip() for a, b in zip(items_1, items_2): loops lists non-examinable zip() looping over two lists pairs loop over two lists zip pair up lists go through two lists together the function `zip()` allows a loop to take one item from each of two lists at the same time. it is useful when two lists belong together. alternatively, `range(len(items))` with an index reads both lists, but `zip()` is shorter and clearer."},{"sid":"E2.15","title":"Building a dictionary from two lists","cmd":"dict(zip(keys, values))","terms":["dictionary from two lists","dict(zip())"],"tags":["loops","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["dictionary from two lists","zip into a dictionary","keys and values lists to dict"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a dictionary from two lists dict(zip(keys, values)) loops dictionaries non-examinable dictionary from two lists dict(zip()) dictionary from two lists zip into a dictionary keys and values lists to dict a common way to make a dictionary from two matching lists is to pair them with `zip()` and give the pairs to `dict()`. it is useful when data arrives as separate lists."},{"sid":"E3.01","title":"Repeating code while a condition is true","cmd":"while condition:","terms":["while loop","loop condition"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["while loop","repeat while a condition holds","loop until","not a fixed number of times"],"examinable":true,"search":"repeating code while a condition is true while condition: loops core while loop loop condition while loop repeat while a condition holds loop until not a fixed number of times a `while` loop allows us to repeat code for as long as a condition stays true. it is useful when the number of repeats is not known in advance. alternatively, a `for` loop is better when the items or the number of repeats are known."},{"sid":"E3.02","title":"Letting the user choose when to quit","cmd":"while value != stop_value:","terms":["quit","sentinel value","input in a loop"],"tags":["loops","core","input"],"task":["let the user quit","loop until the user types quit","sentinel value"],"examinable":true,"search":"letting the user choose when to quit while value != stop_value: loops core input quit sentinel value input in a loop let the user quit loop until the user types quit sentinel value a common way to let the user end a loop is to keep asking until they type a chosen word such as `quit`. it is useful for lists of unknown length."},{"sid":"E3.03","title":"Using a flag to control a loop","cmd":"while keep_going:","terms":["flag","boolean flag"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["flag variable","control a loop with a flag","keep_going"],"examinable":true,"search":"using a flag to control a loop while keep_going: loops core flag boolean flag flag variable control a loop with a flag keep_going a flag is a variable that holds `true` or `false` and is used as the loop condition. it is useful when several different events can end the loop. alternatively, a single comparison is shorter when there is only one reason to stop."},{"sid":"E3.04","title":"Leaving a loop early with `break`","cmd":"break","terms":["break"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["leave a loop early","break","stop the loop","exit when found"],"examinable":true,"search":"leaving a loop early with break break loops core break leave a loop early break stop the loop exit when found the `break` statement allows us to leave a loop at once, whatever the condition says. it is useful when the loop has done its job. alternatively, a flag ends the loop at the next check and lets the rest of the pass finish."},{"sid":"E3.05","title":"Skipping to the next pass with `continue`","cmd":"continue","terms":["continue"],"tags":["loops","core"],"task":["skip to the next iteration","continue","ignore this item","skip a pass"],"examinable":true,"search":"skipping to the next pass with continue continue loops core continue skip to the next iteration continue ignore this item skip a pass the `continue` statement allows us to skip the rest of the current pass and start the next one. it is useful for ignoring unwanted values. alternatively, an `if` around the rest of the pass does the same, but needs one more level of indentation."},{"sid":"E3.06","title":"Moving items from one list to another","cmd":"while items: item = items.pop()","terms":["moving items between lists"],"tags":["loops","lists","core"],"task":["move items between lists","empty one list into another","pop in a while loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"moving items from one list to another while items: item = items.pop() loops lists core moving items between lists move items between lists empty one list into another pop in a while loop a common way to move items between lists is a `while` loop that removes one from the first list and adds it to the second. it is useful for work queues, such as parcels moving from \"to deliver\" to \"delivered\". alternatively, a `for` loop cannot safely remove items from the list that it is reading."},{"sid":"E3.07","title":"Removing every copy of a value from a list","cmd":"while value in items: items.remove(value)","terms":["removing all instances"],"tags":["loops","lists","core"],"task":["remove every copy of a value","delete all occurrences","remove repeated values from a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing every copy of a value from a list while value in items: items.remove(value) loops lists core removing all instances remove every copy of a value delete all occurrences remove repeated values from a list a `while` loop with `remove()` allows us to delete every copy of a value, because `remove()` deletes only the first. it is useful for cleaning a list. alternatively, a new list can be built from the items that are wanted."},{"sid":"E3.08","title":"Filling a dictionary with input from the user","cmd":"while True: ... d[key] = value","terms":["dictionary from input"],"tags":["loops","dictionaries","core","input"],"task":["fill a dictionary from input","collect answers","build a dictionary from the user"],"examinable":true,"search":"filling a dictionary with input from the user while true: ... d[key] = value loops dictionaries core input dictionary from input fill a dictionary from input collect answers build a dictionary from the user a common way to build a dictionary from the user's answers is a loop that asks for a key and a value until the user is finished. it is useful when the data is typed in at run time."},{"sid":"E3.09","title":"Avoiding an infinite loop","cmd":"","terms":["infinite loop","Ctrl+C"],"tags":["loops","core","mistakes"],"task":["infinite loop","program never stops","loop does not end","stop a runaway loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"avoiding an infinite loop  loops core mistakes infinite loop ctrl+c infinite loop program never stops loop does not end stop a runaway loop an infinite loop is a loop whose condition never becomes false, so the program never stops."},{"sid":"E4.01","title":"Building a list in one line","cmd":"[expression for item in items]","terms":["list comprehension"],"tags":["lists","loops","core"],"task":["list comprehension","build a list in one line","transform every item","shorthand for a loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"building a list in one line [expression for item in items] lists loops core list comprehension list comprehension build a list in one line transform every item shorthand for a loop a list comprehension allows us to build a new list from an existing one in a single line. it is useful when every item is changed in the same way."},{"sid":"E4.02","title":"Comparing a list comprehension with a for loop","cmd":"","terms":["comprehension or loop"],"tags":["lists","loops","core"],"task":["list comprehension versus loop","same result two ways","when to use a comprehension"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing a list comprehension with a for loop  lists loops core comprehension or loop list comprehension versus loop same result two ways when to use a comprehension a list comprehension and a `for` loop with `append()` give the same list, and the comprehension packs the loop into one line. it is useful to see them side by side, because the comprehension is clearer once the pattern is known. alternatively, the loop form is better when each pass needs several lines."},{"sid":"E4.03","title":"Keeping only some items with `if`","cmd":"[item for item in items if condition]","terms":["comprehension with if","filtering a list"],"tags":["lists","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["filter in a comprehension","keep only some items","comprehension with if"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping only some items with if [item for item in items if condition] lists loops non-examinable comprehension with if filtering a list filter in a comprehension keep only some items comprehension with if an `if` at the end of a list comprehension allows us to keep only the items that pass a test. it is useful for selecting part of a list."},{"sid":"E4.04","title":"Choosing a loop when a comprehension is hard to read","cmd":"","terms":["when to avoid a comprehension"],"tags":["lists","loops","style","non-examinable"],"task":["comprehension too complicated","when to use a loop instead","readable code"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing a loop when a comprehension is hard to read  lists loops style non-examinable when to avoid a comprehension comprehension too complicated when to use a loop instead readable code a `for` loop is the better choice when each item needs several steps or a long expression. it is useful to choose by readability, because code is read more often than it is written. alternatively, a comprehension suits a short, single expression."},{"sid":"E4.05","title":"Building a list of squares and cubes","cmd":"[expression for n in range(start, stop)]","terms":["list of squares","list of cubes","powers in a list"],"tags":["lists","loops","numbers","core"],"task":["squares and cubes","list of powers","comprehension with range"],"examinable":true,"search":"building a list of squares and cubes [expression for n in range(start, stop)] lists loops numbers core list of squares list of cubes powers in a list squares and cubes list of powers comprehension with range if we have a range of numbers, then a loop or a list comprehension with `**` builds a list of their squares or cubes. it is useful for tables of areas and volumes."},{"sid":"E4.06","title":"Building a dictionary in one line","cmd":"{key: value for item in items}","terms":["dictionary comprehension","building a dictionary in one line"],"tags":["dictionaries","loops","core","non-examinable"],"task":["dictionary comprehension","build a dictionary in one line","key value pairs from a list"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a dictionary in one line {key: value for item in items} dictionaries loops core non-examinable dictionary comprehension building a dictionary in one line dictionary comprehension build a dictionary in one line key value pairs from a list a dictionary comprehension allows a dictionary to be built from a list in one line, with a key and a value for every item. it is useful for lookups worked out from a list, such as the length of each item name. alternatively, a `for` loop that assigns `d[key] = value` builds the same dictionary over several lines."},{"sid":"E5.01","title":"Asking the user for text with `input()`","cmd":"text = input(prompt)","terms":["input()","keyboard input"],"tags":["input","core"],"task":["ask the user a question","input","read typed text","get input from the keyboard"],"examinable":true,"search":"asking the user for text with input() text = input(prompt) input core input() keyboard input ask the user a question input read typed text get input from the keyboard the `input()` function allows a program to stop and read a line that the user types. it is useful when the data is not known until the program runs."},{"sid":"E5.02","title":"Writing a clear prompt","cmd":"input(prompt)","terms":["prompt"],"tags":["input","core"],"task":["write a clear prompt","tell the user what to type","input prompt wording"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing a clear prompt input(prompt) input core prompt write a clear prompt tell the user what to type input prompt wording a clear prompt tells the user what to type, in what form and in what units. it is useful because the user cannot see the program, only the prompt."},{"sid":"E5.03","title":"Accepting a number with `int()`","cmd":"number = int(input(prompt))","terms":["int(input())","numerical input"],"tags":["input","core"],"task":["input as an integer","ask for a whole number","int(input())","convert input to a number"],"examinable":true,"search":"accepting a number with int() number = int(input(prompt)) input core int(input()) numerical input input as an integer ask for a whole number int(input()) convert input to a number the function `int()` allows us to turn the text from `input()` into a whole number. it is useful whenever the answer is used in arithmetic."},{"sid":"E5.04","title":"Keeping asking until the user quits","cmd":"while True: ... if text == stop_value: break","terms":["asking repeatedly","input loop"],"tags":["input","loops","core"],"task":["keep asking","loop until the user quits","repeated prompts","menu loop"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping asking until the user quits while true: ... if text == stop_value: break input loops core asking repeatedly input loop keep asking loop until the user quits repeated prompts menu loop a common way to collect several answers is a loop that asks again after each one until the user types a chosen word. it is useful when the number of answers is not known."},{"sid":"E5.05","title":"Accepting a decimal number with `float()`","cmd":"number = float(input(prompt))","terms":["float(input())"],"tags":["input","non-examinable"],"task":["input as a decimal","ask for a measurement","float input"],"examinable":false,"search":"accepting a decimal number with float() number = float(input(prompt)) input non-examinable float(input()) input as a decimal ask for a measurement float input the function `float()` allows us to turn typed text into a number with a decimal part. it is useful for measurements and prices. alternatively, `int()` accepts whole numbers only."},{"sid":"E5.06","title":"Checking typed text before converting it","cmd":"if answer.isdigit():","terms":["checking input","validating input","isdigit()"],"tags":["input","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["check input before converting","is it digits","avoid ValueError","validate text input"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking typed text before converting it if answer.isdigit(): input conditions non-examinable checking input validating input isdigit() check input before converting is it digits avoid valueerror validate text input a common way to avoid a `valueerror` is to test typed text with `isdigit()` before calling `int()`. it is useful because users type unexpected things, such as a word or an empty line. alternatively, a `try` block catches the error after it happens."},{"sid":"E5.07","title":"Keeping asking until the input is valid","cmd":"while True: ... break","terms":["asking again","valid input loop"],"tags":["input","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["keep asking until valid","input validation loop","retry until correct"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping asking until the input is valid while true: ... break input loops non-examinable asking again valid input loop keep asking until valid input validation loop retry until correct a common way to get a valid answer is a loop that asks again until the answer passes every check, and then leaves the loop with `break`. it is useful for any value with limits."},{"sid":"E5.08","title":"Catching a bad number with try and except","cmd":"try: float(answer) except ValueError:","terms":["try except for input","ValueError for input"],"tags":["input","errors","non-examinable"],"task":["catch a bad number","try and except","handle ValueError","do not crash on bad input"],"examinable":false,"search":"catching a bad number with try and except try: float(answer) except valueerror: input errors non-examinable try except for input valueerror for input catch a bad number try and except handle valueerror do not crash on bad input a `try` block with `except valueerror` allows a program to catch the error from `int()` or `float()` and ask again. it is useful because it accepts every number that the conversion can read, including decimals and negative numbers, which a test such as `isdigit()` would reject. alternatively, `isdigit()` checks first and never causes the error."},{"sid":"E6.01","title":"Counting the items that pass a test","cmd":"if condition: count = count + 1","terms":["counter","counting in a loop"],"tags":["loops","patterns","core","non-examinable"],"task":["count items that pass a test","counter in a loop","how many meet a condition"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting the items that pass a test if condition: count = count + 1 loops patterns core non-examinable counter counting in a loop count items that pass a test counter in a loop how many meet a condition a common way to count items is a counter that starts at 0 and is increased by 1 inside an `if` for each item that passes the test. it is useful for questions such as how many parcels are over the weight limit. alternatively, `sum()` over a list of `true` and `false` values counts them, but needs more care to read."},{"sid":"E6.02","title":"Keeping track of the largest value","cmd":"if value > largest: largest = value","terms":["finding the maximum","largest so far"],"tags":["loops","patterns","core","non-examinable"],"task":["find the largest value","track the maximum","running maximum","biggest so far"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping track of the largest value if value > largest: largest = value loops patterns core non-examinable finding the maximum largest so far find the largest value track the maximum running maximum biggest so far a common way to find the largest value by hand is a variable that holds the largest seen so far, and a loop that replaces it when a larger value appears. it is useful when more than the value is needed. alternatively, `max()` gives the largest value directly."},{"sid":"E6.03","title":"Searching for the first match","cmd":"if code == wanted: ... break","terms":["linear search","searching a list","first match"],"tags":["loops","patterns","core","non-examinable"],"task":["search for the first match","find an item","stop when found","linear search"],"examinable":false,"search":"searching for the first match if code == wanted: ... break loops patterns core non-examinable linear search searching a list first match search for the first match find an item stop when found linear search a common way to search is a loop that checks each item in turn and stops with `break` at the first match. it is useful for finding a record in a list. alternatively, `in` says only whether the value is present, and `index()` gives its position."},{"sid":"E6.04","title":"Building a list of the items that pass a test","cmd":"matches.append(value)","terms":["filtering a list","building a new list"],"tags":["loops","patterns","lists","core","non-examinable"],"task":["collect items that pass a test","filter into a new list","build a list of matches"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a list of the items that pass a test matches.append(value) loops patterns lists core non-examinable filtering a list building a new list collect items that pass a test filter into a new list build a list of matches a common way to select items is to start with an empty list and append each item that passes a test. it is useful for questions such as which parcels are over the weight limit. alternatively, a list comprehension with an `if` does the same in one line."},{"sid":"E6.05","title":"Putting one loop inside another","cmd":"for row in table: for value in row:","terms":["nested loop","loop inside a loop"],"tags":["loops","patterns","core","non-examinable"],"task":["nested loops","loop inside a loop","rows and columns","every pair"],"examinable":false,"search":"putting one loop inside another for row in table: for value in row: loops patterns core non-examinable nested loop loop inside a loop nested loops loop inside a loop rows and columns every pair a loop inside a loop allows every combination of two sets of values to be visited. it is useful for tables, such as a price for every combination of weight band and delivery speed."},{"sid":"E6.06","title":"Choosing the loop pattern for the question","cmd":"","terms":["which loop to use","choosing a loop pattern"],"tags":["loops","patterns","non-examinable"],"task":["which loop pattern","count total search or collect","choose a loop pattern"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing the loop pattern for the question  loops patterns non-examinable which loop to use choosing a loop pattern which loop pattern count total search or collect choose a loop pattern the question that is being asked allows us to choose the shape of the loop: counting, tracking, searching, filtering or combining. it is useful to name the pattern before writing the code, because each one has its own starting variable."},{"sid":"F1.01","title":"Using a function instead of repeating code","cmd":"","terms":["why use a function","function","reuse"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["why use functions","avoid repeating code","reuse code","what is a function for"],"examinable":true,"search":"using a function instead of repeating code  functions core why use a function function reuse why use functions avoid repeating code reuse code what is a function for a function allows us to give a group of lines a name, so that the same steps can be run again from one short line. it is useful when a calculation is needed in several places."},{"sid":"F1.02","title":"Defining and calling a function","cmd":"def show_opening_message():","terms":["def","calling a function","function body"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["define a function","write a function","call a function","def"],"examinable":true,"search":"defining and calling a function def show_opening_message(): functions core def calling a function function body define a function write a function call a function def the word `def` allows us to define a function, and its name followed by brackets allows us to call it. it is useful for steps that are used more than once."},{"sid":"F1.03","title":"Defining a function before calling it","cmd":"","terms":["function defined before it is called","NameError for a function"],"tags":["functions","core","mistakes"],"task":["function must be defined before use","NameError calling a function","order of definition"],"examinable":true,"search":"defining a function before calling it  functions core mistakes function defined before it is called nameerror for a function function must be defined before use nameerror calling a function order of definition a function must be defined before the call to it runs, because python reads the program from the top. in a simple script that means the `def` comes above the line that calls it."},{"sid":"F1.04","title":"Passing information to a function","cmd":"def show_total(total):","terms":["parameter","argument","passing information"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["pass information to a function","parameters","give a function an input"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing information to a function def show_total(total): functions core parameter argument passing information pass information to a function parameters give a function an input a name in the brackets of `def` allows a function to receive a value each time it is called. it is useful when the same steps are needed with different values. alternatively, a function with nothing in its brackets can use only the values that are written inside it."},{"sid":"F1.05","title":"Naming the parts of a function call","cmd":"","terms":["parameter","argument","parameters and arguments"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["parameter versus argument","parts of a function call","terminology"],"examinable":true,"search":"naming the parts of a function call  functions core parameter argument parameters and arguments parameter versus argument parts of a function call terminology a parameter is the name in the function definition, and an argument is the value given in the call."},{"sid":"F1.06","title":"Following the order in which Python runs a function call","cmd":"","terms":["order of execution","flow of a program","call stack"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["order a function runs","follow a function call","trace a call","what runs first"],"examinable":true,"search":"following the order in which python runs a function call  functions core order of execution flow of a program call stack order a function runs follow a function call trace a call what runs first python runs a program from the top, and a call jumps into the function body and then returns to the line after the call."},{"sid":"F1.07","title":"Describing a function with a docstring","cmd":"\"\"\"One-line description.\"\"\"","terms":["docstring"],"tags":["functions","style","non-examinable"],"task":["docstring","describe a function","document a function","triple quote comment"],"examinable":false,"search":"describing a function with a docstring \"\"\"one-line description.\"\"\" functions style non-examinable docstring docstring describe a function document a function triple quote comment a docstring allows us to describe what a function does, in a string placed on the first line of its body. it is useful because the description is kept with the code and shown by `help()`. alternatively, a comment above the function also explains it, but `help()` cannot show it."},{"sid":"F2.01","title":"Passing arguments by position","cmd":"func(value_1, value_2)","terms":["positional arguments","order of arguments"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["positional arguments","pass in order","arguments by position"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing arguments by position func(value_1, value_2) functions core positional arguments order of arguments positional arguments pass in order arguments by position positional arguments allow us to match values to parameters by their order in the call. it is useful for short calls where the order is simple to remember. alternatively, keyword arguments name each value, which does not depend on the order."},{"sid":"F2.02","title":"Passing arguments by keyword","cmd":"func(name_1=value_1, name_2=value_2)","terms":["keyword arguments","name=value"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["keyword arguments","pass by name","named arguments"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing arguments by keyword func(name_1=value_1, name_2=value_2) functions core keyword arguments name=value keyword arguments pass by name named arguments a keyword argument allows us to name the parameter that a value belongs to, written as `name=value`. it is useful when a call has several values, because the names show what each one means and the order no longer matters. alternatively, positional arguments are shorter when there are only one or two values."},{"sid":"F2.03","title":"Giving a parameter a default value","cmd":"def func(parameter, option=default):","terms":["default value","default argument"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["default parameter value","optional argument with a default","parameter default"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving a parameter a default value def func(parameter, option=default): functions core default value default argument default parameter value optional argument with a default parameter default a default value in the `def` line allows a parameter to be left out of the call. it is useful when one value is nearly always the same."},{"sid":"F2.04","title":"Making an argument optional","cmd":"def func(parameter, option=\"\"):","terms":["optional argument","empty string default"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["optional argument","make an argument optional","empty string default"],"examinable":true,"search":"making an argument optional def func(parameter, option=\"\"): functions core optional argument empty string default optional argument make an argument optional empty string default a common way to make an argument optional is a default of an empty string, with an `if` to test whether a value was given. it is useful when some calls need an extra piece of information and others do not. alternatively, two functions could be written, but one function with an optional argument avoids repeating the rest."},{"sid":"F2.05","title":"Using equivalent function calls","cmd":"","terms":["equivalent calls"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["equivalent function calls","same call written differently","positional or keyword"],"examinable":true,"search":"using equivalent function calls  functions core equivalent calls equivalent function calls same call written differently positional or keyword positional arguments, keyword arguments and defaults can be mixed, so several different calls can give the same result."},{"sid":"F2.06","title":"TypeError: missing 1 required positional argument","cmd":"","terms":["TypeError","missing argument","too many arguments"],"tags":["functions","errors","debugging"],"task":["missing required positional argument","TypeError calling a function","forgot an argument"],"examinable":true,"search":"typeerror: missing 1 required positional argument  functions errors debugging typeerror missing argument too many arguments missing required positional argument typeerror calling a function forgot an argument a `typeerror` of this kind tells us that a function was called with too few arguments."},{"sid":"F3.01","title":"Giving a value back with `return`","cmd":"return expression","terms":["return","returned value"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["return a value","give a value back","function output","return statement"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving a value back with return return expression functions core return returned value return a value give a value back function output return statement the `return` statement allows a function to give a value back to the line that called it. it is useful when the caller needs the result for something else. alternatively, a function can print its result, but a printed value cannot be used in a calculation."},{"sid":"F3.02","title":"Using the value that a function returns","cmd":"result = func(value_1, value_2)","terms":["using a returned value","storing a result"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["use the value a function returns","store the result of a call","assign a function result"],"examinable":true,"search":"using the value that a function returns result = func(value_1, value_2) functions core using a returned value storing a result use the value a function returns store the result of a call assign a function result a common way to use a function is to store what it returns in a variable and build the next step on it. it is useful for calculations with several stages, such as a line cost, a total and a final amount. alternatively, the calls can be nested in one line, but named steps are simpler to check."},{"sid":"F3.03","title":"Returning a dictionary","cmd":"return {key: value}","terms":["returning a dictionary"],"tags":["functions","dictionaries","core"],"task":["return a dictionary","function returns several named values","return a record"],"examinable":true,"search":"returning a dictionary return {key: value} functions dictionaries core returning a dictionary return a dictionary function returns several named values return a record a function that returns a dictionary allows several related values to leave the function as one object. it is useful for describing something with named fields. alternatively, the function could return a list, but the fields would then be known only by position."},{"sid":"F3.04","title":"Choosing between return and print","cmd":"","terms":["return or print","None"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["return or print","show or give back","output versus result"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between return and print  functions core return or print none return or print show or give back output versus result a function that prints shows something to the user, and a function that returns gives a value to the program. it is useful to choose deliberately, because a printed value cannot be stored or used in a calculation."},{"sid":"F3.05","title":"Functions without `return`","cmd":"","terms":["None returned","function with no return"],"tags":["functions","non-examinable"],"task":["function without return","returns None","why do I get None","print only function"],"examinable":false,"search":"functions without return  functions non-examinable none returned function with no return function without return returns none why do i get none print only function a function with no `return` statement gives back `none`, which is python's value for \"nothing\"."},{"sid":"F3.06","title":"Leaving a function early with `return`","cmd":"if condition: return None","terms":["early return"],"tags":["functions","non-examinable"],"task":["leave a function early","early return","return None","stop on a bad input"],"examinable":false,"search":"leaving a function early with return if condition: return none functions non-examinable early return leave a function early early return return none stop on a bad input a `return` statement allows a function to end at once, so the lines after it do not run. it is useful for handling a case that makes the rest of the function pointless. alternatively, an `if-else` can run only one branch, but needs more indentation."},{"sid":"F3.07","title":"Returning several values as a tuple","cmd":"return lowest, highest","terms":["returning several values"],"tags":["functions","tuples","non-examinable"],"task":["return several values","return a tuple","return lowest and highest","unpack a result"],"examinable":false,"search":"returning several values as a tuple return lowest, highest functions tuples non-examinable returning several values return several values return a tuple return lowest and highest unpack a result a `return` with several values separated by commas allows a function to give back more than one result. it is useful for a pair of related values. alternatively, a dictionary gives each value a name."},{"sid":"F3.08","title":"Using a function in a while loop","cmd":"while func(value) > 0:","terms":["function in a loop condition"],"tags":["functions","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["function in a while condition","loop until a function says stop","call in a loop test"],"examinable":false,"search":"using a function in a while loop while func(value) > 0: functions loops non-examinable function in a loop condition function in a while condition loop until a function says stop call in a loop test if a function returns a value, then a `while` loop can use that value in its condition. it is useful for keeping the loop's test in one named place. alternatively, the test can be written directly in the loop, but a name explains it."},{"sid":"F4.01","title":"Local variables","cmd":"","terms":["local variable","scope"],"tags":["functions","core","scope"],"task":["local variable","variable inside a function","scope","not defined outside"],"examinable":true,"search":"local variables  functions core scope local variable scope local variable variable inside a function scope not defined outside a variable created inside a function is local, which means that it exists only while the function runs."},{"sid":"F4.02","title":"Reading a variable from outside a function","cmd":"CONSTANT_NAME = value","terms":["global variable","reading a global"],"tags":["functions","core","scope"],"task":["read a variable from outside a function","global constant","use a constant in a function"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a variable from outside a function constant_name = value functions core scope global variable reading a global read a variable from outside a function global constant use a constant in a function a function can read a variable that was created outside it, in the main part of the program. it is useful for fixed values that many functions use. alternatively, the value can be passed in as an argument, which makes the function simpler to reuse."},{"sid":"F4.03","title":"Assigning inside a function","cmd":"","terms":["changing a global variable","same name local and global"],"tags":["functions","core","scope","mistakes"],"task":["assign inside a function","local copy","why the global did not change","UnboundLocalError"],"examinable":true,"search":"assigning inside a function  functions core scope mistakes changing a global variable same name local and global assign inside a function local copy why the global did not change unboundlocalerror an assignment inside a function creates a new local variable, even when a variable of the same name exists outside."},{"sid":"F4.04","title":"Passing values in and returning results out","cmd":"total = func(total)","terms":["passing values in","returning results out"],"tags":["functions","core","scope"],"task":["pass values in and return results","avoid global variables","update a total with a function"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing values in and returning results out total = func(total) functions core scope passing values in returning results out pass values in and return results avoid global variables update a total with a function a common way to change a value with a function is to pass it in as an argument and return the new value. it is useful because the function then depends only on what it is given, so it is simple to check and reuse. alternatively, a function can change a variable outside it, but that hides how the program works."},{"sid":"F5.01","title":"Passing a list to a function","cmd":"func(items)","terms":["list argument","passing a list"],"tags":["functions","lists","core"],"task":["pass a list to a function","list as an argument","function takes a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing a list to a function func(items) functions lists core list argument passing a list pass a list to a function list as an argument function takes a list a list given as an argument allows a function to work with every item in it. it is useful for tasks that apply to a whole collection."},{"sid":"F5.02","title":"Changing a list inside a function","cmd":"def func(items):","terms":["function changes a list"],"tags":["functions","lists","core"],"task":["function changes my list","modify a list in a function","side effect","mutable argument"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing a list inside a function def func(items): functions lists core function changes a list function changes my list modify a list in a function side effect mutable argument a function that receives a list can change that same list, and the change is still there after the function ends. it is useful for tasks that update a collection in place. alternatively, the function can build and return a new list, which leaves the original alone."},{"sid":"F5.03","title":"Protecting a list by passing a copy","cmd":"func(items[:])","terms":["passing a copy of a list"],"tags":["functions","lists","core"],"task":["protect a list","pass a copy","avoid changing the original","slice copy as an argument"],"examinable":true,"search":"protecting a list by passing a copy func(items[:]) functions lists core passing a copy of a list protect a list pass a copy avoid changing the original slice copy as an argument a slice with `[:]` allows us to give a function a copy of a list, so that the original cannot be changed. it is useful when the original prices must be kept. alternatively, the function can be written to return a new list, so it never changes its argument."},{"sid":"F5.04","title":"Returning a new list instead of changing the old one","cmd":"return [expression for item in items]","terms":["function returns a new list"],"tags":["functions","lists","core"],"task":["return a new list","do not change the input","function that builds a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"returning a new list instead of changing the old one return [expression for item in items] functions lists core function returns a new list return a new list do not change the input function that builds a list a common way to avoid changing a list is to build a new list inside the function and return it. it is useful because the caller always knows that the argument is unchanged. alternatively, changing the list in place avoids a second list, but the change can surprise the caller."},{"sid":"F5.05","title":"Changing a list but not a number in a function","cmd":"","terms":["mutable argument","immutable argument"],"tags":["functions","lists","scope","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["list changes but number does not","mutable versus immutable argument","why a number is not updated"],"examinable":false,"search":"changing a list but not a number in a function  functions lists scope objects non-examinable mutable argument immutable argument list changes but number does not mutable versus immutable argument why a number is not updated a function receives a second name for the same object, so a change to a list shows outside, while a change to a number only moves the function's own name."},{"sid":"F6.01","title":"Accepting any number of arguments with `*args`","cmd":"def func(*args):","terms":["*args","arbitrary arguments","asterisk"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["any number of arguments","*args","variable arguments","accept many values"],"examinable":true,"search":"accepting any number of arguments with *args def func(*args): functions core *args arbitrary arguments asterisk any number of arguments *args variable arguments accept many values a parameter that starts with `*` allows a function to accept any number of positional arguments, which arrive together as a tuple. it is useful when the number of values changes from call to call. alternatively, a single list argument holds the same values, but the call then needs square brackets."},{"sid":"F6.02","title":"Accepting any number of keyword arguments with `**kwargs`","cmd":"def make_label(**details):","terms":["**kwargs","arbitrary keyword arguments","double asterisk"],"tags":["functions","dictionaries","core"],"task":["any number of keyword arguments","**kwargs","accept named options","flexible keyword arguments"],"examinable":true,"search":"accepting any number of keyword arguments with **kwargs def make_label(**details): functions dictionaries core **kwargs arbitrary keyword arguments double asterisk any number of keyword arguments **kwargs accept named options flexible keyword arguments a parameter that starts with `**` allows a function to accept any number of keyword arguments, which arrive together as a dictionary. it is useful when the names of the values are not known in advance. alternatively, a dictionary argument holds the same data, but the call then needs curly brackets."},{"sid":"F6.03","title":"Mixing ordinary and arbitrary arguments","cmd":"def func(parameter, *args):","terms":["positional and arbitrary arguments"],"tags":["functions","core"],"task":["mix ordinary and *args","fixed parameter then extras","first argument plus any others"],"examinable":true,"search":"mixing ordinary and arbitrary arguments def func(parameter, *args): functions core positional and arbitrary arguments mix ordinary and *args fixed parameter then extras first argument plus any others a common way to combine fixed and variable arguments is to put the fixed parameters first and the `*` parameter last. it is useful when a call always has some values, such as a customer name, followed by any number of items. alternatively, a list can hold the items, but then the call needs square brackets."},{"sid":"F6.04","title":"Accepting both `*args` and `**kwargs`","cmd":"def log_event(*args, **kwargs):","terms":["*args and **kwargs together","both"],"tags":["functions","dictionaries","core"],"task":["args and kwargs together","accept anything","logging function arguments"],"examinable":true,"search":"accepting both *args and **kwargs def log_event(*args, **kwargs): functions dictionaries core *args and **kwargs together both args and kwargs together accept anything logging function arguments a function can take both `*args` and `**kwargs`, so that it accepts any positional arguments and any keyword arguments in one definition. it is useful for a function that passes everything on to another function, or that records whatever it was given. alternatively, a named parameter list is clearer when the arguments are known."},{"sid":"F7.01","title":"Giving each function one job","cmd":"","terms":["one job per function","single responsibility"],"tags":["functions","core","style","design"],"task":["one job per function","single responsibility","keep functions small"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving each function one job  functions core style design one job per function single responsibility one job per function single responsibility keep functions small a function that does one job allows the steps of a program to be named, read and reused separately. it is useful to keep to one job, because a function that calculates and prints and saves cannot be used for only one of those."},{"sid":"F7.02","title":"Choosing a name that says what the function does","cmd":"","terms":["function names","naming functions"],"tags":["functions","style","design"],"task":["name a function","good function names","verb names","say what it does"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing a name that says what the function does  functions style design function names naming functions name a function good function names verb names say what it does a function name written as a short verb phrase in lower case allows a call to be read like a sentence. it is useful because the call line is usually all that a reader sees. alternatively, a short name saves typing, but a name such as `f` says nothing about the job."},{"sid":"F7.03","title":"Taking values in and returning the result","cmd":"","terms":["inputs as parameters","results returned"],"tags":["functions","core","design"],"task":["values in and result out","function design","input and output","no hidden state"],"examinable":true,"search":"taking values in and returning the result  functions core design inputs as parameters results returned values in and result out function design input and output no hidden state a function that gets its inputs from parameters and gives its result with `return` allows the same function to be used with any values. it is useful because the function does not depend on variables elsewhere in the program."},{"sid":"F7.04","title":"Changing an argument only when stated","cmd":"","terms":["side effects","changing arguments"],"tags":["functions","lists","design"],"task":["change an argument only when stated","side effects","document that a function changes its input"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing an argument only when stated  functions lists design side effects changing arguments change an argument only when stated side effects document that a function changes its input a function that changes a list it was given has a side effect, because the caller's list is different afterwards."},{"sid":"F7.05","title":"Turning repeated code into functions","cmd":"","terms":["refactoring","before and after"],"tags":["functions","core","design","refactoring"],"task":["turn repeated code into a function","remove duplication","refactor into functions"],"examinable":true,"search":"turning repeated code into functions  functions core design refactoring refactoring before and after turn repeated code into a function remove duplication refactor into functions if a script repeats a calculation or mixes several jobs, then moving each job into a function gives a program that does the same thing and is clearer to read. it is useful before adding new features."},{"sid":"F7.06","title":"Checking a small function with a few calls","cmd":"","terms":["checking a function","small functions"],"tags":["functions","core","testing","design"],"task":["test a small function","check with a few calls","simple checks","try known answers"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking a small function with a few calls  functions core testing design checking a function small functions test a small function check with a few calls simple checks try known answers a common way to check a function is to call it with values whose answers are known and compare the results. it is useful because a small function with one job is simple to check, and a change later can be checked again with the same calls. alternatively, a testing tool such as pytest runs many such checks automatically."},{"sid":"F7.07","title":"Calling a function from itself","cmd":"","terms":["recursion","base case","recursive step"],"tags":["functions","recursion","non-examinable"],"task":["recursion","function calls itself","recursive function","base case"],"examinable":false,"search":"calling a function from itself  functions recursion non-examinable recursion base case recursive step recursion function calls itself recursive function base case a recursive function calls itself on a smaller version of the same task, and stops at a case that needs no further call, called the base case. it is useful for tasks that repeat on a smaller piece."},{"sid":"F7.08","title":"Adding up a basket that contains bags","cmd":"","terms":["nested recursion","list inside a list"],"tags":["functions","recursion","non-examinable"],"task":["nested bags","recursive sum","add up a basket containing bags","nested lists"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding up a basket that contains bags  functions recursion non-examinable nested recursion list inside a list nested bags recursive sum add up a basket containing bags nested lists if a list can contain other lists, then a function that calls itself on each inner list can add up everything, however deep the bags are nested. it is useful for data with no fixed depth."},{"sid":"F7.09","title":"RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded","cmd":"","terms":["RecursionError","maximum recursion depth"],"tags":["functions","recursion","errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["RecursionError","maximum recursion depth","infinite recursion","missing base case"],"examinable":false,"search":"recursionerror: maximum recursion depth exceeded  functions recursion errors debugging non-examinable recursionerror maximum recursion depth recursionerror maximum recursion depth infinite recursion missing base case a `recursionerror` tells us that a function called itself too many times, which usually means that the base case is missing."},{"sid":"G1.01","title":"Storing functions in a module","cmd":"","terms":["module",".py file","import"],"tags":["modules","functions","core"],"task":["put functions in a module","save functions in a file","reuse functions in several programs"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing functions in a module  modules functions core module .py file import put functions in a module save functions in a file reuse functions in several programs a module is a file of python code, and importing it allows its functions to be used in another file. it is useful for keeping a program short and for using the same functions in several programs."},{"sid":"G1.02","title":"Importing a module and calling its functions","cmd":"import shop","terms":["import module","dot notation"],"tags":["modules","core"],"task":["import a module","use functions from a file","module.function()","import my own file"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing a module and calling its functions import shop modules core import module dot notation import a module use functions from a file module.function() import my own file the statement `import shop` allows a program to use every function in the module, written as `shop.function_name()`. it is useful when a module has several functions, because the module name shows where each one comes from. alternatively, `from shop import ...` brings in single functions, so the module name is no longer needed."},{"sid":"G1.03","title":"Importing one function from a module","cmd":"from module import name","terms":["from import","importing a function"],"tags":["modules","core"],"task":["import one function","from module import name","use a function without the prefix"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing one function from a module from module import name modules core from import importing a function import one function from module import name use a function without the prefix the statement `from shop import price_with_vat` allows a single function to be used by its own name. it is useful when only one or two functions are needed, because the calls are shorter. alternatively, `import shop` keeps the module name in each call, which shows where the function comes from."},{"sid":"G1.04","title":"Giving a function or module a shorter name","cmd":"from module import name as alias","terms":["as","alias","import as"],"tags":["modules","core"],"task":["shorter name","import as","alias","rename on import"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving a function or module a shorter name from module import name as alias modules core as alias import as shorter name import as alias rename on import the word `as` allows an imported function or module to be given a different name in the program. it is useful when a name is long or clashes with another name. alternatively, the original name can be used, which is clearer when it is already short."},{"sid":"G1.05","title":"Importing every function with `*`","cmd":"","terms":["import *","star import"],"tags":["modules","mistakes","non-examinable"],"task":["import everything","from module import *","why not to import star"],"examinable":false,"search":"importing every function with *  modules mistakes non-examinable import * star import import everything from module import * why not to import star the statement `from shop import *` allows every function in a module to be used without its module name."},{"sid":"G1.06","title":"Using a module from the standard library","cmd":"import math","terms":["standard library","math","random"],"tags":["modules","core","non-examinable"],"task":["standard library","import math","use a built-in module","batteries included"],"examinable":false,"search":"using a module from the standard library import math modules core non-examinable standard library math random standard library import math use a built-in module batteries included the standard library is a set of modules that comes with python, such as `math` and `random`. it is useful for common jobs that need no installation. alternatively, a function for the job could be written, but a standard module has already been tested."},{"sid":"G1.07","title":"Choosing and shuffling at random with the `random` module","cmd":"random.choice(items)   random.shuffle(items)","terms":["random","choice","shuffle","seed","simulation"],"tags":["modules","random","non-examinable"],"task":["random choice","shuffle a list","pick a random item","random numbers"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing and shuffling at random with the random module random.choice(items)   random.shuffle(items) modules random non-examinable random choice shuffle seed simulation random choice shuffle a list pick a random item random numbers the `random` module allows a program to choose, shuffle and draw numbers at random. it is useful for simulations, such as tossing a coin or rolling a die many times. alternatively, fixed test data makes a program repeatable, which random values do not, unless a seed is set."},{"sid":"G1.08","title":"Counting items quickly with `Counter`","cmd":"Counter(items).most_common(n)","terms":["Counter","collections","most_common"],"tags":["modules","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["count items quickly","Counter","most common items","tally"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting items quickly with counter counter(items).most_common(n) modules dictionaries non-examinable counter collections most_common count items quickly counter most common items tally the `counter` class of the `collections` module allows the items of a list to be counted in one call, and gives the commonest ones. it is useful for tallies such as the best-selling items."},{"sid":"G2.01","title":"Explaining code with comments","cmd":"# a comment","terms":["comment","#","commenting"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["add a comment","explain code","hash symbol","note in code"],"examinable":true,"search":"explaining code with comments # a comment style core comment # commenting add a comment explain code hash symbol note in code a line that starts with `#` allows us to write a note for readers that python ignores. it is useful for explaining why a line is there, or what a value means. alternatively, a clear name often removes the need for a comment, so both are used together."},{"sid":"G2.02","title":"Writing good comments","cmd":"","terms":["good comments","why not what"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["good comments","what to comment","why not what","useful comments"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing good comments  style core good comments why not what good comments what to comment why not what useful comments a common rule is that a comment should say why the code does something, because the code already shows what it does. it is useful because a comment that repeats the code adds nothing, and a wrong comment misleads."},{"sid":"G2.03","title":"Indenting with four spaces","cmd":"four spaces per level","terms":["PEP 8","indentation","four spaces"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["indent with four spaces","indentation rule","tabs or spaces","PEP 8 indentation"],"examinable":true,"search":"indenting with four spaces four spaces per level style core pep 8 indentation four spaces indent with four spaces indentation rule tabs or spaces pep 8 indentation pep 8, the python style guide, recommends four spaces for each level of indentation. it is useful to follow because mixed tabs and spaces cause an `indentationerror`, and four spaces look the same in every editor. alternatively, any consistent indent works, but four spaces is what other python programmers expect."},{"sid":"G2.04","title":"Keeping lines to 79 characters","cmd":"a line of at most 79 characters","terms":["line length","79 characters","wrapping lines"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["line length","79 characters","wrap long lines","PEP 8"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping lines to 79 characters a line of at most 79 characters style core line length 79 characters wrapping lines line length 79 characters wrap long lines pep 8 pep 8 recommends lines of at most 79 characters, with long lines wrapped inside brackets. it is useful because short lines can be read without scrolling and shown side by side."},{"sid":"G2.05","title":"Putting blank lines between the parts of a program","cmd":"two blank lines between functions","terms":["blank lines","layout of functions"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["blank lines","spacing between functions","separate parts of a program","layout"],"examinable":true,"search":"putting blank lines between the parts of a program two blank lines between functions style core blank lines layout of functions blank lines spacing between functions separate parts of a program layout pep 8 recommends two blank lines between functions and one blank line to separate steps inside them. it is useful because the gaps show where one part ends and the next begins."},{"sid":"G2.06","title":"The Zen of Python","cmd":"import this","terms":["Zen of Python","import this"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["Zen of Python","import this","Python philosophy","guiding principles"],"examinable":true,"search":"the zen of python import this style core zen of python import this zen of python import this python philosophy guiding principles the zen of python is a short list of principles for writing python, printed by `import this`."},{"sid":"G2.07","title":"PEP 8 naming conventions","cmd":"","terms":["naming conventions","snake_case","CapWords","UPPER_CASE"],"tags":["style","non-examinable"],"task":["naming conventions","snake_case","CamelCase for classes","PEP 8 names"],"examinable":false,"search":"pep 8 naming conventions  style non-examinable naming conventions snake_case capwords upper_case naming conventions snake_case camelcase for classes pep 8 names pep 8 recommends `snake_case` for variables and functions, `capwords` for classes and `upper_case` for constants."},{"sid":"G2.08","title":"PEP 8 spacing of operators and statements","cmd":"","terms":["spaces around operators","style of if statements"],"tags":["style","non-examinable"],"task":["spaces around operators","spacing rules","style of statements","PEP 8 spacing"],"examinable":false,"search":"pep 8 spacing of operators and statements  style non-examinable spaces around operators style of if statements spaces around operators spacing rules style of statements pep 8 spacing pep 8 recommends one space on each side of operators such as `==`, `<` and `=`, and none inside brackets."},{"sid":"G3.01","title":"Splitting a script into input, processing and output","cmd":"","terms":["input processing output","structuring a program"],"tags":["structure","functions","core","non-examinable"],"task":["structure of a script","input processing output","organise a program","split a program into stages"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting a script into input, processing and output  structure functions core non-examinable input processing output structuring a program structure of a script input processing output organise a program split a program into stages a common way to organise a program is three kinds of function: one to get the data, one to work on it and one to show the result. it is useful because each part can be changed or checked on its own, such as a new postage rate with no change to the questions."},{"sid":"G3.02","title":"Putting the program in a `main()` function","cmd":"def main():","terms":["main()","main function"],"tags":["structure","functions","core","non-examinable"],"task":["main function","put the program in main","def main","structure a script"],"examinable":false,"search":"putting the program in a main() function def main(): structure functions core non-examinable main() main function main function put the program in main def main structure a script a common way to start a program is to put the main steps in a function called `main()`, and to call it once at the bottom of the file. it is useful because the steps read as a short outline, and the file holds only functions and one call. alternatively, the steps can sit at the top level of the file, but then they run whenever the file is imported."},{"sid":"G3.03","title":"Running code only when the file is run directly","cmd":"if __name__ == \"__main__\":","terms":["__name__","\"__main__\"","running or importing"],"tags":["structure","modules","core","non-examinable"],"task":["if __name__ == main","run only when executed","import without running","module or script"],"examinable":false,"search":"running code only when the file is run directly if __name__ == \"__main__\": structure modules core non-examinable __name__ \"__main__\" running or importing if __name__ == main run only when executed import without running module or script the test `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` allows code to run when a file is run directly but not when it is imported. it is useful for a file that is both a program and a module of functions."},{"sid":"G3.05","title":"Double-underscore names","cmd":"__name__   __init__   __str__   __len__   __doc__","terms":["double underscore","dunder names","special methods","magic methods","__doc__","__len__"],"tags":["structure","modules","classes","non-examinable"],"task":["what is __name__","double underscore","dunder","magic methods","special methods","underscores in names"],"examinable":false,"search":"double-underscore names __name__   __init__   __str__   __len__   __doc__ structure modules classes non-examinable double underscore dunder names special methods magic methods __doc__ __len__ what is __name__ double underscore dunder magic methods special methods underscores in names names with two underscores at each end, such as `__name__` and `__init__`, are names that python itself defines and uses behind the scenes; they are often called \"dunder\" names, from \"double underscore\". it is useful to recognise them, because they explain how `if __name__ == \"__main__\":`, `__init__` and printing an object work. alternatively, a name with one leading underscore (`_total`) is an ordinary name that the programmer marks as internal."},{"sid":"G3.04","title":"Deciding what goes in which module","cmd":"","terms":["organising modules","where code goes"],"tags":["structure","modules","non-examinable"],"task":["which module for what","organise code into files","decide what goes where","project layout"],"examinable":false,"search":"deciding what goes in which module  structure modules non-examinable organising modules where code goes which module for what organise code into files decide what goes where project layout a program that is larger than one file allows related functions to be grouped, with each group in its own module. it is useful to group by purpose, such as rates, input and output, so that a change touches only one file."},{"sid":"G4.01","title":"Breaking a task into small steps","cmd":"","terms":["decomposition","breaking a problem into steps","planning"],"tags":["planning","core","non-examinable"],"task":["break a task into steps","decompose a problem","plan before coding","where to start"],"examinable":false,"search":"breaking a task into small steps  planning core non-examinable decomposition breaking a problem into steps planning break a task into steps decompose a problem plan before coding where to start breaking a task into small steps allows each step to be written, run and checked on its own. it is useful because a large task that is written all at once is hard to start and hard to fix."},{"sid":"G4.02","title":"Writing pseudocode before the code","cmd":"","terms":["pseudocode","plan in words"],"tags":["planning","core","non-examinable"],"task":["pseudocode","write the plan in words","plan an algorithm","outline before code"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing pseudocode before the code  planning core non-examinable pseudocode plan in words pseudocode write the plan in words plan an algorithm outline before code pseudocode is a plan written in short, plain sentences that look like code but follow no strict rules. it is useful for working out the logic without worrying about syntax. alternatively, a flowchart shows the same plan as a diagram, which suits decisions with many branches."},{"sid":"G4.03","title":"Turning pseudocode into code","cmd":"","terms":["pseudocode to code"],"tags":["planning","core","non-examinable"],"task":["pseudocode to code","translate a plan","turn steps into Python"],"examinable":false,"search":"turning pseudocode into code  planning core non-examinable pseudocode to code pseudocode to code translate a plan turn steps into python if we have pseudocode, then each line can be translated into python, keeping the same order and the same decisions. it is useful because the logic has already been checked, so only the syntax is left to get right."},{"sid":"G4.04","title":"Following a program by hand with a trace table","cmd":"","terms":["trace table","dry run","following code by hand"],"tags":["planning","debugging","core","non-examinable"],"task":["trace table","follow a program by hand","track variable values","dry run"],"examinable":false,"search":"following a program by hand with a trace table  planning debugging core non-examinable trace table dry run following code by hand trace table follow a program by hand track variable values dry run a trace table records the value of every variable after each line or each pass of a loop, so that a program can be followed by hand. it is useful for checking a loop before running it, and for finding where a value goes wrong. alternatively, a debugger or print statements show the same values, but the hand version needs no tools."},{"sid":"G4.05","title":"Choosing test cases before writing the code","cmd":"","terms":["test cases","expected results first"],"tags":["planning","testing","core","non-examinable"],"task":["choose test cases","what to test","examples before code","expected results"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing test cases before writing the code  planning testing core non-examinable test cases expected results first choose test cases what to test examples before code expected results a common way to check a plan is to write down inputs and the results that are expected before writing the code. it is useful because the expected results come from the plan, not from the code, so a wrong answer is not accepted by mistake."},{"sid":"G5.01","title":"Ten principles of good code","cmd":"","terms":["good code","code quality","principles of programming"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["write good code","code quality","good programming practice","clean code","how to write better code"],"examinable":true,"search":"ten principles of good code  style core good code code quality principles of programming write good code code quality good programming practice clean code how to write better code good code gives the right answer and can also be read, checked and changed by someone else, or by its author a month later. these ten principles are a short list to think about while writing and before handing code in."},{"sid":"G5.02","title":"Writing code for the reader","cmd":"","terms":["readable code","readability"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["readable code","make code easier to read","code for other people","tidy up code"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing code for the reader  style core readable code readability readable code make code easier to read code for other people tidy up code code is read far more often than it is written, so the aim is code whose meaning can be seen at a glance, not the shortest code that works."},{"sid":"G5.03","title":"Names that say what a value holds","cmd":"","terms":["meaningful names","descriptive variable names"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["choose good variable names","meaningful names","descriptive names","what to call a variable"],"examinable":true,"search":"names that say what a value holds  style core meaningful names descriptive variable names choose good variable names meaningful names descriptive names what to call a variable a name should say what the value is, with its unit when it has one, so the reader does not need a comment or the rest of the program to understand it."},{"sid":"G5.04","title":"Named constants instead of unexplained numbers","cmd":"","terms":["magic numbers","unexplained numbers","constants"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["magic numbers","avoid repeated numbers","name a fixed value","where to put a rate"],"examinable":true,"search":"named constants instead of unexplained numbers  style core magic numbers unexplained numbers constants magic numbers avoid repeated numbers name a fixed value where to put a rate a fixed value that appears in the code as a bare number (a \"magic number\") says nothing about what it means, and must be found and changed in every place it is used."},{"sid":"G5.05","title":"One job for each function","cmd":"","terms":["single responsibility","one job per function"],"tags":["functions","style","core"],"task":["one job per function","split a function","function does too much","single responsibility"],"examinable":true,"search":"one job for each function  functions style core single responsibility one job per function one job per function split a function function does too much single responsibility a function that does one job can be named clearly, tested on its own and used again; a function that calculates, decides and prints at once can only be used in the one place it was written for."},{"sid":"G5.06","title":"Values in, result out","cmd":"","terms":["avoiding global variables","pure function"],"tags":["functions","style","core"],"task":["avoid global variables","function changes a variable outside","pass values in","return the result"],"examinable":true,"search":"values in, result out  functions style core avoiding global variables pure function avoid global variables function changes a variable outside pass values in return the result a function is easiest to follow and test when everything it uses comes in as arguments and its answer goes out with `return`, instead of reading or changing variables outside it."},{"sid":"G5.07","title":"Writing code once instead of copying it","cmd":"","terms":["DRY","don't repeat yourself","copied code"],"tags":["functions","style","core"],"task":["don't repeat yourself","DRY","copied code","avoid repetition","same code several times"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing code once instead of copying it  functions style core dry don't repeat yourself copied code don't repeat yourself dry copied code avoid repetition same code several times code that is copied and edited must be corrected in every copy when something changes; written once, in a function or a loop, it is corrected in one place. the rule is often called dry, \"don't repeat yourself\"."},{"sid":"G5.08","title":"Simple code before clever code","cmd":"","terms":["simple code","clever code","one-liners"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["keep code simple","avoid clever one-liners","code too complicated","simpler code"],"examinable":true,"search":"simple code before clever code  style core simple code clever code one-liners keep code simple avoid clever one-liners code too complicated simpler code a short line that packs several decisions together is hard to read and hard to correct; the same work written as a named function or a plain loop can be checked one step at a time."},{"sid":"G5.09","title":"Keeping nesting shallow with early returns","cmd":"","terms":["nesting","early return","guard clause"],"tags":["functions","style","core"],"task":["too much nesting","nested if statements","return early","simplify nested ifs","guard clause"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping nesting shallow with early returns  functions style core nesting early return guard clause too much nesting nested if statements return early simplify nested ifs guard clause each level of indentation is one more condition the reader must keep in mind; dealing with the special cases first and returning straight away (a \"guard clause\") keeps the main rule at the left margin."},{"sid":"G5.10","title":"Catching only the error that is expected","cmd":"","terms":["bare except","catching every error","specific exceptions"],"tags":["errors","style","core"],"task":["bare except","catch every error","except Exception","error hidden","which error to catch"],"examinable":true,"search":"catching only the error that is expected  errors style core bare except catching every error specific exceptions bare except catch every error except exception error hidden which error to catch an `except` with no error named catches every error, including mistakes in the code itself, so a program can give a wrong answer with no message; naming the error that is expected lets every other mistake show."},{"sid":"G5.11","title":"Building and testing in small steps","cmd":"","terms":["incremental development","small steps","test as you go"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["test as you go","write code in small steps","incremental development","check each part works"],"examinable":true,"search":"building and testing in small steps  testing core incremental development small steps test as you go test as you go write code in small steps incremental development check each part works a common way to write a program is to add one small piece at a time, run it, and check it on a case whose answer is known before writing the next piece. it is useful because a mistake is then found in the few lines just written, not somewhere in a whole program. alternatively, a test file with pytest keeps the checks and runs them all again after every change."},{"sid":"G5.12","title":"Checking code before handing it in","cmd":"","terms":["checklist","code review","before submitting"],"tags":["style","core"],"task":["checklist for my code","review my own code","before handing in","check code quality","self review"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking code before handing it in  style core checklist code review before submitting checklist for my code review my own code before handing in check code quality self review a checklist of ten questions, one for each principle of this sheet, to read through before code is handed in or shared."},{"sid":"H1.01","title":"Keeping the details of a book together in one object","cmd":"","terms":["object","why use a class","data and behaviour together"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["why use a class","keep details together","object holds data","group related data"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping the details of a book together in one object  classes core objects object why use a class data and behaviour together why use a class keep details together object holds data group related data an object allows the data about one thing, and the actions that belong to it, to be kept together under one name. it is useful when a program handles many similar things, such as the books in a library, because every book then has the same shape."},{"sid":"H1.02","title":"Defining a class and creating an instance","cmd":"class Book:","terms":["class","instance","object","blueprint"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["define a class","create an instance","class and object","class Book"],"examinable":true,"search":"defining a class and creating an instance class book: classes core objects class instance object blueprint define a class create an instance class and object class book the word `class` allows us to define a blueprint for a kind of object, and calling the class allows us to make an object from it, called an instance. it is useful when a program needs several objects of one kind. alternatively, a dictionary holds the same data, but it has no actions of its own."},{"sid":"H1.03","title":"`__init__` and `self`","cmd":"","terms":["__init__","self","constructor"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["__init__","what is self","constructor","set up attributes"],"examinable":true,"search":"__init__ and self  classes core objects __init__ self constructor __init__ what is self constructor set up attributes the method `__init__()` runs automatically when an instance is created, and `self` is the name that the method uses for that new instance."},{"sid":"H1.04","title":"Creating several independent instances","cmd":"obj = ClassName(...)","terms":["several instances","independent objects"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["several instances","independent objects","create many objects from one class"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating several independent instances obj = classname(...) classes core objects several instances independent objects several instances independent objects create many objects from one class calling a class more than once allows us to make as many instances as needed, and each one holds its own values. it is useful for collections of similar things. alternatively, a list of dictionaries holds the same data, but each record then needs its own code to work with it."},{"sid":"H1.05","title":"Reading an attribute of an instance","cmd":"obj.attribute","terms":["attribute","dot notation","instance.attribute"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["read an attribute","dot notation on an object","get a value from an object"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading an attribute of an instance obj.attribute classes core objects attribute dot notation instance.attribute read an attribute dot notation on an object get a value from an object a dot between an instance and a name allows us to read one of its attributes. it is useful for using what an object holds. alternatively, a dictionary reads values by key in square brackets, while an object reads them by name after a dot."},{"sid":"H1.06","title":"Calling a method of an instance","cmd":"obj.method()","terms":["method","calling a method"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["call a method","object method","do something with an object"],"examinable":true,"search":"calling a method of an instance obj.method() classes core objects method calling a method call a method object method do something with an object a dot, a method name and brackets allow us to run one of the actions that belong to an object. it is useful for behaviour that goes with the data. alternatively, a separate function could take the book as an argument, but the method stays with the object."},{"sid":"H1.07","title":"AttributeError: object has no attribute","cmd":"","terms":["AttributeError","no attribute"],"tags":["classes","errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["object has no attribute","AttributeError","misspelt attribute","attribute not defined"],"examinable":false,"search":"attributeerror: object has no attribute  classes errors debugging non-examinable attributeerror no attribute object has no attribute attributeerror misspelt attribute attribute not defined an `attributeerror` tells us that an object has no attribute or method of the name that was used."},{"sid":"H2.01","title":"Set attributes in `__init__`","cmd":"self.title = title   def __init__(self, title, author, year)","terms":["attributes in __init__","self.attribute"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["set attributes in __init__","store parameters on self","initialise an object"],"examinable":true,"search":"set attributes in __init__ self.title = title   def __init__(self, title, author, year) classes core objects attributes in __init__ self.attribute set attributes in __init__ store parameters on self initialise an object a line such as `self.title = title` in `__init__()` allows us to store a value on the new instance as an attribute. it is useful for the details that every object of the class must have. alternatively, attributes can be added after the object is created, but then some objects may be missing them."},{"sid":"H2.02","title":"Giving an attribute a starting value","cmd":"self.on_loan = False   def __init__(self, title, author)","terms":["default attribute value","starting value"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["default attribute value","starting value","attribute not from a parameter","on_loan False"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving an attribute a starting value self.on_loan = false   def __init__(self, title, author) classes core objects default attribute value starting value default attribute value starting value attribute not from a parameter on_loan false setting an attribute to a fixed value in `__init__()` allows every new instance to start in the same state, with no argument needed. it is useful for values that begin the same way each time. alternatively, a default value on a parameter allows a caller to choose another starting value."},{"sid":"H2.03","title":"Changing an attribute directly","cmd":"novel.on_loan = True","terms":["changing an attribute","assigning to an attribute"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["change an attribute directly","set an attribute","update an object value"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing an attribute directly novel.on_loan = true classes core objects changing an attribute assigning to an attribute change an attribute directly set an attribute update an object value assigning to `instance.attribute` allows us to change the value that an object holds. it is useful for a quick change from outside the class. alternatively, a method can make the change, which lets the class control how the value is changed."},{"sid":"H2.04","title":"Changing an attribute through a method","cmd":"novel.lend()","terms":["method that changes an attribute"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["change an attribute through a method","method updates the object","lend a book"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing an attribute through a method novel.lend() classes core objects method that changes an attribute change an attribute through a method method updates the object lend a book a method that changes an attribute allows the class to control how its own data changes. it is useful when a change needs more than one step."},{"sid":"H2.05","title":"Increasing an attribute through a method","cmd":"self.attribute += 1","terms":["incrementing an attribute","counter in a class"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["increase an attribute","add to a counter attribute","self.count += 1"],"examinable":true,"search":"increasing an attribute through a method self.attribute += 1 classes core objects incrementing an attribute counter in a class increase an attribute add to a counter attribute self.count += 1 a common way to keep a count is an attribute that a method increases each time something happens. it is useful for totals that belong to one object."},{"sid":"H2.06","title":"Attributes of each instance","cmd":"","terms":["separate attributes","instances do not share"],"tags":["classes","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["each instance has its own attributes","separate values per object","instance attributes"],"examinable":false,"search":"attributes of each instance  classes objects non-examinable separate attributes instances do not share each instance has its own attributes separate values per object instance attributes every instance keeps its own copy of its attributes, so changing one instance does not change another."},{"sid":"H3.01","title":"Defining a method","cmd":"def describe(self):","terms":["method","def inside a class"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["define a method","function inside a class","self parameter","describe method"],"examinable":true,"search":"defining a method def describe(self): classes core objects method def inside a class define a method function inside a class self parameter describe method a function defined inside a class allows an object to have actions of its own, called methods. it is useful for behaviour that belongs with the data."},{"sid":"H3.02","title":"Passing an argument to a method","cmd":"def late_fee(self, days_late):","terms":["method arguments","parameters of a method"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["method with an argument","pass a value to a method","late fee"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing an argument to a method def late_fee(self, days_late): classes core objects method arguments parameters of a method method with an argument pass a value to a method late fee a method can take arguments after `self`, in the same way as a function. it is useful when the action needs extra information. alternatively, the value could be stored as an attribute first, which suits values that stay with the object."},{"sid":"H3.03","title":"Returning a value from a method","cmd":"return self.attribute + value","terms":["method return value"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["method returns a value","return from a method","calculate from attributes"],"examinable":true,"search":"returning a value from a method return self.attribute + value classes core objects method return value method returns a value return from a method calculate from attributes a method can use `return` to give a value back, using the object's attributes in the calculation. it is useful when the answer depends on the object's own data. alternatively, a method can print the answer, but a returned value can be stored and used."},{"sid":"H3.04","title":"Comparing a method with a function","cmd":"","terms":["method or function","difference between method and function"],"tags":["classes","functions","core"],"task":["method versus function","what is the difference","dot call versus plain call"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing a method with a function  classes functions core method or function difference between method and function method versus function what is the difference dot call versus plain call a method is a function that belongs to a class and is called on an instance, while a function stands alone and receives everything as arguments."},{"sid":"H3.05","title":"Printing an object with `__str__`","cmd":"def __str__(self):","terms":["__str__","printing an object","special method"],"tags":["classes","core","objects"],"task":["print an object","__str__","readable text for an object","why print shows an address"],"examinable":true,"search":"printing an object with __str__ def __str__(self): classes core objects __str__ printing an object special method print an object __str__ readable text for an object why print shows an address the special method `__str__()` allows us to say how an object is shown when it is printed or put in an f-string. it is useful because printing an object otherwise shows only its class name and a memory address. alternatively, a `describe()` method gives the same text, but it must be called by name."},{"sid":"H3.06","title":"Showing an object for programmers with `__repr__`","cmd":"def __repr__(self):","terms":["__repr__","representation"],"tags":["classes","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["__repr__","show an object for programmers","debugging representation","repr"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing an object for programmers with __repr__ def __repr__(self): classes objects non-examinable __repr__ representation __repr__ show an object for programmers debugging representation repr the special method `__repr__()` allows us to say how an object appears in a list, in the console, or in an error. it is useful for programmers, because it can show the exact call that would rebuild the object. alternatively, `__str__()` is used for printing to readers."},{"sid":"H4.01","title":"Saving a class in a module","cmd":"","terms":["class in a module","library.py"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["save a class in a module","class in its own file","reuse a class"],"examinable":true,"search":"saving a class in a module  classes modules core class in a module library.py save a class in a module class in its own file reuse a class a class saved in its own `.py` file allows other files to use it by importing it. it is useful because a class that many programs need is written once, and the programs stay short."},{"sid":"H4.02","title":"Importing a single class","cmd":"from library import Book","terms":["from module import class"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["import a class","from library import Book","use a class from another file"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing a single class from library import book classes modules core from module import class import a class from library import book use a class from another file the statement `from library import book` allows one class to be used by its own name. it is useful when a module holds a class that the program needs and nothing else. alternatively, `import library` brings in the whole module, and the class is then written as `library.book`."},{"sid":"H4.03","title":"Storing several classes in one module","cmd":"library.py holds Book and Loan","terms":["several classes in one module"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["several classes in one module","related classes in a file"],"examinable":true,"search":"storing several classes in one module library.py holds book and loan classes modules core several classes in one module several classes in one module related classes in a file a module can hold several classes that belong together, such as the books and the loans of a library. it is useful when the classes work with one another, because they are then found in one place. alternatively, one file per class suits large classes with little in common."},{"sid":"H4.04","title":"Importing several classes from a module","cmd":"from library import Book, Loan","terms":["importing several classes"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["import several classes","from module import A, B"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing several classes from a module from library import book, loan classes modules core importing several classes import several classes from module import a, b class names separated by commas in an import allow several classes to be brought in with one line. it is useful when a program uses two or three classes of one module. alternatively, `import library` brings in everything, with the module name in front of each class."},{"sid":"H4.05","title":"Importing a whole module","cmd":"import library","terms":["import module","library.Book"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["import a whole module","module.ClassName","use the prefix"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing a whole module import library classes modules core import module library.book import a whole module module.classname use the prefix the statement `import library` allows every class in the module to be used, written as `library.book`. it is useful because the module name shows where each class comes from, and no class names clash. alternatively, `from library import book` gives shorter code when only some classes are needed."},{"sid":"H4.06","title":"Giving an imported class or module an alias","cmd":"from library import Book as LibraryBook","terms":["as","alias for a class","import library as lib"],"tags":["classes","modules","core"],"task":["import with an alias","rename a class on import","as"],"examinable":true,"search":"giving an imported class or module an alias from library import book as librarybook classes modules core as alias for a class import library as lib import with an alias rename a class on import as the word `as` allows an imported class or module to be used under a different name. it is useful when a name is long, or when it clashes with a name in the program. alternatively, the original name is clearer when it is already short."},{"sid":"H4.07","title":"Importing every class with `*`","cmd":"","terms":["import *","star import of classes"],"tags":["classes","modules","mistakes","non-examinable"],"task":["import star","why avoid import *","clashing names"],"examinable":false,"search":"importing every class with *  classes modules mistakes non-examinable import * star import of classes import star why avoid import * clashing names the statement `from library import *` allows every class in a module to be used without its module name."},{"sid":"H4.08","title":"Importing one module into another","cmd":"import library inside members.py","terms":["module that imports a module"],"tags":["classes","modules","non-examinable"],"task":["import one module into another","modules that use modules","dependencies between files"],"examinable":false,"search":"importing one module into another import library inside members.py classes modules non-examinable module that imports a module import one module into another modules that use modules dependencies between files a module can import another module, so a class can build on a class defined elsewhere. it is useful when one file holds the books and another holds the members who borrow them. alternatively, both classes could sit in one file, which suits small programs."},{"sid":"H5.01","title":"Choosing between a dictionary and a class","cmd":"","terms":["class or dictionary","when to use a class"],"tags":["classes","design","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["dictionary or class","when to use a class","choose a data structure","record or object"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing between a dictionary and a class  classes design dictionaries non-examinable class or dictionary when to use a class dictionary or class when to use a class choose a data structure record or object a class is the better choice when the records need actions of their own, and a dictionary is enough when they only hold data. it is useful to choose by what the program must do with the records. alternatively, a dictionary is simpler for a quick record, and a class can be added later."},{"sid":"H5.02","title":"Giving each class one clear responsibility","cmd":"","terms":["one responsibility","class design"],"tags":["classes","design","non-examinable"],"task":["one responsibility per class","design a class","what should a class do","keep classes focused"],"examinable":false,"search":"giving each class one clear responsibility  classes design non-examinable one responsibility class design one responsibility per class design a class what should a class do keep classes focused a class with one clear responsibility allows each part of a program to be understood and changed on its own. it is useful to ask what a class is for in one sentence. alternatively, one class that does everything is quick to start, but each change then risks the other parts."},{"sid":"H5.03","title":"Keeping a list of objects in an attribute","cmd":"self.items = []","terms":["attribute that holds a list","collection class"],"tags":["classes","lists","design","non-examinable"],"task":["list of objects as an attribute","collection inside a class","library holds books","container class"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping a list of objects in an attribute self.items = [] classes lists design non-examinable attribute that holds a list collection class list of objects as an attribute collection inside a class library holds books container class a common way to model a collection is a class with an attribute that is a list, and methods that add to it and report on it. it is useful for things that hold many objects. alternatively, a plain list works for a simple collection, but a class can keep the rules for it in one place."},{"sid":"H5.04","title":"Objects passed to functions","cmd":"","terms":["objects passed to functions","shared object"],"tags":["classes","functions","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["pass objects to functions","object as an argument","function takes an instance"],"examinable":false,"search":"objects passed to functions  classes functions objects non-examinable objects passed to functions shared object pass objects to functions object as an argument function takes an instance an object given to a function is the same object, not a copy, so changes made inside the function are visible outside."},{"sid":"H5.05","title":"Modelling a real-world thing as a class","cmd":"","terms":["modelling","attributes and methods","nouns and verbs"],"tags":["classes","design","non-examinable"],"task":["model a real-world thing","design with classes","what to make a class","objects from the problem"],"examinable":false,"search":"modelling a real-world thing as a class  classes design non-examinable modelling attributes and methods nouns and verbs model a real-world thing design with classes what to make a class objects from the problem a class models a real thing by keeping the facts about it as attributes and the actions on it as methods. it is useful to list the facts (nouns) and the actions (verbs) of the thing before writing the class."},{"sid":"H6.01","title":"Making a child class","cmd":"class EBook(Book):   super().__init__(title, author)","terms":["inheritance","child class","super()"],"tags":["classes","inheritance","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["inheritance","child class","subclass","super().__init__","extend a class"],"examinable":false,"search":"making a child class class ebook(book):   super().__init__(title, author) classes inheritance objects non-examinable inheritance child class super() inheritance child class subclass super().__init__ extend a class a child class allows a new class to start from an existing class, and to inherit its attributes and methods. it is useful when a new kind of object is a special case of an existing one."},{"sid":"H6.02","title":"Adding attributes and methods to a child class","cmd":"self.file_size_mb = file_size_mb","terms":["child class attributes","extending a class"],"tags":["classes","inheritance","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["add attributes to a child class","extra methods in a subclass","extend a parent"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding attributes and methods to a child class self.file_size_mb = file_size_mb classes inheritance objects non-examinable child class attributes extending a class add attributes to a child class extra methods in a subclass extend a parent a child class can add attributes and methods of its own to those that it inherits. it is useful for details that only the special case has."},{"sid":"H6.03","title":"Overriding a method in a child class","cmd":"def describe(self):","terms":["overriding a method"],"tags":["classes","inheritance","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["override a method","replace parent behaviour","method with the same name in a child"],"examinable":false,"search":"overriding a method in a child class def describe(self): classes inheritance objects non-examinable overriding a method override a method replace parent behaviour method with the same name in a child a method in the child with the same name as one in the parent allows the child to replace the parent's version. it is useful when the special case does the same job in a different way. alternatively, the child can call the parent's method with `super()` and add to its result."},{"sid":"H6.04","title":"Using an instance as an attribute","cmd":"self.attribute = parameter","terms":["composition","instance as an attribute"],"tags":["classes","composition","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["object inside an object","instance as an attribute","composition","has a"],"examinable":false,"search":"using an instance as an attribute self.attribute = parameter classes composition objects non-examinable composition instance as an attribute object inside an object instance as an attribute composition has a an instance of one class can be stored as an attribute of another, so that the second object is built from the first. it is useful when something has another thing, rather than being a kind of it. alternatively, inheritance suits \"is a kind of\", and an attribute suits \"has a\"."},{"sid":"H6.05","title":"Writing a record class quickly with `@dataclass`","cmd":"@dataclass","terms":["dataclass","record class"],"tags":["classes","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["dataclass","quick record class","avoid writing __init__","@dataclass"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing a record class quickly with @dataclass @dataclass classes objects non-examinable dataclass record class dataclass quick record class avoid writing __init__ @dataclass the decorator `@dataclass` allows a class that holds data to be written with only its attribute names, and python writes `__init__()` and a readable display for it. it is useful for records such as a book or a loan, where the class has little behaviour. alternatively, a normal class gives full control, and is needed when the methods are the main part."},{"sid":"H6.06","title":"Calculating an attribute with `@property`","cmd":"@property","terms":["property","calculated attribute","decorator"],"tags":["classes","objects","non-examinable"],"task":["property","calculated attribute","attribute without brackets","@property"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating an attribute with @property @property classes objects non-examinable property calculated attribute decorator property calculated attribute attribute without brackets @property the decorator `@property` allows a method to be read like an attribute, with no brackets. it is useful for a value that is worked out from other attributes, such as the price of a parcel from its weight, because it is always up to date."},{"sid":"H7.01","title":"Comparing two instances with `==` and `is`","cmd":"a == b   a is b","terms":["equality of objects","== for instances","is for instances","default equality"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","non-examinable"],"task":["== versus is for objects","equal instances","same object or equal values"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing two instances with == and is a == b   a is b classes objects comparison non-examinable equality of objects == for instances is for instances default equality == versus is for objects equal instances same object or equal values by default, two instances are equal only when they are the very same object. two books built from the same title and author are still two objects, so `==` gives `false`. the operator `is` always asks whether two names refer to the same object, and `==` asks whatever the class has defined for equality."},{"sid":"H7.02","title":"Making instances equal with `__eq__`","cmd":"def __eq__(self, other):","terms":["__eq__","define equality","special method"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","non-examinable"],"task":["make objects equal","__eq__","compare attributes","custom equality"],"examinable":false,"search":"making instances equal with __eq__ def __eq__(self, other): classes objects comparison non-examinable __eq__ define equality special method make objects equal __eq__ compare attributes custom equality a method named `__eq__()` allows a class to say when two of its instances count as equal, so `==` compares contents instead of identity. it is useful when two objects with the same values are interchangeable. python calls the method when `==` is used, and `!=` is then the opposite without more code."},{"sid":"H7.03","title":"Comparing with a different type safely","cmd":"isinstance(other, Book)   return NotImplemented","terms":["NotImplemented","isinstance in __eq__","comparing with another type"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","non-examinable"],"task":["compare with another type","isinstance in __eq__","NotImplemented","safe comparison"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing with a different type safely isinstance(other, book)   return notimplemented classes objects comparison non-examinable notimplemented isinstance in __eq__ comparing with another type compare with another type isinstance in __eq__ notimplemented safe comparison an `__eq__()` that reads `other.title` fails with an `attributeerror` when the other value is not a book, such as `book == 5`. checking `isinstance()` first and returning `notimplemented` lets python answer `false` instead."},{"sid":"H7.04","title":"Testing whether an instance is missing with `is None`","cmd":"if found is None:","terms":["is None","missing object","no result"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","non-examinable"],"task":["is None for objects","missing instance","test for None","found is None"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing whether an instance is missing with is none if found is none: classes objects comparison non-examinable is none missing object no result is none for objects missing instance test for none found is none a search that may find nothing often gives `none` instead of an instance, and `is none` tests for that case. it is useful because `is` cannot be changed by a class, whereas `==` can be redefined by `__eq__()`."},{"sid":"H7.05","title":"Ordering instances with `__lt__`","cmd":"def __lt__(self, other)   sorted(items)   min(items)","terms":["__lt__","order instances","sort objects","less than"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["sort objects","__lt__","order instances","less than for objects"],"examinable":false,"search":"ordering instances with __lt__ def __lt__(self, other)   sorted(items)   min(items) classes objects comparison sorting non-examinable __lt__ order instances sort objects less than sort objects __lt__ order instances less than for objects a method named `__lt__()` (less than) allows `<` to compare two instances, and with it `sorted()` and `min()` can order a list of them without a `key=` argument. it is useful when there is one natural order for the objects."},{"sid":"H7.06","title":"All comparison operators with `total_ordering`","cmd":"@total_ordering   def __eq__(self, other)   def __lt__(self, other)","terms":["functools.total_ordering","__le__","__gt__","__ge__","ordering"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","modules","non-examinable"],"task":["all comparison operators","total_ordering","fill in <= > >=","reduce code"],"examinable":false,"search":"all comparison operators with total_ordering @total_ordering   def __eq__(self, other)   def __lt__(self, other) classes objects comparison modules non-examinable functools.total_ordering __le__ __gt__ __ge__ ordering all comparison operators total_ordering fill in <= > >= reduce code the decorator `@total_ordering` from the `functools` module fills in ` ` and `>=` from `__eq__()` and one ordering method, so only two methods need to be written. it is useful when all four ordering operators must work on an object."},{"sid":"H7.07","title":"Making instances equal and ordered with a dataclass","cmd":"@dataclass(order=True)","terms":["dataclass","order=True","automatic __eq__"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","modules","non-examinable"],"task":["dataclass equality and order","order=True","compare automatically","sortable records"],"examinable":false,"search":"making instances equal and ordered with a dataclass @dataclass(order=true) classes objects comparison modules non-examinable dataclass order=true automatic __eq__ dataclass equality and order order=true compare automatically sortable records the decorator `@dataclass` from the `dataclasses` module writes `__init__()`, `__repr__()` and `__eq__()` from a list of attributes, and `order=true` adds the ordering operators. it is useful for classes that mostly hold values, because it replaces several methods with one line."},{"sid":"H7.08","title":"Using instances in sets and as dictionary keys with `__hash__`","cmd":"def __hash__(self)   def __eq__(self, other)","terms":["__hash__","hashable","instances in a set","instance as a dictionary key"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","sets","non-examinable"],"task":["objects in a set","objects as dictionary keys","__hash__","hashable class"],"examinable":false,"search":"using instances in sets and as dictionary keys with __hash__ def __hash__(self)   def __eq__(self, other) classes objects comparison sets non-examinable __hash__ hashable instances in a set instance as a dictionary key objects in a set objects as dictionary keys __hash__ hashable class instances can be put in a set, or used as dictionary keys, only if they have a `__hash__()` method that gives the same number for equal objects. a class that defines `__eq__()` without `__hash__()` becomes unhashable, so a set of its instances raises a `typeerror`. it is useful whenever instances must be unique, or must be looked up by value."},{"sid":"H7.09","title":"Match the comparison operators to their methods","cmd":"==   !=   <   <=   >   >=","terms":["comparison operators","special methods for comparison","__ne__"],"tags":["classes","objects","comparison","non-examinable"],"task":["comparison operators and methods","which method for <","operator to dunder method","table of dunder methods"],"examinable":false,"search":"match the comparison operators to their methods ==   !=   <   <=   >   >= classes objects comparison non-examinable comparison operators special methods for comparison __ne__ comparison operators and methods which method for < operator to dunder method table of dunder methods each comparison operator calls a special method on the left-hand object. the methods are written once in the class, and then the operators work on its instances in the same way as they do on numbers and strings."},{"sid":"I1.01","title":"Reading a whole file with `read_text()`","cmd":"Path(path).read_text()","terms":["Path","read_text()","pathlib","reading a file"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["read a file","open a text file","read the whole file","read_text"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a whole file with read_text() path(path).read_text() files core non-examinable path read_text() pathlib reading a file read a file open a text file read the whole file read_text the method `read_text()` of a `path` allows us to read the whole of a text file into one string. it is useful for a short file. alternatively, `open()` with `read()` does the same, but `path` needs fewer lines."},{"sid":"I1.02","title":"Reading a file line by line","cmd":"contents.splitlines()","terms":["splitlines()","lines of a file"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["read a file line by line","lines of a file","splitlines","loop over lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a file line by line contents.splitlines() files core non-examinable splitlines() lines of a file read a file line by line lines of a file splitlines loop over lines the string method `splitlines()` allows us to turn the contents of a file into a list with one item for each line. it is useful when every line is a separate record. alternatively, `split(\"\\n\")` also splits the text, but leaves an empty item after the last newline when the file ends with one, as most files do."},{"sid":"I1.15","title":"Reading a file with `open()` and `with`","cmd":"with open(path) as file:  text = file.read()","terms":["open()","with statement","reading a file with open"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["open a file","with open","read a file with open","close a file automatically"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a file with open() and with with open(path) as file:  text = file.read() files core non-examinable open() with statement reading a file with open open a file with open read a file with open close a file automatically the `open()` function inside a `with` block allows a file to be read and then closed automatically when the block ends, even if an error happens inside it. it is useful because most python code and help pages read files this way, and because a file can be read one line at a time. alternatively, `path(path).read_text()` reads the whole file in one call."},{"sid":"I1.03","title":"Working with the numbers in a file","cmd":"numbers = [float(line) for line in lines]","terms":["numbers from a file","converting lines to numbers"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["numbers in a file","convert lines to floats","read a column of numbers"],"examinable":false,"search":"working with the numbers in a file numbers = [float(line) for line in lines] files core non-examinable numbers from a file converting lines to numbers numbers in a file convert lines to floats read a column of numbers if we have a file of numbers, then converting each line with `float()` gives a list that can be used in calculations. it is useful for any data that is stored in a file."},{"sid":"I1.04","title":"Using relative and absolute paths","cmd":"","terms":["file path","relative path","absolute path"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["file path","relative or absolute path","file not found because of the folder","working directory"],"examinable":false,"search":"using relative and absolute paths  files core non-examinable file path relative path absolute path file path relative or absolute path file not found because of the folder working directory a relative path names a file from the folder where the program runs, and an absolute path names it from the top of the disk. it is useful to understand, because a relative path that is right on one computer fails if the program runs from a different folder."},{"sid":"I1.05","title":"Writing a file with `write_text()`","cmd":"Path(path).write_text(text)","terms":["write_text()","writing a file"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["write a file","save text to a file","create a text file","write_text"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing a file with write_text() path(path).write_text(text) files core non-examinable write_text() writing a file write a file save text to a file create a text file write_text the method `write_text()` allows us to save a string in a file. it is useful for keeping results, such as a short report. alternatively, `print()` shows the text on the screen only, and it is lost when the program ends."},{"sid":"I1.06","title":"Adding lines to the end of a file","cmd":"with open(path, \"a\") as file:","terms":["append to a file","open()","with","\"a\" mode"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["append to a file","add lines to the end","do not overwrite","open with a"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding lines to the end of a file with open(path, \"a\") as file: files core non-examinable append to a file open() with \"a\" mode append to a file add lines to the end do not overwrite open with a the function `open()` with the mode `\"a\"` allows us to add text to the end of a file without removing what is there. it is useful for a log that grows. alternatively, `write_text()` replaces the whole file, so older lines would be lost."},{"sid":"I1.07","title":"Reading a CSV file with the `csv` module","cmd":"csv.reader(file)","terms":["csv","csv.reader","comma-separated values"],"tags":["files","csv","core","non-examinable"],"task":["read a CSV","csv module","rows of a CSV file","parse comma separated values"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a csv file with the csv module csv.reader(file) files csv core non-examinable csv csv.reader comma-separated values read a csv csv module rows of a csv file parse comma separated values the `csv` module allows a file of comma-separated values to be read as rows, each one a list of strings. it is useful for data exported from a spreadsheet. alternatively, `read_text()` and `split(\",\")` read simple files, but the `csv` module also handles quoted text that contains commas."},{"sid":"I1.08","title":"Reading each CSV row as a dictionary with `DictReader`","cmd":"csv.DictReader(file)","terms":["DictReader","csv rows as dictionaries"],"tags":["files","csv","core","non-examinable"],"task":["CSV rows as dictionaries","DictReader","use column names","header row"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading each csv row as a dictionary with dictreader csv.dictreader(file) files csv core non-examinable dictreader csv rows as dictionaries csv rows as dictionaries dictreader use column names header row the class `csv.dictreader` allows each row to be read as a dictionary whose keys are the column names. it is useful because a field is then read by name, such as `row[\"rainfall_mm\"]`, not by position. alternatively, `csv.reader` gives lists, which need the column positions to be remembered."},{"sid":"I1.09","title":"Writing a CSV file with `csv.writer`","cmd":"csv.writer(file).writerow([...])","terms":["csv.writer","writerow","writing a csv file"],"tags":["files","csv","core","non-examinable"],"task":["write a CSV","csv.writer","save a table to CSV","writerow"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing a csv file with csv.writer csv.writer(file).writerow([...]) files csv core non-examinable csv.writer writerow writing a csv file write a csv csv.writer save a table to csv writerow the function `csv.writer()` allows rows of values to be written to a file in the csv format. it is useful for saving results that will be opened in a spreadsheet."},{"sid":"I1.10","title":"Writing several lines to a file","cmd":"Path(path).write_text(\"\\n\".join(lines))","terms":["writing several lines","join for a file"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["write several lines","save a list of lines","join lines with newlines"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing several lines to a file path(path).write_text(\"\\n\".join(lines)) files core non-examinable writing several lines join for a file write several lines save a list of lines join lines with newlines a newline between the items of a list, added with `\"\\n\".join()`, allows several lines to be written to a file in one call. it is useful for saving a list of results. alternatively, `open()` in append mode writes one line at a time, which suits a log that grows."},{"sid":"I1.11","title":"Analysing the words in a text file","cmd":"words = text.split()","terms":["analysing text","counting words","split a file"],"tags":["files","strings","core","non-examinable"],"task":["count words in a file","word frequency","analyse a text","split into words"],"examinable":false,"search":"analysing the words in a text file words = text.split() files strings core non-examinable analysing text counting words split a file count words in a file word frequency analyse a text split into words if we have a text file, then `split()` breaks its text into words, and the length of the list gives the number of words. it is useful for summaries of a text. alternatively, `count()` finds how often a single word appears."},{"sid":"I1.12","title":"Working with several files","cmd":"for name in file_names: try: ... except FileNotFoundError:","terms":["several files","loop over files"],"tags":["files","errors","core","non-examinable"],"task":["several files","loop over file names","skip missing files","process a folder of files"],"examinable":false,"search":"working with several files for name in file_names: try: ... except filenotfounderror: files errors core non-examinable several files loop over files several files loop over file names skip missing files process a folder of files a common way to handle several files is a function that processes one file, called in a loop over a list of file names, with `try` and `except` around the call. it is useful because one missing file should be reported without stopping the others. alternatively, `path.glob()` finds the files that exist, so none are missing."},{"sid":"I1.13","title":"Reading a large file without printing all of it","cmd":"for line in text.splitlines()[:n]:","terms":["large files","printing part of a file","reading part of a file"],"tags":["files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["preview a large file","first lines only","avoid printing everything","head of a file"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a large file without printing all of it for line in text.splitlines()[:n]: files core non-examinable large files printing part of a file reading part of a file preview a large file first lines only avoid printing everything head of a file a common way to work with a large file is to print or check only the first few lines, and to use calculations for the rest. it is useful because printing a whole large file floods the screen and hides the result that matters. alternatively, the file can be read line by line with `open()`, which does not hold all of it in memory at once."},{"sid":"I1.14","title":"Searching the contents of a file for text","cmd":"part in text","terms":["searching a file","text in a file"],"tags":["files","strings","core","non-examinable"],"task":["search a file","find text in a file","does the file contain","grep"],"examinable":false,"search":"searching the contents of a file for text part in text files strings core non-examinable searching a file text in a file search a file find text in a file does the file contain grep if we have the contents of a file as a string, then `in` tests whether a piece of text appears anywhere in it. it is useful for checking a code or a name against a stored list. alternatively, looping through the lines finds which line holds the text."},{"sid":"I2.01","title":"Reading a traceback","cmd":"","terms":["traceback","exception","reading an error"],"tags":["errors","core","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["read a traceback","error message lines","what does this error mean","where did it fail"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a traceback  errors core debugging non-examinable traceback exception reading an error read a traceback error message lines what does this error mean where did it fail a traceback is the report that python prints when a program stops with an error, naming the file, the line and the kind of error."},{"sid":"I2.02","title":"Handling an error with `try` and `except`","cmd":"try: ... except ZeroDivisionError:","terms":["try","except","handling an exception"],"tags":["errors","core","non-examinable"],"task":["handle an error","try and except","avoid a crash","ZeroDivisionError"],"examinable":false,"search":"handling an error with try and except try: ... except zerodivisionerror: errors core non-examinable try except handling an exception handle an error try and except avoid a crash zerodivisionerror a `try` block with an `except` block allows a program to carry on after an error, with a message of our own. it is useful when a mistake is likely and recoverable. alternatively, an `if` can test for the problem first, which suits cases that can be tested directly."},{"sid":"I2.03","title":"Handling a missing file","cmd":"except FileNotFoundError:","terms":["FileNotFoundError","missing file"],"tags":["errors","files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["handle a missing file","FileNotFoundError","file does not exist","try to open a file"],"examinable":false,"search":"handling a missing file except filenotfounderror: errors files core non-examinable filenotfounderror missing file handle a missing file filenotfounderror file does not exist try to open a file catching `filenotfounderror` allows a program to cope with a file that is not there. it is useful whenever a file name comes from the user or from a folder that may be empty. alternatively, `path.exists()` checks first, but a file can still vanish between the check and the read."},{"sid":"I2.04","title":"FileNotFoundError: no such file","cmd":"","terms":["FileNotFoundError","no such file or directory"],"tags":["errors","files","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["no such file or directory","FileNotFoundError","wrong path","file in another folder"],"examinable":false,"search":"filenotfounderror: no such file  errors files debugging non-examinable filenotfounderror no such file or directory no such file or directory filenotfounderror wrong path file in another folder a `filenotfounderror` tells us that the file that was named does not exist in the place that was searched."},{"sid":"I2.05","title":"Running code only when there was no error","cmd":"try: ... except: ... else:","terms":["else block","try-except-else"],"tags":["errors","core","non-examinable"],"task":["run code only if no error","try except else","success branch"],"examinable":false,"search":"running code only when there was no error try: ... except: ... else: errors core non-examinable else block try-except-else run code only if no error try except else success branch an `else` block after `except` allows code to run only when the `try` block did not raise an error. it is useful for the steps that depend on the risky step having worked. alternatively, the same steps could sit inside the `try` block, but an error in them would then be caught by mistake."},{"sid":"I2.06","title":"Fail silently with `pass`","cmd":"except ValueError: pass","terms":["pass","failing silently"],"tags":["errors","mistakes","non-examinable"],"task":["ignore an error","pass in except","silent failure","why hiding errors is risky"],"examinable":false,"search":"fail silently with pass except valueerror: pass errors mistakes non-examinable pass failing silently ignore an error pass in except silent failure why hiding errors is risky the statement `pass` in an `except` block allows a program to ignore an error without a message."},{"sid":"I2.07","title":"Raising an error with `raise`","cmd":"raise ValueError(message)","terms":["raise","raising an exception"],"tags":["errors","core","non-examinable"],"task":["raise an error","raise ValueError","signal bad input","throw an exception"],"examinable":false,"search":"raising an error with raise raise valueerror(message) errors core non-examinable raise raising an exception raise an error raise valueerror signal bad input throw an exception the statement `raise` allows a program to stop with an error of its own, with a message that says what is wrong. it is useful for rejecting a value that must not be used. alternatively, an `if` can print a message, but the program would carry on with the bad value."},{"sid":"I2.08","title":"Checking the arguments of a function and raising an error","cmd":"if condition: raise ValueError(...)","terms":["validating arguments","function raises an error"],"tags":["errors","functions","core","non-examinable"],"task":["check function arguments","validate inputs","raise on invalid values","guard clauses"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking the arguments of a function and raising an error if condition: raise valueerror(...) errors functions core non-examinable validating arguments function raises an error check function arguments validate inputs raise on invalid values guard clauses a common way to protect a function is to test its arguments at the start and raise an error for a value that makes no sense. it is useful because the fault is reported at once, with a clear message, and not as a wrong result later. alternatively, returning `none` for a bad value hides the fault until the result is used."},{"sid":"I2.09","title":"Running clean-up code with `finally`","cmd":"try: ... finally:","terms":["finally","clean-up"],"tags":["errors","files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["clean-up code","finally","always run","close a file"],"examinable":false,"search":"running clean-up code with finally try: ... finally: errors files core non-examinable finally clean-up clean-up code finally always run close a file a `finally` block allows code to run whether or not the `try` block raised an error. it is useful for clean-up that must always happen. alternatively, the `with` statement closes files by itself, so `finally` is needed mostly for other clean-up."},{"sid":"I2.10","title":"Deciding which errors to report","cmd":"","terms":["reporting errors","when to report an error"],"tags":["errors","core","non-examinable"],"task":["which errors to catch","report or hide","handle what you expect","design error handling"],"examinable":false,"search":"deciding which errors to report  errors core non-examinable reporting errors when to report an error which errors to catch report or hide handle what you expect design error handling a program should report an error when the user needs to know about it, and may carry on quietly when the error is harmless and expected."},{"sid":"I3.01","title":"Converting data to text with `json.dumps()`","cmd":"json.dumps(obj)","terms":["json","dumps()","serialising"],"tags":["json","core","non-examinable"],"task":["convert data to JSON text","json dumps","serialise a dictionary","Python to JSON"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting data to text with json.dumps() json.dumps(obj) json core non-examinable json dumps() serialising convert data to json text json dumps serialise a dictionary python to json the function `json.dumps()` allows us to turn a dictionary or a list into a piece of text in the json format. it is useful for storing or sending data, because text can be written to a file. alternatively, `str()` also gives text, but not in a form that can be read back reliably."},{"sid":"I3.02","title":"Converting text back to data with `json.loads()`","cmd":"json.loads(text)","terms":["loads()","reading json"],"tags":["json","core","non-examinable"],"task":["convert JSON text to data","json loads","parse JSON","JSON to Python"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting text back to data with json.loads() json.loads(text) json core non-examinable loads() reading json convert json text to data json loads parse json json to python the function `json.loads()` allows us to turn json text back into a dictionary or a list. it is useful for using stored data again, with the original types restored."},{"sid":"I3.03","title":"Saving data to a file","cmd":"path.write_text(json.dumps(data))","terms":["saving data","json file"],"tags":["json","files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["save data to a file","store a dictionary","write JSON to disk","keep data between runs"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving data to a file path.write_text(json.dumps(data)) json files core non-examinable saving data json file save data to a file store a dictionary write json to disk keep data between runs a common way to keep data between runs is to convert it with `json.dumps()` and write the text to a file. it is useful for settings, scores or records that must survive when the program ends. alternatively, a plain text file works for simple lists of lines, but not for nested data."},{"sid":"I3.04","title":"Reading saved data back","cmd":"json.loads(path.read_text())","terms":["loading saved data","default when missing"],"tags":["json","files","core","non-examinable"],"task":["read saved data","load JSON from a file","restore a dictionary","reload data"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading saved data back json.loads(path.read_text()) json files core non-examinable loading saved data default when missing read saved data load json from a file restore a dictionary reload data a common way to load saved data is to read the file and convert it with `json.loads()`, with a fallback when the file does not exist yet. it is useful the first time a program runs, before anything has been saved."},{"sid":"J1.01","title":"Testing a function instead of checking it by eye","cmd":"","terms":["why test","automated test"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["why test","test a function","check automatically","instead of checking by eye"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing a function instead of checking it by eye  testing core why test automated test why test test a function check automatically instead of checking by eye a test is a short piece of code that calls a function with a known input and checks the result. it is useful because a change made later can be checked again in seconds, such as after a new price band is added to the postage function."},{"sid":"J1.02","title":"Installing pytest","cmd":"pip install pytest","terms":["pytest","installing pytest"],"tags":["testing","setup","core"],"task":["install pytest","get pytest","pip install pytest","test framework"],"examinable":true,"search":"installing pytest pip install pytest testing setup core pytest installing pytest install pytest get pytest pip install pytest test framework the command `pip install pytest` allows us to add the pytest testing tool to the python that runs our programs. it is useful before the first test, because the command `pytest` is not part of python itself. alternatively, python includes a tool called `unittest`, which needs more code for each test."},{"sid":"J1.03","title":"Writing and running a first test","cmd":"def test_behaviour():","terms":["test file","test function","test_","running pytest"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["write a test","first test","test function","run pytest"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing and running a first test def test_behaviour(): testing core test file test function test_ running pytest write a test first test test function run pytest a file whose name starts with `test_` and a function whose name starts with `test_` allow pytest to find and run a test by itself. it is useful because one command runs every test in a folder. alternatively, the checks can be written in an ordinary script, but pytest reports the result of each one."},{"sid":"J1.04","title":"Reading a passing result","cmd":"","terms":["passing test","pytest output"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["passing test","green dot","what does pass mean","read test output"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a passing result  testing core passing test pytest output passing test green dot what does pass mean read test output the output of pytest shows one dot for each passing test and a summary line with the number of tests that passed."},{"sid":"J1.05","title":"Reading a failing result","cmd":"","terms":["failing test","assertion error","pytest failure"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["failing test","read the failure message","assert failed","expected versus actual"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a failing result  testing core failing test assertion error pytest failure failing test read the failure message assert failed expected versus actual a failing test shows an `f`, the test that failed, the line of the failed `assert`, and the two values that were compared."},{"sid":"J1.06","title":"Responding to a failed test","cmd":"","terms":["fixing a failed test","fix the function not the test"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["a test failed","what to do","fix the code or the test","debug from a failure"],"examinable":true,"search":"responding to a failed test  testing core fixing a failed test fix the function not the test a test failed what to do fix the code or the test debug from a failure a common way to respond to a failed test is to decide whether the function or the test is wrong, and to change the one that is wrong. it is useful because a test that is changed only to pass proves nothing."},{"sid":"J1.07","title":"Adding more tests","cmd":"several test_ functions in one file","terms":["adding tests","several tests"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["add more tests","several test functions","test file with many tests"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding more tests several test_ functions in one file testing core adding tests several tests add more tests several test functions test file with many tests several test functions in one file allow every price band to be checked with one command. it is useful because each test checks one situation, and a failure names the situation that broke."},{"sid":"J1.08","title":"Unit tests and test cases","cmd":"","terms":["unit test","test case","testing terms"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["unit test","test case","what is a unit","testing vocabulary"],"examinable":true,"search":"unit tests and test cases  testing core unit test test case testing terms unit test test case what is a unit testing vocabulary a unit test checks one behaviour of a small piece of code, and a test case is a group of unit tests that together cover one function or class."},{"sid":"J2.01","title":"Checking that two values are equal with `assert ==`","cmd":"assert func(value) == expected","terms":["assert","assert =="],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["assert equal","check a result","assert func(x) == expected","compare to the expected value"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking that two values are equal with assert == assert func(value) == expected testing core assert assert == assert equal check a result assert func(x) == expected compare to the expected value the statement `assert` with `==` allows a test to say that two values must be equal. it is useful for the most common check, that a function returns the expected result. alternatively, a comparison with `print()` shows the result, but nothing fails when it is wrong."},{"sid":"J2.02","title":"Checking that two values differ with `assert !=`","cmd":"assert a != b","terms":["assert !="],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["assert not equal","check values differ","assert !="],"examinable":true,"search":"checking that two values differ with assert != assert a != b testing core assert != assert not equal check values differ assert != the statement `assert` with `!=` allows a test to say that two values must be different. it is useful for results that must not repeat. alternatively, `assert not a == b` says the same, but `!=` reads more clearly."},{"sid":"J2.03","title":"Checking membership with assert in","cmd":"assert value in items","terms":["assert in","assert not in"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["assert in","check an item is present","membership test in a test"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking membership with assert in assert value in items testing core assert in assert not in assert in check an item is present membership test in a test the statements `assert ... in` and `assert ... not in` allow a test to say that a value is, or is not, in a list. it is useful when a function returns a collection. alternatively, comparing the whole list with `==` checks the order too, which is not always needed."},{"sid":"J2.04","title":"Checking a true or false result with assert","cmd":"assert func(value)","terms":["assert True","assert False","assert not"],"tags":["testing","core"],"task":["assert true","check a boolean result","assert a call"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking a true or false result with assert assert func(value) testing core assert true assert false assert not assert true check a boolean result assert a call the statement `assert` on its own allows a test to check that a result is true, and `assert not` that it is false. it is useful for functions that answer a yes-or-no question."},{"sid":"J2.05","title":"Testing one behaviour in each test","cmd":"","terms":["one behaviour per test","test names"],"tags":["testing","core","design"],"task":["one behaviour per test","small tests","focused tests","test naming"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing one behaviour in each test  testing core design one behaviour per test test names one behaviour per test small tests focused tests test naming a test that checks one behaviour allows a failure to name exactly what went wrong."},{"sid":"J2.06","title":"Testing a normal case and an edge case","cmd":"","terms":["edge case","empty list","normal case"],"tags":["testing","core","design"],"task":["normal case and edge case","boundary values","empty input","what to test"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing a normal case and an edge case  testing core design edge case empty list normal case normal case and edge case boundary values empty input what to test a common way to test a function is to try a normal input and then an edge case, which is an input at the limit, such as an empty list. it is useful because faults hide at the limits, where a function is most likely to fail."},{"sid":"J2.07","title":"Using assert in an ordinary program","cmd":"assert condition, message","terms":["assert message","assert in a program"],"tags":["testing","non-examinable"],"task":["assert in a program","sanity check","stop if wrong","assert with a message"],"examinable":false,"search":"using assert in an ordinary program assert condition, message testing non-examinable assert message assert in a program assert in a program sanity check stop if wrong assert with a message an `assert` with a message allows an ordinary program to stop at once when a value that must be true is not. it is useful for catching a wrong input early, with a clear message. alternatively, an `if` with a `raise` gives a clearer error for users, and it always runs, whereas assertions can be switched off."},{"sid":"J3.01","title":"Testing the methods of a class","cmd":"def test_behaviour():","terms":["testing a class","testing methods"],"tags":["testing","classes","core","non-examinable"],"task":["test a class","test methods","test an object","check attributes after a method"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing the methods of a class def test_behaviour(): testing classes core non-examinable testing a class testing methods test a class test methods test an object check attributes after a method a test for a class creates an instance, calls a method and checks the attributes that should have changed. it is useful because a class holds state, so a method can break something that a function test would not show. alternatively, a quick run with `print()` shows the state, but cannot be repeated automatically."},{"sid":"J3.02","title":"Creating the same object for several tests with a fixture","cmd":"@pytest.fixture","terms":["fixture","pytest.fixture","shared setup"],"tags":["testing","classes","core","non-examinable"],"task":["fixture","shared setup for tests","same object for several tests","pytest fixture"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating the same object for several tests with a fixture @pytest.fixture testing classes core non-examinable fixture pytest.fixture shared setup fixture shared setup for tests same object for several tests pytest fixture a fixture is a function marked with `@pytest.fixture` that makes an object, and a test receives it by naming it as a parameter. it is useful when several tests start from the same object, because the creation is written once."},{"sid":"J4.07","title":"AI code assistants","cmd":"","terms":["GitHub Copilot","AI code assistant","inline suggestions","chat"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["AI code assistant","Copilot","ChatGPT for code","what is an AI assistant"],"examinable":true,"search":"ai code assistants  ai core github copilot ai code assistant inline suggestions chat ai code assistant copilot chatgpt for code what is an ai assistant an ai code assistant, such as github copilot, is a tool built into the editor that suggests code from the comments and code around the cursor, and answers questions in a chat panel."},{"sid":"J4.02","title":"Writing a clear request","cmd":"","terms":["writing a prompt","good request"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["write a clear request","good prompt","ask the assistant well","describe what you want"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing a clear request  ai core writing a prompt good request write a clear request good prompt ask the assistant well describe what you want a clear request gives the assistant the context, the goal, an example and the limits, so that the answer fits what is needed. it is useful because the assistant cannot see the course, the python version or the rules of the task."},{"sid":"J4.08","title":"Writing a comment as a prompt","cmd":"","terms":["comment as a prompt","prompting with comments","inline suggestion"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["comment as a prompt","write a comment and let it complete","prompt in code"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing a comment as a prompt  ai core comment as a prompt prompting with comments inline suggestion comment as a prompt write a comment and let it complete prompt in code a comment that states exactly what the next lines must do allows an assistant to suggest suitable code. it is useful because the comment also documents the code that is kept."},{"sid":"J4.09","title":"Writing a function name and docstring as a prompt","cmd":"","terms":["docstring as a prompt","function signature prompt"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["name and docstring as a prompt","describe a function for an assistant","generate from a signature"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing a function name and docstring as a prompt  ai core docstring as a prompt function signature prompt name and docstring as a prompt describe a function for an assistant generate from a signature a function name, its parameters and a one-line docstring allow an assistant to suggest the body of the function. it is useful because the name and docstring state what the function must do before any code exists."},{"sid":"J4.01","title":"Asking an assistant to explain code","cmd":"","terms":["AI assistant","explaining code","chatbot"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["explain this code","ask what code does","understand generated code","ask for an explanation"],"examinable":true,"search":"asking an assistant to explain code  ai core ai assistant explaining code chatbot explain this code ask what code does understand generated code ask for an explanation a common way to use an ai assistant is to paste a short piece of code and ask what each line does. it is useful for code from a classmate, a book or a search result that the reader does not yet understand. alternatively, the python documentation explains each function exactly, but not the whole program."},{"sid":"J4.03","title":"Checking generated code by running it","cmd":"","terms":["checking AI code","testing generated code"],"tags":["ai","testing","core"],"task":["check generated code","run it before trusting it","test AI code","verify output"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking generated code by running it  ai testing core checking ai code testing generated code check generated code run it before trusting it test ai code verify output a common way to check code that an assistant wrote is to run it on inputs whose answers are already known. it is useful because the code often looks correct and runs without an error while being wrong in a small way. alternatively, reading the code finds some faults, but a run finds the faults that reading misses."},{"sid":"J4.04","title":"Spotting a function that does not exist","cmd":"","terms":["made-up function","hallucination","invented library"],"tags":["ai","errors"],"task":["function does not exist","hallucinated function","made-up method","AttributeError from AI code"],"examinable":true,"search":"spotting a function that does not exist  ai errors made-up function hallucination invented library function does not exist hallucinated function made-up method attributeerror from ai code an assistant can write a call to a function, a method or a whole package that does not exist, in the same confident style as a real one."},{"sid":"J4.05","title":"Asking for tests, then read them","cmd":"","terms":["AI generated tests","checking a test"],"tags":["ai","testing","core"],"task":["ask for tests","check the tests","read generated tests","test generation"],"examinable":true,"search":"asking for tests, then read them  ai testing core ai generated tests checking a test ask for tests check the tests read generated tests test generation a common way to use an assistant for testing is to ask for test cases and then read each expected value against the rules of the task. it is useful for finding cases that were not thought of. alternatively, writing the tests first from the plan avoids trusting the assistant for the expected answers."},{"sid":"J4.10","title":"Review, debug and take ownership of generated code","cmd":"","terms":["reviewing AI code","ownership","debugging generated code"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["take ownership of generated code","review and debug AI code","understand what you submit"],"examinable":true,"search":"review, debug and take ownership of generated code  ai core reviewing ai code ownership debugging generated code take ownership of generated code review and debug ai code understand what you submit a common way to take ownership of generated code is to read every line, run it, test the edges and rewrite what is unclear before it is kept. it is useful because whoever submits the code is responsible for it, whether or not it was suggested."},{"sid":"J4.11","title":"What AI assistants can and cannot do","cmd":"","terms":["limitations of AI","ethics","bias","copyright","privacy"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["what AI can and cannot do","limits of assistants","where AI goes wrong"],"examinable":true,"search":"what ai assistants can and cannot do  ai core limitations of ai ethics bias copyright privacy what ai can and cannot do limits of assistants where ai goes wrong ai assistants are fast at routine code and explanations, and they have limits: they can be wrong, out of date and confident at the same time, and their use raises questions of ownership, honesty and privacy. knowing both sides allows an assistant to be used for what it does well and never trusted blindly."},{"sid":"J4.06","title":"Using an assistant responsibly in coursework","cmd":"","terms":["academic integrity","responsible use","AI in coursework","AI policy","detecting AI code"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["AI in coursework","academic integrity","use AI responsibly","rules for assessed work"],"examinable":true,"search":"using an assistant responsibly in coursework  ai core academic integrity responsible use ai in coursework ai policy detecting ai code ai in coursework academic integrity use ai responsibly rules for assessed work responsible use of an assistant means following the rules of the module, being honest about how it was used, and understanding every line that is submitted. the rules for coursework differ between modules and change over time, so they are read first."},{"sid":"J4.12","title":"How AI use affects learning","cmd":"","terms":["AI and learning","research on AI","cognitive offloading","learning with AI"],"tags":["ai","core"],"task":["AI and learning","does AI help me learn","effect on skills","when to avoid AI"],"examinable":true,"search":"how ai use affects learning  ai core ai and learning research on ai cognitive offloading learning with ai ai and learning does ai help me learn effect on skills when to avoid ai research on ai assistants in education is still young and its results are mixed, with real gains for some uses and real losses for others. the way an assistant is used decides which of them a learner gets, so it helps to know the evidence before choosing how to work."},{"sid":"K1.01","title":"Installing Matplotlib and checking it works","cmd":"pip install matplotlib","terms":["Matplotlib","installing Matplotlib"],"tags":["plotting","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["install Matplotlib","check Matplotlib works","pip install matplotlib","first plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"installing matplotlib and checking it works pip install matplotlib plotting setup non-examinable matplotlib installing matplotlib install matplotlib check matplotlib works pip install matplotlib first plot the command `pip install matplotlib` allows us to add the matplotlib plotting library to the python that runs our programs. it is useful before the first plot, because `import matplotlib` otherwise gives a `modulenotfounderror`. alternatively, anaconda already includes matplotlib, so nothing needs to be installed there."},{"sid":"K1.02","title":"Drawing a line plot","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y)","terms":["plt.plot()","line plot","pyplot"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["line plot","plot x against y","draw a graph","basic plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a line plot plt.plot(x, y) plotting core non-examinable plt.plot() line plot pyplot line plot plot x against y draw a graph basic plot the function `plt.plot()` allows us to draw a line through a series of points. it is useful for showing how a value changes over time. alternatively, `plt.scatter()` draws the points alone, with no line between them."},{"sid":"K1.03","title":"Drawing a scatter plot","cmd":"plt.scatter(x, y)","terms":["plt.scatter()","scatter plot"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["scatter plot","points only","relationship between two variables","plot as dots"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a scatter plot plt.scatter(x, y) plotting core non-examinable plt.scatter() scatter plot scatter plot points only relationship between two variables plot as dots the function `plt.scatter()` allows us to draw each pair of values as a separate point. it is useful for seeing whether two measurements are related. alternatively, `plt.plot()` joins the points with a line, which suits values in time order."},{"sid":"K1.04","title":"Loading data from a file with pandas","cmd":"df = pd.read_csv(path)","terms":["pandas","read_csv","loading data for a plot"],"tags":["plotting","data","core","non-examinable"],"task":["load data from a file","read a CSV with pandas","plot data from a file","read_csv"],"examinable":false,"search":"loading data from a file with pandas df = pd.read_csv(path) plotting data core non-examinable pandas read_csv loading data for a plot load data from a file read a csv with pandas plot data from a file read_csv the function `pd.read_csv()` from the pandas library allows us to load a table of data from a csv file for plotting. it is useful when the readings are stored in a file instead of typed into the program. alternatively, lists typed into the program suit a handful of values."},{"sid":"K2.01","title":"Adding a title and axis labels","cmd":"plt.title(text)   plt.xlabel(text)   plt.ylabel(text)","terms":["plt.title()","plt.xlabel()","plt.ylabel()"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["add a title","label the axes","x label and y label","axis titles"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding a title and axis labels plt.title(text)   plt.xlabel(text)   plt.ylabel(text) plotting core non-examinable plt.title() plt.xlabel() plt.ylabel() add a title label the axes x label and y label axis titles the functions `plt.title()`, `plt.xlabel()` and `plt.ylabel()` allow us to say what a plot shows and what its axes mean. it is useful because a plot with no labels cannot be understood alone, and units belong in the labels. alternatively, a caption in a report can explain it, but the labels stay with the figure."},{"sid":"K2.02","title":"Styling a line with a colour and a marker","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y, color=colour, marker=marker)","terms":["color","marker","linestyle","line style"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["line colour","marker style","style a line","red dashed line"],"examinable":false,"search":"styling a line with a colour and a marker plt.plot(x, y, color=colour, marker=marker) plotting core non-examinable color marker linestyle line style line colour marker style style a line red dashed line the arguments `color=`, `marker=` and `linestyle=` allow us to change how a line is drawn. it is useful for marking each reading, or for telling several lines apart. alternatively, a short format string such as `\"o-\"` sets the marker and the line together."},{"sid":"K2.03","title":"Colouring and sizing the points of a scatter plot","cmd":"plt.scatter(x, y, c=values, s=sizes, cmap=colormap)","terms":["colormap","cmap","point size"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["colour the points","size the points","scatter with colour scale","bubble chart"],"examinable":false,"search":"colouring and sizing the points of a scatter plot plt.scatter(x, y, c=values, s=sizes, cmap=colormap) plotting core non-examinable colormap cmap point size colour the points size the points scatter with colour scale bubble chart the arguments `c=`, `s=` and `cmap=` allow us to colour the points by a third value and to set their size. it is useful for showing a third measurement on the same plot."},{"sid":"K2.04","title":"Adding a legend","cmd":"plt.plot(..., label=text); plt.legend()","terms":["plt.legend()","label"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["add a legend","label lines","name the series","key for several lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding a legend plt.plot(..., label=text); plt.legend() plotting core non-examinable plt.legend() label add a legend label lines name the series key for several lines a `label=` on each line and a call to `plt.legend()` allow a plot to name its lines. it is useful whenever a plot has more than one line. alternatively, a note in the title can name the lines, but a legend is placed next to them."},{"sid":"K2.05","title":"Adding a grid","cmd":"plt.grid()","terms":["plt.grid()","grid lines"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["add a grid","grid lines","background lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding a grid plt.grid() plotting core non-examinable plt.grid() grid lines add a grid grid lines background lines the function `plt.grid()` allows us to draw lines across the plot at each tick, so values can be read off the axes. it is useful when readers need to compare values. alternatively, labelling each point shows exact values, but crowds the plot."},{"sid":"K2.06","title":"Saving a plot to a file","cmd":"plt.savefig(path)","terms":["savefig","saving a figure","png"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["save a plot","write an image file","export a figure","savefig"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving a plot to a file plt.savefig(path) plotting core non-examinable savefig saving a figure png save a plot write an image file export a figure savefig the function `plt.savefig()` allows a figure to be saved as an image file, such as a `.png`. it is useful for putting a plot in a report or a presentation."},{"sid":"K2.07","title":"Drawing several plots in one figure","cmd":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(rows, columns)","terms":["subplots","several plots in one figure","axes"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["several plots in one figure","subplots","panels","grid of plots"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing several plots in one figure fig, axes = plt.subplots(rows, columns) plotting core non-examinable subplots several plots in one figure axes several plots in one figure subplots panels grid of plots the function `plt.subplots()` allows one figure to hold a grid of separate plots. it is useful for comparing related data side by side."},{"sid":"K3.01","title":"Drawing a bar chart","cmd":"plt.bar(categories, values)","terms":["plt.bar()","bar chart"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["bar chart","compare categories","draw bars","column chart"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a bar chart plt.bar(categories, values) plotting core non-examinable plt.bar() bar chart bar chart compare categories draw bars column chart the function `plt.bar()` allows us to draw one bar for each category, with the height showing a value. it is useful for comparing amounts across named groups. alternatively, a table gives exact values, but a chart shows the comparison at a glance."},{"sid":"K3.02","title":"Drawing horizontal bars","cmd":"plt.barh(categories, values)","terms":["plt.barh()","horizontal bars"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["horizontal bar chart","barh","long category names","bars sideways"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing horizontal bars plt.barh(categories, values) plotting core non-examinable plt.barh() horizontal bars horizontal bar chart barh long category names bars sideways the function `plt.barh()` allows us to draw the bars sideways. it is useful when the category names are long, because they then fit along the vertical axis. alternatively, `plt.bar()` suits short names and a small number of bars."},{"sid":"K3.03","title":"Drawing a histogram","cmd":"plt.hist(values, bins=n)","terms":["plt.hist()","histogram","bins"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["histogram","distribution of values","how are the values spread","bins"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a histogram plt.hist(values, bins=n) plotting core non-examinable plt.hist() histogram bins histogram distribution of values how are the values spread bins the function `plt.hist()` allows us to show how a set of numbers is spread, by counting how many fall in each range, called a bin. it is useful for seeing the typical values and the extremes. alternatively, a bar chart shows named categories, not ranges of numbers."},{"sid":"K3.04","title":"Choosing a plot for the data","cmd":"","terms":["which plot to use","choosing a chart"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["which plot to use","choose a chart type","plot for my data","line or bar or scatter"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing a plot for the data  plotting core non-examinable which plot to use choosing a chart which plot to use choose a chart type plot for my data line or bar or scatter the kind of data allows us to choose the plot: a line for change over time, points for two related measurements, bars for named categories, and a histogram for the spread of one measurement. it is useful to choose by the question, because the wrong plot can hide the answer."},{"sid":"K4.01","title":"The two plotting styles","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y)   ax.plot(x, y)","terms":["plt style","ax style","pyplot","fig and ax","object-oriented"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["two plotting styles","plt or ax","pyplot or object oriented","which interface"],"examinable":false,"search":"the two plotting styles plt.plot(x, y)   ax.plot(x, y) plotting core non-examinable plt style ax style pyplot fig and ax object-oriented two plotting styles plt or ax pyplot or object oriented which interface matplotlib can be written in two styles. in the `plt.` style, functions of `plt` draw on the current figure, which is the quickest way to draw one plot. in the `fig, ax` style, `plt.subplots()` makes a figure and its axes, and the methods of `ax` draw on those axes, which is clearer when a figure has several plots. both produce the same picture."},{"sid":"K4.02","title":"Translating `plt.` calls into `ax.` calls","cmd":"plt.xlabel(\"x\")   ax.set_xlabel(\"x\")","terms":["plt to ax","set_xlabel","set_title","equivalents"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["translate plt to ax","set_xlabel versus xlabel","convert plotting code","ax equivalents"],"examinable":false,"search":"translating plt. calls into ax. calls plt.xlabel(\"x\")   ax.set_xlabel(\"x\") plotting core non-examinable plt to ax set_xlabel set_title equivalents translate plt to ax set_xlabel versus xlabel convert plotting code ax equivalents most `plt.` functions have an `ax.` method with the same job. a few change their name: the ones that set something become `set_` methods."},{"sid":"K4.03","title":"Choosing between `plt.` and `ax.`","cmd":"fig, ax = plt.subplots()","terms":["choosing a plotting style","plt or ax"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["plt or ax","choose a style","when to use subplots","figure and axes"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing between plt. and ax. fig, ax = plt.subplots() plotting core non-examinable choosing a plotting style plt or ax plt or ax choose a style when to use subplots figure and axes the `plt.` style suits a single quick plot in a notebook or a short script. the `fig, ax` style suits a figure with several plots, a plot drawn inside a function, and code that is kept and reused. it is useful to learn the `ax.` style, since most matplotlib help, examples and libraries use it."},{"sid":"K4.04","title":"Showing a figure in a script with `plt.show`","cmd":"plt.show()","terms":["plt.show","show a figure in a script","plot window"],"tags":["plotting","core","non-examinable"],"task":["show the plot in a script","plt.show","plot window does not appear","display a figure"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing a figure in a script with plt.show plt.show() plotting core non-examinable plt.show show a figure in a script plot window show the plot in a script plt.show plot window does not appear display a figure in a script, `plt.show()` opens the figure window and shows every figure that has been drawn. the program waits at that line until the window is closed. it is useful as the last line of a plotting script, once all the drawing is done."},{"sid":"K4.05","title":"Showing a figure in a notebook without `plt.show`","cmd":"%matplotlib inline","terms":["matplotlib inline","notebook plots","plt.show in a notebook"],"tags":["plotting","notebooks","core","non-examinable"],"task":["show a plot in a notebook","matplotlib inline","plot appears below the cell"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing a figure in a notebook without plt.show %matplotlib inline plotting notebooks core non-examinable matplotlib inline notebook plots plt.show in a notebook show a plot in a notebook matplotlib inline plot appears below the cell in a jupyter notebook, a figure is displayed under the cell when the cell ends, so `plt.show()` is not needed. it is still used when a cell draws several figures, to show each one in turn, and in code that will also be run as a script. it is useful to know the difference when code moves between a notebook and a script."},{"sid":"K4.06","title":"Closing a figure when it has been saved","cmd":"fig.savefig(path)   plt.close(fig)","terms":["plt.close","many figures","close a figure","memory"],"tags":["plotting","files","non-examinable"],"task":["close a figure","plt.close","too many figures open","save and close"],"examinable":false,"search":"closing a figure when it has been saved fig.savefig(path)   plt.close(fig) plotting files non-examinable plt.close many figures close a figure memory close a figure plt.close too many figures open save and close a figure stays in memory until it is closed. a script that draws many figures, such as one for each station, closes each one after saving it, so that the figures do not pile up and later plots do not draw on top of earlier ones. it is useful for any script that makes more than a few figures."},{"sid":"X1.01","title":"Reading a traceback from the bottom up","cmd":"","terms":["reading a traceback","error message"],"tags":["debugging","core","non-examinable"],"task":["read a traceback","bottom line first","understand an error message","where is the error"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading a traceback from the bottom up  debugging core non-examinable reading a traceback error message read a traceback bottom line first understand an error message where is the error a traceback lists the places where python was working when it stopped, and its last line names the error."},{"sid":"X1.02","title":"Making the smallest input that shows the problem","cmd":"","terms":["smallest example","reproducing a problem"],"tags":["debugging","core","non-examinable"],"task":["smallest example","minimal reproducible example","simplify the problem","isolate a bug"],"examinable":false,"search":"making the smallest input that shows the problem  debugging core non-examinable smallest example reproducing a problem smallest example minimal reproducible example simplify the problem isolate a bug a common way to find a fault is to shrink the program until only the lines that show the problem remain. it is useful because a fault in three lines is far clearer than one in thirty, and the smaller program is ready to show to someone else."},{"sid":"X1.03","title":"Printing values and types to see what the program holds","cmd":"","terms":["print for debugging","checking a type"],"tags":["debugging","core","non-examinable"],"task":["print values and types","see what a variable holds","debug with print","inspect values"],"examinable":false,"search":"printing values and types to see what the program holds  debugging core non-examinable print for debugging checking a type print values and types see what a variable holds debug with print inspect values a common way to find a fault is to print the values that the program holds, with their types. it is useful because the cause is often a value that is not what the code assumes, such as text where a number is expected. alternatively, a debugger shows the values without extra lines, but a print needs no tools."},{"sid":"X1.04","title":"Testing one step at a time","cmd":"","terms":["one step at a time","checking each step"],"tags":["debugging","core","non-examinable"],"task":["test one step at a time","run part of the program","find which step is wrong"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing one step at a time  debugging core non-examinable one step at a time checking each step test one step at a time run part of the program find which step is wrong a common way to find a fault in a calculation with several stages is to print the result of each stage. it is useful because the first stage that gives a wrong result is the one with the fault. alternatively, one long expression gives only the final answer, which hides which part is wrong."},{"sid":"X1.05","title":"Commenting out code to find the faulty line","cmd":"","terms":["commenting out","isolating a fault"],"tags":["debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["comment out code","find the faulty line","binary search the bug","disable lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"commenting out code to find the faulty line  debugging non-examinable commenting out isolating a fault comment out code find the faulty line binary search the bug disable lines putting `#` in front of lines allows us to switch them off, to find which one causes a problem. it is useful when the program runs but gives a wrong result, because removing one suspect line at a time shows its effect."},{"sid":"X1.06","title":"Explaining the code aloud","cmd":"","terms":["rubber duck","explaining code"],"tags":["debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["explain the code aloud","rubber duck debugging","talk through the code"],"examinable":false,"search":"explaining the code aloud  debugging non-examinable rubber duck explaining code explain the code aloud rubber duck debugging talk through the code explaining the code line by line, aloud or in writing, to a person or an object allows a mistake to show itself. it is useful because saying what each line does, rather than what it was meant to do, shows where the two differ."},{"sid":"X1.07","title":"Clearing hidden notebook state with Restart and Run All","cmd":"","terms":["Restart and Run All","hidden state"],"tags":["debugging","notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["hidden notebook state","restart and run all","NameError after editing","clear stale variables"],"examinable":false,"search":"clearing hidden notebook state with restart and run all  debugging notebooks non-examinable restart and run all hidden state hidden notebook state restart and run all nameerror after editing clear stale variables the notebook command restart and run all clears every variable and runs the cells again from the top."},{"sid":"X1.08","title":"Stopping at a breakpoint in VS Code","cmd":"click beside a line number, then Run and Debug","terms":["breakpoint","debugger","VS Code debugger"],"tags":["debugging","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["breakpoint in VS Code","stop at a line","pause the program","debugger"],"examinable":false,"search":"stopping at a breakpoint in vs code click beside a line number, then run and debug debugging tools non-examinable breakpoint debugger vs code debugger breakpoint in vs code stop at a line pause the program debugger a breakpoint allows the program to pause on a chosen line so that the values of its variables can be read. it is useful for loops and functions where a print would give too many lines."},{"sid":"X1.09","title":"Asking for help with a clear question","cmd":"","terms":["asking for help","good question"],"tags":["debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["ask for help","how to ask a good question","share an error","get help from others"],"examinable":false,"search":"asking for help with a clear question  debugging non-examinable asking for help good question ask for help how to ask a good question share an error get help from others a clear question allows someone else to help without guessing. it is useful because most of the time spent on a problem is spent finding out what the problem is."},{"sid":"X1.10","title":"Stepping through a program in VS Code","cmd":"Run and Debug → Step Over / Step Into / Continue","terms":["VS Code debugger","step over","step into","variables panel","call stack"],"tags":["debugging","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["step through a program","step over and step into","watch variables","VS Code debugger"],"examinable":false,"search":"stepping through a program in vs code run and debug → step over / step into / continue debugging tools non-examinable vs code debugger step over step into variables panel call stack step through a program step over and step into watch variables vs code debugger the debugger in vs code allows a program to be paused on a chosen line and run one step at a time, with every variable shown. it is useful for loops and function calls, where a print would give a long list of lines."},{"sid":"X1.11","title":"Debugging a notebook cell in VS Code","cmd":"cell menu → Debug Cell","terms":["debugging a notebook in VS Code","Debug Cell"],"tags":["debugging","tools","notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["debug a notebook cell","debug cell in VS Code","step through a cell"],"examinable":false,"search":"debugging a notebook cell in vs code cell menu → debug cell debugging tools notebooks non-examinable debugging a notebook in vs code debug cell debug a notebook cell debug cell in vs code step through a cell the *debug cell* command in vs code allows a single notebook cell to be run under the debugger, with breakpoints and stepping. it is useful when a cell gives a wrong result and the cause is inside a loop or a function. alternatively, printing values in the cell works anywhere, but needs the cell to be edited."},{"sid":"X1.12","title":"Debugging a notebook in JupyterLab","cmd":"Debugger icon (bug) → click the margin → run the cell","terms":["JupyterLab debugger","debugger panel","breakpoint in a cell"],"tags":["debugging","tools","notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["debug in JupyterLab","debugger icon","breakpoint in a notebook"],"examinable":false,"search":"debugging a notebook in jupyterlab debugger icon (bug) → click the margin → run the cell debugging tools notebooks non-examinable jupyterlab debugger debugger panel breakpoint in a cell debug in jupyterlab debugger icon breakpoint in a notebook the debugger in jupyterlab allows a notebook cell to be paused on a line, and its variables to be inspected, from a panel on the right. it is useful for finding why a cell gives a wrong result, without adding print lines to it. alternatively, `%debug` opens a text debugger after an error, which needs no panel."},{"sid":"X1.13","title":"Inspecting a failed cell with `%debug`","cmd":"%debug","terms":["%debug","post-mortem debugging","pdb"],"tags":["debugging","tools","notebooks","non-examinable"],"task":["inspect a failed cell","%debug","post mortem","look at variables after an error"],"examinable":false,"search":"inspecting a failed cell with %debug %debug debugging tools notebooks non-examinable %debug post-mortem debugging pdb inspect a failed cell %debug post mortem look at variables after an error the command `%debug`, typed in a cell after an error, allows the state of the program at the moment of the error to be inspected. it is useful when a cell has stopped with a traceback and the values of the variables are needed. alternatively, the debugger panel stops the program before the error, but needs the breakpoint to be set first."},{"sid":"X1.14","title":"Pausing a script with `breakpoint()`","cmd":"breakpoint()","terms":["breakpoint()","pdb","debugging in a terminal"],"tags":["debugging","tools","non-examinable"],"task":["breakpoint()","pause a script","drop into the debugger","pdb"],"examinable":false,"search":"pausing a script with breakpoint() breakpoint() debugging tools non-examinable breakpoint() pdb debugging in a terminal breakpoint() pause a script drop into the debugger pdb the function `breakpoint()` allows a script to stop on its own at a chosen line and open a debugger in the terminal. it is useful when no editor with a debugger is available. alternatively, the vs code debugger does the same with buttons instead of commands."},{"sid":"X2.01","title":"Error card: SyntaxError","cmd":"","terms":["SyntaxError","invalid syntax"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["SyntaxError","invalid syntax","Python cannot read my code","missing bracket or colon"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: syntaxerror  errors debugging non-examinable syntaxerror invalid syntax syntaxerror invalid syntax python cannot read my code missing bracket or colon a `syntaxerror` tells us that python cannot read the code, so none of the program runs."},{"sid":"X2.02","title":"Error card: IndentationError","cmd":"","terms":["IndentationError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["IndentationError","indent problem","unexpected indent","expected an indented block"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: indentationerror  errors debugging non-examinable indentationerror indentationerror indent problem unexpected indent expected an indented block an `indentationerror` tells us that the spaces at the start of a line do not match what python expects."},{"sid":"X2.03","title":"Error card: NameError","cmd":"","terms":["NameError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["NameError","name is not defined","variable not defined","misspelt name"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: nameerror  errors debugging non-examinable nameerror nameerror name is not defined variable not defined misspelt name a `nameerror` tells us that a name was used before it was given a value."},{"sid":"X2.04","title":"Error card: TypeError","cmd":"","terms":["TypeError","unsupported operand"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["TypeError","wrong type","unsupported operand","cannot add text and number"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: typeerror  errors debugging non-examinable typeerror unsupported operand typeerror wrong type unsupported operand cannot add text and number a `typeerror` tells us that an operation was used on a value of the wrong type, such as adding text to a number."},{"sid":"X2.05","title":"Error card: ValueError","cmd":"","terms":["ValueError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["ValueError","invalid value","cannot convert","right type wrong value"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: valueerror  errors debugging non-examinable valueerror valueerror invalid value cannot convert right type wrong value a `valueerror` tells us that a function received a value of the right type but a wrong content."},{"sid":"X2.06","title":"Error card: IndexError","cmd":"","terms":["IndexError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["IndexError","index out of range","list index error","too large an index"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: indexerror  errors debugging non-examinable indexerror indexerror index out of range list index error too large an index an `indexerror` tells us that a list position does not exist."},{"sid":"X2.07","title":"Error card: KeyError","cmd":"","terms":["KeyError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["KeyError","key not found","dictionary key missing","misspelt key"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: keyerror  errors debugging non-examinable keyerror keyerror key not found dictionary key missing misspelt key a `keyerror` tells us that a dictionary has no key of the name that was asked for."},{"sid":"X2.08","title":"Error card: AttributeError","cmd":"","terms":["AttributeError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["AttributeError","object has no attribute","no such method","wrong type for the method"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: attributeerror  errors debugging non-examinable attributeerror attributeerror object has no attribute no such method wrong type for the method an `attributeerror` tells us that an object has no attribute or method of the name that was used."},{"sid":"X2.09","title":"Error card: ZeroDivisionError","cmd":"","terms":["ZeroDivisionError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["ZeroDivisionError","divide by zero","division by zero","denominator is zero"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: zerodivisionerror  errors debugging non-examinable zerodivisionerror zerodivisionerror divide by zero division by zero denominator is zero a `zerodivisionerror` tells us that a number was divided by zero."},{"sid":"X2.10","title":"Error card: FileNotFoundError","cmd":"","terms":["FileNotFoundError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","files","non-examinable"],"task":["FileNotFoundError","no such file","wrong path","file in another folder"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: filenotfounderror  errors debugging files non-examinable filenotfounderror filenotfounderror no such file wrong path file in another folder a `filenotfounderror` tells us that the file named in the program is not in the place that python looked."},{"sid":"X2.11","title":"Error card: ModuleNotFoundError","cmd":"","terms":["ModuleNotFoundError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","setup","non-examinable"],"task":["ModuleNotFoundError","no module named","package not installed","wrong environment"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: modulenotfounderror  errors debugging setup non-examinable modulenotfounderror modulenotfounderror no module named package not installed wrong environment a `modulenotfounderror` tells us that python cannot find a module that the program imports."},{"sid":"X2.12","title":"Error card: RecursionError","cmd":"","terms":["RecursionError"],"tags":["errors","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["RecursionError","maximum recursion depth","function calls itself forever"],"examinable":false,"search":"error card: recursionerror  errors debugging non-examinable recursionerror recursionerror maximum recursion depth function calls itself forever a `recursionerror` tells us that a function called itself too many times."},{"sid":"X3.01","title":"Mistake: = and == mixed up","cmd":"","terms":["= and ==","comparison as a statement"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","non-examinable"],"task":["= versus ==","assignment in an if","SyntaxError in a condition","equals mistake"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: = and == mixed up  mistakes debugging non-examinable = and == comparison as a statement = versus == assignment in an if syntaxerror in a condition equals mistake a line with `==` where an update was meant compares two values and throws the result away, so nothing changes and nothing is reported."},{"sid":"X3.02","title":"Mistake: input() gives text, not a number","cmd":"","terms":["input gives a string","text instead of a number"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","input","non-examinable"],"task":["input gives text","cannot add numbers from input","convert input to int","string concatenation instead of addition"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: input() gives text, not a number  mistakes debugging input non-examinable input gives a string text instead of a number input gives text cannot add numbers from input convert input to int string concatenation instead of addition typed input is always text, so adding two typed numbers joins them instead of adding them."},{"sid":"X3.03","title":"Mistake: off by one with range and slices","cmd":"","terms":["off by one","range stops before the stop value"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","ranges","non-examinable"],"task":["off by one","range stops early","slice end is exclusive","last item missing"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: off by one with range and slices  mistakes debugging ranges non-examinable off by one range stops before the stop value off by one range stops early slice end is exclusive last item missing a `range()` or a slice stops before its end value, so a loop that was meant to include the last number stops one short."},{"sid":"X3.04","title":"Mistake: a line is inside or outside the loop","cmd":"","terms":["indentation changes the meaning"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["inside or outside the loop","indentation changes meaning","runs once or every time","print position"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: a line is inside or outside the loop  mistakes debugging loops non-examinable indentation changes the meaning inside or outside the loop indentation changes meaning runs once or every time print position a line indented under a loop runs for every item, and a line that is not indented runs once, so moving one line changes the result without an error."},{"sid":"X3.05","title":"Mistake: x = x.sort() stores None","cmd":"","terms":["sort returns None","append returns None"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["sort returns None","x = x.sort()","list became None","use sorted instead"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: x = x.sort() stores none  mistakes debugging lists non-examinable sort returns none append returns none sort returns none x = x.sort() list became none use sorted instead the methods `sort()`, `append()` and `reverse()` change a list in place and return `none`, so storing their result replaces the list with `none`."},{"sid":"X3.06","title":"Mistake: two names for one list","cmd":"","terms":["alias mistake","unintended shared list"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["two names for one list","other list changed too","copy instead of alias"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: two names for one list  mistakes debugging lists non-examinable alias mistake unintended shared list two names for one list other list changed too copy instead of alias assigning one list to another name does not copy it, so a change through either name changes both."},{"sid":"X3.07","title":"Mistake: changing a list while looping over it","cmd":"","terms":["removing items in a loop","skipped items"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","lists","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["change a list while looping","items skipped","modifying during iteration","loop over a copy"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: changing a list while looping over it  mistakes debugging lists loops non-examinable removing items in a loop skipped items change a list while looping items skipped modifying during iteration loop over a copy removing items from a list while a `for` loop reads it makes the loop skip the item after each removal."},{"sid":"X3.08","title":"Mistake: forgetting the brackets when calling a function","cmd":"","terms":["missing brackets","function object"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["forgot brackets","function not called","prints function object","missing parentheses"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: forgetting the brackets when calling a function  mistakes debugging functions non-examinable missing brackets function object forgot brackets function not called prints function object missing parentheses a function name without brackets refers to the function but does not run it, so no result is produced and no error is shown."},{"sid":"X3.09","title":"Mistake: return inside a loop ends the function early","cmd":"","terms":["early return in a loop"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","functions","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["return in a loop","function ends after first item","return too early","move return outside the loop"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: return inside a loop ends the function early  mistakes debugging functions loops non-examinable early return in a loop return in a loop function ends after first item return too early move return outside the loop a `return` inside a loop ends the whole function at the first pass, so only the first item is used."},{"sid":"X3.10","title":"Mistake: print instead of return","cmd":"","terms":["print instead of return","None result"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["print instead of return","function gives None","cannot use the result","return versus print"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: print instead of return  mistakes debugging functions non-examinable print instead of return none result print instead of return function gives none cannot use the result return versus print a function that prints its answer instead of returning it gives `none` to any line that uses the result."},{"sid":"X3.11","title":"Mistake: a variable named like a built-in function","cmd":"","terms":["shadowing built-in names","naming a variable list or sum"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","names","non-examinable"],"task":["variable named list","shadow a built-in","name clash with sum or max","list is not callable"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: a variable named like a built-in function  mistakes debugging names non-examinable shadowing built-in names naming a variable list or sum variable named list shadow a built-in name clash with sum or max list is not callable a variable named `list`, `sum`, `max` or `input` replaces the built-in function of that name for the rest of the program."},{"sid":"X3.12","title":"Mistake: a list as a default value for a parameter","cmd":"","terms":["mutable default argument"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["mutable default argument","list as default value","shared between calls","default list grows"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: a list as a default value for a parameter  mistakes debugging functions non-examinable mutable default argument mutable default argument list as default value shared between calls default list grows a list written as a default value is created once, when the function is defined, and is shared by every call that does not give its own list."},{"sid":"X3.13","title":"Mistake: a string method does not change the string","cmd":"","terms":["strings are immutable","string method result ignored"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["string method does not change the string","upper does nothing","strings are immutable","assign the result"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: a string method does not change the string  mistakes debugging strings non-examinable strings are immutable string method result ignored string method does not change the string upper does nothing strings are immutable assign the result a string method gives back a new string and leaves the original as it was, so a call whose result is not stored has no effect."},{"sid":"X3.14","title":"Mistake: comparing numbers as text","cmd":"","terms":["comparing strings","\"10\" < \"9\""],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","input","non-examinable"],"task":["compare numbers as text","text sorts differently","10 less than 9","convert before comparing"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: comparing numbers as text  mistakes debugging input non-examinable comparing strings \"10\" < \"9\" compare numbers as text text sorts differently 10 less than 9 convert before comparing text is compared character by character, so the text `\"10\"` counts as smaller than the text `\"9\"`."},{"sid":"X3.15","title":"Mistake: comparing decimal numbers with ==","cmd":"","terms":["comparing floats","0.1 + 0.2","round before comparing"],"tags":["mistakes","debugging","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["compare floats with ==","0.1 + 0.2 equals 0.3 False","use isclose","tolerance"],"examinable":false,"search":"mistake: comparing decimal numbers with ==  mistakes debugging numbers non-examinable comparing floats 0.1 + 0.2 round before comparing compare floats with == 0.1 + 0.2 equals 0.3 false use isclose tolerance decimal numbers are stored with a very small error, so two results that should be equal can differ in the last digit, and `==` then gives `false`."},{"sid":"Z1.01","title":"Summarising a table with pandas","cmd":"import pandas as pd","terms":["pandas","DataFrame","data analysis"],"tags":["libraries","data","non-examinable"],"task":["pandas","table of data","DataFrame","summarise a table"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising a table with pandas import pandas as pd libraries data non-examinable pandas dataframe data analysis pandas table of data dataframe summarise a table the pandas library allows us to hold a table of data and summarise it in one line. it is useful once data has rows and columns. alternatively, lists and dictionaries hold the same data, but each summary then needs a loop."},{"sid":"Z1.02","title":"Calculating with whole arrays using NumPy","cmd":"import numpy as np","terms":["NumPy","array","vectorised arithmetic"],"tags":["libraries","data","non-examinable"],"task":["NumPy","maths on whole arrays","fast number crunching","vectorised calculations"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating with whole arrays using numpy import numpy as np libraries data non-examinable numpy array vectorised arithmetic numpy maths on whole arrays fast number crunching vectorised calculations the numpy library allows us to do arithmetic on a whole list of numbers at once. it is useful for large lists of measurements."},{"sid":"Z1.03","title":"Pulling information out of a web page with Beautiful Soup","cmd":"from bs4 import BeautifulSoup","terms":["Beautiful Soup","bs4","web scraping","HTML"],"tags":["libraries","web","non-examinable"],"task":["web scraping","Beautiful Soup","read a web page","extract information from HTML"],"examinable":false,"search":"pulling information out of a web page with beautiful soup from bs4 import beautifulsoup libraries web non-examinable beautiful soup bs4 web scraping html web scraping beautiful soup read a web page extract information from html the beautiful soup library allows us to read the structure of a web page and pick out the parts that we want. it is useful for collecting information that is published as a page. alternatively, a site may offer the data in a file, which is simpler to read."},{"sid":"Z1.04","title":"Serving a web page with Flask","cmd":"from flask import Flask","terms":["Flask","web application"],"tags":["libraries","web","non-examinable"],"task":["Flask","build a web page","serve a web app","simple web server"],"examinable":false,"search":"serving a web page with flask from flask import flask libraries web non-examinable flask web application flask build a web page serve a web app simple web server the flask library allows us to turn python functions into pages of a web site. it is useful for programs that people use through a browser. alternatively, a program in a terminal needs no web tools, but can only be used on the computer where it runs."},{"sid":"Z1.05","title":"Opening a window with Tkinter","cmd":"import tkinter as tk","terms":["Tkinter","graphical window","GUI"],"tags":["libraries","windows","non-examinable"],"task":["Tkinter","make a window","desktop application","graphical interface"],"examinable":false,"search":"opening a window with tkinter import tkinter as tk libraries windows non-examinable tkinter graphical window gui tkinter make a window desktop application graphical interface the tkinter library allows us to build windows with buttons and text. it is useful for small tools that people click on, instead of typing answers in a terminal."},{"sid":"Z1.06","title":"Organising files with pathlib","cmd":"Path.glob(pattern)","terms":["pathlib","glob","listing files","organising files"],"tags":["libraries","files","non-examinable"],"task":["pathlib","organise files","find files by pattern","glob"],"examinable":false,"search":"organising files with pathlib path.glob(pattern) libraries files non-examinable pathlib glob listing files organising files pathlib organise files find files by pattern glob if we have a folder of files, then `pathlib` can list them, check their names, and move them into folders. it is useful for tidying work that would take many clicks."},{"sid":"Z1.07","title":"Fitting a simple model with scikit-learn","cmd":"from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression","terms":["scikit-learn","machine learning","sklearn"],"tags":["libraries","data","non-examinable"],"task":["scikit-learn","machine learning","fit a model","linear regression model"],"examinable":false,"search":"fitting a simple model with scikit-learn from sklearn.linear_model import linearregression libraries data non-examinable scikit-learn machine learning sklearn scikit-learn machine learning fit a model linear regression model the scikit-learn library allows a model to be fitted to data with a few lines and then used to predict. it is useful for machine learning tasks."},{"sid":"Z1.08","title":"Building a neural network with TensorFlow","cmd":"import tensorflow as tf","terms":["TensorFlow","deep learning","neural network"],"tags":["libraries","data","non-examinable"],"task":["TensorFlow","neural network","deep learning","train a model"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a neural network with tensorflow import tensorflow as tf libraries data non-examinable tensorflow deep learning neural network tensorflow neural network deep learning train a model the tensorflow library allows neural networks to be built and trained on large amounts of data. it is useful for tasks such as recognising images or text, which simpler models cannot do well. alternatively, scikit-learn covers many smaller problems with less code and less computing power."},{"sid":"Z1.09","title":"Building a web site with Django","cmd":"django-admin startproject shop_site","terms":["Django","web framework","web development"],"tags":["libraries","web","non-examinable"],"task":["Django","build a web site","web framework","start a project"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a web site with django django-admin startproject shop_site libraries web non-examinable django web framework web development django build a web site web framework start a project the django framework allows a complete web site, with pages, a database and user accounts, to be built from python. it is useful for large sites, where flask would leave much to be written by hand. alternatively, flask is smaller and quicker to start with, and suits a few pages."},{"sid":"Z1.10","title":"Opening a window with PyQt","cmd":"from PyQt6.QtWidgets import QApplication, QLabel","terms":["PyQt","Qt","graphical interface"],"tags":["libraries","windows","non-examinable"],"task":["PyQt","make a window","desktop application","graphical user interface"],"examinable":false,"search":"opening a window with pyqt from pyqt6.qtwidgets import qapplication, qlabel libraries windows non-examinable pyqt qt graphical interface pyqt make a window desktop application graphical user interface the pyqt library allows windows, buttons and menus to be built with the qt toolkit. it is useful for larger desktop programs, where a more polished look than tkinter is wanted. alternatively, tkinter comes with python and needs no installation."},{"sid":"Z2.01","title":"Summarising a column with pandas","cmd":"df[column].describe()   df[column].median()","terms":["pandas describe","mean median std","summary statistics pandas"],"tags":["pandas","libraries","data","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["summarise a column","describe a column","mean and median of a column","pandas statistics"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising a column with pandas df[column].describe()   df[column].median() pandas libraries data numbers non-examinable pandas describe mean median std summary statistics pandas summarise a column describe a column mean and median of a column pandas statistics the method `describe()` gives the count, mean, standard deviation, minimum, quartiles and maximum of a column in one call, and the methods `mean()`, `median()` and `std()` give the individual values. it is useful for a first look at a column of readings."},{"sid":"Z2.02","title":"Finding the mean of each group with pandas","cmd":"df.groupby(column)[column_2].mean()","terms":["groupby","mean of each group","pandas"],"tags":["pandas","libraries","data","non-examinable"],"task":["mean of each group","group by","average by category","pandas groupby"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding the mean of each group with pandas df.groupby(column)[column_2].mean() pandas libraries data non-examinable groupby mean of each group pandas mean of each group group by average by category pandas groupby the method `groupby()` allows us to split a table into groups, such as one group for each item, and summarise each group in one line. it is useful for questions such as the mean sales of each item."},{"sid":"Z2.03","title":"Exact decimal arithmetic","cmd":"from decimal import Decimal","terms":["decimal","exact decimals","money"],"tags":["modules","numbers","non-examinable"],"task":["exact decimals","decimal arithmetic","money calculations","avoid floating point errors"],"examinable":false,"search":"exact decimal arithmetic from decimal import decimal modules numbers non-examinable decimal exact decimals money exact decimals decimal arithmetic money calculations avoid floating point errors the `decimal` type allows us to add decimal numbers exactly, so money is correct to the penny. it is useful for prices and totals that must match a receipt. strings are used to make the values, since a float would carry its error in."}],"ds":[{"sid":"A1.01","title":"Running a cell","cmd":"Shift + Enter","terms":["Shift+Enter","running cells"],"tags":["python","jupyter"],"task":["run a cell","execute code in Jupyter","shortcut to run","Shift Enter"],"examinable":true,"search":"running a cell shift + enter python jupyter shift+enter running cells run a cell execute code in jupyter shortcut to run shift enter pressing `shift+enter` allows us to run the code in a cell and see its result immediately. it is useful when an idea is tested one step at a time. alternatively, the ▶ button or *run all* runs the whole notebook."},{"sid":"A1.02","title":"Hiding the text output of a plot","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y);","terms":["semicolon (;)","hiding output"],"tags":["python","jupyter","matplotlib"],"task":["stop the text under a plot","hide the output of plt.plot","remove the Line2D line","semicolon after a plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"hiding the text output of a plot plt.plot(x, y); python jupyter matplotlib semicolon (;) hiding output stop the text under a plot hide the output of plt.plot remove the line2d line semicolon after a plot a trailing semicolon allows us to stop jupyter printing the text representation of the last object. it is useful when a plot command would otherwise show a line such as `[ ]` above the plot. alternatively, finish the plot with `plt.show()`."},{"sid":"A1.03","title":"Restarting the kernel and running all cells","cmd":"Kernel > Restart & Run All","terms":["restart kernel","Run All"],"tags":["python","jupyter"],"task":["restart the kernel","run everything again","clear the state","fix a NameError after editing","run all cells"],"examinable":true,"search":"restarting the kernel and running all cells kernel > restart & run all python jupyter restart kernel run all restart the kernel run everything again clear the state fix a nameerror after editing run all cells restarting the kernel and running all cells allows us to check that a notebook works from top to bottom with a clean memory. it is useful before work is submitted, or when results look unexpected after cells have been run out of order."},{"sid":"A1.04","title":"Installing a package with `%pip`","cmd":"%pip install statsmodels","terms":["%pip install","installing packages"],"tags":["python","jupyter","setup"],"task":["install a package","pip install in Jupyter","missing module","ModuleNotFoundError"],"examinable":true,"search":"installing a package with %pip %pip install statsmodels python jupyter setup %pip install installing packages install a package pip install in jupyter missing module modulenotfounderror the `%pip` command allows us to install a missing package into the environment that the notebook is using. it is useful when `modulenotfounderror` appears. alternatively, run `pip install` in a terminal and then restart the kernel."},{"sid":"A1.05","title":"Basic arithmetic","cmd":"+ - * /","terms":["+ - * /","arithmetic"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","operators"],"task":["add subtract multiply divide","do a calculation","use Python as a calculator"],"examinable":true,"search":"basic arithmetic + - * / python arithmetic operators + - * / arithmetic add subtract multiply divide do a calculation use python as a calculator the operators `+ - * /` allow us to do ordinary arithmetic in python. they are useful for any calculation that could be done on a calculator. alternatively, functions such as `sum()` add up a whole list of numbers."},{"sid":"A1.06","title":"Calculating powers and square roots","cmd":"x ** 2, x ** 0.5","terms":["** (power)","square root"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","operators"],"task":["square a number","square root","raise to a power","exponent"],"examinable":true,"search":"calculating powers and square roots x ** 2, x ** 0.5 python arithmetic operators ** (power) square root square a number square root raise to a power exponent the `**` operator allows us to raise a number to a power, including `0.5` for a square root. it is useful for squares, growth formulas and standard deviations. alternatively, `np.sqrt()` gives square roots and also works on whole arrays."},{"sid":"A1.07","title":"Dividing with `//` and `%`","cmd":"a // b, a % b","terms":["// (floor division)","% (remainder)"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","operators"],"task":["integer division","remainder","modulo","divide and round down","is it even"],"examinable":true,"search":"dividing with // and % a // b, a % b python arithmetic operators // (floor division) % (remainder) integer division remainder modulo divide and round down is it even the `//` and `%` operators allow us to split a number into whole groups and a remainder. they are useful for converting units (minutes to hours) or for checking whether a number is even (`n % 2 == 0`). alternatively, `divmod(a, b)` returns both at once."},{"sid":"A1.08","title":"Writing big numbers and scientific notation","cmd":"100_000_000, 1e9, 1.23e-5","terms":["1e9 (scientific notation)","underscores in numbers"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","notation"],"task":["write a large number","scientific notation","1e9","underscores in numbers"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing big numbers and scientific notation 100_000_000, 1e9, 1.23e-5 python arithmetic notation 1e9 (scientific notation) underscores in numbers write a large number scientific notation 1e9 underscores in numbers underscores and `e` notation allow us to write very large or very small numbers clearly. they are useful for populations, concentrations and any number with many zeros. alternatively, write the full number, but the zeros can be miscounted."},{"sid":"A1.09","title":"Controlling the order of operations","cmd":"(a + b) * c","terms":["brackets","operator precedence"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","operators"],"task":["brackets in a calculation","order of operations","BODMAS","precedence"],"examinable":true,"search":"controlling the order of operations (a + b) * c python arithmetic operators brackets operator precedence brackets in a calculation order of operations bodmas precedence python's operator precedence determines which part of a calculation is done first, and brackets override it. it is useful when a formula gives an unexpected answer. alternatively, store intermediate results in variables so that each step is clear."},{"sid":"A1.10","title":"Creating a variable","cmd":"x = 5","terms":["= (assignment)","variables"],"tags":["python","variables"],"task":["store a value","assign a variable","name a number","set a variable"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a variable x = 5 python variables = (assignment) variables store a value assign a variable name a number set a variable the `=` sign allows us to store a value under a name so that it can be reused. it is useful whenever a number appears more than once or needs a meaningful label. alternatively, type the number each time, but changing it later is much harder."},{"sid":"A1.11","title":"Checking the type of a value","cmd":"type(x)","terms":["type()","data types"],"tags":["python","variables","types"],"task":["what type is this","is it a number or text","check the type"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking the type of a value type(x) python variables types type() data types what type is this is it a number or text check the type the `type()` function allows us to see what type of value a variable holds. it is useful when code fails with a `typeerror`, or when a number is stored as text. alternatively, `df.dtypes` shows the type of every column of a dataframe."},{"sid":"A1.12","title":"Converting between types","cmd":"int(x), float(x), str(x)","terms":["int()","float()","str()","type conversion"],"tags":["python","variables","types"],"task":["convert text to a number","int float str","change type","turn a string into an integer"],"examinable":true,"search":"converting between types int(x), float(x), str(x) python variables types int() float() str() type conversion convert text to a number int float str change type turn a string into an integer the functions `int()`, `float()` and `str()` allow us to change a value from one type to another. they are useful when numbers arrive as text, or when a number is needed inside a message. alternatively, `.astype()` converts a whole pandas column."},{"sid":"A1.13","title":"Rounding a number","cmd":"round(x, n)","terms":["round()","decimal places"],"tags":["python","arithmetic","rounding"],"task":["round a number","decimal places","round to two places"],"examinable":true,"search":"rounding a number round(x, n) python arithmetic rounding round() decimal places round a number decimal places round to two places the `round(x, n)` function allows us to round a number to `n` decimal places. it is useful for tidying results before they are reported. alternatively, an f-string such as `{x:.2f}` rounds only the *display*, leaving the stored value unchanged."},{"sid":"A1.14","title":"Updating a variable with `+=` and `abs()`","cmd":"x += 1, abs(x)","terms":["+=","abs()"],"tags":["python","variables","arithmetic","non-examinable"],"task":["add one to a variable","increment","absolute value","update a number"],"examinable":false,"search":"updating a variable with += and abs() x += 1, abs(x) python variables arithmetic non-examinable += abs() add one to a variable increment absolute value update a number operators such as `+=` allow us to update a variable in place, and `abs()` removes a minus sign. they are useful for counters and running totals. alternatively, write the long form `x = x + 1`, which has the same effect."},{"sid":"A1.15","title":"Printing several values","cmd":"print(a, b)","terms":["print()"],"tags":["python","printing"],"task":["print two things","show several values","output multiple items"],"examinable":true,"search":"printing several values print(a, b) python printing print() print two things show several values output multiple items the `print()` function allows us to show text and values, separated by spaces. it is useful for checking what a variable holds or for reporting a result inside a loop. alternatively, an f-string gives more control over the layout."},{"sid":"A1.16","title":"Building text with an f-string","cmd":"f\"w = {w}\"","terms":["f-string","string formatting"],"tags":["python","printing","f-strings"],"task":["put a variable in text","f-string","build a message","insert a value in a string"],"examinable":true,"search":"building text with an f-string f\"w = {w}\" python printing f-strings f-string string formatting put a variable in text f-string build a message insert a value in a string an f-string allows us to put variables and calculations directly inside a piece of text. it is useful for readable messages with values inside. alternatively, `+` with `str()` joins text and numbers, but every number must be converted first."},{"sid":"A1.17","title":"Formatting numbers in an f-string","cmd":"{x:.1f}  {x:.3g}  {x:.2e}","terms":["{x:.2f} format codes","significant figures"],"tags":["python","printing","f-strings","rounding"],"task":["format decimals","number of decimal places","significant figures","scientific format in text"],"examinable":true,"search":"formatting numbers in an f-string {x:.1f}  {x:.3g}  {x:.2e} python printing f-strings rounding {x:.2f} format codes significant figures format decimals number of decimal places significant figures scientific format in text format codes after a colon allow us to control how a number is displayed: decimals (`.1f`), significant figures (`.3g`) or scientific notation (`.2e`). they are useful for reporting results neatly. alternatively, `round()` changes the value itself rather than only its display."},{"sid":"A1.18","title":"Starting a new line in text","cmd":"\"\\n\"","terms":["\\n (new line)"],"tags":["python","printing"],"task":["new line in text","line break","print on separate lines","backslash n"],"examinable":true,"search":"starting a new line in text \"\\n\" python printing \\n (new line) new line in text line break print on separate lines backslash n the `\\n` character allows us to start a new line inside one piece of text. it is useful for multi-line messages from a single `print`. alternatively, call `print()` several times."},{"sid":"A1.19","title":"Showing thousands separators and percentages","cmd":"{x:,}  {p:.1%}","terms":["{x:,}","{p:.1%}"],"tags":[],"task":["thousands separator","show a percentage","comma in numbers","format as percent"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing thousands separators and percentages {x:,}  {p:.1%}  {x:,} {p:.1%} thousands separator show a percentage comma in numbers format as percent format codes `,` and `%` allow us to display `1,000,000` or `12.5%`. they are useful when reporting large numbers or probabilities. alternatively, multiply by 100 and add a `%` sign as text."},{"sid":"A1.20","title":"Asking for help with `help()`","cmd":"help(func)","terms":["help()","func?"],"tags":["python","jupyter","non-examinable"],"task":["help on a function","what does this function do","documentation","how to use"],"examinable":false,"search":"asking for help with help() help(func) python jupyter non-examinable help() func? help on a function what does this function do documentation how to use the `help()` function allows us to read the documentation of any function without leaving jupyter. it is useful when the arguments of a function are not remembered. alternatively, type `func?` in a cell, or search the online documentation."},{"sid":"A1.21","title":"NameError: name is not defined","cmd":"NameError: name 'x' is not defined","terms":["NameError","error messages"],"tags":["python","errors"],"task":["name not defined","NameError","variable does not exist","forgot to run the cell"],"examinable":true,"search":"nameerror: name is not defined nameerror: name 'x' is not defined python errors nameerror error messages name not defined nameerror variable does not exist forgot to run the cell a `nameerror` tells us that python does not recognise a name; the usual causes are a typo or a cell that has not been run."},{"sid":"A1.22","title":"IndentationError: unexpected indent","cmd":"IndentationError","terms":["IndentationError","error messages"],"tags":["python","errors"],"task":["unexpected indent","IndentationError","spaces at start of line","code will not run"],"examinable":true,"search":"indentationerror: unexpected indent indentationerror python errors indentationerror error messages unexpected indent indentationerror spaces at start of line code will not run an `indentationerror` tells us that the indentation of a block is missing or inconsistent, usually after a line starting with `if`, `for` or `def`."},{"sid":"A1.23","title":"IndexError: index out of range","cmd":"IndexError: list index out of range","terms":["IndexError","error messages"],"tags":["python","errors","lists"],"task":["list index out of range","IndexError","too large an index","off by one"],"examinable":true,"search":"indexerror: index out of range indexerror: list index out of range python errors lists indexerror error messages list index out of range indexerror too large an index off by one an `indexerror` tells us that a position beyond the end of a list was requested, often by a loop or an index that runs one past the end."},{"sid":"A1.24","title":"KeyError: key not found","cmd":"KeyError: 'colname'","terms":["KeyError","error messages"],"tags":["python","errors","pandas"],"task":["key not found","KeyError","column does not exist","misspelt column name"],"examinable":true,"search":"keyerror: key not found keyerror: 'colname' python errors pandas keyerror error messages key not found keyerror column does not exist misspelt column name a `keyerror` tells us that a dictionary key or dataframe column name does not exist; capital letters and spaces are the common causes."},{"sid":"A1.25","title":"TypeError: wrong type for an operation","cmd":"TypeError","terms":["TypeError","error messages"],"tags":["python","errors","types"],"task":["TypeError","wrong type","cannot add text and number","unsupported operand"],"examinable":true,"search":"typeerror: wrong type for an operation typeerror python errors types typeerror error messages typeerror wrong type cannot add text and number unsupported operand a `typeerror` tells us that an operation does not work on the types given, such as adding text to a number, usually because data are stored as the wrong type."},{"sid":"A1.26","title":"Checking a value with `print()`","cmd":"print(f\"x = {x}\")","terms":["print for checking values","debugging"],"tags":["python","debugging","printing","non-examinable"],"task":["debug with print","show a variable","check a value"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a value with print() print(f\"x = {x}\") python debugging printing non-examinable print for checking values debugging debug with print show a variable check a value printing a value at chosen points allows us to see what a variable holds while the code runs. it is useful for finding where a calculation goes wrong. alternatively, a jupyter cell displays the value of its last line without `print`."},{"sid":"A1.27","title":"Checking the type, size and contents of an object","cmd":"type(x), len(x), repr(x)","terms":["type()","len()","repr()","inspecting objects"],"tags":[],"task":["inspect an object","type and length","what is this variable","repr"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking the type, size and contents of an object type(x), len(x), repr(x)  type() len() repr() inspecting objects inspect an object type and length what is this variable repr checking the type, the length and the contents of an object allows us to confirm that it is what the code assumes. it is useful when a result looks wrong, because text that looks like a number, or an empty list, causes many errors. alternatively, `help(obj)` and `dir(obj)` list what an object can do."},{"sid":"A1.28","title":"Splitting a calculation into steps to find an error","cmd":"step1 = ...; step1","terms":["debugging a calculation","intermediate values"],"tags":["python","debugging","arithmetic","non-examinable"],"task":["find an error in a calculation","break a calculation into steps","debug a formula"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting a calculation into steps to find an error step1 = ...; step1 python debugging arithmetic non-examinable debugging a calculation intermediate values find an error in a calculation break a calculation into steps debug a formula breaking a long expression into named steps and checking each one allows us to find which part gives an unexpected value. it is useful when a formula returns a wrong answer without an error message. alternatively, test the formula with simple numbers whose answer is known."},{"sid":"A1.29","title":"Testing an assumption with `assert`","cmd":"assert condition, \"message\"","terms":["assert","checking assumptions"],"tags":["python","debugging","conditions","non-examinable"],"task":["assert","check an assumption","stop if wrong","fail with a message"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing an assumption with assert assert condition, \"message\" python debugging conditions non-examinable assert checking assumptions assert check an assumption stop if wrong fail with a message an `assert` statement allows us to state what must be true and stops the code with an error if it is not. it is useful for catching problems early. alternatively, an `if` statement with `print` reports a problem without stopping the code."},{"sid":"A2.01","title":"Comparing values","cmd":"> < == != >= <=","terms":["== != > < >= <=","comparison"],"tags":["python","conditions","comparison"],"task":["compare two values","equal to","not equal","greater than","less than","True or False"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing values > < == != >= <= python conditions comparison == != > < >= <= comparison compare two values equal to not equal greater than less than true or false comparison operators allow us to test how two values relate, giving `true` or `false`. they are useful for conditions in `if` statements and for filtering data. alternatively, in pandas the same operators build whole masks of true/false values."},{"sid":"A2.02","title":"Combining conditions with `and`, `or`, `not`","cmd":"and, or, not","terms":["and","or","not"],"tags":["python","conditions","boolean"],"task":["and or not","combine conditions","both true","either true","negate"],"examinable":true,"search":"combining conditions with and, or, not and, or, not python conditions boolean and or not and or not combine conditions both true either true negate the words `and`, `or` and `not` allow us to combine several tests into one. they are useful when a decision depends on more than one thing. alternatively, in pandas use `&`, `|` and `~` instead."},{"sid":"A2.03","title":"Testing membership with `in`","cmd":"x in mylist, x not in mylist","terms":["in","not in"],"tags":["python","conditions","lists"],"task":["is it in the list","membership test","value in a list","not in"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing membership with in x in mylist, x not in mylist python conditions lists in not in is it in the list membership test value in a list not in the `in` operator allows us to ask whether a value is present in a list, text or dictionary. it is useful for checking before a value is looked up. alternatively, `list.count(x) > 0` does the same, but `in` is clearer."},{"sid":"A2.04","title":"Choosing with `if` and `else`","cmd":"if cond: ... else: ...","terms":["if","else","conditions"],"tags":["python","conditions","if-else"],"task":["if else","do one thing or another","make a decision","conditional"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing with if and else if cond: ... else: ... python conditions if-else if else conditions if else do one thing or another make a decision conditional an `if … else` statement allows us to run different code depending on a condition. it is useful for decisions that depend on the data. alternatively, `np.where()` makes the same choice for every value in a column at once."},{"sid":"A2.05","title":"Choosing between several cases with `elif`","cmd":"if ... elif ... else","terms":["elif"],"tags":["python","conditions","if-else"],"task":["several cases","elif","categorise a value","multiple conditions in order"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between several cases with elif if ... elif ... else python conditions if-else elif several cases elif categorise a value multiple conditions in order adding `elif` allows us to test several conditions in order and run only the first that is true. it is useful for more than two outcomes. alternatively, `pd.cut()` sorts a whole column into categories."},{"sid":"A2.06","title":"Repeating n times with `range(n)`","cmd":"for i in range(n):","terms":["for loop","range()"],"tags":["python","loops","range"],"task":["repeat n times","for loop","do something several times","loop with range"],"examinable":true,"search":"repeating n times with range(n) for i in range(n): python loops range for loop range() repeat n times for loop do something several times loop with range the `for i in range(n)` loop allows us to repeat code `n` times. it is useful when the number of repeats is known in advance. alternatively, loop directly over a list, or use a numpy array operation to avoid the loop."},{"sid":"A2.07","title":"Counting from a to b with `range(a, b)`","cmd":"range(a, b)","terms":["range(a, b)"],"tags":["python","loops","range"],"task":["count from a to b","range between two numbers","loop from 1 to 10"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting from a to b with range(a, b) range(a, b) python loops range range(a, b) count from a to b range between two numbers loop from 1 to 10 giving `range` two numbers allows us to start somewhere other than zero. it is useful for years, ids or any sequence with a chosen first value. alternatively, `np.arange(a, b)` does the same and also handles decimals."},{"sid":"A2.08","title":"Looping over a list","cmd":"for item in mylist:","terms":["for item in list"],"tags":["python","loops","lists"],"task":["loop over a list","for each item","go through a list","iterate"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping over a list for item in mylist: python loops lists for item in list loop over a list for each item go through a list iterate a `for` loop over a list allows us to do something with each item in turn. it is useful when a collection is held and every element must be processed. alternatively, a list comprehension builds a new list in one line."},{"sid":"A2.09","title":"Counting in steps with `range(a, b, step)`","cmd":"range(a, b, step)","terms":["range(a, b, step)"],"tags":[],"task":["count in steps","every second number","step in a loop","range with step"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting in steps with range(a, b, step) range(a, b, step)  range(a, b, step) count in steps every second number step in a loop range with step a third argument to `range` allows us to skip values or count backwards. it is useful for every-other-item sequences. alternatively, `np.arange(start, stop, step)` allows decimal steps."},{"sid":"A2.10","title":"Counting with a counter","cmd":"counter = counter + 2","terms":["counter","counting in a loop"],"tags":["python","loops","patterns"],"task":["counter in a loop","count how many","add two each time","running count"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting with a counter counter = counter + 2 python loops patterns counter counting in a loop counter in a loop count how many add two each time running count the counter pattern allows us to count or total things as a loop runs. it is useful whenever a loop must count or total what it sees. alternatively, pandas does this in one step with `(df['col'] == 6).sum()`."},{"sid":"A2.11","title":"Updating a value on each pass","cmd":"n = n + n * rate","terms":["growth model","updating a variable"],"tags":["python","loops","patterns","modelling"],"task":["update a value each pass","compound growth","interest each year","accumulate"],"examinable":true,"search":"updating a value on each pass n = n + n * rate python loops patterns modelling growth model updating a variable update a value each pass compound growth interest each year accumulate the update pattern allows us to change a variable each time round a loop, using its previous value. it is useful when each step depends on the one before. alternatively, a formula such as `n0 * (1 + rate) ** t` gives the answer directly."},{"sid":"A2.12","title":"Collecting results in a list with `append`","cmd":"year.append(i)","terms":["append in a loop","collecting results"],"tags":["python","loops","patterns","lists"],"task":["collect results in a list","append in a loop","build a list","store each result"],"examinable":true,"search":"collecting results in a list with append year.append(i) python loops patterns lists append in a loop collecting results collect results in a list append in a loop build a list store each result starting with an empty list and calling `.append()` in a loop allows us to collect results as we go. it is useful when the result of every step must be kept. alternatively, a list comprehension or a numpy array can build the results without an explicit loop."},{"sid":"A2.13","title":"Repeating until a condition fails with `while`","cmd":"while cond:","terms":["while"],"tags":["python","loops","while","non-examinable"],"task":["while loop","repeat until","loop until a condition","do while"],"examinable":false,"search":"repeating until a condition fails with while while cond: python loops while non-examinable while while loop repeat until loop until a condition do while a `while` loop allows us to repeat code until a condition stops being true. it is useful when the number of repeats is not known in advance. alternatively, a `for` loop with `range` suits a known number of repeats."},{"sid":"A2.14","title":"Numbering items with `enumerate`","cmd":"for i, x in enumerate(lst):","terms":["enumerate()"],"tags":["python","loops","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["index and item","enumerate","number the items","loop with a counter"],"examinable":false,"search":"numbering items with enumerate for i, x in enumerate(lst): python loops lists non-examinable enumerate() index and item enumerate number the items loop with a counter the `enumerate` function allows us to get each item together with its position. it is useful for numbering items during a loop. alternatively, `range(len(lst))` gives the positions, and the items are then read by index."},{"sid":"A2.15","title":"Looping over two lists with `zip`","cmd":"for a, b in zip(l1, l2):","terms":["zip()"],"tags":["python","loops","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["loop over two lists","pair items","zip","go through two lists together"],"examinable":false,"search":"looping over two lists with zip for a, b in zip(l1, l2): python loops lists non-examinable zip() loop over two lists pair items zip go through two lists together the `zip` function allows us to loop over two (or more) lists in step with each other. it is useful when the lists are matched up."},{"sid":"A2.16","title":"Writing a function with `def`","cmd":"def f(x): return ...","terms":["def","functions","return"],"tags":["python","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["define a function","write a function","reuse code","def and return"],"examinable":false,"search":"writing a function with def def f(x): return ... python functions non-examinable def functions return define a function write a function reuse code def and return the `def` statement allows us to package code under a name so we can reuse it. it is useful when the same calculation is repeated with different inputs. alternatively, a `lambda` makes a short one-line function."},{"sid":"A3.01","title":"Creating a list","cmd":"l = [item_1, item_2, item_3]","terms":["list","square brackets"],"tags":["python","lists"],"task":["make a list","store several values","list of numbers","square brackets"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a list l = [item_1, item_2, item_3] python lists list square brackets make a list store several values list of numbers square brackets square brackets allow us to store several values in order in one variable. it is useful for small collections of values. alternatively, a numpy array is better for maths, and a pandas series for labelled data."},{"sid":"A3.02","title":"Summarising a list with len, sum, max, min","cmd":"len(l)  sum(l)  max(l)  min(l)","terms":["len()","sum()","max()","min()"],"tags":["python","lists","summary"],"task":["length of a list","add up a list","biggest value in a list","smallest value","how many items"],"examinable":true,"search":"summarising a list with len, sum, max, min len(l)  sum(l)  max(l)  min(l) python lists summary len() sum() max() min() length of a list add up a list biggest value in a list smallest value how many items the built-in functions `len`, `sum`, `max` and `min` allow us to summarise a list quickly. they are useful for a quick count, total, or range. alternatively, convert to an array or series and use `.mean()`, `.std()` and friends."},{"sid":"A3.03","title":"Adding an item to a list","cmd":"l.append(x)","terms":["append()"],"tags":["python","lists"],"task":["add to a list","append","add an item at the end","grow a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding an item to a list l.append(x) python lists append() add to a list append add an item at the end grow a list the `.append()` method allows us to add one item to the end of a list. it is useful when building a list during a loop. alternatively, `list1 + list2` joins two lists, and `.extend()` adds several items at once."},{"sid":"A3.04","title":"Removing an item from a list","cmd":"l.pop(), l.pop(i)","terms":["pop()"],"tags":["python","lists"],"task":["remove from a list","delete the last item","pop","take an item out"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing an item from a list l.pop(), l.pop(i) python lists pop() remove from a list delete the last item pop take an item out the `.pop()` method allows us to remove an item and keep its value. it is useful for taking items off a list one at a time. alternatively, `.remove(x)` deletes by value and `del l[i]` deletes by position."},{"sid":"A3.05","title":"Joining two lists","cmd":"l1 + l2","terms":["list + list","concatenate lists"],"tags":["python","lists"],"task":["join lists","combine two lists","concatenate lists","list plus list"],"examinable":true,"search":"joining two lists l1 + l2 python lists list + list concatenate lists join lists combine two lists concatenate lists list plus list the `+` operator allows us to join two lists into a new longer one. it is useful for combining results. alternatively, `.extend()` adds to an existing list in place, and `pd.concat` joins dataframes."},{"sid":"A3.06","title":"Picking an item from a list by position","cmd":"l[0], l[-1]","terms":["list indexing","negative index"],"tags":["python","lists","indexing"],"task":["get an item from a list","first item","last item","index a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"picking an item from a list by position l[0], l[-1] python lists indexing list indexing negative index get an item from a list first item last item index a list square brackets with a number allow us to pick out a single item by its position. it is useful for the first or last value in a list. alternatively, `.iloc[]` does the same job for a pandas column."},{"sid":"A3.07","title":"Slicing a list","cmd":"l[1:4], l[:3], l[2:], l[:]","terms":["list slicing [a:b]"],"tags":["python","lists","slicing"],"task":["slice a list","first three items","part of a list","sublist"],"examinable":true,"search":"slicing a list l[1:4], l[:3], l[2:], l[:] python lists slicing list slicing [a:b] slice a list first three items part of a list sublist a slice `l[a:b]` allows us to take a section of a list. it is useful for the first few values or everything after a point. alternatively, `.iloc[a:b]` slices rows of a dataframe in the same way."},{"sid":"A3.08","title":"Slicing with a step and reversing a list","cmd":"l[0:6:2], l[::-1]","terms":["[::-1] (reverse)","slice step"],"tags":["python","lists","slicing"],"task":["reverse a list","every second item","step through a list","backwards"],"examinable":true,"search":"slicing with a step and reversing a list l[0:6:2], l[::-1] python lists slicing [::-1] (reverse) slice step reverse a list every second item step through a list backwards adding a step to a slice allows us to take every n-th item, or reverse the order with `-1`. it is useful for sampling regularly or flipping a list. alternatively, `reversed(l)` and `sorted(l, reverse=true)` do similar things."},{"sid":"A3.09","title":"Changing an item in a list","cmd":"l[i] = value","terms":["mutable","assigning to a list item"],"tags":["python","lists"],"task":["change an item","replace a list element","update by position"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing an item in a list l[i] = value python lists mutable assigning to a list item change an item replace a list element update by position assigning to a position allows us to replace an item in a list. this is useful for correcting a value. alternatively, a tuple cannot be changed, which is safer when values must stay fixed."},{"sid":"A3.10","title":"Copies and aliases of a list","cmd":"b = a   vs   b = a.copy()","terms":["copy()","aliasing"],"tags":["python","lists","copying"],"task":["copy a list","why did my other list change","alias","independent copy"],"examinable":true,"search":"copies and aliases of a list b = a   vs   b = a.copy() python lists copying copy() aliasing copy a list why did my other list change alias independent copy the `.copy()` method allows us to make an independent copy of a list, whereas `b = a` only gives the same list a second name. it is useful whenever one list must be changed without affecting the other. alternatively, `a[:]` also makes a copy."},{"sid":"A3.11","title":"Sorting a list","cmd":"sorted(l), l.sort()","terms":["sorted()","sort()"],"tags":["python","lists","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["sort a list","order from smallest","sorted versus sort","sort in place"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting a list sorted(l), l.sort() python lists sorting non-examinable sorted() sort() sort a list order from smallest sorted versus sort sort in place the `sorted()` function and `.sort()` method allow us to put a list in order. they are useful for ranking values. alternatively, in pandas use `sort_values()`."},{"sid":"A3.12","title":"Searching and editing a list","cmd":"l.count(x), l.index(x), l.remove(x), l.insert(i, x)","terms":["count()","index()","remove()","insert()"],"tags":["python","lists","non-examinable"],"task":["count in a list","find the position","remove a value","insert at a position","search a list"],"examinable":false,"search":"searching and editing a list l.count(x), l.index(x), l.remove(x), l.insert(i, x) python lists non-examinable count() index() remove() insert() count in a list find the position remove a value insert at a position search a list methods such as `.count()`, `.index()`, `.remove()` and `.insert()` allow us to search and edit a list. they are useful for small data-cleaning jobs. alternatively, pandas methods do similar things for whole columns."},{"sid":"A3.13","title":"Building a list with a list comprehension","cmd":"[x * 2 for x in l]","terms":["list comprehension"],"tags":["python","lists","loops","non-examinable"],"task":["list comprehension","build a list in one line","transform every item","shorthand for a loop"],"examinable":false,"search":"building a list with a list comprehension [x * 2 for x in l] python lists loops non-examinable list comprehension list comprehension build a list in one line transform every item shorthand for a loop a list comprehension allows us to build a new list from an old one in a single line. it is useful for transforming or filtering values. alternatively, a `for` loop with `.append()` builds the same list and suits steps that need several lines."},{"sid":"A3.14","title":"Unpacking a tuple","cmd":"rows, cols = df.shape","terms":["tuple","unpacking"],"tags":["python","tuples","unpacking"],"task":["unpack a tuple","assign several variables","rows and columns from shape","split a pair"],"examinable":true,"search":"unpacking a tuple rows, cols = df.shape python tuples unpacking tuple unpacking unpack a tuple assign several variables rows and columns from shape split a pair unpacking allows us to split a tuple into separate named variables in one line. it is useful for `df.shape`, which returns (rows, columns). alternatively, index the tuple with `shape[0]` and `shape[1]`."},{"sid":"A3.15","title":"Unpacking the output of `linregress`","cmd":"slope, intercept, r, p, se = linregress(x, y)","terms":["unpacking linregress output"],"tags":["python","tuples","unpacking","regression"],"task":["unpack linregress","get the slope and intercept","name the regression results"],"examinable":true,"search":"unpacking the output of linregress slope, intercept, r, p, se = linregress(x, y) python tuples unpacking regression unpacking linregress output unpack linregress get the slope and intercept name the regression results if a function returns several values as a tuple, then unpacking gives each one a name. it is useful for `linregress`, which returns five results. alternatively, store the result and read named attributes, like `result.slope`."},{"sid":"A3.16","title":"Creating a dictionary","cmd":"d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2}","terms":["dictionary","{key: value}"],"tags":["python","dictionaries"],"task":["make a dictionary","key value pairs","store labelled values","curly brackets"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a dictionary d = {key_1: value_1, key_2: value_2} python dictionaries dictionary {key: value} make a dictionary key value pairs store labelled values curly brackets a dictionary allows us to store values under meaningful names (keys) rather than positions. it is useful for records with named fields, or for lookups. alternatively, a pandas series with labels behaves very similarly."},{"sid":"A3.17","title":"Looking up a value in a dictionary","cmd":"d['age']","terms":["dictionary lookup d[key]"],"tags":["python","dictionaries"],"task":["look up a value","get by key","dictionary lookup","find a value from its name"],"examinable":true,"search":"looking up a value in a dictionary d['age'] python dictionaries dictionary lookup d[key] look up a value get by key dictionary lookup find a value from its name square brackets with a key allow us to fetch the matching value from a dictionary. it is useful when the name of the required item is known. alternatively, `.get(key, default)` avoids an error if the key is missing."},{"sid":"A3.18","title":"Adding or changing a dictionary entry","cmd":"d['dept'] = 'Bio'","terms":["dictionary assignment"],"tags":["python","dictionaries"],"task":["add a dictionary entry","change a dictionary value","update a key","set a key"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding or changing a dictionary entry d['dept'] = 'bio' python dictionaries dictionary assignment add a dictionary entry change a dictionary value update a key set a key assigning to a key allows us to add a new entry or overwrite an existing one. it is useful for building a dictionary up step by step. alternatively, `.update(other_dict)` merges in several entries at once."},{"sid":"A3.19","title":"Listing keys, values and items","cmd":"d.keys(), d.values(), d.items()","terms":["keys()","values()","items()"],"tags":[],"task":["list the keys","list the values","keys and values","dictionary items"],"examinable":true,"search":"listing keys, values and items d.keys(), d.values(), d.items()  keys() values() items() list the keys list the values keys and values dictionary items the methods `.keys()`, `.values()` and `.items()` allow us to see what a dictionary contains. they are useful for checking or looping over its contents. alternatively, `list(d)` gives only the keys."},{"sid":"A3.20","title":"Testing whether a key exists","cmd":"'age' in d","terms":["key in dictionary"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","conditions"],"task":["does a key exist","key in dictionary","check before lookup","avoid KeyError"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing whether a key exists 'age' in d python dictionaries conditions key in dictionary does a key exist key in dictionary check before lookup avoid keyerror the `in` operator allows us to check whether a key exists before using it. it is useful for avoiding a `keyerror`. alternatively, `.get(key)` returns `none` when the key is missing."},{"sid":"A3.21","title":"Looping over a dictionary","cmd":"for k, v in d.items():","terms":["for k, v in d.items()"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","loops"],"task":["loop over a dictionary","for each key and value","iterate items"],"examinable":true,"search":"looping over a dictionary for k, v in d.items(): python dictionaries loops for k, v in d.items() loop over a dictionary for each key and value iterate items the `.items()` method allows us to loop over keys and values together. it is useful for printing or processing every entry. alternatively, loop over `d` (keys only) and look up each value."},{"sid":"A3.22","title":"Looking up with a default value using `get`","cmd":"d.get(key, default)","terms":["get()"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["get with a default","avoid KeyError","value if the key is missing","safe lookup"],"examinable":false,"search":"looking up with a default value using get d.get(key, default) python dictionaries non-examinable get() get with a default avoid keyerror value if the key is missing safe lookup the `.get()` method allows us to look up a key but give a fallback when it is missing. it is useful when some records lack a field. alternatively, test `key in d` first with an `if`."},{"sid":"A3.23","title":"Turning a dictionary into a DataFrame","cmd":"pd.DataFrame({'col': values})","terms":["dictionary to DataFrame"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","pandas"],"task":["dictionary to DataFrame","make a table from a dictionary","columns from lists"],"examinable":true,"search":"turning a dictionary into a dataframe pd.dataframe({'col': values}) python dictionaries pandas dictionary to dataframe dictionary to dataframe make a table from a dictionary columns from lists if we have our data as a dictionary of lists, then passing it to `pd.dataframe` makes a table with one column per key. it is useful for small tables typed straight into the notebook. alternatively, `pd.read_csv` loads a table from a file."},{"sid":"A3.24","title":"Renaming pandas columns with a dictionary","cmd":"df.rename(columns={'old': 'new'})","terms":["rename() with a dictionary"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","pandas","renaming"],"task":["rename columns with a dictionary","map old names to new names","dictionary of renames"],"examinable":true,"search":"renaming pandas columns with a dictionary df.rename(columns={'old': 'new'}) python dictionaries pandas renaming rename() with a dictionary rename columns with a dictionary map old names to new names dictionary of renames if we want to change only some column names, then a dictionary of old name → new name passed to `rename(columns=...)` does it. it is useful for fixing a few awkward names. alternatively, assign a full list to `df.columns`."},{"sid":"A3.25","title":"Recoding values with a dictionary","cmd":"s.replace({old: new}), s.map(d)","terms":["replace() with a dictionary"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","pandas"],"task":["recode with a dictionary","replace values using a mapping","map codes to labels"],"examinable":true,"search":"recoding values with a dictionary s.replace({old: new}), s.map(d) python dictionaries pandas replace() with a dictionary recode with a dictionary replace values using a mapping map codes to labels if a column holds codes like `'a'` and `'g'`, then a dictionary passed to `.replace()` recodes them into readable labels. it is useful for tidying category labels. alternatively, `.map()` does the same but turns unmatched values into `nan`."},{"sid":"A3.26","title":"Passing plot options as a dictionary","cmd":"line_kws={'color': 'red'}","terms":["line_kws","dictionary of options"],"tags":["python","dictionaries","seaborn"],"task":["plot options in a dictionary","line_kws","pass keyword arguments as a dict","style a regression line"],"examinable":true,"search":"passing plot options as a dictionary line_kws={'color': 'red'} python dictionaries seaborn line_kws dictionary of options plot options in a dictionary line_kws pass keyword arguments as a dict style a regression line if a plotting argument such as `line_kws` takes several options, then a dictionary allows us to pass them together. it is useful for styling the fitted line in `regplot`. alternatively, plot the points and line separately with matplotlib."},{"sid":"A4.01","title":"Creating an array","cmd":"np.array(mylist)","terms":["np.array()","arrays"],"tags":["numpy","arrays"],"task":["make an array","list to array","numpy array","create a vector"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating an array np.array(mylist) numpy arrays np.array() arrays make an array list to array numpy array create a vector the `np.array()` function allows us to turn a list into an array that supports whole-array maths. it is useful for calculations on many numbers at once. alternatively, a pandas series does the same and adds labels."},{"sid":"A4.02","title":"Indexing and slicing an array","cmd":"arr[3], arr[1:4]","terms":["array indexing","array slicing"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","indexing","slicing"],"task":["index an array","slice an array","get elements","part of an array"],"examinable":true,"search":"indexing and slicing an array arr[3], arr[1:4] numpy arrays indexing slicing array indexing array slicing index an array slice an array get elements part of an array indexing and slicing allow us to pick out single values or sections of an array. they are useful for selecting values by position, in the same way as for lists. alternatively, use boolean masks to select by condition instead of position."},{"sid":"A4.03","title":"Views and copies of an array slice","cmd":"arr[1:4].copy()","terms":["views","copy()"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","copying","gotcha"],"task":["copy of an array slice","slice changes the original","array view","independent copy"],"examinable":true,"search":"views and copies of an array slice arr[1:4].copy() numpy arrays copying gotcha views copy() copy of an array slice slice changes the original array view independent copy the `.copy()` method allows us to take an independent copy of part of an array. it is useful because a slice of an array shares memory with the original, so editing it changes the original. alternatively, list slices make copies automatically."},{"sid":"A4.04","title":"Checking the shape of an array","cmd":"arr.shape, len(arr)","terms":["shape","len()"],"tags":[],"task":["size of an array","shape","how many elements","dimensions"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking the shape of an array arr.shape, len(arr)  shape len() size of an array shape how many elements dimensions the `.shape` attribute and `len()` allow us to see how big an array is. they are useful for checking that the data have the expected size. alternatively, `.size` gives the total number of elements."},{"sid":"A4.05","title":"Maths on a whole array","cmd":"arr + n, arr ** 2, a * b","terms":["array arithmetic","vectorised maths"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","arithmetic"],"task":["maths on every element","add a number to an array","multiply arrays","vectorised arithmetic","square all values"],"examinable":true,"search":"maths on a whole array arr + n, arr ** 2, a * b numpy arrays arithmetic array arithmetic vectorised maths maths on every element add a number to an array multiply arrays vectorised arithmetic square all values arithmetic operators on arrays allow us to calculate on every element at once. this is useful for converting units or combining columns without a loop. alternatively, a list comprehension works on plain lists, without numpy."},{"sid":"A4.06","title":"Applying maths functions to an array","cmd":"np.log10(a), np.log(a), np.sqrt(a)","terms":["np.log10()","np.sqrt()","np.exp()"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","arithmetic"],"task":["log of an array","square root of all values","apply numpy functions","natural log","log base 10"],"examinable":true,"search":"applying maths functions to an array np.log10(a), np.log(a), np.sqrt(a) numpy arrays arithmetic np.log10() np.sqrt() np.exp() log of an array square root of all values apply numpy functions natural log log base 10 functions such as `np.log10`, `np.sqrt` and `np.exp` allow us to transform every value in an array. they are useful for log-transforming skewed data. alternatively, the `math` module does the same for a single number only."},{"sid":"A4.07","title":"Summarising an array","cmd":"arr.mean(), arr.std(), arr.max()","terms":["mean()","std()","max()"],"tags":[],"task":["mean of an array","standard deviation","maximum","summarise numbers"],"examinable":true,"search":"summarising an array arr.mean(), arr.std(), arr.max()  mean() std() max() mean of an array standard deviation maximum summarise numbers methods like `.mean()`, `.max()` and `.std()` allow us to summarise an array in one call. they are useful for quick descriptive statistics. alternatively, pandas columns have the same methods, plus `.describe()`."},{"sid":"A4.08","title":"Finding the median and percentiles","cmd":"np.median(arr), np.percentile(arr, q)","terms":["np.median()","np.percentile()"],"tags":[],"task":["median","percentile","quartile of an array","90th percentile"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding the median and percentiles np.median(arr), np.percentile(arr, q)  np.median() np.percentile() median percentile quartile of an array 90th percentile the functions `np.median` and `np.percentile` allow us to find the middle value and quartiles of an array. they are useful when outliers would distort the mean. alternatively, pandas has `.median()` and `.quantile()`."},{"sid":"A4.09","title":"Taking differences and sums of an array","cmd":"np.diff(a), np.sum(a)","terms":["np.diff()","np.sum()"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","summary"],"task":["differences between neighbours","sum an array","np.diff","consecutive differences"],"examinable":true,"search":"taking differences and sums of an array np.diff(a), np.sum(a) numpy arrays summary np.diff() np.sum() differences between neighbours sum an array np.diff consecutive differences the `np.diff` function allows us to calculate the change between neighbouring values. it is useful for rates of change and the durbin–watson test. alternatively, pandas has `.diff()` for columns."},{"sid":"A4.10","title":"Choosing the standard deviation formula with `ddof`","cmd":"np.std(x)  vs  Series.std()","terms":["ddof","standard deviation: population vs sample"],"tags":["numpy","pandas","summary","gotcha"],"task":["std differs between numpy and pandas","ddof","sample versus population standard deviation","which std"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing the standard deviation formula with ddof np.std(x)  vs  series.std() numpy pandas summary gotcha ddof standard deviation: population vs sample std differs between numpy and pandas ddof sample versus population standard deviation which std the `ddof` argument allows us to choose between population (n) and sample (n − 1) standard deviation. it is useful when results from numpy and pandas disagree, because they use different defaults. alternatively, always use pandas `.std()`, which already gives the sample version."},{"sid":"A4.11","title":"Filtering an array with a mask","cmd":"arr[arr > 5]","terms":["boolean mask (array)"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","filtering","non-examinable"],"task":["filter an array","keep values above a number","boolean mask on an array","select by condition"],"examinable":false,"search":"filtering an array with a mask arr[arr > 5] numpy arrays filtering non-examinable boolean mask (array) filter an array keep values above a number boolean mask on an array select by condition a boolean mask allows us to select only the array elements that meet a condition. it is useful for filtering data without a loop. alternatively, pandas masks do the same for whole dataframes."},{"sid":"A4.12","title":"Rounding, accumulating and taking absolute values","cmd":"np.round, np.cumsum, np.abs, np.exp, np.pi","terms":["np.round()","np.cumsum()","np.abs()"],"tags":[],"task":["round an array","running total","absolute value","exponential","pi","cumulative sum"],"examinable":false,"search":"rounding, accumulating and taking absolute values np.round, np.cumsum, np.abs, np.exp, np.pi  np.round() np.cumsum() np.abs() round an array running total absolute value exponential pi cumulative sum functions like `np.round`, `np.cumsum` and `np.abs` allow us to tidy and accumulate array values. they are useful for running totals and distances. alternatively, pandas columns have `.round()` and `.cumsum()` methods."},{"sid":"A4.13","title":"Creating arrays of zeros and ones","cmd":"np.zeros(n), np.ones(n)","terms":["np.zeros()","np.ones()"],"tags":["numpy","arrays","non-examinable"],"task":["array of zeros","array of ones","empty array to fill","initialise an array"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating arrays of zeros and ones np.zeros(n), np.ones(n) numpy arrays non-examinable np.zeros() np.ones() array of zeros array of ones empty array to fill initialise an array the `np.zeros` and `np.ones` functions allow us to create an array of a given size already filled. they are useful for a container to fill in during a loop. alternatively, build a list with `.append()` and convert it later."},{"sid":"A4.14","title":"Creating a sequence with `np.arange`","cmd":"np.arange(start, stop, step)","terms":["np.arange()"],"tags":["numpy","sequences"],"task":["sequence of numbers","range as an array","step through values","arange"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a sequence with np.arange np.arange(start, stop, step) numpy sequences np.arange() sequence of numbers range as an array step through values arange the `np.arange` function allows us to create a sequence of numbers with a chosen step. it is useful for x-values and bin edges. alternatively, `np.linspace` allows the number of points to be chosen rather than the step."},{"sid":"A4.15","title":"Creating evenly spaced points with `np.linspace`","cmd":"np.linspace(start, stop, n)","terms":["np.linspace()"],"tags":["numpy","sequences"],"task":["evenly spaced numbers","points for a plot","linspace","100 points between a and b"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating evenly spaced points with np.linspace np.linspace(start, stop, n) numpy sequences np.linspace() evenly spaced numbers points for a plot linspace 100 points between a and b the `np.linspace` function allows us to create a chosen number of evenly spaced points, including both end points. it is useful for smooth curves and plots. alternatively, `np.arange` allows the step to be chosen instead."},{"sid":"A4.16","title":"Drawing uniform random numbers","cmd":"np.random.rand(n), np.random.uniform(a, b)","terms":["np.random.rand()","np.random.uniform()"],"tags":["numpy","random"],"task":["random numbers between 0 and 1","uniform random","generate random values"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing uniform random numbers np.random.rand(n), np.random.uniform(a, b) numpy random np.random.rand() np.random.uniform() random numbers between 0 and 1 uniform random generate random values the `rand` and `uniform` functions allow us to draw random numbers spread evenly over a range. they are useful for simulations and for random noise. alternatively, `rng.uniform()` from the newer generator does the same."},{"sid":"A4.17","title":"Drawing random integers","cmd":"np.random.randint(low, high, size)","terms":["np.random.randint()"],"tags":["numpy","random"],"task":["random whole numbers","simulate a dice roll","random integers","randint"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing random integers np.random.randint(low, high, size) numpy random np.random.randint() random whole numbers simulate a dice roll random integers randint the `np.random.randint` function allows us to draw random whole numbers, such as dice rolls. it is useful for simulating discrete events. alternatively, `rng.integers()` is the newer equivalent."},{"sid":"A4.18","title":"Drawing normal random numbers","cmd":"np.random.normal(mu, sd, n)","terms":["np.random.normal()"],"tags":[],"task":["normal random numbers","random numbers with a mean and standard deviation","simulate normal data","Gaussian"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing normal random numbers np.random.normal(mu, sd, n)  np.random.normal() normal random numbers random numbers with a mean and standard deviation simulate normal data gaussian the `np.random.normal` function allows us to draw numbers from a bell-shaped distribution with a chosen mean and standard deviation. it is useful for simulating measurements. alternatively, `scipy.stats.norm.rvs()` does the same."},{"sid":"A4.19","title":"Drawing exponential random numbers","cmd":"np.random.exponential(scale, n)","terms":["np.random.exponential()"],"tags":["numpy","random"],"task":["exponential random numbers","waiting times","skewed random data"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing exponential random numbers np.random.exponential(scale, n) numpy random np.random.exponential() exponential random numbers waiting times skewed random data the `np.random.exponential` function allows us to draw skewed, always-positive numbers. it is useful for demonstrating what non-normal data looks like. alternatively, `scipy.stats.expon.rvs()` does the same."},{"sid":"A4.20","title":"Making random numbers repeatable with a seed","cmd":"np.random.seed(n)","terms":["np.random.seed()","reproducibility"],"tags":["numpy","random"],"task":["same random numbers every time","set the seed","reproducible random numbers"],"examinable":true,"search":"making random numbers repeatable with a seed np.random.seed(n) numpy random np.random.seed() reproducibility same random numbers every time set the seed reproducible random numbers setting a seed with `np.random.seed()` allows us to get the same random numbers every time. it is useful so that results (and marking) are repeatable. alternatively, `np.random.default_rng(seed)` gives a seeded generator object."},{"sid":"A4.21","title":"Using the newer random generator","cmd":"rng = np.random.default_rng(1)","terms":["default_rng()"],"tags":["numpy","random"],"task":["default_rng","new random generator","modern random numbers","rng"],"examinable":true,"search":"using the newer random generator rng = np.random.default_rng(1) numpy random default_rng() default_rng new random generator modern random numbers rng the `default_rng` function allows us to create a modern, self-contained random number generator. it is useful because its seed does not affect other code. alternatively, the older `np.random.*` functions still work and are common in notes."},{"sid":"A4.22","title":"Choosing random items from a list","cmd":"np.random.choice(lst, n)","terms":["np.random.choice()"],"tags":[],"task":["pick random items","random choice","sample from a list","random selection"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing random items from a list np.random.choice(lst, n)  np.random.choice() pick random items random choice sample from a list random selection the `np.random.choice` function allows us to pick random items from a list. it is useful for sampling or simulating coin flips. alternatively, `df.sample()` picks random rows from a dataframe."},{"sid":"B1.01","title":"Importing pandas","cmd":"import pandas as pd","terms":["import pandas as pd"],"tags":["pandas","setup"],"task":["load pandas","start using pandas","import the library","pd"],"examinable":true,"search":"importing pandas import pandas as pd pandas setup import pandas as pd load pandas start using pandas import the library pd the `import pandas as pd` line allows us to use all of pandas' tools under the short name `pd`. it is useful at the top of every notebook. alternatively, `from pandas import dataframe` imports one tool only, but `pd` is the convention."},{"sid":"B1.02","title":"Creating a Series","cmd":"pd.Series(values)","terms":["Series","pd.Series()"],"tags":["pandas","series"],"task":["make a Series","one column of values","create a list with labels","build a Series from a list"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a series pd.series(values) pandas series series pd.series() make a series one column of values create a list with labels build a series from a list a series allows us to hold a single labelled column of values. it is useful when selecting a single column, because every column of a dataframe is a series. alternatively, a numpy array holds numbers without labels."},{"sid":"B1.03","title":"Creating a DataFrame from a dictionary","cmd":"pd.DataFrame({'col': values})","terms":["pd.DataFrame()","DataFrame from a dictionary"],"tags":["pandas","dataframe","dictionaries","create"],"task":["make a table","build a DataFrame","create a table from a dictionary","start a DataFrame from scratch"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a dataframe from a dictionary pd.dataframe({'col': values}) pandas dataframe dictionaries create pd.dataframe() dataframe from a dictionary make a table build a dataframe create a table from a dictionary start a dataframe from scratch passing a dictionary to `pd.dataframe` allows us to build a table by hand, one column per key. it is useful for small examples and for calculation tables. alternatively, load a table from a file with `pd.read_csv`."},{"sid":"B1.04","title":"Creating a DataFrame with row labels","cmd":"pd.DataFrame(data, index=labels)","terms":["index=","row labels"],"tags":["pandas","dataframe","create","index"],"task":["make a table with row names","give rows labels","create a DataFrame with an index"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a dataframe with row labels pd.dataframe(data, index=labels) pandas dataframe create index index= row labels make a table with row names give rows labels create a dataframe with an index the `index=` argument allows us to label rows with our own names instead of 0, 1, 2…. it is useful when rows have meaningful identifiers like ids or dates. alternatively, create the table first and call `set_index()` on a column."},{"sid":"B1.05","title":"Creating a DataFrame from a list of rows","cmd":"pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=names)","terms":["DataFrame from a list of rows"],"tags":["pandas","dataframe","create","lists"],"task":["make a table from rows","build a DataFrame from a list of lists","create a table row by row"],"examinable":true,"search":"creating a dataframe from a list of rows pd.dataframe(rows, columns=names) pandas dataframe create lists dataframe from a list of rows make a table from rows build a dataframe from a list of lists create a table row by row passing a list of rows plus `columns=` allows us to build a dataframe row by row. it is useful when the data naturally arrive as records. alternatively, a dictionary of columns is often tidier."},{"sid":"B1.06","title":"Reading a CSV file","cmd":"pd.read_csv(\"file.csv\")","terms":["pd.read_csv()","loading data"],"tags":["pandas","loading","csv"],"task":["open a CSV file","load a CSV","read a file into a table","import data from CSV"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a csv file pd.read_csv(\"file.csv\") pandas loading csv pd.read_csv() loading data open a csv file load a csv read a file into a table import data from csv the `pd.read_csv()` function allows us to load a comma-separated file into a dataframe. it is useful for nearly all real datasets. alternatively, `pd.read_excel()` loads spreadsheets, and `sns.load_dataset()` loads built-in examples."},{"sid":"B1.07","title":"Reading a CSV with a column as the index","cmd":"pd.read_csv(\"f.csv\", index_col='name')","terms":["index_col"],"tags":["pandas","loading","csv","index"],"task":["load a CSV with row labels","use a column as the index when reading","read a file with an id column"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading a csv with a column as the index pd.read_csv(\"f.csv\", index_col='name') pandas loading csv index index_col load a csv with row labels use a column as the index when reading read a file with an id column the `index_col=` argument allows us to choose a column as the row labels while loading. it is useful for id columns. alternatively, load normally and then call `set_index()`."},{"sid":"B1.08","title":"Loading a built-in seaborn dataset","cmd":"sns.load_dataset(name)","terms":["sns.load_dataset()"],"tags":["pandas","loading","seaborn"],"task":["load example data","get a practice dataset","load penguins","sample data from seaborn"],"examinable":true,"search":"loading a built-in seaborn dataset sns.load_dataset(name) pandas loading seaborn sns.load_dataset() load example data get a practice dataset load penguins sample data from seaborn the `sns.load_dataset()` function allows us to load well-known example data such as penguins or tips. it is useful for practice without needing a file. alternatively, download the csv and use `pd.read_csv`."},{"sid":"B1.09","title":"Reading an Excel file","cmd":"pd.read_excel(\"file.xlsx\")","terms":["pd.read_excel()"],"tags":["pandas","loading"],"task":["open an Excel file","load a spreadsheet","read xlsx","import data from Excel"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading an excel file pd.read_excel(\"file.xlsx\") pandas loading pd.read_excel() open an excel file load a spreadsheet read xlsx import data from excel the `pd.read_excel()` function allows us to load a sheet from an excel workbook. it is useful when the data are kept in a spreadsheet. alternatively, save the sheet as a csv and use `read_csv`."},{"sid":"B1.10","title":"Previewing the first and last rows","cmd":"df.head(n), df.tail(n)","terms":["head()","tail()"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting"],"task":["look at the top of the data","see the first rows","see the last rows","peek at the table"],"examinable":true,"search":"previewing the first and last rows df.head(n), df.tail(n) pandas inspecting head() tail() look at the top of the data see the first rows see the last rows peek at the table the `.head()` and `.tail()` methods allow us to preview the start and end of a table. they are useful straight after loading, to check the data looks right. alternatively, `.sample()` shows random rows."},{"sid":"B1.11","title":"Checking the size of a DataFrame","cmd":"df.shape, len(df)","terms":["shape","len(df)"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting"],"task":["how many rows","how many columns","count the rows and columns","size of the table"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking the size of a dataframe df.shape, len(df) pandas inspecting shape len(df) how many rows how many columns count the rows and columns size of the table the `.shape` attribute and `len()` allow us to see how many rows and columns we have. they are useful for checking a load or a filter worked as expected. alternatively, `df.info()` shows size plus types."},{"sid":"B1.12","title":"Checking the column types","cmd":"df.dtypes","terms":["dtypes"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting","types"],"task":["what type is each column","see column types","check data types","are the columns numbers or text"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking the column types df.dtypes pandas inspecting types dtypes what type is each column see column types check data types are the columns numbers or text the `.dtypes` attribute allows us to see the data type of every column. it is useful for spotting numbers stored as text or dates not yet converted. alternatively, `df.info()` shows types and non-null counts together."},{"sid":"B1.13","title":"Summarising a DataFrame with `info()`","cmd":"df.info()","terms":["info()"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting","missing-data"],"task":["summary of the table","see column types and missing values","overview of a DataFrame"],"examinable":true,"search":"summarising a dataframe with info() df.info() pandas inspecting missing-data info() summary of the table see column types and missing values overview of a dataframe the `.info()` method allows us to see the types, size and non-missing counts of every column at once. it is useful as a first check on any new dataset. alternatively, `df.isna().sum()` focuses on missing values."},{"sid":"B1.14","title":"Listing the column names and index","cmd":"df.columns, df.index","terms":["columns","index"],"tags":[],"task":["list the columns","see the column names","show the row labels","what columns are there"],"examinable":true,"search":"listing the column names and index df.columns, df.index  columns index list the columns see the column names show the row labels what columns are there the `.columns` and `.index` attributes allow us to see the labels of our columns and rows. they are useful for checking exact spellings before selecting a column. alternatively, `list(df)` gives the column names."},{"sid":"B1.15","title":"Looking at random rows","cmd":"df.sample(n)","terms":["sample()"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting","non-examinable"],"task":["look at random rows","take a random sample","sample some rows","see a few rows at random"],"examinable":false,"search":"looking at random rows df.sample(n) pandas inspecting non-examinable sample() look at random rows take a random sample sample some rows see a few rows at random the `.sample()` method allows us to look at random rows. it is useful when the data are sorted so `head()` is unrepresentative. alternatively, `df.iloc[::50]` takes every 50th row."},{"sid":"B1.16","title":"Showing every column","cmd":"pd.set_option('display.max_columns', None)","terms":["pd.set_option()","display options"],"tags":["pandas","inspecting","display","non-examinable"],"task":["show all columns","stop columns being hidden","display more columns","see the whole table width"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing every column pd.set_option('display.max_columns', none) pandas inspecting display non-examinable pd.set_option() display options show all columns stop columns being hidden display more columns see the whole table width the `pd.set_option()` function allows us to change how pandas displays tables. it is useful for wide tables whose middle columns are hidden. alternatively, `df.t` transposes the table so columns become rows."},{"sid":"B1.17","title":"Saving to a CSV file","cmd":"df.to_csv(\"out.csv\", index=False)","terms":["to_csv()","saving data"],"tags":["pandas","saving","csv"],"task":["save to CSV","write a file","export the table","save without the index column"],"examinable":true,"search":"saving to a csv file df.to_csv(\"out.csv\", index=false) pandas saving csv to_csv() saving data save to csv write a file export the table save without the index column the `.to_csv()` method allows us to write a dataframe to a file. it is useful for sharing or saving cleaned data. alternatively, `.to_excel()` writes an excel file and `.to_pickle()` keeps data types."},{"sid":"B1.18","title":"Saving and reloading with `pickle`","cmd":"df.to_pickle(f), pd.read_pickle(f)","terms":["to_pickle()","read_pickle()"],"tags":["pandas","saving"],"task":["save the table quickly","keep the data types when saving","store a DataFrame","pickle"],"examinable":true,"search":"saving and reloading with pickle df.to_pickle(f), pd.read_pickle(f) pandas saving to_pickle() read_pickle() save the table quickly keep the data types when saving store a dataframe pickle the `.to_pickle()` and `pd.read_pickle()` pair allows us to save a dataframe exactly as it is, including types. it is useful for intermediate results that include dates or categories. alternatively, csv is more portable but loses type information."},{"sid":"B1.19","title":"Checking a DataFrame after each step","cmd":"df.shape, df.head(), df.dtypes","terms":["checking a DataFrame","shape","head()","dtypes","debugging"],"tags":["pandas","debugging","inspecting","non-examinable"],"task":["check my work","see if a step worked","look at the table after a change","sanity check a DataFrame"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a dataframe after each step df.shape, df.head(), df.dtypes pandas debugging inspecting non-examinable checking a dataframe shape head() dtypes debugging check my work see if a step worked look at the table after a change sanity check a dataframe looking at the shape, the first rows and the column types after each step allows us to see exactly what a step changed. it is useful when a later step fails or a result looks wrong. alternatively, `df.info()` shows the size, types and missing values together."},{"sid":"B1.20","title":"Checking a mask before using it","cmd":"mask.sum(), mask.mean()","terms":["checking a mask","sum() of a mask","empty selection"],"tags":["pandas","debugging","masks","non-examinable"],"task":["count how many rows match","check a filter","how many True values","what fraction matches"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a mask before using it mask.sum(), mask.mean() pandas debugging masks non-examinable checking a mask sum() of a mask empty selection count how many rows match check a filter how many true values what fraction matches counting the true values of a mask allows us to check that a condition selects the rows that were intended. it is useful because a misspelt value selects no rows and gives an empty table without any error. alternatively, `df[mask].shape` shows the size of the selection directly."},{"sid":"B1.21","title":"Checking a new column against a hand calculation","cmd":"df.loc[row, cols]","terms":["checking a calculation","verifying a column"],"tags":[],"task":["check a calculation by hand","verify a new column","check one row","test a formula column"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a new column against a hand calculation df.loc[row, cols]  checking a calculation verifying a column check a calculation by hand verify a new column check one row test a formula column calculating one value by hand allows us to check that a whole column was calculated as intended. it is useful after any formula applied to a column, because a wrong unit or column gives plausible but wrong numbers. alternatively, compare summary statistics (such as the mean) before and after the calculation."},{"sid":"B1.22","title":"Checking data with `assert`","cmd":"assert df['c'].min() > 0","terms":["assert for data","checking data"],"tags":["pandas","debugging","missing-data","non-examinable"],"task":["test an assumption","stop if the data is wrong","check values are valid","assert"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking data with assert assert df['c'].min() > 0 pandas debugging missing-data non-examinable assert for data checking data test an assumption stop if the data is wrong check values are valid assert an `assert` statement on a dataframe allows us to state what must be true of the data, such as no negative values or no missing values. it is useful for stopping the analysis when a loaded file is not as expected. alternatively, `df.describe()` and `df.isna().sum()` show the same information for inspection."},{"sid":"B2.01","title":"Selecting one column","cmd":"df['age']  or  df.age","terms":["selecting a column","df['col']"],"tags":["pandas","columns","selecting"],"task":["get one column","pick a column","look at a single column","column to Series"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting one column df['age']  or  df.age pandas columns selecting selecting a column df['col'] get one column pick a column look at a single column column to series square brackets with a column name allow us to pick out one column as a series. it is useful for calculating a statistic or plotting one variable. alternatively, dot notation `df.age` works when the name is simple."},{"sid":"B2.02","title":"Selecting several columns","cmd":"df[['age', 'height']]","terms":["selecting several columns","df[['a', 'b']]"],"tags":["pandas","columns","selecting"],"task":["get several columns","keep only some columns","subset columns","choose columns"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting several columns df[['age', 'height']] pandas columns selecting selecting several columns df[['a', 'b']] get several columns keep only some columns subset columns choose columns square brackets with a *list* of names allow us to select several columns as a new dataframe. it is useful for focusing on the variables that are needed. alternatively, `df.loc[:, ['a', 'b']]` does the same."},{"sid":"B2.03","title":"Renaming columns with a dictionary","cmd":"df.rename(columns={'old': 'new'})","terms":["rename()","renaming columns"],"tags":["pandas","columns","renaming","dictionaries"],"task":["change a column name","rename a column","rename some columns","fix a column heading"],"examinable":true,"search":"renaming columns with a dictionary df.rename(columns={'old': 'new'}) pandas columns renaming dictionaries rename() renaming columns change a column name rename a column rename some columns fix a column heading the `rename(columns={...})` method allows us to change the names of chosen columns. it is useful for fixing a few awkward names. alternatively, assign a whole list to `df.columns` to rename everything."},{"sid":"B2.04","title":"Replacing all column names","cmd":"df.columns = ['a', 'b', 'c']","terms":["df.columns ="],"tags":["pandas","columns","renaming"],"task":["rename every column","set new column names","overwrite all headings"],"examinable":true,"search":"replacing all column names df.columns = ['a', 'b', 'c'] pandas columns renaming df.columns = rename every column set new column names overwrite all headings assigning a list to `df.columns` allows us to rename every column at once. it is useful when the original names are missing or unusable. alternatively, use `rename` to change only a few."},{"sid":"B2.05","title":"Lowercasing every column name","cmd":"df.columns = df.columns.str.lower()","terms":["df.columns.str.lower()"],"tags":["pandas","columns","renaming","strings"],"task":["make column names lowercase","clean column names","standardise headings"],"examinable":true,"search":"lowercasing every column name df.columns = df.columns.str.lower() pandas columns renaming strings df.columns.str.lower() make column names lowercase clean column names standardise headings the `.str.lower()` method on `df.columns` allows us to standardise all column names in one line. it is useful for tidy, consistent names. alternatively, `df.rename(columns=str.lower)` does the same."},{"sid":"B2.06","title":"Renaming row labels","cmd":"df.rename(index={'a1': 'A1'})","terms":["rename(index=)"],"tags":["pandas","index","renaming","dictionaries"],"task":["change a row label","rename a row","rename index values"],"examinable":true,"search":"renaming row labels df.rename(index={'a1': 'a1'}) pandas index renaming dictionaries rename(index=) change a row label rename a row rename index values the `rename(index={...})` method allows us to change chosen row labels. it is useful for correcting ids. alternatively, assign a new list to `df.index`."},{"sid":"B2.07","title":"Naming the index and column axes","cmd":"df.index.name = 'id'; df.rename_axis(None, axis=1)","terms":["index.name","rename_axis()"],"tags":["pandas","index","renaming","pivot"],"task":["give the index a name","remove the columns label","name the axes","label the index"],"examinable":true,"search":"naming the index and column axes df.index.name = 'id'; df.rename_axis(none, axis=1) pandas index renaming pivot index.name rename_axis() give the index a name remove the columns label name the axes label the index setting `df.index.name` or using `rename_axis` allows us to give (or remove) the label of the index or the columns. it is useful for tidying the stray name left by `pivot`. alternatively, `reset_index()` turns the index into a normal column."},{"sid":"B2.08","title":"Reordering columns by listing them","cmd":"df[['name', 'height', 'age']]","terms":["reordering columns"],"tags":["pandas","columns","reordering"],"task":["change column order","put columns in a different order","arrange columns"],"examinable":true,"search":"reordering columns by listing them df[['name', 'height', 'age']] pandas columns reordering reordering columns change column order put columns in a different order arrange columns selecting with a list of names allows us to put columns in any order we choose. it is useful for presenting a table neatly. alternatively, `reindex(columns=...)` does the same without raising an error for missing names."},{"sid":"B2.09","title":"Reordering columns with `reindex`","cmd":"df.reindex(columns=cols)","terms":["reindex(columns=)"],"tags":["pandas","columns","reordering","reindex"],"task":["reorder columns","change column order with a list","fill missing column names"],"examinable":true,"search":"reordering columns with reindex df.reindex(columns=cols) pandas columns reordering reindex reindex(columns=) reorder columns change column order with a list fill missing column names the `reindex(columns=[...])` method allows us to impose an exact column order. it is useful when some columns might not exist, because it fills them with `nan`. alternatively, plain selection `df[[...]]` raises an error for missing names."},{"sid":"B2.10","title":"Inserting or popping a column","cmd":"df.insert(pos, 'col', values); df.pop('col')","terms":["insert()","pop()"],"tags":["pandas","columns","reordering","non-examinable"],"task":["add a column at a position","insert a column","remove a column and keep it","take a column out"],"examinable":false,"search":"inserting or popping a column df.insert(pos, 'col', values); df.pop('col') pandas columns reordering non-examinable insert() pop() add a column at a position insert a column remove a column and keep it take a column out the `.insert()` and `.pop()` methods allow us to add a column at a chosen position or remove one and keep it. they are useful for fine control over layout. alternatively, `df.drop(columns=...)` removes without keeping."},{"sid":"B2.11","title":"Moving one column to the front","cmd":"df[['c'] + [x for x in df.columns if x != 'c']]","terms":["moving a column"],"tags":["pandas","columns","reordering","non-examinable"],"task":["move a column to the front","put a column first","make a column the first column"],"examinable":false,"search":"moving one column to the front df[['c'] + [x for x in df.columns if x != 'c']] pandas columns reordering non-examinable moving a column move a column to the front put a column first make a column the first column a list built from the column names allows us to move a single column to the first position. it is useful for putting an id or key column first. alternatively, `insert` and `pop` achieve the same."},{"sid":"B2.12","title":"Setting a column as the index","cmd":"df.set_index('name')","terms":["set_index()"],"tags":["pandas","index","set-index"],"task":["use a column as row labels","make a column the index","set the index"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting a column as the index df.set_index('name') pandas index set-index set_index() use a column as row labels make a column the index set the index the `set_index()` method allows us to use a column as the row labels. it is useful for id or date columns, so that rows can be selected with `loc`. alternatively, `index_col=` does it while loading the file."},{"sid":"B2.13","title":"Setting the index and keeping the column","cmd":"df.set_index('name', drop=False)","terms":["set_index(drop=False)"],"tags":["pandas","index","set-index"],"task":["make a column the index but keep it","keep the index column too"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting the index and keeping the column df.set_index('name', drop=false) pandas index set-index set_index(drop=false) make a column the index but keep it keep the index column too the `drop=false` option allows us to use a column as the index while keeping it as a column too. it is useful when the column is still needed for filtering or plotting. alternatively, `reset_index()` brings it back later."},{"sid":"B2.14","title":"Setting a multi-level index","cmd":"df.set_index(['id', 'gender'])","terms":["multi-index","set_index([...])"],"tags":["pandas","index","set-index","reshaping"],"task":["use two columns as the index","multi-level index","hierarchical index"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting a multi-level index df.set_index(['id', 'gender']) pandas index set-index reshaping multi-index set_index([...]) use two columns as the index multi-level index hierarchical index passing a list to `set_index()` allows us to build an index from several columns. it is useful before reshaping with `melt`, so identifier columns travel with the data. alternatively, keep them as columns and use `id_vars` in `melt`."},{"sid":"B2.15","title":"Moving the index back into a column","cmd":"df.reset_index()","terms":["reset_index()"],"tags":["pandas","index","reset-index"],"task":["turn the index into a column","undo set_index","get the index back as a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"moving the index back into a column df.reset_index() pandas index reset-index reset_index() turn the index into a column undo set_index get the index back as a column the `.reset_index()` method allows us to turn the index back into an ordinary column. it is useful after `groupby`, `pivot` or `set_index`. alternatively, `reset_index(drop=true)` discards the index instead."},{"sid":"B2.16","title":"Discarding the old index","cmd":"df.reset_index(drop=True)","terms":["reset_index(drop=True)"],"tags":["pandas","index","reset-index","filtering"],"task":["renumber the rows","reset the row numbers","throw away the index","start the index at zero again"],"examinable":true,"search":"discarding the old index df.reset_index(drop=true) pandas index reset-index filtering reset_index(drop=true) renumber the rows reset the row numbers throw away the index start the index at zero again the `reset_index(drop=true)` method allows us to renumber rows 0 to n−1 and forget the old labels. it is useful after filtering or concatenating. alternatively, `ignore_index=true` in `pd.concat` does this while combining."},{"sid":"B2.17","title":"Keeping a result by reassigning or with `inplace`","cmd":"df = df.set_index('c')  vs  inplace=True","terms":["inplace"],"tags":["pandas","index","set-index"],"task":["save the result of a change","why nothing changed","inplace or reassign","update the DataFrame"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping a result by reassigning or with inplace df = df.set_index('c')  vs  inplace=true pandas index set-index inplace save the result of a change why nothing changed inplace or reassign update the dataframe reassigning (`df = df.set_index(...)`) or `inplace=true` allows us to keep the result of a method that otherwise returns a new table. it is useful whenever a method changes a table, because forgetting both means the change is lost. alternatively, chain the methods and assign once at the end."},{"sid":"B2.18","title":"Sorting by the index","cmd":"df.sort_index()","terms":["sort_index()"],"tags":[],"task":["sort by row label","order rows by index","sort the index"],"examinable":true,"search":"sorting by the index df.sort_index()  sort_index() sort by row label order rows by index sort the index the `.sort_index()` method allows us to put rows in order of their labels. it is useful before slicing with `loc` or time-series work. alternatively, `sort_values()` sorts by a column."},{"sid":"B2.19","title":"Reordering or selecting rows with `reindex`","cmd":"df.reindex(['a3', 'a1', 'a2'])","terms":["reindex()"],"tags":["pandas","index","reindex"],"task":["put rows in a chosen order","pick rows by label list","reorder rows","select rows with reindex"],"examinable":true,"search":"reordering or selecting rows with reindex df.reindex(['a3', 'a1', 'a2']) pandas index reindex reindex() put rows in a chosen order pick rows by label list reorder rows select rows with reindex the `reindex([...])` method allows us to pick exact row labels in a chosen order. it is useful for reordering or for making sure every category appears. alternatively, `df.loc[[...]]` selects by labels but raises an error for missing ones."},{"sid":"B2.20","title":"Filling reindexed labels that are missing","cmd":"df.reindex(labels, fill_value=0)","terms":["reindex(fill_value=)"],"tags":["pandas","index","reindex","missing-data"],"task":["add missing rows","fill in absent labels","reindex with a default value"],"examinable":true,"search":"filling reindexed labels that are missing df.reindex(labels, fill_value=0) pandas index reindex missing-data reindex(fill_value=) add missing rows fill in absent labels reindex with a default value the `fill_value` argument of `reindex` allows us to choose what appears for labels that are not in the data. it is useful for tables where a missing category should be 0, not `nan`. alternatively, `.fillna(0)` afterwards does the same."},{"sid":"B2.21","title":"Choosing between `reset_index`, `sort_index` and `reindex`","cmd":"reset_index(drop=True) vs reindex vs sort_index","terms":["reset_index vs reindex"],"tags":["pandas","index","reset-index","reindex","sorting"],"task":["which of reset_index, sort_index or reindex","renumber or reorder rows","choose an index method"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing between reset_index, sort_index and reindex reset_index(drop=true) vs reindex vs sort_index pandas index reset-index reindex sorting reset_index vs reindex which of reset_index, sort_index or reindex renumber or reorder rows choose an index method comparing `reset_index(drop=true)`, `sort_index()` and `reindex()` allows us to choose the right tool for each tidying job, because they look similar but do different things."},{"sid":"B2.22","title":"Dropping columns","cmd":"df.drop(columns=['age'])","terms":["drop(columns=)"],"tags":["pandas","columns","dropping"],"task":["remove a column","delete a column","drop columns","get rid of a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"dropping columns df.drop(columns=['age']) pandas columns dropping drop(columns=) remove a column delete a column drop columns get rid of a column the `drop(columns=...)` method allows us to remove columns we do not need. it is useful for tidying a wide table. alternatively, select only the columns to keep with `df[[...]]`."},{"sid":"B2.23","title":"Dropping rows by label","cmd":"df.drop(index=['a1'])","terms":["drop(index=)"],"tags":[],"task":["remove a row","delete a row","drop a row by label","get rid of rows"],"examinable":true,"search":"dropping rows by label df.drop(index=['a1'])  drop(index=) remove a row delete a row drop a row by label get rid of rows the `drop(index=...)` method allows us to remove specific rows by label. it is useful for deleting known bad records. alternatively, filter with a mask to keep only the good rows."},{"sid":"B2.24","title":"Choosing rows or columns with `axis`","cmd":"df.drop('age', axis=1)","terms":["axis"],"tags":["pandas","columns","dropping"],"task":["drop rows or columns","what is axis","axis 0 or 1","drop by axis"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing rows or columns with axis df.drop('age', axis=1) pandas columns dropping axis drop rows or columns what is axis axis 0 or 1 drop by axis the `axis` argument allows us to say whether `drop` should work on rows (0) or columns (1). it is useful when reading other people's code, which often sets `axis` without comment. alternatively, `columns=` and `index=` are clearer."},{"sid":"B2.25","title":"Changing a column's type","cmd":"df['age'].astype(float)","terms":["astype()"],"tags":["pandas","columns","types"],"task":["change a column to numbers","convert a column type","turn text into numbers","make a column float","convert to integer"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing a column's type df['age'].astype(float) pandas columns types astype() change a column to numbers convert a column type turn text into numbers make a column float convert to integer the `.astype()` method allows us to convert a column to another type, such as int or float. it is useful when numbers are stored as text. alternatively, `pd.to_numeric()` is more forgiving of bad values."},{"sid":"B3.01","title":"Selecting one row by position","cmd":"df.iloc[0]","terms":["iloc"],"tags":["pandas","rows","selecting","iloc"],"task":["get a row by number","first row","get the nth row","select a row by position"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting one row by position df.iloc[0] pandas rows selecting iloc iloc get a row by number first row get the nth row select a row by position the `.iloc[]` indexer allows us to select a row by its integer position. it is useful when the first or last row is needed, regardless of labels. alternatively, `.loc[]` selects by label."},{"sid":"B3.02","title":"Selecting a range of rows by position","cmd":"df.iloc[1:3]","terms":["iloc slice"],"tags":["pandas","rows","selecting","iloc"],"task":["get rows 1 to 3","slice rows","select a block of rows","first few rows by number"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting a range of rows by position df.iloc[1:3] pandas rows selecting iloc iloc slice get rows 1 to 3 slice rows select a block of rows first few rows by number a slice inside `.iloc[]` allows us to select several consecutive rows by position (end excluded). it is useful for the first n rows. alternatively, `df.head(n)` does the same for the start."},{"sid":"B3.03","title":"Selecting one cell by position","cmd":"df.iloc[0, 1]","terms":["iloc[row, col]"],"tags":["pandas","selecting","iloc"],"task":["get one value by position","single cell by row and column number","value at row 0 column 1"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting one cell by position df.iloc[0, 1] pandas selecting iloc iloc[row, col] get one value by position single cell by row and column number value at row 0 column 1 two numbers in `.iloc[row, col]` allow us to pick out one cell by position. it is useful for a single known value. alternatively, `.iat[row, col]` is a faster version for single cells."},{"sid":"B3.04","title":"Selecting rows and columns by position lists","cmd":"df.iloc[[0, 2], [0, 2]]","terms":["iloc with lists"],"tags":["pandas","selecting","iloc"],"task":["pick rows and columns by number","select a sub-table by position","choose rows and columns by index number"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting rows and columns by position lists df.iloc[[0, 2], [0, 2]] pandas selecting iloc iloc with lists pick rows and columns by number select a sub-table by position choose rows and columns by index number lists inside `.iloc[]` allow us to pick several non-adjacent rows and columns by position. it is useful for custom sub-tables. alternatively, `.loc[]` with lists of labels does the same by name."},{"sid":"B3.05","title":"Selecting one row by label","cmd":"df.loc['a1']","terms":["loc"],"tags":["pandas","rows","selecting","loc"],"task":["get a row by name","select a row by label","row with a given index"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting one row by label df.loc['a1'] pandas rows selecting loc loc get a row by name select a row by label row with a given index the `.loc[]` indexer allows us to select a row by its label. it is useful when rows have meaningful labels. alternatively, `.iloc[]` selects by position."},{"sid":"B3.06","title":"Selecting a range of rows by label","cmd":"df.loc['a1':'a3']","terms":["loc slice"],"tags":["pandas","rows","selecting","loc"],"task":["get rows between two labels","slice by label","select rows from a to b"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting a range of rows by label df.loc['a1':'a3'] pandas rows selecting loc loc slice get rows between two labels slice by label select rows from a to b a slice inside `.loc[]` allows us to select consecutive rows by label, with the end **included**. it is useful for date ranges and ordered ids. alternatively, `.iloc[]` slices by position with the end excluded."},{"sid":"B3.07","title":"Selecting one cell by label","cmd":"df.loc['a2', 'age']","terms":["loc[row, col]"],"tags":["pandas","selecting","loc"],"task":["get one value by label","single cell by row name and column","look up a value"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting one cell by label df.loc['a2', 'age'] pandas selecting loc loc[row, col] get one value by label single cell by row name and column look up a value the `.loc[row, column]` form allows us to pick out one cell by row label and column name. it is useful for looking up a single value. alternatively, `.at[row, col]` is a faster version."},{"sid":"B3.08","title":"Selecting rows and columns by label lists","cmd":"df.loc[['a1', 'a3'], ['age', 'height']]","terms":["loc with lists"],"tags":[],"task":["pick rows and columns by name","select a sub-table by label","choose rows and columns by label"],"examinable":true,"search":"selecting rows and columns by label lists df.loc[['a1', 'a3'], ['age', 'height']]  loc with lists pick rows and columns by name select a sub-table by label choose rows and columns by label lists inside `.loc[]` allow us to pick specific rows and columns by name. it is useful for building a custom sub-table. alternatively, `df[[cols]]` picks columns only."},{"sid":"B3.09","title":"Building a mask","cmd":"mask = df['col'] > value","terms":["boolean mask","mask"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","masks"],"task":["make a True/False mask","flag rows that meet a condition","test a condition on a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"building a mask mask = df['col'] > value pandas filtering masks boolean mask mask make a true/false mask flag rows that meet a condition test a condition on a column a comparison on a column allows us to build a mask, a true/false value for every row. it is useful as the first step of filtering. alternatively, `df.query()` writes the condition as text."},{"sid":"B3.10","title":"Filtering rows with a mask","cmd":"df[mask]  /  df.loc[mask]","terms":["df[mask]","filtering rows"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","masks"],"task":["keep rows that match","filter the table","select rows by a condition","remove rows that do not match"],"examinable":true,"search":"filtering rows with a mask df[mask]  /  df.loc[mask] pandas filtering masks df[mask] filtering rows keep rows that match filter the table select rows by a condition remove rows that do not match putting a mask inside `df[...]` allows us to keep only the rows where it is `true`. it is useful for selecting a subset of the rows. alternatively, `df.loc[mask]` does the same."},{"sid":"B3.11","title":"Filtering rows and choosing columns","cmd":"df.loc[mask, ['age', 'height']]","terms":["loc[mask, cols]"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","masks","loc"],"task":["filter rows and keep some columns","select rows and columns together","conditional subset"],"examinable":true,"search":"filtering rows and choosing columns df.loc[mask, ['age', 'height']] pandas filtering masks loc loc[mask, cols] filter rows and keep some columns select rows and columns together conditional subset the form `df.loc[mask, columns]` allows us to filter rows and select columns in one step. it is useful when only a few variables are needed for a subset. alternatively, filter first, then select columns in a second step."},{"sid":"B3.12","title":"Filtering rows in one line","cmd":"df[df['col'] == value]","terms":["df[df['c'] == x]"],"tags":[],"task":["filter in one line","quick filter on a value","keep rows equal to a value"],"examinable":true,"search":"filtering rows in one line df[df['col'] == value]  df[df['c'] == x] filter in one line quick filter on a value keep rows equal to a value writing the condition inside the brackets allows us to filter in one line. it is useful for quick, one-off subsets. alternatively, store the mask in a variable first if it is used more than once."},{"sid":"B3.13","title":"Combining conditions with `&` and `|`","cmd":"(cond1) & (cond2),  (cond1) | (cond2)","terms":["& and |","combining conditions"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","masks","boolean"],"task":["filter on two conditions","and or together","both conditions","either condition","use & and |"],"examinable":true,"search":"combining conditions with & and | (cond1) & (cond2),  (cond1) | (cond2) pandas filtering masks boolean & and | combining conditions filter on two conditions and or together both conditions either condition use & and | the `&` and `|` operators allow us to combine several masks, requiring all or any to be true. they are useful for filtering on more than one column. alternatively, `isin()` tests one column against several values."},{"sid":"B3.14","title":"Copying a filtered table","cmd":"df[mask].copy()","terms":["copy()","SettingWithCopyWarning"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","copying","gotcha"],"task":["avoid SettingWithCopyWarning","copy a subset","make an independent copy","change a filtered table safely"],"examinable":true,"search":"copying a filtered table df[mask].copy() pandas filtering copying gotcha copy() settingwithcopywarning avoid settingwithcopywarning copy a subset make an independent copy change a filtered table safely the `.copy()` method allows us to turn a filtered view into its own independent table. it is useful before adding columns, to avoid `settingwithcopywarning`. alternatively, use `.loc` for assignments when the original is meant to change."},{"sid":"B3.15","title":"Filtering with `~`, `isin()` and `between()`","cmd":"~mask, s.isin(values), s.between(a, b)","terms":["~ (not)","isin()","between()"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","masks","non-examinable"],"task":["rows not in a list","keep rows in a set of values","rows between two numbers","negate a condition","exclude rows"],"examinable":false,"search":"filtering with ~, isin() and between() ~mask, s.isin(values), s.between(a, b) pandas filtering masks non-examinable ~ (not) isin() between() rows not in a list keep rows in a set of values rows between two numbers negate a condition exclude rows the `~`, `isin()` and `between()` tools allow us to negate a mask, match a list of values, or test a range. they are useful to shorten longer conditions. alternatively, write the equivalent with `==`, `|` and two comparisons."},{"sid":"B3.16","title":"Filtering with `query`","cmd":"df.query(\"col > value\")","terms":["query()"],"tags":["pandas","filtering","non-examinable"],"task":["filter with a string condition","query a table","easier filter syntax","filter by expression"],"examinable":false,"search":"filtering with query df.query(\"col > value\") pandas filtering non-examinable query() filter with a string condition query a table easier filter syntax filter by expression the `.query()` method allows us to write a row filter as a piece of text. it is useful for readable conditions. alternatively, ordinary masks give more control and are the standard method in this course."},{"sid":"B3.17","title":"Sorting by a column","cmd":"df.sort_values(by='height')","terms":["sort_values()"],"tags":[],"task":["order rows by a column","sort the table","sort ascending","arrange rows"],"examinable":true,"search":"sorting by a column df.sort_values(by='height')  sort_values() order rows by a column sort the table sort ascending arrange rows the `sort_values()` method allows us to order the rows by the values in a column. it is useful for rankings and for putting data in order. alternatively, `.nlargest(n, 'col')` gives the top n directly."},{"sid":"B3.18","title":"Sorting in descending order","cmd":"df.sort_values(by='height', ascending=False)","terms":["sort_values(ascending=False)"],"tags":[],"task":["sort largest first","sort high to low","descending order","top values first"],"examinable":true,"search":"sorting in descending order df.sort_values(by='height', ascending=false)  sort_values(ascending=false) sort largest first sort high to low descending order top values first the `ascending=false` option allows us to sort from largest to smallest. it is useful for top-10 style lists. alternatively, `df.nlargest()` or `.head()` after sorting limits the output."},{"sid":"B3.19","title":"Sorting by several columns","cmd":"df.sort_values(by=['age', 'height'], ascending=[False, True])","terms":["sort_values(by=[...])"],"tags":["pandas","sorting"],"task":["sort by two columns","sort by one column then another","tie-break sorting","mixed ascending and descending"],"examinable":true,"search":"sorting by several columns df.sort_values(by=['age', 'height'], ascending=[false, true]) pandas sorting sort_values(by=[...]) sort by two columns sort by one column then another tie-break sorting mixed ascending and descending a list passed to `sort_values(by=[...])` allows us to sort by one column and break ties with another. it is useful for ordering groups and then values within them. alternatively, sort twice with `kind=\"stable\"`, starting with the less important column (the default sort does not keep the earlier order of ties)."},{"sid":"B4.01","title":"Adding a constant column","cmd":"df['job'] = 'Student'","terms":["new column","constant column"],"tags":[],"task":["add a column with the same value","new column of one value","label every row","default value column"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a constant column df['job'] = 'student'  new column constant column add a column with the same value new column of one value label every row default value column assigning a single value to a new column name allows us to add a column where every row has the same entry. it is useful for labelling rows before tables are combined. alternatively, `df.assign(job=\"student\")` returns a new table with the column added."},{"sid":"B4.02","title":"Adding a calculated column","cmd":"df['new'] = df['col'] / n","terms":["new column","calculated column"],"tags":[],"task":["add a new column from a calculation","derive a column","divide a column","convert units"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a calculated column df['new'] = df['col'] / n  new column calculated column add a new column from a calculation derive a column divide a column convert units assigning an expression to a new column name allows us to create a column from existing ones, calculated for every row at once. it is useful for unit conversions and derived variables. alternatively, `df.assign(height_m=lambda d: d[\"height\"] / 100)` does it inside a method chain."},{"sid":"B4.03","title":"Adding a column from two columns","cmd":"df['new'] = df['col_2'] - df['col_1']","terms":["column arithmetic"],"tags":[],"task":["add the difference of two columns","combine two columns","subtract one column from another","add a column from other columns"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a column from two columns df['new'] = df['col_2'] - df['col_1']  column arithmetic add the difference of two columns combine two columns subtract one column from another add a column from other columns doing arithmetic between two columns allows us to build a new column from both, row by row. it is useful for differences, ratios and totals. alternatively, `df.diff(axis=1)` takes differences across neighbouring columns."},{"sid":"B4.04","title":"Adding a column with a NumPy function","cmd":"df['new'] = np.log10(df['col'])","terms":["np.log10() on a column"],"tags":[],"task":["take the log of a column","apply a numpy function to a column","square root column","transform a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a column with a numpy function df['new'] = np.log10(df['col'])  np.log10() on a column take the log of a column apply a numpy function to a column square root column transform a column passing a column to a numpy function allows us to transform every value, for example by taking a logarithm. it is useful for log-transforming skewed data before analysis. alternatively, pandas has some built-in methods such as `.abs()` and `.round()`."},{"sid":"B4.05","title":"Changing values in a column","cmd":"df.loc[mask, 'col'] = value","terms":["loc[mask, col] = value"],"tags":[],"task":["change some values","update values that match","replace values in rows","edit cells by condition"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing values in a column df.loc[mask, 'col'] = value  loc[mask, col] = value change some values update values that match replace values in rows edit cells by condition the form `df.loc[mask, 'col'] = value` allows us to overwrite values only in the rows that meet a condition. it is useful for correcting errors or capping values. alternatively, `np.where(cond, a, b)` builds a whole new column with two options."},{"sid":"B4.06","title":"Turning counts into probabilities","cmd":"df['prob'] = df['count'] / total","terms":["probability column"],"tags":["pandas","probability","columns","new-column"],"task":["turn counts into proportions","probability column","divide by the total","relative frequency"],"examinable":true,"search":"turning counts into probabilities df['prob'] = df['count'] / total pandas probability columns new-column probability column turn counts into proportions probability column divide by the total relative frequency if we have a column of counts out of a known total, then dividing by the total gives a column of probabilities. it is useful for turning enumerated outcomes into a probability distribution. alternatively, `value_counts(normalize=true)` gives proportions directly."},{"sid":"B4.07","title":"Calculating cumulative sums","cmd":"df['new'] = df['col'].cumsum()","terms":["cumsum()"],"tags":[],"task":["running total","cumulative sum","accumulate values","total so far"],"examinable":true,"search":"calculating cumulative sums df['new'] = df['col'].cumsum()  cumsum() running total cumulative sum accumulate values total so far the `.cumsum()` method allows us to calculate a running total down a column. it is useful for cumulative probabilities, p(x ≤ x). alternatively, `np.cumsum()` does the same on an array."},{"sid":"B4.08","title":"Method chaining","cmd":"df.method1().method2().method3()","terms":["method chaining","chaining"],"tags":["pandas","chaining","non-examinable"],"task":["chain methods","do several steps in a row","pipeline of pandas steps","method chain"],"examinable":false,"search":"method chaining df.method1().method2().method3() pandas chaining non-examinable method chaining chaining chain methods do several steps in a row pipeline of pandas steps method chain method chaining allows us to apply several pandas methods in a row, each working on the result of the one before. it is useful because a cleaning recipe then reads top to bottom like a list of instructions, without many temporary variables. alternatively, do one step per line and keep re-assigning the same variable; switch to *step by step* to see the equivalent."},{"sid":"B4.09","title":"Chaining methods on one line","cmd":"s.value_counts().to_frame().sort_values(by=col)","terms":["method chaining"],"tags":[],"task":["chain on one line","count and sort in one expression","short chain"],"examinable":false,"search":"chaining methods on one line s.value_counts().to_frame().sort_values(by=col)  method chaining chain on one line count and sort in one expression short chain method chaining allows us to apply several steps in a row, each working on the result of the previous one. it is useful for short sequences like count → table → sort. alternatively, do one step per line and keep re-assigning the same variable, which lets each step be checked on its own."},{"sid":"B4.10","title":"Chaining methods over several lines","cmd":"( df.method().method() )","terms":["method chaining","( ) brackets for chains"],"tags":["pandas","chaining","non-examinable"],"task":["write a chain over several lines","readable chain","wrap a chain in brackets","tidy long chain"],"examinable":false,"search":"chaining methods over several lines ( df.method().method() ) pandas chaining non-examinable method chaining ( ) brackets for chains write a chain over several lines readable chain wrap a chain in brackets tidy long chain wrapping a chain in brackets allows us to write one step per line. it is useful for longer chains, because each step can be read and commented separately. alternatively, do one step per line and keep re-assigning the same variable (step by step)."},{"sid":"B4.11","title":"Checking a chain step by step","cmd":"( df.step1().step2().step3() )","terms":["debugging a chain"],"tags":["pandas","chaining","non-examinable"],"task":["debug a chain","check each step of a chain","find which step went wrong","inspect part of a chain"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a chain step by step ( df.step1().step2().step3() ) pandas chaining non-examinable debugging a chain debug a chain check each step of a chain find which step went wrong inspect part of a chain running a chain with its later steps left off allows us to see what each step produced, which is how to find the step where something goes wrong. it is useful whenever a long chain gives an unexpected result. alternatively, write the chain as separate lines and display the variable after each one."},{"sid":"B4.12","title":"Avoiding `inplace=True` in a chain","cmd":"df.dropna(inplace=True).head()","terms":["inplace"],"tags":[],"task":["why inplace returns None","chain after dropna","avoid inplace","method returns None"],"examinable":false,"search":"avoiding inplace=true in a chain df.dropna(inplace=true).head()  inplace why inplace returns none chain after dropna avoid inplace method returns none a method called with `inplace=true` changes the table itself and returns `none`, so there is nothing left to chain onto. it is useful when a chain fails with a `'nonetype' object has no attribute` error, which this mistake causes. alternatively, drop `inplace` and keep the returned table."},{"sid":"B4.13","title":"Binning numbers into categories","cmd":"pd.cut(df['col'], bins=edges, labels=names)","terms":["pd.cut()","binning"],"tags":["pandas","binning","columns","new-column"],"task":["group numbers into ranges","make age groups","bin values","turn numbers into categories","discretise"],"examinable":true,"search":"binning numbers into categories pd.cut(df['col'], bins=edges, labels=names) pandas binning columns new-column pd.cut() binning group numbers into ranges make age groups bin values turn numbers into categories discretise the `pd.cut()` function allows us to sort a continuous column into labelled groups (bins). it is useful for turning a number into categories. alternatively, `np.where` or `pd.qcut` (equal-sized groups) can also create categories."},{"sid":"B4.17","title":"Comparing a variable across binned groups with a box plot","cmd":"pd.cut(df['col'], bins=edges, labels=names); sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='col')","terms":["box plot of binned groups","pd.cut() with boxplot","categories from a numeric variable"],"tags":["pandas","binning","seaborn","boxplot","plotting","non-examinable"],"task":["compare groups of a number","box plot by bin","plot a variable across bins","box plot of binned groups"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing a variable across binned groups with a box plot pd.cut(df['col'], bins=edges, labels=names); sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='col') pandas binning seaborn boxplot plotting non-examinable box plot of binned groups pd.cut() with boxplot categories from a numeric variable compare groups of a number box plot by bin plot a variable across bins box plot of binned groups if a numeric variable has been cut into labelled bins, then a box plot of a second variable for each bin shows how the second variable changes across the range of the first. it is useful for judging whether a relationship is steady, curved or changing in spread without assuming a straight line. alternatively, a scatter plot shows every point, and `groupby` on the bins gives the numbers behind the boxes."},{"sid":"B4.18","title":"Binning numbers into equal-sized groups with `qcut`","cmd":"pd.qcut(df['col'], q=n, labels=names)","terms":["pd.qcut()","quantile bins","quartiles as categories"],"tags":[],"task":["equal-sized groups","split into quartiles","make quantile bins","qcut","groups with the same number of rows"],"examinable":false,"search":"binning numbers into equal-sized groups with qcut pd.qcut(df['col'], q=n, labels=names)  pd.qcut() quantile bins quartiles as categories equal-sized groups split into quartiles make quantile bins qcut groups with the same number of rows the `pd.qcut()` function allows us to cut a column into groups that each hold about the same number of rows, such as quartiles. it is useful when the spread of the values is uneven, so that fixed-width bins would leave some groups almost empty. alternatively, `pd.cut` makes bins of chosen width, which is better when the edges have a meaning."},{"sid":"B4.14","title":"Replacing and mapping values","cmd":"s.replace(d), s.map(d)","terms":["replace()","map()"],"tags":["pandas","columns","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["replace values","recode a column","swap labels","map codes to names","change categories"],"examinable":false,"search":"replacing and mapping values s.replace(d), s.map(d) pandas columns dictionaries non-examinable replace() map() replace values recode a column swap labels map codes to names change categories the `.replace()` and `.map()` methods allow us to recode values using a dictionary. they are useful for turning codes into readable labels. alternatively, `np.where` handles a simple two-way choice."},{"sid":"B4.15","title":"Adding a conditional column with `np.where`","cmd":"np.where(cond, a, b)","terms":["np.where()"],"tags":["pandas","numpy","columns","new-column","non-examinable"],"task":["if else for a column","choose between two values","conditional column","set a value by condition"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding a conditional column with np.where np.where(cond, a, b) pandas numpy columns new-column non-examinable np.where() if else for a column choose between two values conditional column set a value by condition the `np.where(condition, a, b)` function allows us to fill a new column with `a` where a condition is true and `b` elsewhere. it is useful for flags that depend on a condition. alternatively, `pd.cut` handles more than two groups."},{"sid":"B4.16","title":"Clipping and rounding values","cmd":"s.clip(lower, upper), s.round(n), df.round(n)","terms":["clip()","round()"],"tags":[],"task":["cap values","limit to a range","round a column","round the table","clip outliers"],"examinable":false,"search":"clipping and rounding values s.clip(lower, upper), s.round(n), df.round(n)  clip() round() cap values limit to a range round a column round the table clip outliers the `.clip()` and `.round()` methods allow us to limit values to a range and tidy decimals. they are useful for capping outliers and neat output. alternatively, `.loc[mask, 'col'] = value` caps values by condition."},{"sid":"B5.01","title":"Calculating summary statistics","cmd":"df['c'].mean(), .median(), .std(), .min(), .max(), .count()","terms":["mean()","median()","std()","count()"],"tags":["pandas","summary"],"task":["average of a column","find the mean","calculate the median","standard deviation","minimum and maximum","summarise a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"calculating summary statistics df['c'].mean(), .median(), .std(), .min(), .max(), .count() pandas summary mean() median() std() count() average of a column find the mean calculate the median standard deviation minimum and maximum summarise a column methods such as `.mean()`, `.median()` and `.std()` allow us to summarise a column with a single number. they are useful for describing the centre and spread of a variable. alternatively, `.describe()` gives all of them at once."},{"sid":"B5.02","title":"Finding quartiles","cmd":"df['col'].quantile(q)","terms":["quantile()"],"tags":[],"task":["find the quartiles","25th percentile","lower and upper quartile","quantile of a column"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding quartiles df['col'].quantile(q)  quantile() find the quartiles 25th percentile lower and upper quartile quantile of a column the `.quantile()` method allows us to find the value below which a given fraction of the data falls. it is useful for quartiles and for box plot summaries. alternatively, `np.percentile` uses percentages (25, 50, 75) rather than fractions."},{"sid":"B5.29","title":"Finding outliers with the IQR rule","cmd":"q1, q3 = s.quantile([0.25, 0.75]); mask = (s < q1 - 1.5 * iqr) | (s > q3 + 1.5 * iqr)","terms":["outliers","IQR rule"],"tags":[],"task":["find outliers","detect unusual values","flag extreme values","values beyond the whiskers"],"examinable":false,"search":"finding outliers with the iqr rule q1, q3 = s.quantile([0.25, 0.75]); mask = (s < q1 - 1.5 * iqr) | (s > q3 + 1.5 * iqr)  outliers iqr rule find outliers detect unusual values flag extreme values values beyond the whiskers if we want to flag unusual values, then we can mark any value more than 1.5 times the interquartile range beyond the quartiles as an outlier. it is useful for deciding which points a box plot will draw individually, and for checking them before they affect a mean. alternatively, `sns.boxplot` shows the same points without calculating them."},{"sid":"B5.03","title":"Describing the whole table","cmd":"df.describe()","terms":["describe()"],"tags":[],"task":["summarise the whole table","summary statistics for every column","first look at the data","describe"],"examinable":true,"search":"describing the whole table df.describe()  describe() summarise the whole table summary statistics for every column first look at the data describe the `.describe()` method allows us to produce count, mean, std, min, quartiles and max for every numeric column. it is useful as a first look at a dataset. alternatively, call individual methods for single statistics."},{"sid":"B5.30","title":"Correlating all numeric columns","cmd":"df.corr(numeric_only=True)","terms":["corr()"],"tags":[],"task":["correlation matrix","which columns are related","correlate every pair of columns","correlation table"],"examinable":false,"search":"correlating all numeric columns df.corr(numeric_only=true)  corr() correlation matrix which columns are related correlate every pair of columns correlation table the `.corr()` method allows us to calculate the correlation between every pair of numeric columns in one table. it is useful for spotting which variables move together before plotting or fitting a model. alternatively, `sns.heatmap` draws the same table as colours."},{"sid":"B5.32","title":"Ranking values","cmd":"s.rank(), s.rank(ascending=False, method='min')","terms":["rank()"],"tags":[],"task":["rank the values","position in the sorted order","ranking with ties","who is first"],"examinable":false,"search":"ranking values s.rank(), s.rank(ascending=false, method='min')  rank() rank the values position in the sorted order ranking with ties who is first the `.rank()` method allows us to replace each value with its position in the sorted order, with 1 for the smallest. it is useful for comparing rows by order rather than size, and for breaking ties in a stated way. alternatively, `sort_values` orders the rows but does not record the rank."},{"sid":"B5.04","title":"Finding the row of the largest value","cmd":"idx = df['c'].idxmax(); df.loc[idx]","terms":["idxmax()","idxmin()"],"tags":[],"task":["row with the maximum","find the biggest row","row with the minimum","which row has the largest value"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding the row of the largest value idx = df['c'].idxmax(); df.loc[idx]  idxmax() idxmin() row with the maximum find the biggest row row with the minimum which row has the largest value the `.idxmax()` method allows us to find the *label* of the row with the largest value, which we can then look up with `loc`. it is useful when the whole record of the maximum is needed, not only its value. alternatively, `df.sort_values(...).head(1)` returns the top row."},{"sid":"B5.05","title":"Summing and accumulating values","cmd":"s.sum(), s.cumsum()","terms":["sum()","cumsum()"],"tags":[],"task":["add up a column","total of a column","running total","sum"],"examinable":true,"search":"summing and accumulating values s.sum(), s.cumsum()  sum() cumsum() add up a column total of a column running total sum the `.sum()` and `.cumsum()` methods allow us to total a column or build a running total. they are useful for totals and cumulative probabilities. alternatively, `np.sum` and `np.cumsum` work on arrays."},{"sid":"B5.06","title":"Calculating variance, mode and standard error","cmd":"s.var(), s.mode(), s.sem()","terms":["var()","mode()","sem()"],"tags":[],"task":["variance of a column","most common value","standard error of the mean","mode"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating variance, mode and standard error s.var(), s.mode(), s.sem()  var() mode() sem() variance of a column most common value standard error of the mean mode the `.var()`, `.mode()` and `.sem()` methods allow us to measure spread, the most common value and the precision of the mean. they are useful for confidence intervals and categorical data. alternatively, `.std() ** 2` gives the variance."},{"sid":"B5.31","title":"Measuring skewness and kurtosis","cmd":"s.skew(), s.kurt()","terms":["skew()","kurt()"],"tags":[],"task":["is the data symmetric","skewed data","how heavy are the tails","lopsided distribution"],"examinable":false,"search":"measuring skewness and kurtosis s.skew(), s.kurt()  skew() kurt() is the data symmetric skewed data how heavy are the tails lopsided distribution the `.skew()` and `.kurt()` methods allow us to measure how lopsided a distribution is and how heavy its tails are. they are useful for judging whether a column is close to symmetric before using a mean. alternatively, a histogram shows the shape directly."},{"sid":"B5.07","title":"Listing the unique values","cmd":"s.unique(), s.nunique()","terms":["unique()","nunique()"],"tags":[],"task":["list the distinct values","what values are in a column","how many different values","unique values"],"examinable":false,"search":"listing the unique values s.unique(), s.nunique()  unique() nunique() list the distinct values what values are in a column how many different values unique values the `.unique()` and `.nunique()` methods allow us to list the different values in a column and count them. they are useful for checking categories and spotting typos. alternatively, `value_counts()` also shows how often each occurs."},{"sid":"B5.08","title":"Describing text columns too","cmd":"df.describe(include='all')","terms":["describe(include='all')"],"tags":[],"task":["describe text columns","summary including categories","describe everything","summary for all columns"],"examinable":false,"search":"describing text columns too df.describe(include='all')  describe(include='all') describe text columns summary including categories describe everything summary for all columns the `include='all'` option allows us to summarise text columns (count, unique, most common) as well as numeric ones. it is useful for a first look at mixed data. alternatively, `df.select_dtypes('object').describe()` shows only the text columns."},{"sid":"B5.09","title":"Counting each category","cmd":"df['c'].value_counts()","terms":["value_counts()"],"tags":[],"task":["count each category","frequency of each value","how many of each","tally a column","value counts"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting each category df['c'].value_counts()  value_counts() count each category frequency of each value how many of each tally a column value counts the `.value_counts()` method allows us to count how many rows fall in each category. it is useful for sample sizes in each group. alternatively, `groupby(...).size()` gives the same counts and `sns.countplot` draws them."},{"sid":"B5.10","title":"Keeping the category order when counting","cmd":"value_counts(sort=False)","terms":["value_counts(sort=False)"],"tags":[],"task":["count without sorting","keep the original order of categories","category order when counting"],"examinable":true,"search":"keeping the category order when counting value_counts(sort=false)  value_counts(sort=false) count without sorting keep the original order of categories category order when counting the `sort=false` option allows us to keep categories in their original order instead of largest first. it is useful for categories that have a natural order. alternatively, `.sort_index()` after counting sorts by category."},{"sid":"B5.11","title":"Turning counts into a table","cmd":".value_counts().to_frame().reset_index()","terms":["to_frame()","value_counts to table"],"tags":[],"task":["counts as a table","value counts to a DataFrame","counts with column names"],"examinable":true,"search":"turning counts into a table .value_counts().to_frame().reset_index()  to_frame() value_counts to table counts as a table value counts to a dataframe counts with column names chaining `.to_frame().reset_index()` allows us to turn the result of `value_counts` into an ordinary dataframe. it is useful for plotting with seaborn or merging with other tables. alternatively, `rename_axis(...).reset_index(name=...)` sets clearer column names."},{"sid":"B5.12","title":"Counting proportions instead of numbers","cmd":"value_counts(normalize=True)","terms":["value_counts(normalize=True)"],"tags":[],"task":["percentages of each category","proportion of each value","relative frequency","share of each group"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting proportions instead of numbers value_counts(normalize=true)  value_counts(normalize=true) percentages of each category proportion of each value relative frequency share of each group the `normalize=true` option allows us to get proportions that add up to 1 instead of counts. it is useful for probabilities and for comparing groups of different sizes. alternatively, divide the counts by `len(df)`."},{"sid":"B5.13","title":"Counting two categories together","cmd":"pd.crosstab(df['a'], df['b'])","terms":["pd.crosstab()"],"tags":[],"task":["count two categories together","cross tabulation","contingency table","table of counts for two columns"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting two categories together pd.crosstab(df['a'], df['b'])  pd.crosstab() count two categories together cross tabulation contingency table table of counts for two columns the `pd.crosstab()` function allows us to count how many rows fall in each combination of two categories. it is useful for questions about two categories at once. alternatively, `groupby([a, b]).size()` gives the same numbers in long form."},{"sid":"B5.14","title":"Comparing groups with masks","cmd":"mask per group, then a statistic","terms":["comparing groups"],"tags":[],"task":["compare two groups","mean of each group by hand","compare species","statistics for one group at a time"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing groups with masks mask per group, then a statistic  comparing groups compare two groups mean of each group by hand compare species statistics for one group at a time if we want a statistic for each of several groups, then we can build one mask per group and calculate the statistic on each. it is useful for comparing, say, two species of penguin. alternatively, `groupby` does this for every group in one line (see the non-examinable groupby sheet)."},{"sid":"B5.15","title":"Building a summary table","cmd":"pd.DataFrame(d).set_index('col').round(n)","terms":["summary table"],"tags":[],"task":["make a summary table","table of group statistics","side by side statistics","report table"],"examinable":true,"search":"building a summary table pd.dataframe(d).set_index('col').round(n)  summary table make a summary table table of group statistics side by side statistics report table if we have several group statistics, then we can put them in a small dataframe to present them side by side. it is useful for tables in a report. alternatively, `groupby(...).agg([...])` produces such a table automatically."},{"sid":"B5.33","title":"Saving a grouped table","cmd":"df.groupby('group')['col'].agg(funcs).reset_index().to_csv(\"out.csv\", index=False)","terms":["saving a summary"],"tags":[],"task":["save group statistics to a file","export a summary","write grouped results to CSV"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving a grouped table df.groupby('group')['col'].agg(funcs).reset_index().to_csv(\"out.csv\", index=false)  saving a summary save group statistics to a file export a summary write grouped results to csv if we have a grouped summary, then we can write it to a file with `to_csv` once the group labels have been moved back into a column. it is useful for passing a table of group statistics to a report or another program. alternatively, `to_pickle` keeps the exact types."},{"sid":"B5.16","title":"Counting missing values","cmd":"df.isna().sum()","terms":["isna()","missing values"],"tags":["pandas","missing-data"],"task":["count missing values","how many NaN","find blanks","missing data per column"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting missing values df.isna().sum() pandas missing-data isna() missing values count missing values how many nan find blanks missing data per column the `.isna().sum()` combination allows us to count the missing values in each column. it is useful as the first check on any dataset, because missing values affect statistics and fits. alternatively, `df.info()` shows non-null counts instead."},{"sid":"B5.17","title":"Comparing `count()` with `len()`","cmd":"df.info(), df['c'].count() vs len(df)","terms":["count() vs len()"],"tags":[],"task":["why count is smaller than length","count versus len","non-missing count"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing count() with len() df.info(), df['c'].count() vs len(df)  count() vs len() why count is smaller than length count versus len non-missing count comparing `count()` with `len()` allows us to see how many values are missing in a column. it is useful because `count()` skips missing values but `len()` does not. alternatively, `isna().sum()` gives the same number directly."},{"sid":"B5.18","title":"Showing the rows with missing values","cmd":"df[df['bill_length_mm'].isna()]","terms":["isna() as a mask","notna()"],"tags":["pandas","missing-data","filtering"],"task":["show rows with missing values","find rows with NaN","inspect missing rows","which rows are blank"],"examinable":true,"search":"showing the rows with missing values df[df['bill_length_mm'].isna()] pandas missing-data filtering isna() as a mask notna() show rows with missing values find rows with nan inspect missing rows which rows are blank using `isna()` as a mask allows us to look at exactly which rows have missing values. it is useful for deciding whether to drop, fill or investigate them. alternatively, `notna()` gives the complete rows instead."},{"sid":"B5.19","title":"Removing rows with missing values","cmd":"df.dropna(), df.dropna(subset=['bill_length_mm'])","terms":["dropna()"],"tags":["pandas","missing-data","dropping"],"task":["remove rows with missing values","drop NaN","delete incomplete rows","clean missing data"],"examinable":true,"search":"removing rows with missing values df.dropna(), df.dropna(subset=['bill_length_mm']) pandas missing-data dropping dropna() remove rows with missing values drop nan delete incomplete rows clean missing data the `.dropna()` method allows us to remove rows that contain missing values. it is useful before calculations that cannot handle `nan`. alternatively, `fillna()` replaces missing values rather than removing the rows."},{"sid":"B5.20","title":"Filling missing values","cmd":"df['c'].fillna(0), df['c'].fillna(df['c'].mean())","terms":["fillna()"],"tags":["pandas","missing-data"],"task":["fill in missing values","replace NaN","impute with the mean","replace blanks with zero"],"examinable":true,"search":"filling missing values df['c'].fillna(0), df['c'].fillna(df['c'].mean()) pandas missing-data fillna() fill in missing values replace nan impute with the mean replace blanks with zero the `.fillna()` method allows us to replace missing values with a chosen value, such as 0 or the mean. it is useful when a missing value genuinely means zero, or when a complete column is needed. alternatively, `dropna()` removes the incomplete rows instead."},{"sid":"B5.21","title":"How statistics treat `NaN`","cmd":"s.mean() skips NaN;  value_counts(dropna=False)","terms":["NaN in statistics","dropna=False"],"tags":[],"task":["does mean ignore NaN","how statistics treat missing values","count missing as a category"],"examinable":true,"search":"how statistics treat nan s.mean() skips nan;  value_counts(dropna=false)  nan in statistics dropna=false does mean ignore nan how statistics treat missing values count missing as a category knowing that pandas statistics skip `nan` by default allows us to interpret results correctly. it is useful when a column has missing values, because the count behind a mean may be smaller than the table length. alternatively, `value_counts(dropna=false)` shows missing values as their own category."},{"sid":"B5.22","title":"Handling `NaN` before `linregress`","cmd":"stats.linregress with NaN","terms":["NaN in linregress"],"tags":["scipy","missing-data","regression","gotcha"],"task":["linregress gives nan","regression with missing values","remove NaN before fitting"],"examinable":true,"search":"handling nan before linregress stats.linregress with nan scipy missing-data regression gotcha nan in linregress linregress gives nan regression with missing values remove nan before fitting dropping missing values before calling `linregress` allows us to avoid `nan` results. it is useful because, unlike pandas, scipy does not skip missing values. alternatively, `df.dropna(subset=[...])` keeps the other columns intact."},{"sid":"B5.23","title":"Finding and dropping duplicates","cmd":"df.duplicated(), df.drop_duplicates()","terms":["duplicated()","drop_duplicates()"],"tags":[],"task":["find duplicate rows","remove duplicates","drop repeated rows","check for repeats"],"examinable":true,"search":"finding and dropping duplicates df.duplicated(), df.drop_duplicates()  duplicated() drop_duplicates() find duplicate rows remove duplicates drop repeated rows check for repeats the `.duplicated()` and `.drop_duplicates()` methods allow us to detect and remove repeated rows. they are useful for data entered twice. alternatively, `subset=` limits the check to key columns such as an id."},{"sid":"B5.24","title":"Plotting a scatter with pandas","cmd":"df.plot.scatter(x='col_1', y='col_2')","terms":["df.plot.scatter()"],"tags":[],"task":["scatter plot from a DataFrame","quick scatter","plot two columns against each other"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a scatter with pandas df.plot.scatter(x='col_1', y='col_2')  df.plot.scatter() scatter plot from a dataframe quick scatter plot two columns against each other the `df.plot.scatter()` method allows us to draw a scatter plot straight from a dataframe. it is useful for a quick look at the relationship between two columns. alternatively, `sns.scatterplot` or `plt.scatter` give more control."},{"sid":"B5.25","title":"Plotting a histogram with pandas","cmd":"df['col'].plot.hist(bins=n)","terms":["df.plot.hist()"],"tags":[],"task":["quick histogram","plot the distribution of a column","histogram from pandas"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a histogram with pandas df['col'].plot.hist(bins=n)  df.plot.hist() quick histogram plot the distribution of a column histogram from pandas the `.plot.hist()` method allows us to draw the distribution of a column. it is useful for checking shape and outliers. alternatively, `sns.histplot` and `plt.hist` do the same."},{"sid":"B5.26","title":"Plotting a box plot with pandas","cmd":"df.plot.box(), df.boxplot(column='col', by='group')","terms":["df.boxplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["quick box plot","box plot by group","compare groups visually","boxplot from a DataFrame"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a box plot with pandas df.plot.box(), df.boxplot(column='col', by='group')  df.boxplot() quick box plot box plot by group compare groups visually boxplot from a dataframe the `df.boxplot(column=, by=)` method allows us to compare the spread of a column across groups. it is useful for spotting differences and outliers between groups. alternatively, `sns.boxplot` draws grouped boxes with more styling options."},{"sid":"B5.27","title":"Using other pandas plot kinds","cmd":"df.plot.line() .bar() .barh() .kde() .area() .pie() .hexbin()","terms":["df.plot","plot kinds"],"tags":[],"task":["other plot types","line plot from a DataFrame","bar chart from pandas","pie chart","density plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"using other pandas plot kinds df.plot.line() .bar() .barh() .kde() .area() .pie() .hexbin()  df.plot plot kinds other plot types line plot from a dataframe bar chart from pandas pie chart density plot the `.plot. ()` family allows us to draw bar, line, area, pie and density plots from pandas objects. they are useful for a quick look without importing seaborn. alternatively, `df.plot(kind=\"bar\")` is the same thing."},{"sid":"B5.28","title":"Following an exploration checklist","cmd":"load → shape → missing → stats → plots → compare → interpret","terms":["exploring a dataset","checklist"],"tags":["pandas","workflow"],"task":["what to do first with new data","exploring a dataset","checklist for exploration","steps for exploratory analysis"],"examinable":true,"search":"following an exploration checklist load → shape → missing → stats → plots → compare → interpret pandas workflow exploring a dataset checklist what to do first with new data exploring a dataset checklist for exploration steps for exploratory analysis a fixed checklist allows us to explore any new dataset in a sensible order, which suits open-ended tasks where the starting point is unclear."},{"sid":"B6.01","title":"Stacking rows","cmd":"pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=0, ignore_index=True)","terms":["pd.concat(axis=0)","stacking tables"],"tags":["pandas","combining","concat"],"task":["join tables underneath","append rows","stack two DataFrames","combine tables vertically","concat"],"examinable":true,"search":"stacking rows pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=0, ignore_index=true) pandas combining concat pd.concat(axis=0) stacking tables join tables underneath append rows stack two dataframes combine tables vertically concat the `pd.concat()` function with `axis=0` allows us to stack tables one under another. it is useful when the same type of data has been collected in several files or batches. alternatively, `pd.merge` joins tables side by side by a key instead of stacking them."},{"sid":"B6.02","title":"Placing tables side by side","cmd":"pd.concat([df1, df3], axis=1)","terms":["pd.concat(axis=1)"],"tags":[],"task":["join tables side by side","add columns from another table","combine tables horizontally"],"examinable":true,"search":"placing tables side by side pd.concat([df1, df3], axis=1)  pd.concat(axis=1) join tables side by side add columns from another table combine tables horizontally the `pd.concat()` function with `axis=1` allows us to put tables next to each other, matching rows by their index. it is useful when columns for the same rows come from different sources. alternatively, `pd.merge` matches on a key column rather than on the index."},{"sid":"B6.03","title":"Combining tables with different columns","cmd":"pd.concat with different columns","terms":["pd.concat","mismatched columns"],"tags":[],"task":["stack tables with different columns","combine mismatched tables","missing columns after concat"],"examinable":true,"search":"combining tables with different columns pd.concat with different columns  pd.concat mismatched columns stack tables with different columns combine mismatched tables missing columns after concat concatenating tables with different columns allows us to keep every column, with `nan` where a table has no data. it is useful when tables come from different sources and do not share every column, because no error is raised and the missing values are filled with nan. alternatively, `join=\"inner\"` keeps only the columns both tables share."},{"sid":"B6.04","title":"Merging on a key column","cmd":"pd.merge(df_left, df_right, on='key')","terms":["pd.merge()","merge"],"tags":["pandas","combining","merge"],"task":["join two tables","look up data from another table","merge on a column","vlookup","combine by key"],"examinable":true,"search":"merging on a key column pd.merge(df_left, df_right, on='key') pandas combining merge pd.merge() merge join two tables look up data from another table merge on a column vlookup combine by key the `pd.merge()` function allows us to combine two tables by matching rows on a key column. it is useful when information about the same things is held in separate tables. alternatively, `concat(axis=1)` matches on the index instead."},{"sid":"B6.05","title":"Choosing the join type","cmd":"pd.merge(df_left, df_right, how='left' / 'right' / 'outer')","terms":["merge how=","left join","outer join"],"tags":["pandas","combining","merge"],"task":["left join","outer join","keep all rows","inner join","which rows survive a merge"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing the join type pd.merge(df_left, df_right, how='left' / 'right' / 'outer') pandas combining merge merge how= left join outer join left join outer join keep all rows inner join which rows survive a merge the `how=` argument allows us to decide which rows to keep when the keys do not all match. it is useful for keeping every row from one table (`left`, `right`) or from both (`outer`). alternatively, the default `inner` keeps only the matches."},{"sid":"B6.06","title":"Merging on keys with different names","cmd":"pd.merge(l, r, left_on='a', right_on='b')","terms":["left_on","right_on"],"tags":["pandas","combining","merge","non-examinable"],"task":["merge columns with different names","join on two different column names","left_on right_on"],"examinable":false,"search":"merging on keys with different names pd.merge(l, r, left_on='a', right_on='b') pandas combining merge non-examinable left_on right_on merge columns with different names join on two different column names left_on right_on the `left_on=` and `right_on=` arguments allow us to merge when the key column has a different name in each table. it is useful for tables from different sources. alternatively, rename one column first so both have the same name."},{"sid":"B6.07","title":"Showing where each row came from","cmd":"pd.merge(..., indicator=True)","terms":["merge indicator=True"],"tags":[],"task":["which rows did not match","where did each row come from","check a merge","unmatched rows"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing where each row came from pd.merge(..., indicator=true)  merge indicator=true which rows did not match where did each row come from check a merge unmatched rows the `indicator=true` option allows us to add a column saying whether each row came from the left table, the right table or both. it is useful for spotting unmatched records. alternatively, compare the key columns with `isin()`."},{"sid":"B6.08","title":"Reshaping wide to long with `set_index` and `melt`","cmd":"set_index → melt(var_name, value_name, ignore_index=False) → reset_index","terms":["melt()","wide to long"],"tags":["pandas","reshaping","melt"],"task":["wide to long","unpivot","melt a table","make a long table","columns into rows"],"examinable":true,"search":"reshaping wide to long with set_index and melt set_index → melt(var_name, value_name, ignore_index=false) → reset_index pandas reshaping melt melt() wide to long wide to long unpivot melt a table make a long table columns into rows the `set_index` → `melt` → `reset_index` sequence allows us to turn one column per measurement into one row per measurement. it is useful because seaborn and many statistical tools need *long* format. alternatively, `melt(id_vars=...)` does it in one step."},{"sid":"B6.09","title":"Reshaping wide to long with `melt` and `id_vars`","cmd":"df.melt(id_vars=ids, var_name='var', value_name='value')","terms":["melt(id_vars=)"],"tags":[],"task":["melt with id columns","wide to long keeping identifiers","unpivot columns"],"examinable":false,"search":"reshaping wide to long with melt and id_vars df.melt(id_vars=ids, var_name='var', value_name='value')  melt(id_vars=) melt with id columns wide to long keeping identifiers unpivot columns the `melt(id_vars=...)` method allows us to reshape wide to long without the index steps. it is useful when a few identifier columns stay as they are and all other columns are measurements. alternatively, the course method with `set_index` achieves the same result."},{"sid":"B6.10","title":"Reshaping long to wide with `set_index` and `pivot`","cmd":"set_index → pivot(columns, values) → reset_index().rename_axis(None, axis=1)","terms":["pivot()","long to wide"],"tags":[],"task":["long to wide","pivot a table","spread rows into columns","make a wide table"],"examinable":true,"search":"reshaping long to wide with set_index and pivot set_index → pivot(columns, values) → reset_index().rename_axis(none, axis=1)  pivot() long to wide long to wide pivot a table spread rows into columns make a wide table the `set_index` → `pivot` → `reset_index` sequence allows us to turn one row per measurement back into one column per measurement. it is useful for tables that people read, or for calculating differences between columns. alternatively, `pivot(index=...)` does it in one step."},{"sid":"B6.11","title":"Reshaping long to wide with `pivot` and `index`","cmd":"df.pivot(index='ID', columns='Exam', values='Grade')","terms":["pivot(index=)"],"tags":[],"task":["pivot long to wide","rows to columns","reshape with pivot","one row per id"],"examinable":false,"search":"reshaping long to wide with pivot and index df.pivot(index='id', columns='exam', values='grade')  pivot(index=) pivot long to wide rows to columns reshape with pivot one row per id the `pivot(index=, columns=, values=)` method allows us to reshape long to wide in one call. it is useful when there is only one identifier column. alternatively, `pivot_table` handles repeated entries by averaging them."},{"sid":"B7.01","title":"Converting a column to string type","cmd":"s.astype(\"string\")","terms":["astype('string')"],"tags":["pandas","strings","types","non-examinable"],"task":["make a column text","convert to string","numbers to text","string type"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting a column to string type s.astype(\"string\") pandas strings types non-examinable astype('string') make a column text convert to string numbers to text string type the `.astype(\"string\")` method allows us to turn a column into pandas' text type so the `.str` methods can be used. it is useful for numeric ids or codes that should be treated as text. alternatively, `.astype(str)` also works but stores the text as the older `object` type."},{"sid":"B7.02","title":"Counting characters in text","cmd":"s.str.len()","terms":["str.len()"],"tags":[],"task":["length of text","count characters","how long is each string","string length"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting characters in text s.str.len()  str.len() length of text count characters how long is each string string length the `.str.len()` method allows us to count the characters in every entry of a text column. it is useful for finding entries that are too short or long, or for checking codes have the right length. alternatively, `.apply(len)` does the same more slowly."},{"sid":"B7.03","title":"Checking what characters text contains","cmd":"s.str.isdigit(), .isalpha(), .isupper() ...","terms":["str.isdigit()","str.isalpha()"],"tags":[],"task":["is the text digits","check for letters only","test for uppercase","check characters"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking what characters text contains s.str.isdigit(), .isalpha(), .isupper() ...  str.isdigit() str.isalpha() is the text digits check for letters only test for uppercase check characters the `.str.isdigit()`, `.isalpha()` and `.isupper()` methods allow us to test what type of characters each entry contains. they are useful for finding entries that are not numbers, or are in the wrong case. alternatively, `pd.to_numeric(errors=\"coerce\")` tests whether text can be a number."},{"sid":"B7.04","title":"Stripping whitespace","cmd":"s.str.strip()","terms":["str.strip()"],"tags":[],"task":["remove spaces","trim whitespace","strip leading and trailing spaces","clean spaces"],"examinable":false,"search":"stripping whitespace s.str.strip()  str.strip() remove spaces trim whitespace strip leading and trailing spaces clean spaces the `.str.strip()` method allows us to remove spaces from the start and end of each entry. it is useful because invisible spaces stop values matching (`\"ada\"` is not `\"ada \"`). alternatively, `.lstrip()` and `.rstrip()` clean only one end."},{"sid":"B7.05","title":"Changing case","cmd":"s.str.lower(), .upper(), .title()","terms":["str.lower()","str.upper()","str.title()"],"tags":[],"task":["make lowercase","make uppercase","title case","change capitalisation"],"examinable":false,"search":"changing case s.str.lower(), .upper(), .title()  str.lower() str.upper() str.title() make lowercase make uppercase title case change capitalisation the `.str.lower()`, `.upper()` and `.title()` methods allow us to standardise the capital letters in text. they are useful so that `\"adelie\"`, `\"adelie\"` and `\"adelie\"` count as the same category. alternatively, `.str.casefold()` is a stricter lowercase for comparisons."},{"sid":"B7.06","title":"Chaining string methods","cmd":"s.str.strip().str.lower()","terms":["chaining .str methods"],"tags":["pandas","strings","chaining","cleaning","non-examinable"],"task":["clean text in one go","strip and lowercase","several string steps","chain string methods"],"examinable":false,"search":"chaining string methods s.str.strip().str.lower() pandas strings chaining cleaning non-examinable chaining .str methods clean text in one go strip and lowercase several string steps chain string methods chaining `.str` methods allows us to clean text in several steps on one line. it is useful for the usual \"strip, then fix the case\" routine. alternatively, do each step on its own line and reassign, which lets each step be checked on its own."},{"sid":"B7.07","title":"Replacing text","cmd":"s.str.replace('_', ' ', regex=False)","terms":["str.replace()"],"tags":["pandas","strings","editing","non-examinable"],"task":["replace text in a column","swap characters","replace underscores","find and replace"],"examinable":false,"search":"replacing text s.str.replace('_', ' ', regex=false) pandas strings editing non-examinable str.replace() replace text in a column swap characters replace underscores find and replace the `.str.replace()` method allows us to swap one piece of text for another in every entry. it is useful for fixing typos, removing characters or tidying column names. alternatively, `series.replace({...})` swaps *whole* values using a dictionary rather than parts of text."},{"sid":"B7.08","title":"Removing a prefix or suffix","cmd":"s.str.removeprefix(), s.str.removesuffix()","terms":["str.removeprefix()","str.removesuffix()"],"tags":[],"task":["remove a prefix","strip a suffix","cut off the start of text","remove the ending"],"examinable":false,"search":"removing a prefix or suffix s.str.removeprefix(), s.str.removesuffix()  str.removeprefix() str.removesuffix() remove a prefix strip a suffix cut off the start of text remove the ending the `.str.removeprefix()` and `.str.removesuffix()` methods allow us to cut a known start or end off each entry. they are useful for stripping units or domain names. alternatively, `.str.replace()` can do the same but would also change matches in the middle."},{"sid":"B7.09","title":"Replacing part of a string by position","cmd":"s.str.slice_replace(start, stop, repl)","terms":["str.slice_replace()"],"tags":[],"task":["replace by position","overwrite part of a string","replace characters 2 to 4"],"examinable":false,"search":"replacing part of a string by position s.str.slice_replace(start, stop, repl)  str.slice_replace() replace by position overwrite part of a string replace characters 2 to 4 the `.str.slice_replace()` method allows us to overwrite characters between two positions. it is useful for masking or fixing fixed-width codes. alternatively, slice and join with `.str[:2] + \"xx\" + .str[2:]`."},{"sid":"B7.10","title":"Joining text columns","cmd":"df['a'] + ' ' + df['b']","terms":["joining text columns"],"tags":[],"task":["join two text columns","combine first and last name","concatenate strings","add text to a column"],"examinable":false,"search":"joining text columns df['a'] + ' ' + df['b']  joining text columns join two text columns combine first and last name concatenate strings add text to a column the `+` operator on text columns allows us to join them into one. it is useful for building full names or labels from parts. alternatively, `.str.cat(other, sep=\" \")` joins with a separator in one call."},{"sid":"B7.11","title":"Testing for contained, starting and ending text","cmd":"s.str.contains(), .startswith(), .endswith()","terms":["str.contains()","str.startswith()","str.endswith()"],"tags":["pandas","strings","filtering","masks","non-examinable"],"task":["does text contain","starts with","ends with","search within text","test for a substring"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing for contained, starting and ending text s.str.contains(), .startswith(), .endswith() pandas strings filtering masks non-examinable str.contains() str.startswith() str.endswith() does text contain starts with ends with search within text test for a substring the `.str.contains()`, `.startswith()` and `.endswith()` methods allow us to test each entry for a piece of text, giving true/false. they are useful for building masks to filter on text. alternatively, `.isin([...])` tests for exact matches against a list."},{"sid":"B7.12","title":"Filtering rows by text","cmd":"df[df['col'].str.contains(pattern)]","terms":["filtering by text"],"tags":[],"task":["filter rows by text","keep rows containing a word","search a column","find rows that match text"],"examinable":false,"search":"filtering rows by text df[df['col'].str.contains(pattern)]  filtering by text filter rows by text keep rows containing a word search a column find rows that match text using `.str.contains()` as a mask allows us to keep only the rows whose text matches. it is useful for selecting records by a keyword. alternatively, `isin()` suits exact matches against a list."},{"sid":"B7.13","title":"Counting occurrences in text","cmd":"s.str.count(',')","terms":["str.count()"],"tags":[],"task":["count a character","how many commas","count occurrences of text","count words matching"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting occurrences in text s.str.count(',')  str.count() count a character how many commas count occurrences of text count words matching the `.str.count()` method allows us to count how many times a piece of text appears in each entry. it is useful for counting items in a comma-separated list. alternatively, `.str.split(\",\").str.len()` counts the parts directly."},{"sid":"B7.14","title":"Splitting into a list","cmd":"s.str.split(', ')","terms":["str.split()"],"tags":[],"task":["split text","split on a comma","break a string into parts","make a list from text"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting into a list s.str.split(', ')  str.split() split text split on a comma break a string into parts make a list from text the `.str.split()` method allows us to break each entry into pieces at a separator, giving a list. it is useful for comma-separated values packed into one cell. alternatively, `expand=true` puts the pieces in separate columns."},{"sid":"B7.15","title":"Splitting into columns","cmd":"s.str.split(' ', n=1, expand=True)","terms":["str.split(expand=True)"],"tags":["pandas","strings","splitting","non-examinable"],"task":["split into two columns","separate first and last name","split a column","expand into columns"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting into columns s.str.split(' ', n=1, expand=true) pandas strings splitting non-examinable str.split(expand=true) split into two columns separate first and last name split a column expand into columns the `expand=true` option allows us to split text into separate columns. it is useful for turning `\"ada lovelace\"` into first and last names. alternatively, `.str.extract()` with a regular expression picks out chosen parts."},{"sid":"B7.16","title":"Taking one part of a split","cmd":"s.str.split(' ', expand=True)[0]","terms":["str.split()[0]"],"tags":[],"task":["get the first word","take the nth part","keep one piece of a split"],"examinable":false,"search":"taking one part of a split s.str.split(' ', expand=true)[0]  str.split()[0] get the first word take the nth part keep one piece of a split indexing the expanded result with `[0]` allows us to keep only one piece of each split. it is useful for first names or file extensions. alternatively, `.str.split(...).str[0]` does the same without `expand`."},{"sid":"B7.17","title":"Splitting from the right","cmd":"s.str.rsplit(' ', n=1)","terms":["str.rsplit()"],"tags":[],"task":["split from the right","last word","split at the last space","rsplit"],"examinable":false,"search":"splitting from the right s.str.rsplit(' ', n=1)  str.rsplit() split from the right last word split at the last space rsplit the `.str.rsplit()` method allows us to split starting at the right-hand end. it is useful for taking off the last word or piece. alternatively, `.str.rpartition()` returns the three parts around the last separator."},{"sid":"B7.18","title":"Partitioning around a separator","cmd":"s.str.partition(': ')","terms":["str.partition()"],"tags":[],"task":["split into three parts","before and after a separator","partition text","split at the first colon"],"examinable":false,"search":"partitioning around a separator s.str.partition(': ')  str.partition() split into three parts before and after a separator partition text split at the first colon the `.str.partition()` method allows us to split each entry into three columns: before, separator, after. it is useful for `key: value` text. alternatively, `split(..., n=1, expand=true)` drops the separator column."},{"sid":"B8.01","title":"Converting text to dates","cmd":"pd.to_datetime(s)","terms":["pd.to_datetime()","datetime"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","non-examinable"],"task":["convert text to dates","make a date column","parse dates","turn strings into datetimes"],"examinable":false,"search":"converting text to dates pd.to_datetime(s) pandas datetime converting non-examinable pd.to_datetime() datetime convert text to dates make a date column parse dates turn strings into datetimes the `pd.to_datetime()` function allows us to turn text such as `12/21/2023 14:30` into real datetime values. it is useful because dates loaded from files are usually only text, which cannot be sorted by date, subtracted or plotted properly. alternatively, use `parse_dates=` in `read_csv` to convert while loading."},{"sid":"B8.02","title":"Reading day-first dates","cmd":"pd.to_datetime(s, dayfirst=True)","terms":["dayfirst=True"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","non-examinable"],"task":["dates with day first","UK date format","dd/mm/yyyy","day before month"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading day-first dates pd.to_datetime(s, dayfirst=true) pandas datetime converting non-examinable dayfirst=true dates with day first uk date format dd/mm/yyyy day before month the `dayfirst=true` option allows us to read dates written day/month/year. it is useful because pandas assumes us month-first order, so `01/02/2023` would silently become 2 january. alternatively, give an explicit `format=` such as `%d/%m/%y`."},{"sid":"B8.03","title":"Reading mixed date styles","cmd":"pd.to_datetime(s, dayfirst=True, format=\"mixed\")","terms":["format='mixed'"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","non-examinable"],"task":["mixed date formats","dates in different styles","parse messy dates"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading mixed date styles pd.to_datetime(s, dayfirst=true, format=\"mixed\") pandas datetime converting non-examinable format='mixed' mixed date formats dates in different styles parse messy dates the `format=\"mixed\"` option allows us to parse a column where each value is written in a different style. it is useful for inconsistent data from several sources. alternatively, clean the strings into one style first, or parse each style separately."},{"sid":"B8.04","title":"Giving an explicit date format","cmd":"pd.to_datetime(s, format=\"%d/%m/%Y\")","terms":["to_datetime(format=)"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","non-examinable"],"task":["specify the date format","format string for parsing","read dates with a known pattern"],"examinable":false,"search":"giving an explicit date format pd.to_datetime(s, format=\"%d/%m/%y\") pandas datetime converting non-examinable to_datetime(format=) specify the date format format string for parsing read dates with a known pattern the `format=` argument allows us to state exactly how the dates are laid out, using codes like `%d/%m/%y`. it is useful because it is unambiguous and fast. alternatively, `dayfirst=true` lets pandas guess the rest."},{"sid":"B8.05","title":"Handling invalid dates as `NaT`","cmd":"pd.to_datetime(s, errors=\"coerce\")","terms":["errors='coerce'","NaT"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","missing-data","non-examinable"],"task":["invalid dates become NaT","bad dates","ignore date errors","coerce dates"],"examinable":false,"search":"handling invalid dates as nat pd.to_datetime(s, errors=\"coerce\") pandas datetime converting missing-data non-examinable errors='coerce' nat invalid dates become nat bad dates ignore date errors coerce dates the `errors=\"coerce\"` option allows us to turn unparseable values into `nat` (missing) instead of stopping with an error. it is useful for finding or removing the bad entries in a column. alternatively, leave the default and fix the data when the error message points to it."},{"sid":"B8.06","title":"Creating a range of dates","cmd":"pd.date_range(start, periods=n, freq=code)","terms":["pd.date_range()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","creating","non-examinable"],"task":["make a sequence of dates","generate dates","daily dates","list of dates","every week"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating a range of dates pd.date_range(start, periods=n, freq=code) pandas datetime creating non-examinable pd.date_range() make a sequence of dates generate dates daily dates list of dates every week the `pd.date_range()` function allows us to generate a regular series of dates. it is useful for building a time axis or an empty calendar. alternatively, `np.arange` works on plain numbers but not on dates."},{"sid":"B8.07","title":"Creating a single date or time","cmd":"pd.Timestamp(\"YYYY-MM-DD\")","terms":["pd.Timestamp()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","creating","non-examinable"],"task":["make one date","a single timestamp","create a date value"],"examinable":false,"search":"creating a single date or time pd.timestamp(\"yyyy-mm-dd\") pandas datetime creating non-examinable pd.timestamp() make one date a single timestamp create a date value a `pd.timestamp` allows us to represent one specific moment. it is useful for comparing with a date column or marking a date on a plot. alternatively, python's `datetime.datetime` is very similar and pandas converts it automatically."},{"sid":"B8.08","title":"Reading dates when loading a CSV","cmd":"pd.read_csv(f, parse_dates=['date'], index_col='date')","terms":["parse_dates"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","loading","csv","index","non-examinable"],"task":["read dates when loading","parse dates in read_csv","date column as index when reading"],"examinable":false,"search":"reading dates when loading a csv pd.read_csv(f, parse_dates=['date'], index_col='date') pandas datetime loading csv index non-examinable parse_dates read dates when loading parse dates in read_csv date column as index when reading the `parse_dates=` argument allows us to convert date columns as the file is read. it is useful when a file has date columns, because it avoids a separate `to_datetime` step. alternatively, load the file normally and convert afterwards."},{"sid":"B8.09","title":"Checking a column is a datetime","cmd":"df.dtypes, df['date'].dtype","terms":["datetime64 dtype"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","inspecting","types","non-examinable"],"task":["is the column a date","check datetime type","are my dates text"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a column is a datetime df.dtypes, df['date'].dtype pandas datetime inspecting types non-examinable datetime64 dtype is the column a date check datetime type are my dates text the `.dtypes` attribute allows us to confirm a column is `datetime64` rather than text. it is useful before any `.dt` operation, which fails on text. alternatively, `df.info()` shows the types of all columns at once."},{"sid":"B8.10","title":"Extracting year, month and day","cmd":"s.dt.year, s.dt.month, s.dt.day","terms":[".dt.year",".dt.month",".dt.day"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","columns","new-column","non-examinable"],"task":["get the year","extract the month","get the day","split a date into parts"],"examinable":false,"search":"extracting year, month and day s.dt.year, s.dt.month, s.dt.day pandas datetime columns new-column non-examinable .dt.year .dt.month .dt.day get the year extract the month get the day split a date into parts the `.dt` accessor allows us to extract the year, month and day of every date as numbers. it is useful for grouping, filtering by season, or making new columns. alternatively, with a datetime index use `df.index.year` and so on."},{"sid":"B8.11","title":"Getting weekday and month names","cmd":"s.dt.day_name(), s.dt.month_name(), s.dt.dayofweek","terms":[".dt.day_name()",".dt.month_name()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","columns","non-examinable"],"task":["day of the week","name of the month","weekday name","is it a weekend"],"examinable":false,"search":"getting weekday and month names s.dt.day_name(), s.dt.month_name(), s.dt.dayofweek pandas datetime columns non-examinable .dt.day_name() .dt.month_name() day of the week name of the month weekday name is it a weekend the `.dt.day_name()` and `.dt.month_name()` methods allow us to get the weekday or month as words. they are useful for labels in tables and charts. alternatively, `.dt.dayofweek` gives a number (monday = 0) that sorts correctly."},{"sid":"B8.12","title":"Extracting hours, minutes and day of year","cmd":"s.dt.hour, s.dt.minute, s.dt.dayofyear","terms":[".dt.hour",".dt.minute",".dt.dayofyear"],"tags":[],"task":["get the hour","get the minute","day of the year","time of day"],"examinable":false,"search":"extracting hours, minutes and day of year s.dt.hour, s.dt.minute, s.dt.dayofyear  .dt.hour .dt.minute .dt.dayofyear get the hour get the minute day of the year time of day the `.dt.hour`, `.dt.minute` and `.dt.dayofyear` attributes allow us to extract finer parts of a timestamp. they are useful for questions like \"is the hour after midday?\" or comparing years day by day. alternatively, `strftime` can produce them as text."},{"sid":"B8.13","title":"Formatting dates as text","cmd":"s.dt.strftime(\"%d %b %Y\")","terms":[".dt.strftime()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["dates to text","format a date","show a date as dd Mon yyyy","strftime"],"examinable":false,"search":"formatting dates as text s.dt.strftime(\"%d %b %y\") pandas datetime converting strings non-examinable .dt.strftime() dates to text format a date show a date as dd mon yyyy strftime the `.dt.strftime()` method allows us to convert dates into text laid out the way we want. it is useful for report tables and chart labels. alternatively, matplotlib's `dateformatter` formats the axis directly."},{"sid":"B8.14","title":"Formatting codes for dates and times","cmd":"%d %a %A | %m %b %B | %y %Y | %H %M %S","terms":["strftime codes (%d %m %Y)"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","converting","strings","non-examinable"],"task":["date format codes","what does %d mean","strftime codes","%Y %m %d"],"examinable":false,"search":"formatting codes for dates and times %d %a %a | %m %b %b | %y %y | %h %m %s pandas datetime converting strings non-examinable strftime codes (%d %m %y) date format codes what does %d mean strftime codes %y %m %d format codes such as `%d`, `%b` and `%y` allow us to describe a date layout. they are useful because the same codes are used both to read dates (`format=`) and to write them (`strftime`). alternatively, look them up in the python `datetime` documentation."},{"sid":"B8.15","title":"Subtracting dates","cmd":"d2 - d1, (d2 - d1).dt.days","terms":["date subtraction","Timedelta"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","arithmetic","non-examinable"],"task":["days between two dates","difference between dates","how long between","subtract dates"],"examinable":false,"search":"subtracting dates d2 - d1, (d2 - d1).dt.days pandas datetime arithmetic non-examinable date subtraction timedelta days between two dates difference between dates how long between subtract dates subtracting two dates allows us to calculate how much time lies between them, as a `timedelta`. it is useful for durations between two dates. alternatively, `.diff()` gives the gap between consecutive rows."},{"sid":"B8.16","title":"Adding or subtracting time","cmd":"d + pd.Timedelta(days=7), d + pd.DateOffset(months=1)","terms":["pd.Timedelta()","pd.DateOffset()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","arithmetic","non-examinable"],"task":["add days to a date","add a month","move a date forward","subtract a week"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding or subtracting time d + pd.timedelta(days=7), d + pd.dateoffset(months=1) pandas datetime arithmetic non-examinable pd.timedelta() pd.dateoffset() add days to a date add a month move a date forward subtract a week `pd.timedelta` and `pd.dateoffset` allow us to move a date forwards or backwards. they are useful for deadlines and follow-up dates. alternatively, `pd.date_range` generates a whole series of dates."},{"sid":"B8.17","title":"Adding days from a column","cmd":"df['date'] + pd.to_timedelta(df['delivery_days'], unit='D')","terms":["pd.to_timedelta()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","arithmetic","columns","non-examinable"],"task":["add a number of days from a column","delivery date","date plus days","add timedelta"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding days from a column df['date'] + pd.to_timedelta(df['delivery_days'], unit='d') pandas datetime arithmetic columns non-examinable pd.to_timedelta() add a number of days from a column delivery date date plus days add timedelta the `pd.to_timedelta()` function allows us to turn a column of numbers into durations that can be added to dates. it is useful when each row has its own number of days. alternatively, use `pd.timedelta(days=5)` when the same duration applies to every row."},{"sid":"B8.18","title":"Turning timedeltas into numbers","cmd":"(d2 - d1).dt.days, .dt.total_seconds()","terms":[".dt.days",".dt.total_seconds()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","arithmetic","non-examinable"],"task":["timedelta to days","convert a duration to numbers","total seconds","time difference as a number"],"examinable":false,"search":"turning timedeltas into numbers (d2 - d1).dt.days, .dt.total_seconds() pandas datetime arithmetic non-examinable .dt.days .dt.total_seconds() timedelta to days convert a duration to numbers total seconds time difference as a number the `.dt.days` and `.dt.total_seconds()` attributes allow us to convert a duration into an ordinary number. they are useful because calculations and plots cannot use \"3 days\" directly. alternatively, divide by `pd.timedelta(days=1)` to get fractional days."},{"sid":"B8.19","title":"Filtering rows by date","cmd":"df[df['date'] >= 'YYYY-MM-DD']","terms":["filtering by date"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","filtering","masks","non-examinable"],"task":["filter by date","rows after a date","rows between dates","keep recent rows"],"examinable":false,"search":"filtering rows by date df[df['date'] >= 'yyyy-mm-dd'] pandas datetime filtering masks non-examinable filtering by date filter by date rows after a date rows between dates keep recent rows comparison operators allow us to filter rows by date, using timestamps or `yyyy-mm-dd` text. it is useful for choosing a period of interest. alternatively, `.between()` or slicing on a datetime index does the same."},{"sid":"B8.20","title":"Sorting by date","cmd":"df.sort_values('date')","terms":["sorting by date"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","sorting","non-examinable"],"task":["sort by date","oldest first","order by time","chronological order"],"examinable":false,"search":"sorting by date df.sort_values('date') pandas datetime sorting non-examinable sorting by date sort by date oldest first order by time chronological order the `sort_values()` method allows us to put rows in chronological order. it is useful before plotting or slicing a time series. alternatively, `sort_index()` sorts when the date is the index."},{"sid":"B8.21","title":"Rounding dates to a period","cmd":"s.dt.to_period(\"M\"), s.dt.floor(\"D\")","terms":[".dt.to_period()",".dt.floor()"],"tags":[],"task":["round dates to the month","dates to periods","floor a timestamp","month of each date"],"examinable":false,"search":"rounding dates to a period s.dt.to_period(\"m\"), s.dt.floor(\"d\")  .dt.to_period() .dt.floor() round dates to the month dates to periods floor a timestamp month of each date the `.dt.to_period()` and `.dt.floor()` methods allow us to reduce a timestamp to its month or day. they are useful for grouping data by period. alternatively, `resample` works on a datetime index."},{"sid":"B8.22","title":"Using dates as the index","cmd":"df.set_index('date')","terms":["datetime index"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","index","set-index","non-examinable"],"task":["use dates as the index","time series index","make the date the row label"],"examinable":false,"search":"using dates as the index df.set_index('date') pandas datetime index set-index non-examinable datetime index use dates as the index time series index make the date the row label the `set_index()` method allows us to make the date column the row labels. it is useful because a datetime index enables date slicing, `resample` and `rolling`. alternatively, `index_col` and `parse_dates` do it while loading."},{"sid":"B8.23","title":"Selecting rows by date text","cmd":"df.loc['YYYY-MM'], df.loc['YYYY-MM':'YYYY-MM']","terms":["slicing by date text"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","selecting","loc","index","non-examinable"],"task":["select a month","rows in a given year","slice by date","get data for one month"],"examinable":false,"search":"selecting rows by date text df.loc['yyyy-mm'], df.loc['yyyy-mm':'yyyy-mm'] pandas datetime selecting loc index non-examinable slicing by date text select a month rows in a given year slice by date get data for one month partial date text inside `.loc[]` allows us to select a whole year, a month or a date range. it is useful for quick time slices. alternatively, a comparison mask on a date column does the same."},{"sid":"B8.24","title":"Resampling to another frequency","cmd":"df.resample('W').mean()","terms":["resample()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","resample","summary","non-examinable"],"task":["weekly averages","monthly totals","change the frequency","downsample","resample"],"examinable":false,"search":"resampling to another frequency df.resample('w').mean() pandas datetime resample summary non-examinable resample() weekly averages monthly totals change the frequency downsample resample the `.resample()` method allows us to summarise data into weekly, monthly or yearly values. it is useful for smoothing noisy daily data. alternatively, `groupby` on the month or year gives a similar result."},{"sid":"B8.25","title":"Calculating changes between rows","cmd":"s.diff(), s.pct_change(), s.shift(1)","terms":["diff()","pct_change()","shift()"],"tags":[],"task":["change from the previous row","percentage change","difference between rows","shift a column","lag"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating changes between rows s.diff(), s.pct_change(), s.shift(1)  diff() pct_change() shift() change from the previous row percentage change difference between rows shift a column lag the `.diff()`, `.pct_change()` and `.shift()` methods allow us to compare each value with the previous one. they are useful for growth and day-to-day change. alternatively, `s - s.shift(1)` gives the same result and shows what `diff()` does."},{"sid":"B8.26","title":"Calculating a rolling average","cmd":"s.rolling(7).mean()","terms":["rolling()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","rolling","summary","non-examinable"],"task":["moving average","smooth a series","rolling mean","seven day average"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating a rolling average s.rolling(7).mean() pandas datetime rolling summary non-examinable rolling() moving average smooth a series rolling mean seven day average the `.rolling(n).mean()` method allows us to average each value with its neighbours. it is useful for smoothing out day-to-day noise to show a trend. alternatively, `resample` summarises by calendar period instead."},{"sid":"B8.27","title":"Grouping by month","cmd":"df.groupby(df['date'].dt.month)['temp_c'].mean()","terms":["grouping by month"],"tags":[],"task":["average by month","group by month","monthly pattern","seasonal means"],"examinable":false,"search":"grouping by month df.groupby(df['date'].dt.month)['temp_c'].mean()  grouping by month average by month group by month monthly pattern seasonal means grouping by `.dt.month` allows us to summarise data for each month of the year. it is useful for seasonal patterns. alternatively, `resample('me')` groups by each calendar month of each year separately."},{"sid":"B8.28","title":"Plotting a time series","cmd":"plt.plot(df['date'], df['temp_c'])","terms":["time series plot"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","matplotlib","line-plot","non-examinable"],"task":["plot over time","time series plot","line chart of dates","plot a column against date"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting a time series plt.plot(df['date'], df['temp_c']) pandas datetime plotting matplotlib line-plot non-examinable time series plot plot over time time series plot line chart of dates plot a column against date passing a date column to `plt.plot` allows us to draw how a measurement changes over time. it is useful for any data collected through time. alternatively, `df.plot()` or `sns.lineplot` do the same."},{"sid":"B8.29","title":"Rotating the date labels","cmd":"plt.xticks(rotation=angle), fig.autofmt_xdate()","terms":["autofmt_xdate()","rotating labels"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","matplotlib","labels","non-examinable"],"task":["rotate date labels","dates overlap on the axis","tilt the x labels","fix crowded dates"],"examinable":false,"search":"rotating the date labels plt.xticks(rotation=angle), fig.autofmt_xdate() pandas datetime plotting matplotlib labels non-examinable autofmt_xdate() rotating labels rotate date labels dates overlap on the axis tilt the x labels fix crowded dates the `plt.xticks(rotation=…)` function or `fig.autofmt_xdate()` allows us to stop long date labels overlapping. it is useful whenever dates fill the axis. alternatively, a shorter date format with `dateformatter` makes labels narrower."},{"sid":"B8.30","title":"Formatting the date axis","cmd":"ax.xaxis.set_major_locator(mdates.MonthLocator()), DateFormatter","terms":["matplotlib.dates","DateFormatter"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","matplotlib","ticks","non-examinable"],"task":["format the date axis","show months on the axis","tick every month","date tick labels"],"examinable":false,"search":"formatting the date axis ax.xaxis.set_major_locator(mdates.monthlocator()), dateformatter pandas datetime plotting matplotlib ticks non-examinable matplotlib.dates dateformatter format the date axis show months on the axis tick every month date tick labels locators and formatters from `matplotlib.dates` allow us to choose where ticks appear and how they read. they are useful for tidy monthly or quarterly axes. alternatively, accept the automatic ticks for a quick look."},{"sid":"B8.31","title":"Plotting a time series with seaborn","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='date', y='temp_c')","terms":["sns.lineplot() with dates"],"tags":[],"task":["seaborn time series","lineplot with dates","plot dates with seaborn"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting a time series with seaborn sns.lineplot(data=df, x='date', y='temp_c')  sns.lineplot() with dates seaborn time series lineplot with dates plot dates with seaborn the `sns.lineplot()` function allows us to draw a time series from a dataframe in one line. it is useful for matching the style of other seaborn plots. alternatively, `plt.plot` or `df.plot()` give the same picture."},{"sid":"B8.32","title":"Plotting daily and monthly values together","cmd":"df['col'].resample('ME').mean()","terms":["resampled plot"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","resample","matplotlib","legend","non-examinable"],"task":["daily and monthly on one plot","plot averages with raw data","overlay a resampled line"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting daily and monthly values together df['col'].resample('me').mean() pandas datetime plotting resample matplotlib legend non-examinable resampled plot daily and monthly on one plot plot averages with raw data overlay a resampled line if we have a long noisy series, then resampling it and plotting the summary over the raw values shows the trend without hiding the detail. it is useful for readable time series plots. alternatively, plot only the summary if the raw data are too dense."},{"sid":"B8.33","title":"Plotting a rolling average","cmd":"plt.plot(df.index, df['col'].rolling(n).mean())","terms":["rolling mean plot"],"tags":[],"task":["plot a moving average","smooth line over data","rolling mean on a plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting a rolling average plt.plot(df.index, df['col'].rolling(n).mean())  rolling mean plot plot a moving average smooth line over data rolling mean on a plot plotting `.rolling().mean()` over the raw values allows us to show a smoothed trend line. it is useful for noisy series. alternatively, `resample` gives non-overlapping period averages."},{"sid":"B8.34","title":"Plotting a time series with pandas","cmd":"df['col'].plot()","terms":["Series.plot()"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","line-plot","non-examinable"],"task":["quick time series plot","plot a column from pandas","df plot over time"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting a time series with pandas df['col'].plot() pandas datetime plotting line-plot non-examinable series.plot() quick time series plot plot a column from pandas df plot over time the `.plot()` method on a series with a datetime index allows us to draw a time series without writing any matplotlib. it is useful for quick checks. alternatively, use matplotlib directly when more control is needed."},{"sid":"B8.35","title":"Plotting monthly totals as bars","cmd":"monthly.plot.bar()","terms":["monthly totals bar chart"],"tags":[],"task":["bar chart of monthly totals","monthly bars","totals per month plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting monthly totals as bars monthly.plot.bar()  monthly totals bar chart bar chart of monthly totals monthly bars totals per month plot the `.plot.bar()` method allows us to show totals per month as bars. it is useful for amounts that accumulate over time. alternatively, `plt.bar` or `sns.barplot` give the same chart."},{"sid":"B8.36","title":"Marking a date on a plot","cmd":"plt.axvline(pd.Timestamp(\"YYYY-MM-DD\"))","terms":["axvline() with dates"],"tags":[],"task":["mark a date on a plot","vertical line at a date","show an event on a timeline"],"examinable":false,"search":"marking a date on a plot plt.axvline(pd.timestamp(\"yyyy-mm-dd\"))  axvline() with dates mark a date on a plot vertical line at a date show an event on a timeline the `plt.axvline()` function allows us to draw a vertical line at a chosen date. it is useful for marking an event on a time axis. alternatively, `plt.axvspan` shades a period instead."},{"sid":"B8.37","title":"Shading a period","cmd":"plt.axvspan(start, end, alpha=0.3)","terms":["axvspan()"],"tags":[],"task":["shade a period","highlight a date range","shaded band on a time series"],"examinable":false,"search":"shading a period plt.axvspan(start, end, alpha=0.3)  axvspan() shade a period highlight a date range shaded band on a time series the `plt.axvspan()` function allows us to shade a stretch of time on a plot. it is useful for highlighting a season or an intervention period. alternatively, `plt.axvline` marks a single date."},{"sid":"B8.38","title":"Plotting months as box plots","cmd":"sns.boxplot(data=df, x='month', y='temp_c')","terms":["seasonal box plot"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","seaborn","boxplot","non-examinable"],"task":["box plot by month","monthly spread","compare months","seasonal box plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting months as box plots sns.boxplot(data=df, x='month', y='temp_c') pandas datetime plotting seaborn boxplot non-examinable seasonal box plot box plot by month monthly spread compare months seasonal box plot if we have dates and a measurement, then extracting the month and plotting it as a category compares the spread for each month. it is useful for seasonal patterns. alternatively, `groupby(...).mean()` shows only the average."},{"sid":"B8.39","title":"Plotting one line per year","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='dayofyear', y='temp_c', hue='year')","terms":["one line per year"],"tags":["pandas","datetime","plotting","seaborn","hue","non-examinable"],"task":["one line per year","compare years","overlay years","seasonal lines"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting one line per year sns.lineplot(data=df, x='dayofyear', y='temp_c', hue='year') pandas datetime plotting seaborn hue non-examinable one line per year one line per year compare years overlay years seasonal lines if we have several years of daily data, then plotting day-of-year with `hue=year` overlays the years on one axis. it is useful for comparing the same season across years. alternatively, use separate subplots, one per year."},{"sid":"B8.40","title":"Plotting the weekday pattern","cmd":"df.groupby('weekday')['visits'].mean().plot.bar()","terms":["weekday pattern"],"tags":[],"task":["average by weekday","weekday pattern","busiest day","bar chart by day of week"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting the weekday pattern df.groupby('weekday')['visits'].mean().plot.bar()  weekday pattern average by weekday weekday pattern busiest day bar chart by day of week if we have dated counts, then grouping by weekday name and plotting the mean shows how a quantity varies through the week. it is useful for visits, sales or traffic data. alternatively, a box plot per weekday shows the spread as well as the mean."},{"sid":"B9.01","title":"Grouping and summarising with `groupby`","cmd":"df.groupby('group')['col'].mean()","terms":["groupby()"],"tags":[],"task":["group by","average per group","mean by category","split apply combine","summarise by group"],"examinable":false,"search":"grouping and summarising with groupby df.groupby('group')['col'].mean()  groupby() group by average per group mean by category split apply combine summarise by group the `groupby()` method allows us to split rows into groups, calculate a statistic for each group and combine the results in one table. it is useful for comparing categories in one line. alternatively, build a mask for each group (see the exploring sheet) when only one or two groups are needed."},{"sid":"B9.02","title":"Grouping by several columns","cmd":"df.groupby(['group_1', 'group_2'])['col'].mean()","terms":["groupby() with several columns"],"tags":[],"task":["group by two columns","average by two categories","summary by two keys"],"examinable":false,"search":"grouping by several columns df.groupby(['group_1', 'group_2'])['col'].mean()  groupby() with several columns group by two columns average by two categories summary by two keys passing a list to `groupby` allows us to split by a combination of columns, such as species *and* sex. it is useful when two factors both matter. alternatively, `pd.pivot_table` lays the same numbers out as a grid."},{"sid":"B9.03","title":"Counting rows per group","cmd":"g.size(), g['c'].count()","terms":["size()","count() in groupby"],"tags":["pandas","groupby","counting","missing-data","non-examinable"],"task":["count rows in each group","how many per group","group sizes"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting rows per group g.size(), g['c'].count() pandas groupby counting missing-data non-examinable size() count() in groupby count rows in each group how many per group group sizes the `.size()` and `.count()` methods allow us to count the rows in each group. they are useful for sample sizes. alternatively, `value_counts()` counts a single column without `groupby`."},{"sid":"B9.04","title":"Calculating several statistics per group","cmd":"g['c'].agg(['mean', 'std', 'count'])","terms":["agg()"],"tags":[],"task":["several statistics per group","mean and std by group","agg with a list","summary by group"],"examinable":false,"search":"calculating several statistics per group g['c'].agg(['mean', 'std', 'count'])  agg() several statistics per group mean and std by group agg with a list summary by group the `.agg()` method allows us to calculate several statistics per group in one go. it is useful for building a summary table with mean, spread and count. alternatively, call each statistic separately and combine with `pd.concat`."},{"sid":"B9.05","title":"Using named aggregation","cmd":"agg(new_name=('col', 'mean'))","terms":["named aggregation"],"tags":[],"task":["name the aggregate columns","named aggregation","rename after groupby","custom column names for summaries"],"examinable":false,"search":"using named aggregation agg(new_name=('col', 'mean'))  named aggregation name the aggregate columns named aggregation rename after groupby custom column names for summaries named aggregation allows us to calculate different statistics for different columns and name the result columns. it is useful for tidy, report-ready tables. alternatively, pass a dictionary: `agg({\"col\": \"mean\"})`."},{"sid":"B9.06","title":"Returning a flat table from groupby","cmd":"as_index=False, .reset_index()","terms":["as_index=False"],"tags":[],"task":["groupby result as a normal table","keep the group as a column","as_index false","flatten groupby"],"examinable":false,"search":"returning a flat table from groupby as_index=false, .reset_index()  as_index=false groupby result as a normal table keep the group as a column as_index false flatten groupby the `as_index=false` option allows us to keep the group names as an ordinary column rather than the index. it is useful for plotting with seaborn or merging with other tables. alternatively, call `.reset_index()` on the result."},{"sid":"B9.07","title":"Summarising by two groups","cmd":"df.groupby(['group_1', 'group_2'])['col'].agg(funcs)","terms":["groupby two columns"],"tags":[],"task":["summary by two groups","two-way group statistics","several statistics for two keys"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising by two groups df.groupby(['group_1', 'group_2'])['col'].agg(funcs)  groupby two columns summary by two groups two-way group statistics several statistics for two keys grouping by two columns and aggregating allows us to reproduce the \"species by sex\" comparison in one line. it is useful for comparing every combination of two categories. alternatively, `pivot_table` shows one statistic in a grid."},{"sid":"B9.08","title":"Summarising into a grid with `pivot_table`","cmd":"pd.pivot_table(df, index='col_1', columns='col_2', values='col_3', aggfunc='mean')","terms":["pivot_table()","pd.pivot_table()"],"tags":[],"task":["pivot table","excel style pivot","grid of group means","cross summary"],"examinable":false,"search":"summarising into a grid with pivot_table pd.pivot_table(df, index='col_1', columns='col_2', values='col_3', aggfunc='mean')  pivot_table() pd.pivot_table() pivot table excel style pivot grid of group means cross summary the `pd.pivot_table()` function allows us to summarise a value across two categories laid out as a grid. it is useful for cross-tab style reports, and, unlike `pivot`, it can combine repeated entries. alternatively, `groupby(...).mean().unstack()` gives the same layout."},{"sid":"B9.09","title":"Adding a group statistic to every row","cmd":"g['c'].transform('mean')","terms":["transform()"],"tags":[],"task":["add the group mean to each row","transform","group statistic per row","compare each row to its group"],"examinable":false,"search":"adding a group statistic to every row g['c'].transform('mean')  transform() add the group mean to each row transform group statistic per row compare each row to its group the `.transform()` method allows us to calculate a group statistic and give it back with the *same length* as the original, so it can be added as a column. it is useful for deviations from a group mean. alternatively, merge a `groupby` result back onto the table."},{"sid":"B9.10","title":"Looping over groups","cmd":"for name, df_group in df.groupby('group'):","terms":["looping over groupby"],"tags":[],"task":["loop over groups","do something for each group","for each category","iterate a groupby"],"examinable":false,"search":"looping over groups for name, df_group in df.groupby('group'):  looping over groupby loop over groups do something for each group for each category iterate a groupby looping over a `groupby` object allows us to handle each group as its own small dataframe. it is useful for per-group plots or tests. alternatively, `.apply(func)` runs a function on every group."},{"sid":"B9.11","title":"Mapping values with a dictionary or function","cmd":"s.map(dict_or_func)","terms":["map()"],"tags":["pandas","apply","dictionaries","non-examinable"],"task":["map values","replace with a dictionary","recode with a function","lookup values from a dict"],"examinable":false,"search":"mapping values with a dictionary or function s.map(dict_or_func) pandas apply dictionaries non-examinable map() map values replace with a dictionary recode with a function lookup values from a dict the `.map()` method allows us to replace each value using a dictionary or a function. it is useful for recoding categories. alternatively, `.replace()` leaves unmatched values unchanged."},{"sid":"B9.12","title":"Applying a function","cmd":"s.apply(func), df.apply(func, axis=1)","terms":["apply()","lambda"],"tags":["pandas","apply","functions","non-examinable"],"task":["apply a function","use my own function on a column","row by row calculation","apply"],"examinable":false,"search":"applying a function s.apply(func), df.apply(func, axis=1) pandas apply functions non-examinable apply() lambda apply a function use my own function on a column row by row calculation apply the `.apply()` method allows us to run any function on every value, or on every row with `axis=1`. it is useful when no built-in method does the job. alternatively, vectorised operations (`df['c'] / 1000`) are shorter and much faster."},{"sid":"C1.01","title":"Choosing between `plt` and `ax`","cmd":"plt.title(...)  <->  ax.set_title(...)","terms":["plt vs ax","object-oriented plotting","ax.set_title()"],"tags":["matplotlib","labels","subplots","style","non-examinable"],"task":["plt or ax","two plotting styles","pyplot versus axes","which plotting interface"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing between plt and ax plt.title(...)  <->  ax.set_title(...) matplotlib labels subplots style non-examinable plt vs ax object-oriented plotting ax.set_title() plt or ax two plotting styles pyplot versus axes which plotting interface matplotlib can be driven in two equivalent ways: `plt.` commands act on \"the current plot\", while `ax.` methods act on an axes object created with `fig, ax = plt.subplots()`. it is useful when reading other people's code, because notes, forums and seaborn mix both styles, and the `ax.` style is needed for multi-panel figures."},{"sid":"C1.02","title":"Drawing a simple line plot","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y)","terms":["plt.plot()","line plot"],"tags":["matplotlib","line-plot"],"task":["line plot","plot x against y","draw a line graph","basic plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a simple line plot plt.plot(x, y) matplotlib line-plot plt.plot() line plot line plot plot x against y draw a line graph basic plot the `plt.plot(x, y)` function allows us to draw a line through a set of points. it is useful for showing how one quantity changes with another. alternatively, `sns.lineplot` or `df.plot()` draw from a dataframe."},{"sid":"C1.03","title":"Plotting markers only","cmd":"plt.plot(x, y, 'x')  'o'  '^'  '.'  's'","terms":["markers","'o' 's' '^'"],"tags":["matplotlib","line-plot","markers","style"],"task":["points only","plot with markers","no line between points","crosses and circles"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting markers only plt.plot(x, y, 'x')  'o'  '^'  '.'  's' matplotlib line-plot markers style markers 'o' 's' '^' points only plot with markers no line between points crosses and circles a format string such as `'o'` or `'s'` allows us to draw individual points instead of a connected line. it is useful for measurements, where joining the dots would suggest values in between. alternatively, `plt.scatter` draws points with more options."},{"sid":"C1.04","title":"Styling a line with a format string","cmd":"'bx-'  'r+-'  'go-'  'm.--'  'k^:'","terms":["format strings ('bx-')"],"tags":["matplotlib","line-plot","style"],"task":["colour and marker shorthand","format string","red dashed line","style in one string"],"examinable":true,"search":"styling a line with a format string 'bx-'  'r+-'  'go-'  'm.--'  'k^:' matplotlib line-plot style format strings ('bx-') colour and marker shorthand format string red dashed line style in one string a format string combining a colour letter, a marker and a line style allows us to style a line in three characters, such as `'bx-'`. it is useful for quick, compact styling. alternatively, keyword arguments (`color=`, `marker=`, `linestyle=`) name each choice and allow more options."},{"sid":"C1.05","title":"Styling a line with keyword arguments","cmd":"color=c, linestyle=style, linewidth=w, markersize=m, alpha=a","terms":["color=","linestyle=","linewidth="],"tags":[],"task":["line colour","line width","dashed line","marker size","style a line"],"examinable":true,"search":"styling a line with keyword arguments color=c, linestyle=style, linewidth=w, markersize=m, alpha=a  color= linestyle= linewidth= line colour line width dashed line marker size style a line keyword arguments such as `color=`, `linestyle=`, `linewidth=` and `markersize=` allow us to style a line by name. they are useful when full control and readable code are needed. alternatively, a format string is quicker for simple styles."},{"sid":"C1.06","title":"Adding a legend for several lines","cmd":"plt.plot(..., label=text); plt.legend()","terms":["label=","plt.legend()"],"tags":["matplotlib","line-plot","legend"],"task":["add a legend","label the lines","name each line","key for several lines"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a legend for several lines plt.plot(..., label=text); plt.legend() matplotlib line-plot legend label= plt.legend() add a legend label the lines name each line key for several lines the `label=` argument with `plt.legend()` allows us to name each line and show a key. it is useful whenever a plot has more than one line. alternatively, in seaborn `hue=` creates the legend automatically."},{"sid":"C1.07","title":"Moving the legend","cmd":"plt.legend(loc='upper left')","terms":["legend(loc=)"],"tags":["matplotlib","legend","non-examinable"],"task":["move the legend","legend position","legend in the corner","legend outside"],"examinable":false,"search":"moving the legend plt.legend(loc='upper left') matplotlib legend non-examinable legend(loc=) move the legend legend position legend in the corner legend outside the `loc=` argument of `plt.legend()` allows us to place the key where it does not cover the data. it is useful for crowded plots. alternatively, `loc=\"best\"` lets matplotlib choose."},{"sid":"C1.08","title":"Adding a title and axis labels","cmd":"plt.title(), plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel()","terms":["plt.title()","plt.xlabel()","plt.ylabel()"],"tags":["matplotlib","labels"],"task":["add a title","label the axes","axis titles","x label and y label"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a title and axis labels plt.title(), plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel() matplotlib labels plt.title() plt.xlabel() plt.ylabel() add a title label the axes axis titles x label and y label the functions `plt.title()`, `plt.xlabel()` and `plt.ylabel()` allow us to say what a plot shows and what the axes mean. they are useful for *every* plot that is presented, with units where relevant. alternatively, `ax.set(title=..., xlabel=..., ylabel=...)` sets all three at once."},{"sid":"C1.09","title":"Annotating with text","cmd":"plt.text(x, y, \"text\")","terms":["plt.text()"],"tags":[],"task":["write text on a plot","annotate a point","add a label inside the graph"],"examinable":true,"search":"annotating with text plt.text(x, y, \"text\")  plt.text() write text on a plot annotate a point add a label inside the graph the `plt.text(x, y, \"text\")` function allows us to write a label at a chosen position in data coordinates. it is useful for equations, a note on an outlier or a value. alternatively, `plt.annotate` can add an arrow."},{"sid":"C1.10","title":"Setting axis limits","cmd":"plt.xlim(low, high), plt.ylim(low, high)","terms":["plt.xlim()","plt.ylim()"],"tags":["matplotlib","axes"],"task":["change the axis range","zoom in","set limits","start the axis at zero"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting axis limits plt.xlim(low, high), plt.ylim(low, high) matplotlib axes plt.xlim() plt.ylim() change the axis range zoom in set limits start the axis at zero the `plt.xlim()` and `plt.ylim()` functions allow us to choose the range shown on each axis. they are useful for zooming in or making several plots comparable. alternatively, `ax.set_xlim()` does the same on an axes object."},{"sid":"C1.11","title":"Setting tick positions","cmd":"plt.xticks(positions)","terms":["plt.xticks()","plt.yticks()"],"tags":["matplotlib","axes","ticks"],"task":["choose tick marks","set tick positions","change axis ticks","tick labels"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting tick positions plt.xticks(positions) matplotlib axes ticks plt.xticks() plt.yticks() choose tick marks set tick positions change axis ticks tick labels the `plt.xticks()` and `plt.yticks()` functions allow us to choose exactly where tick marks and labels appear. they are useful for neat, regular scales. alternatively, `plt.locator_params(nbins=…)` sets how many ticks there are."},{"sid":"C1.12","title":"Adding a grid","cmd":"plt.grid()","terms":["plt.grid()"],"tags":["matplotlib","axes"],"task":["add grid lines","show a grid","background lines"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a grid plt.grid() matplotlib axes plt.grid() add grid lines show a grid background lines the `plt.grid()` function allows us to draw light grid lines behind the data. it is useful for reading values off a plot. alternatively, `sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")` adds grid lines to every plot."},{"sid":"C1.13","title":"Using a log scale","cmd":"plt.yscale('log'), plt.xscale('log')","terms":["plt.yscale('log')"],"tags":["matplotlib","axes","log-scale"],"task":["log scale","logarithmic axis","log axis","wide range of values"],"examinable":true,"search":"using a log scale plt.yscale('log'), plt.xscale('log') matplotlib axes log-scale plt.yscale('log') log scale logarithmic axis log axis wide range of values the `plt.yscale('log')` and `plt.xscale('log')` functions allow us to use a logarithmic axis. it is useful for data spanning many orders of magnitude, or for checking exponential growth (a straight line). alternatively, plot `np.log10(y)` and label the axis accordingly."},{"sid":"C1.14","title":"Drawing a horizontal line","cmd":"plt.axhline(0, color='black', linestyle='--')","terms":["plt.axhline()"],"tags":[],"task":["horizontal reference line","line at zero","draw across the plot","axhline"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a horizontal line plt.axhline(0, color='black', linestyle='--')  plt.axhline() horizontal reference line line at zero draw across the plot axhline the `plt.axhline()` function allows us to draw a horizontal line across the whole plot. it is useful for zero lines, targets or a carrying capacity. alternatively, `plt.plot([x0, x1], [y, y])` draws a line between two chosen points."},{"sid":"C1.15","title":"Drawing a vertical line","cmd":"plt.axvline(x)","terms":["plt.axvline()"],"tags":[],"task":["vertical reference line","mark a value on the x axis","axvline"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a vertical line plt.axvline(x)  plt.axvline() vertical reference line mark a value on the x axis axvline the `plt.axvline()` function allows us to draw a vertical line at a chosen x value. it is useful for marking a mean, a threshold or an observed value. alternatively, `plt.axvspan` shades a whole range."},{"sid":"C1.16","title":"Drawing a line y = mx + c","cmd":"plt.axline((0, c), slope=m)","terms":["plt.axline()","y = mx + c","slope and intercept"],"tags":["matplotlib","reference-line","regression","non-examinable"],"task":["draw y = mx + c","line with a slope","line through a point with a gradient"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a line y = mx + c plt.axline((0, c), slope=m) matplotlib reference-line regression non-examinable plt.axline() y = mx + c slope and intercept draw y = mx + c line with a slope line through a point with a gradient the `plt.axline` function allows us to draw a straight line of unlimited length through a point with a given slope. it is useful for adding a fitted line or a reference line `y = mx + c` to a plot. alternatively, calculate `y = m * x + c` for an array of x values and draw the points with `plt.plot`."},{"sid":"C1.17","title":"Drawing a line between two points","cmd":"plt.plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1], 'r-')","terms":["plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1])"],"tags":[],"task":["line between two points","draw a segment","connect two points"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a line between two points plt.plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1], 'r-')  plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1]) line between two points draw a segment connect two points if we know two points on a line, then giving `plt.plot` two x values and two y values draws the segment between them. it is useful for drawing a fitted line from its end points. alternatively, `sns.regplot` draws the fitted line automatically."},{"sid":"C1.18","title":"Setting figure size and resolution","cmd":"plt.figure(figsize=(width, height), dpi=n)","terms":["figsize","dpi"],"tags":["matplotlib","figure"],"task":["bigger figure","figure size","resolution","dpi","change plot size"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting figure size and resolution plt.figure(figsize=(width, height), dpi=n) matplotlib figure figsize dpi bigger figure figure size resolution dpi change plot size the `figsize` and `dpi` arguments of `plt.figure()` allow us to choose how big and how sharp a figure is. they are useful for fitting a figure into a report or making a slide readable. alternatively, `plt.subplots(figsize=...)` sets the size when the axes are created."},{"sid":"C1.19","title":"Changing the plot defaults","cmd":"plt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = (width, height)","terms":["rcParams"],"tags":[],"task":["change the defaults","set default figure size","rcParams","apply to every plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"changing the plot defaults plt.rcparams['figure.figsize'] = (width, height)  rcparams change the defaults set default figure size rcparams apply to every plot the `plt.rcparams` dictionary allows us to change default settings, such as figure size, for every later plot. it is useful at the top of a notebook so that settings are not repeated. alternatively, set the size on each figure individually."},{"sid":"C1.20","title":"Finishing a figure with `plt.show()`","cmd":"plt.show()","terms":["plt.show()"],"tags":[],"task":["show the plot","plt.show","finish a figure","display the figure"],"examinable":true,"search":"finishing a figure with plt.show() plt.show()  plt.show() show the plot plt.show finish a figure display the figure the `plt.show()` function allows us to finish the current figure, so the next plotting commands start a fresh one. it is useful when one cell draws several plots that must stay separate. alternatively, a trailing semicolon hides the text output but does not separate figures."},{"sid":"C1.21","title":"Saving a figure","cmd":"plt.savefig('file.png', dpi=n, bbox_inches='tight')","terms":["plt.savefig()"],"tags":["matplotlib","figure","saving","non-examinable"],"task":["save the plot","export an image","write a png","savefig"],"examinable":false,"search":"saving a figure plt.savefig('file.png', dpi=n, bbox_inches='tight') matplotlib figure saving non-examinable plt.savefig() save the plot export an image write a png savefig the `plt.savefig()` function allows us to write a plot to an image file. it is useful for including figures in a report or slides. alternatively, right-click the plot in jupyter and \"save image as\", but that is less repeatable."},{"sid":"C1.22","title":"Making points or lines transparent","cmd":"alpha=0.5","terms":["alpha="],"tags":[],"task":["see-through points","transparency","alpha","overlapping points"],"examinable":false,"search":"making points or lines transparent alpha=0.5  alpha= see-through points transparency alpha overlapping points the `alpha=` argument allows us to make a plot element partly see-through (0 = invisible, 1 = solid). it is useful when many points overlap, because dense areas then look darker. alternatively, smaller markers (`s=`) reduce overlap too."},{"sid":"C2.01","title":"Drawing a scatter plot","cmd":"plt.scatter(x, y)","terms":["plt.scatter()","scatter plot"],"tags":[],"task":["scatter plot","plot two variables as points","relationship between two columns"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a scatter plot plt.scatter(x, y)  plt.scatter() scatter plot scatter plot plot two variables as points relationship between two columns the `plt.scatter(x, y)` function allows us to plot one point per observation. it is useful for seeing the relationship between two numeric variables. alternatively, `sns.scatterplot` and `df.plot.scatter` draw from a dataframe."},{"sid":"C2.02","title":"Colouring points by a value","cmd":"plt.scatter(x, y, c=vals, cmap='viridis'); plt.colorbar()","terms":["c=","cmap","colorbar()"],"tags":[],"task":["colour points by a number","colour scale","colorbar","heat colours on a scatter"],"examinable":true,"search":"colouring points by a value plt.scatter(x, y, c=vals, cmap='viridis'); plt.colorbar()  c= cmap colorbar() colour points by a number colour scale colorbar heat colours on a scatter the `c=` and `cmap=` arguments allow us to colour each point by a third variable, with `plt.colorbar()` as the key. it is useful for showing a third dimension on a 2-d plot. alternatively, `hue=` in `sns.scatterplot` colours by category."},{"sid":"C2.03","title":"Sizing points by a value","cmd":"plt.scatter(x, y, s=sizes)","terms":["s= (point size)","bubble plot"],"tags":[],"task":["bubble chart","size points by a value","bigger points for bigger values"],"examinable":true,"search":"sizing points by a value plt.scatter(x, y, s=sizes)  s= (point size) bubble plot bubble chart size points by a value bigger points for bigger values the `s=` argument allows us to size each point by another variable. it is useful for a \"bubble\" plot. alternatively, colour by value or use `size=` in `sns.scatterplot`."},{"sid":"C2.04","title":"Drawing a bar chart","cmd":"plt.bar(x, h, color=c, width=w)","terms":["plt.bar()","bar chart"],"tags":[],"task":["bar chart","draw bars","compare categories","column chart"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a bar chart plt.bar(x, h, color=c, width=w)  plt.bar() bar chart bar chart draw bars compare categories column chart the `plt.bar(x, height)` function allows us to draw one bar per category. it is useful for counts or other values per group. alternatively, `sns.barplot`, `sns.countplot` or `series.plot.bar()` draw from the data."},{"sid":"C2.05","title":"Drawing horizontal bars","cmd":"plt.barh(y, w)","terms":["plt.barh()"],"tags":[],"task":["horizontal bar chart","barh","long category names","bars sideways"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing horizontal bars plt.barh(y, w)  plt.barh() horizontal bar chart barh long category names bars sideways the `plt.barh()` function allows us to draw bars sideways. it is useful when category names are long. alternatively, swap the axes in seaborn with `y=` for the category."},{"sid":"C2.06","title":"Drawing a probability bar plot","cmd":"plt.bar(events, P_events, width=0.6)","terms":["probability bar plot"],"tags":[],"task":["bar chart of probabilities","probability distribution plot","plot P of each outcome"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a probability bar plot plt.bar(events, p_events, width=0.6)  probability bar plot bar chart of probabilities probability distribution plot plot p of each outcome if we have a list of outcomes and their probabilities, then a bar plot shows the shape of the probability distribution. it is useful for discrete outcomes with known probabilities. alternatively, `sns.barplot` on a table with a probability column does the same."},{"sid":"C2.07","title":"Drawing a histogram","cmd":"plt.hist(data, bins=n)","terms":["plt.hist()","bins="],"tags":[],"task":["histogram","distribution of values","frequency plot","how spread out is the data"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a histogram plt.hist(data, bins=n)  plt.hist() bins= histogram distribution of values frequency plot how spread out is the data the `plt.hist()` function allows us to show how values are distributed by counting them in bins. it is useful for checking the shape, centre and spread of a variable. alternatively, `sns.histplot` or `series.plot.hist()` do the same."},{"sid":"C2.08","title":"Choosing bin edges","cmd":"bins=np.arange(start, stop, step), bins=np.linspace(start, stop, n)","terms":["bins with np.arange"],"tags":[],"task":["choose the bin edges","bin width","make bins a given size","histogram bins"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing bin edges bins=np.arange(start, stop, step), bins=np.linspace(start, stop, n)  bins with np.arange choose the bin edges bin width make bins a given size histogram bins passing an array of edges to `bins=` allows us to choose exactly where bins start and end. it is useful for round numbers or when comparing histograms. alternatively, `bins=n` lets matplotlib choose the edges."},{"sid":"C2.09","title":"Scaling a histogram as a density","cmd":"plt.hist(data, density=True)","terms":["density=True"],"tags":[],"task":["histogram as density","area equals one","compare histogram with a curve","normalise"],"examinable":false,"search":"scaling a histogram as a density plt.hist(data, density=true)  density=true histogram as density area equals one compare histogram with a curve normalise the `density=true` option allows us to scale a histogram so its total area is 1. it is useful for comparing with a probability density curve. alternatively, `sns.histplot(stat=\"density\")` does the same."},{"sid":"C2.10","title":"Drawing a box plot","cmd":"plt.boxplot(data)","terms":["plt.boxplot()","box plot"],"tags":[],"task":["box plot","whisker plot","median and quartiles plot","show outliers"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a box plot plt.boxplot(data)  plt.boxplot() box plot box plot whisker plot median and quartiles plot show outliers the `plt.boxplot()` function allows us to summarise a variable's median, quartiles and outliers in one picture. it is useful for spotting skew and outliers. alternatively, `sns.boxplot` adds grouping and colour."},{"sid":"C2.11","title":"Drawing several box plots","cmd":"plt.boxplot([s1, s2], tick_labels=names)","terms":["boxplot with a list","tick_labels"],"tags":["matplotlib","boxplot"],"task":["box plots side by side","compare groups with box plots","several boxes on one axis"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing several box plots plt.boxplot([s1, s2], tick_labels=names) matplotlib boxplot boxplot with a list tick_labels box plots side by side compare groups with box plots several boxes on one axis passing a list of arrays to `plt.boxplot()` allows us to compare groups side by side. it is useful for judging whether groups differ in centre or spread. alternatively, `sns.boxplot(data=df, x=, y=)` does the splitting automatically."},{"sid":"C2.12","title":"Drawing a pie chart","cmd":"plt.pie(sizes, labels=names, explode=offsets, autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=angle)","terms":["plt.pie()","pie chart"],"tags":[],"task":["pie chart","show proportions","percentages in a circle","slices"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a pie chart plt.pie(sizes, labels=names, explode=offsets, autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=angle)  plt.pie() pie chart pie chart show proportions percentages in a circle slices the `plt.pie()` function allows us to show how a whole is divided into parts. it is useful for a few categories with clear shares. alternatively, a bar chart is usually read more accurately."},{"sid":"C2.13","title":"Drawing panels with `plt.subplot`","cmd":"plt.subplot(rows, cols, i)","terms":["plt.subplot()","multiple panels"],"tags":[],"task":["several plots in one figure","subplot","grid of plots","panels"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing panels with plt.subplot plt.subplot(rows, cols, i)  plt.subplot() multiple panels several plots in one figure subplot grid of plots panels the `plt.subplot(rows, cols, i)` function allows us to put several plots in a grid, choosing the panel number each time. it is useful for side-by-side comparison. alternatively, `plt.subplots()` creates all the panels at once."},{"sid":"C2.14","title":"Adding an overall title and tight layout","cmd":"plt.suptitle(), plt.tight_layout()","terms":["suptitle()","tight_layout()"],"tags":["matplotlib","subplots","labels"],"task":["title for the whole figure","stop plots overlapping","tight layout","suptitle"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding an overall title and tight layout plt.suptitle(), plt.tight_layout() matplotlib subplots labels suptitle() tight_layout() title for the whole figure stop plots overlapping tight layout suptitle the `plt.suptitle()` and `plt.tight_layout()` functions allow us to add a title above all panels and stop panels overlapping. they are useful for any multi-panel figure. alternatively, `fig.suptitle()` and `constrained_layout=true` do the same."},{"sid":"C2.15","title":"Drawing panels with `plt.subplots`","cmd":"fig, axs = plt.subplots(rows, cols); axs[0].set(title=text)","terms":["plt.subplots()","fig, ax"],"tags":[],"task":["subplots","grid of axes","multiple panels","fig and axs","side by side plots"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing panels with plt.subplots fig, axs = plt.subplots(rows, cols); axs[0].set(title=text)  plt.subplots() fig, ax subplots grid of axes multiple panels fig and axs side by side plots the `fig, axs = plt.subplots()` pattern allows us to create the figure and all its axes together, then draw on each axis by name. it is useful for more complex figures and because `ax.set(...)` can set several labels at once. alternatively, `plt.subplot` with `plt.*` commands works for simple cases."},{"sid":"C2.16","title":"Shading an area","cmd":"plt.fill_between(x, y, alpha=0.3)","terms":["fill_between()"],"tags":[],"task":["shade under a curve","fill between lines","shade an area","confidence band"],"examinable":false,"search":"shading an area plt.fill_between(x, y, alpha=0.3)  fill_between() shade under a curve fill between lines shade an area confidence band the `plt.fill_between()` function allows us to shade the area under a curve. it is useful for showing a tail probability or a confidence band. alternatively, `plt.axvspan` shades a vertical strip."},{"sid":"C2.17","title":"Drawing error bars","cmd":"plt.errorbar(x, y, yerr=err)","terms":["errorbar()"],"tags":[],"task":["error bars","show uncertainty","standard error bars","plot with errors"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing error bars plt.errorbar(x, y, yerr=err)  errorbar() error bars show uncertainty standard error bars plot with errors the `plt.errorbar()` function allows us to draw points with bars showing their uncertainty. it is useful for means with standard errors or confidence intervals. alternatively, `sns.barplot` and `sns.pointplot` add intervals automatically."},{"sid":"C3.01","title":"Setting a seaborn theme","cmd":"import seaborn as sns; sns.set_theme()","terms":["sns.set_theme()"],"tags":["seaborn","setup","style"],"task":["seaborn theme","nicer plot style","default seaborn look","set the style"],"examinable":true,"search":"setting a seaborn theme import seaborn as sns; sns.set_theme() seaborn setup style sns.set_theme() seaborn theme nicer plot style default seaborn look set the style the `sns.set_theme()` function allows us to give every seaborn plot a consistent, clean style. it is useful once at the top of a notebook. alternatively, skip it and use matplotlib's default style."},{"sid":"C3.02","title":"The seaborn data, x, y, hue pattern","cmd":"sns.plotname(data=df, x='col', y='col', hue='col')","terms":["data=, x=, y=, hue="],"tags":[],"task":["how seaborn plots work","data x y hue","seaborn pattern","plot from a DataFrame"],"examinable":true,"search":"the seaborn data, x, y, hue pattern sns.plotname(data=df, x='col', y='col', hue='col')  data=, x=, y=, hue= how seaborn plots work data x y hue seaborn pattern plot from a dataframe seaborn's `data=`, `x=`, `y=` and `hue=` arguments allow us to say which dataframe and which columns to plot, by name. it is useful because every seaborn plot works this way, so once learnt it applies everywhere. alternatively, matplotlib takes the arrays directly (`plt.scatter(df[\"a\"], df[\"b\"])`)."},{"sid":"C3.03","title":"Labelling a seaborn plot","cmd":"ax = sns.histplot(...); ax.set(title=text, xlabel=text)","terms":["ax.set()","labelling seaborn plots"],"tags":[],"task":["title for a seaborn plot","label seaborn axes","set title and labels","ax set"],"examinable":true,"search":"labelling a seaborn plot ax = sns.histplot(...); ax.set(title=text, xlabel=text)  ax.set() labelling seaborn plots title for a seaborn plot label seaborn axes set title and labels ax set matplotlib's `plt.title()` and `plt.xlabel()`, or the `ax.set(...)` method on the returned axes, allow us to add titles and labels to a seaborn plot. they are useful because seaborn labels axes with raw column names. alternatively, rename the columns first so the default labels read well."},{"sid":"C3.04","title":"Drawing a histogram with `histplot`","cmd":"sns.histplot(data=df, x='col', bins=n)","terms":["sns.histplot()","histogram"],"tags":[],"task":["seaborn histogram","histplot","distribution of a column","bins"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a histogram with histplot sns.histplot(data=df, x='col', bins=n)  sns.histplot() histogram seaborn histogram histplot distribution of a column bins the `sns.histplot()` function allows us to see how the values of a variable are distributed. it is useful for the shape, centre and spread of one variable. alternatively, `plt.hist` and `series.plot.hist()` give a similar plot."},{"sid":"C3.05","title":"Centring histogram bars on integers","cmd":"sns.histplot(..., discrete=True)","terms":["histplot(discrete=True)"],"tags":[],"task":["bars centred on whole numbers","histogram of integers","discrete histogram","dice outcomes"],"examinable":true,"search":"centring histogram bars on integers sns.histplot(..., discrete=true)  histplot(discrete=true) bars centred on whole numbers histogram of integers discrete histogram dice outcomes the `discrete=true` option allows us to centre one bar on each whole number. it is useful for counts and dice totals, where values cannot lie between integers. alternatively, `sns.countplot` counts categories directly."},{"sid":"C3.06","title":"Adding a smooth density curve","cmd":"sns.histplot(..., kde=True)","terms":["histplot(kde=True)"],"tags":["seaborn","histogram","distribution"],"task":["histogram with a smooth curve","kde on a histogram","density line over bars"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a smooth density curve sns.histplot(..., kde=true) seaborn histogram distribution histplot(kde=true) histogram with a smooth curve kde on a histogram density line over bars the `kde=true` option allows us to overlay a smoothed density curve on a histogram. it is useful for seeing the overall shape without being distracted by individual bins. alternatively, `sns.kdeplot` draws the curve alone."},{"sid":"C3.07","title":"Overlaying groups in a histogram","cmd":"sns.histplot(..., hue='group', element='step')","terms":["histplot(hue=)"],"tags":[],"task":["compare groups in a histogram","overlay histograms","one histogram per group","transparent bars"],"examinable":false,"search":"overlaying groups in a histogram sns.histplot(..., hue='group', element='step')  histplot(hue=) compare groups in a histogram overlay histograms one histogram per group transparent bars the `hue=` argument allows us to draw a separate histogram per group on the same axes. it is useful for comparing distributions. alternatively, `col=` in `displot` gives each group its own panel."},{"sid":"C3.08","title":"Normalising a histogram","cmd":"sns.histplot(..., stat='density' / 'probability')","terms":["histplot(stat=)"],"tags":[],"task":["histogram as density","histogram as proportion","normalised histogram","stat density"],"examinable":false,"search":"normalising a histogram sns.histplot(..., stat='density' / 'probability')  histplot(stat=) histogram as density histogram as proportion normalised histogram stat density the `stat=` argument allows us to show density or probability instead of counts. it is useful for comparing groups of different sizes and for overlaying a theoretical curve. alternatively, matplotlib's `density=true`."},{"sid":"C3.09","title":"Drawing a density curve with `kdeplot`","cmd":"sns.kdeplot(data=df, x='col', hue='group')","terms":["sns.kdeplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["density curve","kde plot","smooth distribution","compare distributions"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a density curve with kdeplot sns.kdeplot(data=df, x='col', hue='group')  sns.kdeplot() density curve kde plot smooth distribution compare distributions the `sns.kdeplot()` function allows us to draw a smooth estimate of the distribution without bars. it is useful for comparing several groups cleanly. alternatively, `histplot(kde=true)` shows bars as well."},{"sid":"C3.10","title":"Drawing a cumulative distribution with `ecdfplot`","cmd":"sns.ecdfplot(data=df, x='col')","terms":["sns.ecdfplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["cumulative distribution","ecdf","proportion below each value","cumulative plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a cumulative distribution with ecdfplot sns.ecdfplot(data=df, x='col')  sns.ecdfplot() cumulative distribution ecdf proportion below each value cumulative plot the `sns.ecdfplot()` function allows us to plot the proportion of values at or below each x. it is useful for reading percentiles and cumulative probabilities straight off the plot. alternatively, compute `cumsum()` of the probabilities."},{"sid":"C3.11","title":"Drawing a box plot with `boxplot`","cmd":"sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='col', hue='group_2')","terms":["sns.boxplot()","box plot"],"tags":[],"task":["box plot by group","seaborn boxplot","compare distributions across categories","boxes by category"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a box plot with boxplot sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='col', hue='group_2')  sns.boxplot() box plot box plot by group seaborn boxplot compare distributions across categories boxes by category the `sns.boxplot()` function allows us to compare a numeric variable across categories using medians and quartiles. it is useful for spotting differences and outliers between groups. alternatively, `sns.violinplot` shows the whole distribution shape."},{"sid":"C3.12","title":"Drawing a violin plot","cmd":"sns.violinplot(data=df, x='group', y='col', hue='group_2')","terms":["sns.violinplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["violin plot","shape of the distribution by group","violin"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a violin plot sns.violinplot(data=df, x='group', y='col', hue='group_2')  sns.violinplot() violin plot shape of the distribution by group violin the `sns.violinplot()` function allows us to show the full shape of each group's distribution, not only quartiles. it is useful when group shapes differ (for example, two humps). alternatively, `sns.boxplot` shows the same groups with quartiles and outliers only."},{"sid":"C3.13","title":"Drawing a strip plot","cmd":"sns.stripplot(..., alpha=a, jitter=False)","terms":["sns.stripplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["strip plot","show every point","points by category","jitter"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a strip plot sns.stripplot(..., alpha=a, jitter=false)  sns.stripplot() strip plot show every point points by category jitter the `sns.stripplot()` function allows us to draw every individual observation per category. it is useful for small datasets, where hiding points inside a box would lose information. alternatively, `sns.swarmplot` avoids overlapping points."},{"sid":"C3.14","title":"Drawing a bar plot of values","cmd":"sns.barplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')","terms":["sns.barplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["bar plot of means","bars with error bars","average by category","barplot"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a bar plot of values sns.barplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')  sns.barplot() bar plot of means bars with error bars average by category barplot the `sns.barplot()` function allows us to draw a bar for each category with a value that is supplied (by default the mean). it is useful for plotting a table of counts or probabilities. alternatively, `sns.countplot` counts the rows automatically."},{"sid":"C3.15","title":"Counting categories with `countplot`","cmd":"sns.countplot(data=df, x='col')","terms":["sns.countplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["count categories","countplot","bar chart of counts","how many in each category"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting categories with countplot sns.countplot(data=df, x='col')  sns.countplot() count categories countplot bar chart of counts how many in each category the `sns.countplot()` function allows us to count and plot the rows in each category in one step. it is useful for quick frequency charts. alternatively, `value_counts()` followed by `sns.barplot` also gives the table."},{"sid":"C3.16","title":"Drawing a swarm plot","cmd":"sns.swarmplot(...)","terms":["sns.swarmplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["swarm plot","points that do not overlap","show every point by group","beeswarm"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a swarm plot sns.swarmplot(...)  sns.swarmplot() swarm plot points that do not overlap show every point by group beeswarm the `sns.swarmplot()` function allows us to draw every observation, nudged sideways so none overlap. it is useful for small to medium datasets. alternatively, `sns.stripplot` is quicker for large data."},{"sid":"C3.17","title":"Drawing a scatter plot with `scatterplot`","cmd":"sns.scatterplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')","terms":["sns.scatterplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["seaborn scatter plot","scatterplot","two variables","plot from a DataFrame"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a scatter plot with scatterplot sns.scatterplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')  sns.scatterplot() seaborn scatter plot scatterplot two variables plot from a dataframe the `sns.scatterplot()` function allows us to show the relationship between two numeric variables. it is useful for spotting trends, clusters and outliers. alternatively, `plt.scatter` or `df.plot.scatter` do the same."},{"sid":"C3.18","title":"Colouring points by group","cmd":"sns.scatterplot(..., hue='group')","terms":["hue="],"tags":[],"task":["colour points by category","scatter with groups","hue","different colour for each group"],"examinable":false,"search":"colouring points by group sns.scatterplot(..., hue='group')  hue= colour points by category scatter with groups hue different colour for each group the `hue=` argument allows us to colour each point by a category. it is useful for revealing groups hidden in the overall scatter (here, three species). alternatively, `style=` varies the marker shape instead."},{"sid":"C3.19","title":"Drawing a line plot","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', marker='o')","terms":["sns.lineplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["seaborn line plot","lineplot","trend over x","line with markers"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a line plot sns.lineplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', marker='o')  sns.lineplot() seaborn line plot lineplot trend over x line with markers the `sns.lineplot()` function allows us to join points in order along x. it is useful for trends over time or any ordered x. alternatively, `plt.plot` draws the same line."},{"sid":"C3.20","title":"Overlaying lines on one axis","cmd":"sns.lineplot(..., label=text, ax=ax); ax.legend()","terms":["ax=ax","overlaying lines"],"tags":[],"task":["several lines on one plot","overlay lines","legend with seaborn lines","line per series"],"examinable":true,"search":"overlaying lines on one axis sns.lineplot(..., label=text, ax=ax); ax.legend()  ax=ax overlaying lines several lines on one plot overlay lines legend with seaborn lines line per series passing the same `ax=` to several seaborn calls allows us to draw them on one set of axes. it is useful for comparing curves on the same axes. alternatively, `hue=` on long-format data draws several lines in one call."},{"sid":"C3.21","title":"Drawing a scatter with a fitted line","cmd":"sns.regplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', line_kws={'color': c})","terms":["sns.regplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["scatter with a trend line","regression line","regplot","best fit line on a plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a scatter with a fitted line sns.regplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', line_kws={'color': c})  sns.regplot() scatter with a trend line regression line regplot best fit line on a plot the `sns.regplot()` function allows us to draw a scatter plot with a least-squares line and confidence band. it is useful for a quick look at a linear relationship. alternatively, `stats.linregress` gives the numbers behind the line."},{"sid":"C3.22","title":"Drawing a residual plot","cmd":"sns.residplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')","terms":["sns.residplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["residual plot","check the fit","residplot","residuals against x"],"examinable":true,"search":"drawing a residual plot sns.residplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')  sns.residplot() residual plot check the fit residplot residuals against x the `sns.residplot()` function allows us to plot the residuals of a straight-line fit against x. it is useful for checking the linear and equal-variance assumptions (a random cloud around 0 is good). alternatively, calculate the residuals and draw them with `plt.scatter`, which gives full control of the plot."},{"sid":"C3.23","title":"Plotting every pair of variables","cmd":"sns.pairplot(df, hue='group')","terms":["sns.pairplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["pair plot","scatter matrix","every variable against every other","pairplot"],"examinable":false,"search":"plotting every pair of variables sns.pairplot(df, hue='group')  sns.pairplot() pair plot scatter matrix every variable against every other pairplot the `sns.pairplot()` function allows us to draw scatter plots of every pair of numeric columns. it is useful as a first overview of a dataset. alternatively, plot the pairs of interest with `sns.scatterplot`."},{"sid":"C3.24","title":"Drawing a correlation heatmap","cmd":"sns.heatmap(df.corr(numeric_only=True), annot=True)","terms":["sns.heatmap()","correlation matrix"],"tags":[],"task":["heatmap","correlation heatmap","coloured correlation table","which variables are related"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing a correlation heatmap sns.heatmap(df.corr(numeric_only=true), annot=true)  sns.heatmap() correlation matrix heatmap correlation heatmap coloured correlation table which variables are related the `sns.heatmap()` function allows us to colour a table of numbers, such as a correlation matrix. it is useful for spotting related variables at a glance. alternatively, print `df.corr()` as a table."},{"sid":"C3.25","title":"Drawing small multiples with facets","cmd":"sns.displot(..., col='group'), sns.catplot(..., kind='box')","terms":["displot(col=)","catplot","facets"],"tags":[],"task":["small multiples","facet grid","one panel per group","split a plot by category","catplot"],"examinable":false,"search":"drawing small multiples with facets sns.displot(..., col='group'), sns.catplot(..., kind='box')  displot(col=) catplot facets small multiples facet grid one panel per group split a plot by category catplot figure-level functions such as `displot`, `catplot` and `relplot` with `col=` allow us to draw one panel per category. it is useful for comparing many groups without overlap. alternatively, loop over groups and use `plt.subplots`."},{"sid":"C3.26","title":"Choosing a colour palette","cmd":"palette='Set2'","terms":["palette="],"tags":[],"task":["change the colours","palette","colour scheme","colourblind friendly"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing a colour palette palette='set2'  palette= change the colours palette colour scheme colourblind friendly the `palette=` argument allows us to choose the colours a seaborn plot uses. it is useful for readability, or colour-blind-friendly output. alternatively, `sns.set_palette()` sets one for the whole notebook."},{"sid":"C4.01","title":"Six principles of good plots","cmd":"","terms":["good plots","plot quality","principles of visualisation"],"tags":["matplotlib","seaborn","style","non-examinable"],"task":["make a good plot","plot quality","how to improve a plot","good figures for a report"],"examinable":false,"search":"six principles of good plots  matplotlib seaborn style non-examinable good plots plot quality principles of visualisation make a good plot plot quality how to improve a plot good figures for a report a good plot answers one question, can be read without the code, and gives an honest picture of the data. these six principles are a short list to think about while plotting and before a figure goes into a report."},{"sid":"C4.02","title":"Choosing a plot for the question","cmd":"spread → histplot   groups → boxplot   relationship → scatterplot","terms":["choosing a plot","which plot to use"],"tags":["seaborn","matplotlib","style","non-examinable"],"task":["which plot should I use","choose a chart","what kind of plot","best plot for my data"],"examinable":false,"search":"choosing a plot for the question spread → histplot   groups → boxplot   relationship → scatterplot seaborn matplotlib style non-examinable choosing a plot which plot to use which plot should i use choose a chart what kind of plot best plot for my data the question decides the plot: a histogram shows how one variable is spread, a box plot compares a variable across groups, and a scatter plot shows how two variables are related."},{"sid":"C4.03","title":"Labelling axes with names and units","cmd":"ax.set(title=\"…\", xlabel=\"Name (unit)\", ylabel=\"Name (unit)\")","terms":["axis labels with units","readable labels"],"tags":["matplotlib","seaborn","labels","non-examinable"],"task":["label axes with units","readable axis labels","column names on the plot","add units to a plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"labelling axes with names and units ax.set(title=\"…\", xlabel=\"name (unit)\", ylabel=\"name (unit)\") matplotlib seaborn labels non-examinable axis labels with units readable labels label axes with units readable axis labels column names on the plot add units to a plot plotting functions label the axes with the column names, which are written for code (`body_mass_g`); a reader needs words and units, and a title that says what the plot shows."},{"sid":"C4.04","title":"Starting bar charts at zero","cmd":"ax.set_ylim(0, …)","terms":["truncated axis","misleading bar chart","y axis from zero"],"tags":["matplotlib","bar","axes","non-examinable"],"task":["misleading bar chart","truncated y axis","bar chart start at zero","exaggerated differences"],"examinable":false,"search":"starting bar charts at zero ax.set_ylim(0, …) matplotlib bar axes non-examinable truncated axis misleading bar chart y axis from zero misleading bar chart truncated y axis bar chart start at zero exaggerated differences a bar is read by its length, so a bar chart whose axis starts above zero makes small differences look large."},{"sid":"C4.05","title":"Showing the data, not only the averages","cmd":"sns.boxplot(...)   sns.stripplot(...)","terms":["bar of means","showing spread","box and strip plot"],"tags":["seaborn","box","strip","non-examinable"],"task":["show spread not just mean","bar chart of means hides data","show every point","overlap between groups"],"examinable":false,"search":"showing the data, not only the averages sns.boxplot(...)   sns.stripplot(...) seaborn box strip non-examinable bar of means showing spread box and strip plot show spread not just mean bar chart of means hides data show every point overlap between groups a bar for each mean hides how far the values spread and how much the groups overlap; a box plot with the points on top shows both, as well as the middle."},{"sid":"C4.06","title":"Colours that every reader can tell apart","cmd":"palette=\"colorblind\"   style=\"col\"","terms":["colour-blind friendly","accessible colours","marker shapes"],"tags":["seaborn","colours","style","non-examinable"],"task":["colour blind friendly plot","accessible colours","red and green","colours for groups"],"examinable":false,"search":"colours that every reader can tell apart palette=\"colorblind\"   style=\"col\" seaborn colours style non-examinable colour-blind friendly accessible colours marker shapes colour blind friendly plot accessible colours red and green colours for groups about one man in twelve cannot tell red from green, and a figure printed in grey loses colour altogether, so groups are shown with a colour-blind-friendly palette and a second cue such as marker shape."},{"sid":"C4.07","title":"One message for each figure","cmd":"","terms":["cluttered plot","one message per figure"],"tags":["seaborn","style","non-examinable"],"task":["plot too busy","cluttered plot","too much on one plot","simplify a figure"],"examinable":false,"search":"one message for each figure  seaborn style non-examinable cluttered plot one message per figure plot too busy cluttered plot too much on one plot simplify a figure every extra colour, size and shape is one more thing the reader must decode; a figure that shows only what supports its message can be read at once, and a second message gets a second figure."},{"sid":"C4.08","title":"Checking a plot before using it","cmd":"ax.get_title()   ax.get_xlabel()   ax.get_ylim()","terms":["plot checklist","checking a figure"],"tags":["matplotlib","labels","style","non-examinable"],"task":["check my plot","plot checklist","figure ready for a report","before submitting a plot"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking a plot before using it ax.get_title()   ax.get_xlabel()   ax.get_ylim() matplotlib labels style non-examinable plot checklist checking a figure check my plot plot checklist figure ready for a report before submitting a plot a short check of every figure before it goes into a report or a presentation, with a function that reports the parts a reader needs."},{"sid":"D1.01","title":"Checking that probabilities sum to 1","cmd":"Σ P(xi) = 1","terms":["probability rules","sum of probabilities"],"tags":["probability","rules"],"task":["do probabilities add to one","check a distribution","probabilities sum to 1","valid distribution"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking that probabilities sum to 1 σ p(xi) = 1 probability rules probability rules sum of probabilities do probabilities add to one check a distribution probabilities sum to 1 valid distribution adding up all the probabilities in a distribution allows us to check it is valid, since they must total 1. it is useful as a sanity check on any table of probabilities."},{"sid":"D1.02","title":"Adding probabilities for \"or\"","cmd":"P(5 or 6) = P(5) + P(6)","terms":["addition rule"],"tags":["probability","rules"],"task":["or probability","add probabilities","P(A or B)","either event"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding probabilities for \"or\" p(5 or 6) = p(5) + p(6) probability rules addition rule or probability add probabilities p(a or b) either event if outcomes cannot happen together, then adding their probabilities gives the probability that one of them occurs. it is useful for \"or\" questions about separate outcomes. alternatively, count the favourable outcomes in the table and divide by the total."},{"sid":"D1.03","title":"Multiplying probabilities for \"and\"","cmd":"P(A and B) = P(A) · P(B)","terms":["multiplication rule","independence"],"tags":[],"task":["and probability","multiply probabilities","independent events","P(A and B)"],"examinable":true,"search":"multiplying probabilities for \"and\" p(a and b) = p(a) · p(b)  multiplication rule independence and probability multiply probabilities independent events p(a and b) if events are independent, then multiplying their probabilities gives the chance that both happen. it is useful for any run of independent trials. alternatively, count outcomes in the full table of combinations."},{"sid":"D1.04","title":"The complement rule","cmd":"P(X ≥ k) = 1 − P(X ≤ k − 1)","terms":["complement rule"],"tags":[],"task":["at least probability","one minus","complement","P of not","at least one"],"examinable":true,"search":"the complement rule p(x ≥ k) = 1 − p(x ≤ k − 1)  complement rule at least probability one minus complement p of not at least one if we want the probability of \"at least\" or \"not a\", then the complement rule `p(not a) = 1 − p(a)` finds it from the opposite event. it is useful when the opposite event is simpler to count. alternatively, count the favourable outcomes directly."},{"sid":"D1.05","title":"Expected value","cmd":"E(X) = Σ P(xi) · xi","terms":["expected value E(X)"],"tags":["probability","rules","expectation"],"task":["expected value","mean of a distribution","long run average","expectation"],"examinable":true,"search":"expected value e(x) = σ p(xi) · xi probability rules expectation expected value e(x) expected value mean of a distribution long run average expectation if we have each outcome and its probability, then multiplying each outcome by its probability and adding gives the expected value, `e(x) = σ p(xi)·xi`. it is useful for the long-run average of a random quantity. alternatively, simulate many trials and take the mean."},{"sid":"D1.06","title":"Plotting a probability distribution","cmd":"plt.bar(events, P_events, width=0.6)","terms":["probability distribution plot"],"tags":[],"task":["plot a distribution","bar chart of probabilities","probability mass function plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a probability distribution plt.bar(events, p_events, width=0.6)  probability distribution plot plot a distribution bar chart of probabilities probability mass function plot if we have the outcomes and their probabilities, then a bar plot shows the shape of a discrete distribution. it is useful for comparing distributions and spotting symmetry. alternatively, `sns.barplot` on a table with a probability column does the same."},{"sid":"D1.07","title":"Plotting a cumulative distribution","cmd":"plt.bar(x, np.cumsum(P))","terms":["cumulative distribution plot"],"tags":[],"task":["plot cumulative probabilities","cdf bar chart","cumulative distribution plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a cumulative distribution plt.bar(x, np.cumsum(p))  cumulative distribution plot plot cumulative probabilities cdf bar chart cumulative distribution plot if we have the probabilities of each outcome, then plotting their running total shows `p(x ≤ x)` at a glance. it is useful for reading percentiles. alternatively, `sns.ecdfplot` draws the same thing from raw data."},{"sid":"D1.08","title":"Favourable outcomes over total outcomes","cmd":"mask = df['col'] == value; len(df[mask]) / len(df)","terms":["favourable / total"],"tags":[],"task":["probability from data","proportion of rows","favourable over total","empirical probability"],"examinable":true,"search":"favourable outcomes over total outcomes mask = df['col'] == value; len(df[mask]) / len(df)  favourable / total probability from data proportion of rows favourable over total empirical probability if all outcomes are equally likely and we have a table listing every one, then the probability of an event is the number of favourable rows divided by the total. it is useful whenever the outcomes can be enumerated. alternatively, `mask.mean()` gives the same proportion in one step."},{"sid":"D1.09","title":"Counting combined events","cmd":"(df['col_1'] <= a) & (df['col_2'] <= b)","terms":["combined events","& and | on outcomes"],"tags":[],"task":["probability of two events together","count rows meeting both conditions","joint probability from data"],"examinable":true,"search":"counting combined events (df['col_1'] <= a) & (df['col_2'] <= b)  combined events & and | on outcomes probability of two events together count rows meeting both conditions joint probability from data if an event needs several conditions to hold at once, then combining masks with `&` or `|` counts the outcomes that satisfy them. it is useful for \"and\" / \"or\" events across different dice. alternatively, multiply probabilities when the events are independent."},{"sid":"D1.10","title":"Building a frequency table","cmd":"df['col'].value_counts().to_frame().sort_values(by='col')","terms":["frequency table"],"tags":[],"task":["frequency table","counts of outcomes","table of how often each value occurs","tally"],"examinable":true,"search":"building a frequency table df['col'].value_counts().to_frame().sort_values(by='col')  frequency table frequency table counts of outcomes table of how often each value occurs tally if we have a table of every outcome, then counting each total gives a frequency table, the first step to a probability distribution. it is useful for seeing how often each result occurs. alternatively, `pd.crosstab` or `groupby().size()` give the same counts."},{"sid":"D1.11","title":"Adding a probability column","cmd":"df['prob'] = df['count'] / total","terms":["probability column"],"tags":[],"task":["probability column","relative frequency table","divide counts by the total","add probabilities to a table"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a probability column df['prob'] = df['count'] / total  probability column probability column relative frequency table divide counts by the total add probabilities to a table if we have a column of counts, then dividing by 6³ adds a column of probabilities. it is useful for a full probability distribution table. alternatively, `value_counts(normalize=true)` gives probabilities directly."},{"sid":"D1.12","title":"Expectation from a table","cmd":"(df['prob'] * df['value']).sum()","terms":["expected value from a table"],"tags":[],"task":["expected value from a table","sum of probability times value","mean from a distribution table"],"examinable":true,"search":"expectation from a table (df['prob'] * df['value']).sum()  expected value from a table expected value from a table sum of probability times value mean from a distribution table if we have a table with a probability column and an outcome column, then multiplying them and summing gives the expected value. it is useful when the distribution is known but not the raw rolls. alternatively, `mean()` of the raw outcomes gives the same answer."},{"sid":"D1.13","title":"Adding a cumulative probability column","cmd":"df['cum_prob'] = df['prob'].cumsum()","terms":["cumulative probability"],"tags":[],"task":["cumulative probability column","running total of probabilities","cdf column","P less than or equal"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a cumulative probability column df['cum_prob'] = df['prob'].cumsum()  cumulative probability cumulative probability column running total of probabilities cdf column p less than or equal if we have a column of probabilities in order, then `.cumsum()` adds a column of `p(x ≤ x)`. it is useful for \"at most\" and \"at least\" questions and for ranges. alternatively, sum a masked column: `prob[total <= x].sum()`."},{"sid":"D1.14","title":"Range probabilities","cmd":"P(a < X ≤ b) = P(X ≤ b) − P(X ≤ a)","terms":["P(a < X ≤ b)"],"tags":[],"task":["probability between two values","P(a < X <= b)","range probability","difference of cdfs"],"examinable":true,"search":"range probabilities p(a < x ≤ b) = p(x ≤ b) − p(x ≤ a)  p(a < x ≤ b) probability between two values p(a < x <= b) range probability difference of cdfs if we have a cumulative probability column, then subtracting two of its values gives the probability of a range, `p(a < x ≤ b) = p(x ≤ b) − p(x ≤ a)`. it is useful for \"between\" questions. alternatively, `.between()` on the raw outcomes gives the same."},{"sid":"D1.15","title":"Simulating random draws","cmd":"np.random.choice(outcomes, size, p=None)","terms":["simulation","np.random.choice()"],"tags":["probability","numpy","random","simulation","non-examinable"],"task":["simulate draws","random sample with probabilities","simulate an experiment","pick outcomes at random"],"examinable":false,"search":"simulating random draws np.random.choice(outcomes, size, p=none) probability numpy random simulation non-examinable simulation np.random.choice() simulate draws random sample with probabilities simulate an experiment pick outcomes at random the `np.random.choice()` function allows us to simulate random draws from any list of outcomes: die faces, `\"h\"` and `\"t\"`, colours of balls in a bag. it is useful for acting out a probability experiment on the computer. alternatively, `np.random.randint(1, 7)` is a shortcut for fair dice, and `rng.choice()` is the newer-generator version."},{"sid":"D1.16","title":"Simulating many draws and comparing with the theory","cmd":"pd.Series(draws).value_counts(normalize=True)","terms":["simulation vs theory"],"tags":["probability","numpy","random","simulation","counting","non-examinable"],"task":["simulate and compare with theory","do simulated results match","empirical versus theoretical","law of large numbers"],"examinable":false,"search":"simulating many draws and comparing with the theory pd.series(draws).value_counts(normalize=true) probability numpy random simulation counting non-examinable simulation vs theory simulate and compare with theory do simulated results match empirical versus theoretical law of large numbers if we can draw random outcomes, then counting many of them estimates the probabilities by experiment. it is useful for checking an exact answer or for problems that are hard to enumerate. alternatively, calculate the exact probability from the table of all outcomes."},{"sid":"D1.17","title":"Simulating many dice rolls","cmd":"np.random.randint(low, high, size=(rows, cols)).sum(axis=1)","terms":["simulating dice"],"tags":[],"task":["roll dice many times","sum of two dice","simulate dice","repeated experiments"],"examinable":false,"search":"simulating many dice rolls np.random.randint(low, high, size=(rows, cols)).sum(axis=1)  simulating dice roll dice many times sum of two dice simulate dice repeated experiments if we simulate thousands of throws of three dice, then the proportion of throws meeting a condition estimates its probability. it is useful for checking exact answers or for problems too hard to enumerate. alternatively, enumerate every outcome (as above) when the number is small."},{"sid":"D1.18","title":"Binomial probabilities","cmd":"binom.pmf(k, n, p), binom.cdf(k, n, p)","terms":["binom.pmf()","binom.cdf()"],"tags":[],"task":["binomial probability","number of successes","coin flips","binom pmf","at most k successes"],"examinable":false,"search":"binomial probabilities binom.pmf(k, n, p), binom.cdf(k, n, p)  binom.pmf() binom.cdf() binomial probability number of successes coin flips binom pmf at most k successes the `binom.pmf()` and `binom.cdf()` functions allow us to calculate the probability of a given number of successes in n independent trials. they are useful for coin tosses or any yes/no repeated trial. alternatively, `math.comb` counts the outcomes directly, and the probabilities follow from them."},{"sid":"D1.19","title":"Counting combinations","cmd":"math.comb(n, k)","terms":["math.comb()"],"tags":[],"task":["number of combinations","n choose k","ways to choose","combinations"],"examinable":false,"search":"counting combinations math.comb(n, k)  math.comb() number of combinations n choose k ways to choose combinations the `math.comb(n, k)` function allows us to count the ways to choose k items from n. it is useful for counting arrangements in probability problems. alternatively, `scipy.special.comb` works on arrays."},{"sid":"D1.20","title":"Variance","cmd":"Var(X) = Σ P(xi)(xi − μ)²","terms":["variance of a distribution"],"tags":[],"task":["variance of a distribution","spread of a distribution","sum of squared deviations","variance from a table"],"examinable":false,"search":"variance var(x) = σ p(xi)(xi − μ)²  variance of a distribution variance of a distribution spread of a distribution sum of squared deviations variance from a table if we have a probability distribution, then weighting the squared distances from the mean by their probabilities gives the variance, `σ p(xi)(xi − μ)²`. it is useful for measuring how spread out a distribution is. alternatively, simulate and take `.var()`."},{"sid":"D1.21","title":"Comparing decimals safely","cmd":"np.isclose(a, b)","terms":["np.isclose()"],"tags":[],"task":["compare floating point numbers","are these equal","isclose","rounding error in comparison"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing decimals safely np.isclose(a, b)  np.isclose() compare floating point numbers are these equal isclose rounding error in comparison the `np.isclose(a, b)` function allows us to test whether two decimals are equal within rounding error. it is useful because numbers like 1/6 or 0.1 are stored approximately, so `==` can fail. alternatively, `round(a, 8) == round(b, 8)` also works."},{"sid":"D2.01","title":"Probability density","cmd":"norm.pdf(x, mu, sd)","terms":["norm.pdf()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","pdf"],"task":["height of the normal curve","normal pdf","probability density","density at a value"],"examinable":true,"search":"probability density norm.pdf(x, mu, sd) probability scipy normal pdf norm.pdf() height of the normal curve normal pdf probability density density at a value the `norm.pdf(x, mu, sd)` function allows us to find the height of the bell curve at x. it is useful for drawing the curve and for bayes calculations. alternatively, `scipy.stats.norm(mu, sd).pdf(x)` fixes the parameters first. note that a density is **not** a probability."},{"sid":"D2.02","title":"P(X ≤ x) with the CDF","cmd":"norm.cdf(x, mu, sd)","terms":["norm.cdf()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","cdf"],"task":["probability below a value","normal cdf","P less than x","area to the left"],"examinable":true,"search":"p(x ≤ x) with the cdf norm.cdf(x, mu, sd) probability scipy normal cdf norm.cdf() probability below a value normal cdf p less than x area to the left the `norm.cdf(x, mu, sd)` function allows us to find `p(x ≤ x)`, the area under the curve to the left of x. it is useful for \"what is the chance of a value below…?\" questions. alternatively, `1 - norm.cdf(...)` or `norm.sf` gives the upper tail."},{"sid":"D2.03","title":"P(X > x)","cmd":"1 - norm.cdf(x, mu, sd)","terms":["1 - norm.cdf()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","cdf"],"task":["probability above a value","upper tail","P greater than x","area to the right"],"examinable":true,"search":"p(x > x) 1 - norm.cdf(x, mu, sd) probability scipy normal cdf 1 - norm.cdf() probability above a value upper tail p greater than x area to the right subtracting the cdf from 1 allows us to find `p(x > x)`, the upper tail. it is useful for \"what is the chance of exceeding…?\" questions. alternatively, `norm.sf(x, mu, sd)` does the same and is more accurate far into the tail."},{"sid":"D2.04","title":"P(a < X < b)","cmd":"norm.cdf(b, mu, sd) - norm.cdf(a, mu, sd)","terms":["cdf(b) - cdf(a)"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","cdf"],"task":["probability between two values","area between a and b","normal probability in a range"],"examinable":true,"search":"p(a < x < b) norm.cdf(b, mu, sd) - norm.cdf(a, mu, sd) probability scipy normal cdf cdf(b) - cdf(a) probability between two values area between a and b normal probability in a range subtracting two cdf values allows us to find `p(a < x < b)`. it is useful for the probability of a range of values. alternatively, count the proportion of data in the range with a mask."},{"sid":"D2.05","title":"Tables of PDF values","cmd":"x = np.arange(start, stop, step); df['pdf'] = norm.pdf(x, mu, sd)","terms":["table of pdf values"],"tags":[],"task":["table of density values","make a pdf table","values for plotting a curve"],"examinable":true,"search":"tables of pdf values x = np.arange(start, stop, step); df['pdf'] = norm.pdf(x, mu, sd)  table of pdf values table of density values make a pdf table values for plotting a curve if we want to draw a curve or combine distributions, then we can evaluate `norm.pdf` over a range of x values and store the (x, y) points in a table. it is useful for plotting and for bayes calculations. alternatively, `np.linspace` with `plt.plot` draws a curve without a table."},{"sid":"D2.06","title":"Plotting a PDF from a table","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', label=text)","terms":["plotting a pdf"],"tags":[],"task":["plot the normal curve","plot a pdf","draw a bell curve"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a pdf from a table sns.lineplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', label=text)  plotting a pdf plot the normal curve plot a pdf draw a bell curve if we have a set of data points giving the x and y coordinates of a probability density function (a pdf), then we can plot them as a line using `sns.lineplot`. it is useful for comparing two distributions on one set of axes. alternatively, `plt.plot(x, norm.pdf(x, mu, sd))` draws the curve without a dataframe."},{"sid":"D2.07","title":"Plotting a CDF from a table","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='x', y='cdf')","terms":["plotting a cdf"],"tags":[],"task":["plot a cdf","cumulative curve","draw the cumulative distribution"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a cdf from a table sns.lineplot(data=df, x='x', y='cdf')  plotting a cdf plot a cdf cumulative curve draw the cumulative distribution if we have a set of data points giving the x and y coordinates of a cumulative distribution function (a cdf), then we can plot them as a line using `sns.lineplot`. it is useful for reading percentiles off a plot. alternatively, `sns.ecdfplot` draws an empirical cdf from raw data."},{"sid":"D2.08","title":"Upper tails with `sf`","cmd":"norm.sf(x, mu, sd)","terms":["norm.sf()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["survival function","upper tail probability","sf","right tail"],"examinable":false,"search":"upper tails with sf norm.sf(x, mu, sd) probability scipy normal non-examinable norm.sf() survival function upper tail probability sf right tail the `norm.sf()` (\"survival function\") allows us to find `p(x > x)` directly. it is useful for very small upper-tail probabilities, where `1 - cdf` loses accuracy. alternatively, `1 - norm.cdf(x, mu, sd)` is fine for everyday values."},{"sid":"D2.09","title":"Percentiles with `ppf`","cmd":"norm.ppf(q, mu, sd)","terms":["norm.ppf()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["value for a given percentile","inverse cdf","ppf","find the cut-off","quantile of a normal"],"examinable":false,"search":"percentiles with ppf norm.ppf(q, mu, sd) probability scipy normal non-examinable norm.ppf() value for a given percentile inverse cdf ppf find the cut-off quantile of a normal the `norm.ppf(q, mu, sd)` function allows us to find the value below which a fraction q of the distribution lies. it is useful for critical values and percentiles. alternatively, `np.percentile` finds percentiles of data rather than of a model."},{"sid":"D2.10","title":"Z-scores","cmd":"z = (x - mu) / sd; norm.cdf(z)","terms":["z-score"],"tags":["probability","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["z score","standardise a value","how many standard deviations","convert to standard normal"],"examinable":false,"search":"z-scores z = (x - mu) / sd; norm.cdf(z) probability normal non-examinable z-score z score standardise a value how many standard deviations convert to standard normal if we have a value and the mean and standard deviation of its distribution, then the z-score `(x − μ) / σ` says how many standard deviations the value lies from the mean. it is useful for comparing values from different distributions. alternatively, `norm.cdf(x, mu, sd)` gives the same probability without converting."},{"sid":"D2.11","title":"Random samples with `rvs`","cmd":"norm.rvs(mu, sd, size=n)","terms":["norm.rvs()"],"tags":["probability","scipy","normal","random","non-examinable"],"task":["simulate normal data","random sample from a normal distribution","rvs","generate normal values"],"examinable":false,"search":"random samples with rvs norm.rvs(mu, sd, size=n) probability scipy normal random non-examinable norm.rvs() simulate normal data random sample from a normal distribution rvs generate normal values the `norm.rvs(mu, sd, size=n)` function allows us to draw random values from a normal distribution. it is useful for simulation. alternatively, `np.random.normal(mu, sd, n)` does the same."},{"sid":"D2.12","title":"The 68-95-99.7 rule","cmd":"norm.cdf(k) - norm.cdf(-k)","terms":["68-95-99.7 rule","1-sigma rule","2-sigma rule","3-sigma rule","empirical rule"],"tags":["probability","normal","1-sigma-rule","2-sigma-rule","3-sigma-rule","non-examinable"],"task":["68 95 99.7 rule","empirical rule","within k standard deviations","how much falls within"],"examinable":false,"search":"the 68-95-99.7 rule norm.cdf(k) - norm.cdf(-k) probability normal 1-sigma-rule 2-sigma-rule 3-sigma-rule non-examinable 68-95-99.7 rule 1-sigma rule 2-sigma rule 3-sigma rule empirical rule 68 95 99.7 rule empirical rule within k standard deviations how much falls within if a distribution is normal, then about 68% of it lies within 1 standard deviation of the mean (the 1-sigma rule), about 95% within 2 and about 99.7% within 3. it is useful for quick sanity checks and for spotting unusual values. alternatively, count the proportion of the data within each range."},{"sid":"D2.13","title":"Comparing a histogram with a normal curve","cmd":"plt.hist(data, density=True); plt.plot(x, norm.pdf(x, mu, sd))","terms":["histogram with normal curve"],"tags":[],"task":["is the data normal","histogram with normal curve","check normality visually","overlay a bell curve"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing a histogram with a normal curve plt.hist(data, density=true); plt.plot(x, norm.pdf(x, mu, sd))  histogram with normal curve is the data normal histogram with normal curve check normality visually overlay a bell curve if we have data and a fitted normal distribution, then drawing a density-scaled histogram with the normal curve on top allows us to judge whether the data look normal. it is useful before using methods that assume normality. alternatively, a qq plot is a sharper check."},{"sid":"D2.14","title":"Shading a tail of a distribution","cmd":"plt.fill_between(x, norm.pdf(x, mu, sd), where=x < limit)","terms":["fill_between() for a tail"],"tags":[],"task":["shade the tail","shaded area under the normal curve","show a p-value","shade a region"],"examinable":false,"search":"shading a tail of a distribution plt.fill_between(x, norm.pdf(x, mu, sd), where=x < limit)  fill_between() for a tail shade the tail shaded area under the normal curve show a p-value shade a region if we have a curve and a cut-off, then `fill_between` with a `where=` condition shades the region that represents a probability. it is useful for showing what a p-value is. alternatively, `plt.axvline` marks the cut-off only."},{"sid":"D2.15","title":"Other distributions","cmd":"stats.uniform, stats.expon, stats.t","terms":["stats.uniform","stats.expon","stats.t"],"tags":[],"task":["uniform distribution","exponential distribution","t distribution","other scipy distributions"],"examinable":false,"search":"other distributions stats.uniform, stats.expon, stats.t  stats.uniform stats.expon stats.t uniform distribution exponential distribution t distribution other scipy distributions the classes `stats.uniform`, `stats.expon` and `stats.t` allow us to use other distributions with the same `.pdf/.cdf/.ppf` methods. they are useful when data are not normal, and `stats.t` is used in t-tests. alternatively, `np.random` can draw from them but has no `cdf`."},{"sid":"D2.16","title":"The total probability rule","cmd":"f(flipper) = f(flipper | A)·P(A) + f(flipper | C)·P(C)","terms":["total probability"],"tags":["probability","bayes","pandas","new-column"],"task":["total probability","law of total probability","split a probability across groups","marginal probability"],"examinable":true,"search":"the total probability rule f(flipper) = f(flipper | a)·p(a) + f(flipper | c)·p(c) probability bayes pandas new-column total probability total probability law of total probability split a probability across groups marginal probability if we know how common each group is and the density within each group, then weighting each group's density by its share gives the overall density, `f(flipper) = f(flipper | adelie)p(adelie) + f(flipper | chinstrap)p(chinstrap)`, where f marks a density because flipper length is continuous. it is useful as the denominator in bayes' theorem. alternatively, with data, count the proportion of all penguins in each flipper-length range."},{"sid":"D2.17","title":"Bayes' theorem","cmd":"P(A | flipper) = f(flipper | A) · P(A) / f(flipper)","terms":["Bayes' theorem","conditional probability"],"tags":["probability","bayes","pandas","new-column"],"task":["Bayes theorem","reverse conditional probability","posterior","update a probability"],"examinable":true,"search":"bayes' theorem p(a | flipper) = f(flipper | a) · p(a) / f(flipper) probability bayes pandas new-column bayes' theorem conditional probability bayes theorem reverse conditional probability posterior update a probability if we know the density `f(flipper | adelie)`, then bayes' theorem turns it into `p(adelie | flipper)`, answering \"given this flipper length, how likely is the penguin to be an adelie?\". it is useful for classification and updating beliefs. alternatively, with data, count how many penguins of a given flipper length are adelie."},{"sid":"D2.18","title":"Plotting a posterior probability curve","cmd":"sns.lineplot(data=df, x='flipper', y='prob_c_given_flipper')","terms":["posterior probability plot"],"tags":["probability","bayes","plotting"],"task":["plot Bayes result","posterior curve","probability of a species given a measurement"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a posterior probability curve sns.lineplot(data=df, x='flipper', y='prob_c_given_flipper') probability bayes plotting posterior probability plot plot bayes result posterior curve probability of a species given a measurement if we have calculated `p(chinstrap | flipper)` for a range of flipper lengths, then a line plot shows how the evidence changes with the measurement. it is useful for seeing where a classifier switches from \"probably adelie\" to \"probably chinstrap\". alternatively, tabulate the values at chosen flipper lengths."},{"sid":"D3.01","title":"Null and alternative hypotheses","cmd":"H0: x comes from N(mu, sd)   vs   H1: x is lower  or  x differs","terms":["null hypothesis (H0)","alternative hypothesis (H1)","hypothesis test"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","probability","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["null hypothesis","alternative hypothesis","H0 and H1","state the hypotheses"],"examinable":false,"search":"null and alternative hypotheses h0: x comes from n(mu, sd)   vs   h1: x is lower  or  x differs hypothesis-testing probability normal non-examinable null hypothesis (h0) alternative hypothesis (h1) hypothesis test null hypothesis alternative hypothesis h0 and h1 state the hypotheses writing down h₀ (the null hypothesis) and h₁ (the alternative) before looking at the result allows us to say exactly what a test is asking. the direction of h₁ decides whether a one-tailed or a two-tailed p-value is needed."},{"sid":"D3.02","title":"One-tailed p-values","cmd":"p = norm.cdf(observed, mu, sd)","terms":["one-tailed p-value","p-value"],"tags":[],"task":["one-tailed p value","p value for a lower tail","probability as extreme as observed"],"examinable":true,"search":"one-tailed p-values p = norm.cdf(observed, mu, sd)  one-tailed p-value p-value one-tailed p value p value for a lower tail probability as extreme as observed if we know the distribution of the measurement under h₀ (here a normal distribution), then the cdf at the observed value gives a one-tailed p-value: the probability of a result at least this extreme if h₀ is true. it is useful when h₁ was stated in one direction before the data were seen. alternatively, use a two-tailed p-value when a difference in either direction would count as evidence, or when no direction was fixed in advance."},{"sid":"D3.03","title":"Two-tailed p-values","cmd":"norm.cdf(mu - diff, mu, sd) + (1 - norm.cdf(mu + diff, mu, sd))","terms":["two-tailed p-value"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","probability","normal"],"task":["two-tailed p value","p value both tails","extreme in either direction"],"examinable":true,"search":"two-tailed p-values norm.cdf(mu - diff, mu, sd) + (1 - norm.cdf(mu + diff, mu, sd)) hypothesis-testing probability normal two-tailed p-value two-tailed p value p value both tails extreme in either direction if either a very low or a very high value would count as evidence against h₀, then we add the probability in **both** tails to get a two-tailed p-value. it is useful when the alternative hypothesis is \"different from\", not \"lower than\". alternatively, `2 * norm.cdf(-abs(z))` is the shortcut for a symmetric distribution."},{"sid":"D3.04","title":"Estimating a p-value by simulation","cmd":"(simulated <= observed).mean()","terms":["p-value by simulation","Monte Carlo"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","probability","simulation","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["p value by simulation","Monte Carlo test","permutation style estimate","simulate the null"],"examinable":false,"search":"estimating a p-value by simulation (simulated <= observed).mean() hypothesis-testing probability simulation normal non-examinable p-value by simulation monte carlo p value by simulation monte carlo test permutation style estimate simulate the null if we can draw random values from the null distribution, then the proportion of simulated values at least as extreme as the observation estimates the p-value, without any formula. it is useful for seeing what a p-value *means*, and when no formula exists. alternatively, use `norm.cdf` for the exact value when the distribution is known."},{"sid":"D3.05","title":"Deciding whether to reject H0","cmd":"if p < alpha: reject H0","terms":["alpha","significance level","reject H0"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","probability","conditions"],"task":["reject the null","significant or not","compare p with alpha","make a decision"],"examinable":true,"search":"deciding whether to reject h0 if p < alpha: reject h0 hypothesis-testing probability conditions alpha significance level reject h0 reject the null significant or not compare p with alpha make a decision comparing the p-value with a significance level α (often 0.05) allows us to decide whether the evidence is strong enough to reject h₀. it is useful as the final step of any test. alternatively, report the exact p-value and let the reader judge."},{"sid":"D3.06","title":"Critical values","cmd":"norm.ppf(alpha / 2, mu, sd), norm.ppf(1 - alpha / 2, mu, sd)   one-sided: norm.ppf(alpha, mu, sd)","terms":["critical value","norm.ppf()"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","probability","normal","non-examinable"],"task":["critical value","rejection region","cut-off for significance","alpha 0.05 boundary"],"examinable":false,"search":"critical values norm.ppf(alpha / 2, mu, sd), norm.ppf(1 - alpha / 2, mu, sd)   one-sided: norm.ppf(alpha, mu, sd) hypothesis-testing probability normal non-examinable critical value norm.ppf() critical value rejection region cut-off for significance alpha 0.05 boundary if we choose a significance level, then `norm.ppf` gives the critical value: how far from the mean a result must be before we reject h₀. it is useful as an equivalent to the p-value decision (reject if the observation is beyond the critical value). alternatively, compare the p-value with α."},{"sid":"D3.07","title":"False positives (type I error)","cmd":"(p_values < 0.05).mean()","terms":["type I error","false positive"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","simulation","t-test","non-examinable"],"task":["type I error","false positive rate","how often is H0 wrongly rejected","significance level"],"examinable":false,"search":"false positives (type i error) (p_values < 0.05).mean() hypothesis-testing simulation t-test non-examinable type i error false positive type i error false positive rate how often is h0 wrongly rejected significance level running many tests on data where h₀ is *true* allows us to see that about α of them still give p < α by chance (a type i error). so a 5% significance level means that about 1 in 20 true nulls is \"rejected\", which is why many tests need adjusting."},{"sid":"D3.08","title":"Missed effects (power)","cmd":"(p_values < 0.05).mean()","terms":["power","type II error"],"tags":["hypothesis-testing","simulation","t-test","power","non-examinable"],"task":["type II error","power","chance of detecting an effect","false negatives"],"examinable":false,"search":"missed effects (power) (p_values < 0.05).mean() hypothesis-testing simulation t-test power non-examinable power type ii error type ii error power chance of detecting an effect false negatives running many tests on data where h₀ is *false* allows us to see how often a real difference is detected; that proportion is the power, and the rest are type ii errors. power is what a sample-size plan aims to raise."},{"sid":"D4.01","title":"Choosing a t-test","cmd":"one sample vs paired vs independent","terms":["choosing a t-test"],"tags":["t-test","decision"],"task":["which t-test","one sample paired or independent","choose the right test","compare means"],"examinable":true,"search":"choosing a t-test one sample vs paired vs independent t-test decision choosing a t-test which t-test one sample paired or independent choose the right test compare means asking how the data were collected allows us to choose the right t-test: one group against a value, the same people twice, or two different groups. it is useful because the wrong test gives a wrong answer. alternatively, a non-parametric test (such as mann–whitney) is the choice when the data are clearly not normal."},{"sid":"D4.02","title":"One-sample t-tests","cmd":"stats.ttest_1samp(x, popmean=mu0, alternative=side)","terms":["ttest_1samp()"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","one-sample"],"task":["one sample t test","test a mean against a value","ttest_1samp","is the mean different from"],"examinable":true,"search":"one-sample t-tests stats.ttest_1samp(x, popmean=mu0, alternative=side) t-test scipy one-sample ttest_1samp() one sample t test test a mean against a value ttest_1samp is the mean different from the `stats.ttest_1samp()` function allows us to test whether the mean of one group differs from a given value. it is useful for comparing a sample with a target or known standard. alternatively, a confidence interval for the mean gives the same information."},{"sid":"D4.03","title":"Paired t-tests","cmd":"stats.ttest_rel(after, before)","terms":["ttest_rel()"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","paired"],"task":["paired t test","before and after","matched samples","same subjects measured twice"],"examinable":true,"search":"paired t-tests stats.ttest_rel(after, before) t-test scipy paired ttest_rel() paired t test before and after matched samples same subjects measured twice the `stats.ttest_rel()` function allows us to test whether the mean difference between two measurements on the same subjects is zero. it is useful for before/after designs. alternatively, calculate the differences and run a one-sample test on them."},{"sid":"D4.04","title":"A paired test as a one-sample test","cmd":"stats.ttest_1samp(after - before, 0)","terms":["paired = one-sample on differences"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","paired","one-sample"],"task":["paired test the other way","test the differences","one sample test on differences"],"examinable":true,"search":"a paired test as a one-sample test stats.ttest_1samp(after - before, 0) t-test scipy paired one-sample paired = one-sample on differences paired test the other way test the differences one sample test on differences testing the differences against 0 with `ttest_1samp` allows us to reproduce a paired t-test exactly. it is useful for understanding what \"paired\" means: it is a one-sample test on the changes. alternatively, use `ttest_rel` directly."},{"sid":"D4.05","title":"Independent (Welch) t-tests","cmd":"stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=False)","terms":["ttest_ind()","Welch"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","independent"],"task":["two sample t test","compare two groups","independent samples","Welch test","difference in means"],"examinable":true,"search":"independent (welch) t-tests stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=false) t-test scipy independent ttest_ind() welch two sample t test compare two groups independent samples welch test difference in means the `stats.ttest_ind(..., equal_var=false)` function allows us to compare the means of two different groups without assuming equal variances. it is useful for control vs treatment experiments. alternatively, `equal_var=true` gives the classic student test."},{"sid":"D4.06","title":"Student or Welch","cmd":"equal_var=True / False","terms":["equal_var"],"tags":[],"task":["equal variances or not","Student versus Welch","equal_var","which two sample test"],"examinable":true,"search":"student or welch equal_var=true / false  equal_var equal variances or not student versus welch equal_var which two sample test the `equal_var` argument allows us to choose between student's t-test (equal variances assumed) and welch's (not assumed). it is useful when the two groups may have different spreads, because welch's test remains valid and is rarely much worse. alternatively, test the equal-variance assumption first with levene's test."},{"sid":"D4.07","title":"One- or two-sided tests","cmd":"alternative=\"two-sided\" / \"less\" / \"greater\"","terms":["alternative=","one-sided test"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","hypothesis-testing"],"task":["one-sided test","two-sided test","alternative less or greater","directional hypothesis"],"examinable":true,"search":"one- or two-sided tests alternative=\"two-sided\" / \"less\" / \"greater\" t-test scipy hypothesis-testing alternative= one-sided test one-sided test two-sided test alternative less or greater directional hypothesis the `alternative=` argument allows us to run a one-sided test (`\"less\"` or `\"greater\"`) instead of the two-sided default. it is useful when h₁ names one direction before the data are collected, such as a treatment that matters only if it **lowers** blood pressure. alternatively, keep the two-sided default whenever an effect in either direction would matter."},{"sid":"D4.08","title":"Reading the t-test output","cmd":"result.statistic, result.pvalue, result.df","terms":[".statistic",".pvalue",".df"],"tags":[],"task":["read the t test result","t statistic and p value","degrees of freedom","interpret output"],"examinable":true,"search":"reading the t-test output result.statistic, result.pvalue, result.df  .statistic .pvalue .df read the t test result t statistic and p value degrees of freedom interpret output the attributes `.statistic`, `.pvalue` and `.df` of a test result allow us to pull out single numbers for a report. they are useful for writing `t(df) = …, p = …`. alternatively, print the whole result object."},{"sid":"D4.09","title":"Confidence intervals","cmd":"result.confidence_interval(confidence_level=level)","terms":["confidence_interval()"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","confidence-interval"],"task":["confidence interval","interval for a mean difference","95% interval","CI from a t test"],"examinable":true,"search":"confidence intervals result.confidence_interval(confidence_level=level) t-test scipy confidence-interval confidence_interval() confidence interval interval for a mean difference 95% interval ci from a t test the `result.confidence_interval()` method allows us to get a range of plausible values for the difference in means. it is useful because it shows the size and precision of an effect, not only whether it is \"significant\". alternatively, build the interval from the standard error and `stats.t.ppf`."},{"sid":"D4.10","title":"Critical t values","cmd":"stats.t.ppf(q, df)","terms":["stats.t.ppf()"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","confidence-interval","non-examinable"],"task":["critical t value","t cut-off","ppf for t","t table"],"examinable":false,"search":"critical t values stats.t.ppf(q, df) t-test scipy confidence-interval non-examinable stats.t.ppf() critical t value t cut-off ppf for t t table the critical value `stats.t.ppf(0.975, df)` allows us to see what multiplies the standard error to make a 95% interval. it is useful for understanding confidence intervals. alternatively, `result.confidence_interval()` does this automatically."},{"sid":"D4.11","title":"The t statistic by hand","cmd":"(x.mean() - mu0) / (x.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(len(x)))","terms":["t statistic by hand"],"tags":["t-test","numpy","summary","non-examinable"],"task":["calculate t by hand","t statistic formula","check the t test result","manual t test"],"examinable":false,"search":"the t statistic by hand (x.mean() - mu0) / (x.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(len(x))) t-test numpy summary non-examinable t statistic by hand calculate t by hand t statistic formula check the t test result manual t test calculating `(mean − μ₀) / (sd / √n)` ourselves allows us to see what the t statistic measures: how many standard errors the mean is from the target."},{"sid":"D4.12","title":"Comparing groups with a box plot","cmd":"sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='bp')","terms":["box plot of groups"],"tags":[],"task":["compare groups visually","box plot before a t test","look at the groups first"],"examinable":true,"search":"comparing groups with a box plot sns.boxplot(data=df, x='group', y='bp')  box plot of groups compare groups visually box plot before a t test look at the groups first if we have measurements for two groups, then box plots side by side show differences, spread and outliers before we test. it is useful as a check that a test is sensible. alternatively, `sns.histplot(hue=...)` shows the full distributions."},{"sid":"D4.13","title":"QQ plots","cmd":"stats.probplot(data, dist=\"norm\", plot=plt)","terms":["stats.probplot()","QQ plot"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","normality","plotting"],"task":["QQ plot","check normality","probability plot","are the points on the line"],"examinable":true,"search":"qq plots stats.probplot(data, dist=\"norm\", plot=plt) t-test scipy normality plotting stats.probplot() qq plot qq plot check normality probability plot are the points on the line the `stats.probplot()` function allows us to compare the data's quantiles with those of a normal distribution. it is useful for checking the normality assumption: points on the line mean approximately normal. alternatively, `stats.shapiro` gives a single p-value for normality."},{"sid":"D4.14","title":"Testing for normality","cmd":"stats.shapiro(x)","terms":["stats.shapiro()","normality test"],"tags":["t-test","scipy","normality","non-examinable"],"task":["test for normality","Shapiro Wilk","is the data normal","shapiro"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing for normality stats.shapiro(x) t-test scipy normality non-examinable stats.shapiro() normality test test for normality shapiro wilk is the data normal shapiro the `stats.shapiro()` function allows us to test the null hypothesis that data are normally distributed. it is useful as a formal backup to the qq plot. alternatively, trust the qq plot, especially for large samples, where the test flags trivial departures."},{"sid":"D4.15","title":"Testing for equal variances","cmd":"stats.levene(a, b)","terms":["stats.levene()"],"tags":[],"task":["test equal variances","Levene test","same spread in both groups"],"examinable":false,"search":"testing for equal variances stats.levene(a, b)  stats.levene() test equal variances levene test same spread in both groups the `stats.levene()` function allows us to test whether two groups have equal variances. it is useful for deciding between student's and welch's test. alternatively, use welch's test, which does not need the assumption."},{"sid":"D4.16","title":"Reporting a t-test","cmd":"t(df) = , p = , mean difference (95% CI)","terms":["reporting results"],"tags":["t-test","reporting","f-strings","non-examinable"],"task":["report a t test","write up the result","how to write t(df) p","APA style report"],"examinable":false,"search":"reporting a t-test t(df) = , p = , mean difference (95% ci) t-test reporting f-strings non-examinable reporting results report a t test write up the result how to write t(df) p apa style report a fixed sentence pattern allows us to report a test clearly: `t(df) = …, p = …, mean difference (95% ci …)`. it is useful because it gives the test, the evidence and the effect size in one line. alternatively, a table works for many tests."},{"sid":"D4.17","title":"Non-parametric tests","cmd":"stats.mannwhitneyu(a, b), stats.wilcoxon(d)","terms":["mannwhitneyu()","wilcoxon()"],"tags":[],"task":["non parametric test","Mann Whitney","Wilcoxon","when the data is not normal","rank test"],"examinable":false,"search":"non-parametric tests stats.mannwhitneyu(a, b), stats.wilcoxon(d)  mannwhitneyu() wilcoxon() non parametric test mann whitney wilcoxon when the data is not normal rank test the `stats.mannwhitneyu()` and `stats.wilcoxon()` functions allow us to compare groups using ranks rather than means. they are useful when data are clearly not normal. alternatively, transform the data (for example with logs) and use a t-test."},{"sid":"D4.18","title":"Cohen's d","cmd":"d = (m1 - m2) / pooled_sd","terms":["Cohen's d","effect size"],"tags":[],"task":["effect size","Cohen d","how big is the difference","standardised difference"],"examinable":false,"search":"cohen's d d = (m1 - m2) / pooled_sd  cohen's d effect size effect size cohen d how big is the difference standardised difference dividing the difference in means by the pooled standard deviation allows us to measure effect size independent of sample size. it is useful because a very small difference can be \"significant\" in a big sample. alternatively, report the raw difference with its confidence interval."},{"sid":"D4.19","title":"Power analysis","cmd":"TTestIndPower().solve_power(...)","terms":["TTestIndPower","power"],"tags":[],"task":["power analysis","sample size","how many samples do I need","solve power"],"examinable":false,"search":"power analysis ttestindpower().solve_power(...)  ttestindpower power power analysis sample size how many samples do i need solve power the `ttestindpower` class allows us to find how many subjects per group are needed to detect an effect of a given size. it is useful when planning an experiment. alternatively, simulate experiments with `np.random.normal`."},{"sid":"D4.20","title":"Comparing more than two groups","cmd":"stats.f_oneway(a, b, c)","terms":["f_oneway()","ANOVA"],"tags":[],"task":["more than two groups","ANOVA","compare three groups","f_oneway"],"examinable":false,"search":"comparing more than two groups stats.f_oneway(a, b, c)  f_oneway() anova more than two groups anova compare three groups f_oneway the `stats.f_oneway()` function (anova) allows us to test whether any of three or more group means differ. it is useful instead of many t-tests, which inflate the false-positive rate. alternatively, `stats.kruskal` is the non-parametric version."},{"sid":"D4.21","title":"Adjusting p-values for many tests","cmd":"multipletests(pvals, method=\"holm\")","terms":["multipletests()","multiple testing"],"tags":[],"task":["adjust p values","multiple comparisons","Bonferroni","Holm","many tests"],"examinable":false,"search":"adjusting p-values for many tests multipletests(pvals, method=\"holm\")  multipletests() multiple testing adjust p values multiple comparisons bonferroni holm many tests the `multipletests()` function allows us to adjust p-values when several tests are run. it is useful because the more tests are run, the more likely one is \"significant\" by chance. alternatively, apply a bonferroni correction by multiplying p-values by the number of tests."},{"sid":"D5.01","title":"Fitting a line","cmd":"slope, intercept, rvalue, pvalue, stderr = stats.linregress(x, y)","terms":["stats.linregress()","linear regression"],"tags":["regression","scipy","tuples"],"task":["fit a line","linear regression","best fit line","slope and intercept","linregress"],"examinable":true,"search":"fitting a line slope, intercept, rvalue, pvalue, stderr = stats.linregress(x, y) regression scipy tuples stats.linregress() linear regression fit a line linear regression best fit line slope and intercept linregress the `stats.linregress(x, y)` function allows us to fit a straight line `y = slope × x + intercept` by least squares. it is useful for describing a linear relationship and predicting from it. alternatively, `smf.ols` in statsmodels gives a fuller summary."},{"sid":"D5.02","title":"R squared","cmd":"rsquared = rvalue ** 2","terms":["R squared","rvalue"],"tags":["regression","scipy"],"task":["R squared","how much variation is explained","coefficient of determination"],"examinable":true,"search":"r squared rsquared = rvalue ** 2 regression scipy r squared rvalue r squared how much variation is explained coefficient of determination squaring the correlation `rvalue` allows us to find r², the proportion of the variation in y explained by x. it is useful for judging how well the line fits (1 is perfect, 0 is none). alternatively, `model.rsquared` in statsmodels."},{"sid":"D5.03","title":"Reporting a regression","cmd":"f\"slope = {slope:.3g}\"","terms":["reporting a regression"],"tags":["regression","reporting","f-strings"],"task":["report a regression","write up the slope","format regression results"],"examinable":true,"search":"reporting a regression f\"slope = {slope:.3g}\" regression reporting f-strings reporting a regression report a regression write up the slope format regression results an f-string with format codes allows us to report the fitted equation and its quality neatly. it is useful for the write-up of any regression. alternatively, a table of coefficients from statsmodels."},{"sid":"D5.04","title":"Checking the correlation first","cmd":"df['x'].corr(df['y']), stats.pearsonr(x, y)","terms":["corr()","pearsonr()"],"tags":[],"task":["correlation coefficient","check correlation before regression","Pearson r","are x and y related"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking the correlation first df['x'].corr(df['y']), stats.pearsonr(x, y)  corr() pearsonr() correlation coefficient check correlation before regression pearson r are x and y related the `.corr()` method and `stats.pearsonr()` allow us to measure the strength of the linear relationship between two variables. it is useful as a first check before fitting a line. alternatively, `df.corr()` gives every pair at once."},{"sid":"D5.05","title":"Adding a prediction column","cmd":"df['pred'] = df['x'] * slope + intercept","terms":["predictions","prediction column"],"tags":[],"task":["add predictions to the table","fitted values column","predict every row"],"examinable":true,"search":"adding a prediction column df['pred'] = df['x'] * slope + intercept  predictions prediction column add predictions to the table fitted values column predict every row if we have fitted a line, then applying `x * slope + intercept` to the x column adds a column of predicted values. it is useful for comparing predictions with the real values. alternatively, `model.predict()` in statsmodels."},{"sid":"D5.06","title":"Predicting a single value","cmd":"x * slope + intercept","terms":["predicting a value"],"tags":["regression","scipy"],"task":["predict a value","use the fitted line","y for a given x","make a prediction"],"examinable":true,"search":"predicting a single value x * slope + intercept regression scipy predicting a value predict a value use the fitted line y for a given x make a prediction putting a chosen x into the fitted equation allows us to predict y for a new case. it is useful for answering \"what weight would a 30 cm bream have?\". alternatively, `model.predict(pd.dataframe({...}))` for several new values."},{"sid":"D5.07","title":"Plotting a fitted line by hand","cmd":"plt.plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1], 'r-')","terms":["plotting a fitted line"],"tags":[],"task":["draw the fitted line","plot the regression line by hand","line over a scatter"],"examinable":true,"search":"plotting a fitted line by hand plt.plot([x0, x1], [y0, y1], 'r-')  plotting a fitted line draw the fitted line plot the regression line by hand line over a scatter if we have the slope and intercept, then drawing a line between two fitted points on top of the scatter shows the data and the model together. it is useful when control over the line's style is needed. alternatively, `sns.regplot` draws the points and line in one call."},{"sid":"D5.08","title":"Writing the equation on the plot","cmd":"plt.text(x, y, text)","terms":["equation on a plot"],"tags":[],"task":["write the equation on the plot","label the fitted line","annotate the regression"],"examinable":true,"search":"writing the equation on the plot plt.text(x, y, text)  equation on a plot write the equation on the plot label the fitted line annotate the regression if we have a fitted line, then `plt.text` with an f-string puts the equation and r² on the plot. it is useful so the figure stands alone. alternatively, put the equation in the title or caption."},{"sid":"D5.09","title":"Checking the fit with `regplot`","cmd":"sns.regplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', line_kws={'color': c})","terms":["sns.regplot()"],"tags":["regression","seaborn","plotting"],"task":["regression line on a scatter","check the fit","regplot","scatter with trend line"],"examinable":true,"search":"checking the fit with regplot sns.regplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', line_kws={'color': c}) regression seaborn plotting sns.regplot() regression line on a scatter check the fit regplot scatter with trend line the `sns.regplot()` function allows us to draw a scatter plot, fitted line and confidence band in one call. it is useful as a quick check of the manual fit. alternatively, `sns.lmplot` does the same and can split by group."},{"sid":"D5.10","title":"Predicted versus actual","cmd":"plt.plot([lo, hi], [lo, hi], 'k--')","terms":["predicted vs actual"],"tags":[],"task":["predicted against actual","how well does the model fit","perfect fit line","calibration plot"],"examinable":true,"search":"predicted versus actual plt.plot([lo, hi], [lo, hi], 'k--')  predicted vs actual predicted against actual how well does the model fit perfect fit line calibration plot if we have predictions and the real values, then plotting them against each other with a 1:1 line shows the quality of the fit. it is useful because points close to the dashed line mean accurate predictions. alternatively, summarise with r² or the residual standard error."},{"sid":"D5.11","title":"Extrapolation beyond the data","cmd":"prediction at x = 0","terms":["extrapolation"],"tags":[],"task":["extrapolation","predict outside the data","risk of extrapolating","value at zero"],"examinable":true,"search":"extrapolation beyond the data prediction at x = 0  extrapolation extrapolation predict outside the data risk of extrapolating value at zero predicting outside the range of the data allows us to see how a straight-line model can fail. it is useful as a warning: the relationship may not hold beyond what was measured. alternatively, restrict predictions to the observed range."},{"sid":"D5.12","title":"Residuals","cmd":"df['res'] = df['y'] - df['pred']","terms":["residuals"],"tags":[],"task":["residuals","observed minus predicted","errors of the model","calculate residuals"],"examinable":true,"search":"residuals df['res'] = df['y'] - df['pred']  residuals residuals observed minus predicted errors of the model calculate residuals if we have actual and predicted values, then subtracting gives the residuals, the part the line does not explain. it is useful as the raw material for checking assumptions. alternatively, `model.resid` in statsmodels."},{"sid":"D5.13","title":"Residuals versus fitted values","cmd":"plt.scatter(fitted, residuals); plt.axhline(0, ...)","terms":["residuals vs fitted"],"tags":[],"task":["residual plot","residuals against fitted","check linearity","check equal variance"],"examinable":true,"search":"residuals versus fitted values plt.scatter(fitted, residuals); plt.axhline(0, ...)  residuals vs fitted residual plot residuals against fitted check linearity check equal variance if we have residuals and fitted values, then plotting one against the other checks the linear and equal-variance assumptions. it is useful because curves or a funnel shape show problems, whereas a random cloud around 0 is good. alternatively, `sns.residplot` does it directly from the data."},{"sid":"D5.28","title":"Residuals across binned fitted values","cmd":"pd.cut(df['pred'], bins=4); sns.boxplot(data=df, x='pred_group', y='res')","terms":["residuals by group","box plot of residuals","constant variance check"],"tags":[],"task":["residual spread in bins","box plot of residuals by fitted value","check equal variance with boxes"],"examinable":false,"search":"residuals across binned fitted values pd.cut(df['pred'], bins=4); sns.boxplot(data=df, x='pred_group', y='res')  residuals by group box plot of residuals constant variance check residual spread in bins box plot of residuals by fitted value check equal variance with boxes if the fitted values are cut into bins, then a box plot of the residuals for each bin shows whether the spread and the centre of the residuals stay the same across the range of the fit. it is useful as a check of the linear and equal-variance assumptions, because boxes that widen or drift away from zero signal a problem. alternatively, plot the residuals against the fitted values directly."},{"sid":"D5.14","title":"Residual plots with seaborn","cmd":"sns.residplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')","terms":["sns.residplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["seaborn residual plot","residplot","quick residuals","residuals against x"],"examinable":true,"search":"residual plots with seaborn sns.residplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2')  sns.residplot() seaborn residual plot residplot quick residuals residuals against x the `sns.residplot()` function allows us to plot residuals against x in one call. it is useful as a quick check of the linearity assumption. alternatively, calculate the residuals and plot them with `plt.scatter`."},{"sid":"D5.15","title":"QQ plots of residuals","cmd":"stats.probplot(residuals, dist=\"norm\", plot=plt)","terms":["QQ plot of residuals"],"tags":[],"task":["QQ plot of residuals","normal residuals","check normality of errors"],"examinable":true,"search":"qq plots of residuals stats.probplot(residuals, dist=\"norm\", plot=plt)  qq plot of residuals qq plot of residuals normal residuals check normality of errors a qq plot of the residuals allows us to check they are roughly normal, as regression assumes. it is useful for spotting skew and heavy tails. alternatively, `stats.shapiro` gives a p-value."},{"sid":"D5.16","title":"Testing the residuals for normality","cmd":"stats.shapiro(residuals)","terms":["shapiro() on residuals"],"tags":["regression","residuals","normality"],"task":["test residuals for normality","Shapiro on residuals","are the errors normal"],"examinable":true,"search":"testing the residuals for normality stats.shapiro(residuals) regression residuals normality shapiro() on residuals test residuals for normality shapiro on residuals are the errors normal the `stats.shapiro()` function allows us to test whether the residuals are normally distributed. it is useful as a formal check alongside the qq plot. alternatively, rely on the qq plot for large samples."},{"sid":"D5.17","title":"The LINE assumptions","cmd":"Linear, Independent, Normal, Equal variance","terms":["LINE assumptions"],"tags":["regression","residuals","assumptions"],"task":["regression assumptions","LINE","linear independent normal equal variance","check assumptions"],"examinable":true,"search":"the line assumptions linear, independent, normal, equal variance regression residuals assumptions line assumptions regression assumptions line linear independent normal equal variance check assumptions the four line assumptions allow us to judge whether a regression is trustworthy: linear, independent, normal and equal variance residuals."},{"sid":"D5.18","title":"Log-log models","cmd":"df['log_y'] = np.log10(df['y'])","terms":["log-log transformation","np.log10()"],"tags":["regression","numpy","transformation","new-column"],"task":["log transform","log log model","power law","straighten a curve","transform both variables"],"examinable":true,"search":"log-log models df['log_y'] = np.log10(df['y']) regression numpy transformation new-column log-log transformation np.log10() log transform log log model power law straighten a curve transform both variables if a relationship is curved (a power law), then taking logs of both variables can straighten it so a straight-line fit works. it is useful for growth and size relationships. alternatively, fit a curve directly, though that needs more advanced tools."},{"sid":"D5.19","title":"Back-transforming predictions","cmd":"10 ** pred","terms":["back-transforming"],"tags":["regression","numpy","transformation"],"task":["undo the log","back transform","convert predictions to original units","power of ten"],"examinable":true,"search":"back-transforming predictions 10 ** pred regression numpy transformation back-transforming undo the log back transform convert predictions to original units power of ten if we fitted a model on log values, then raising 10 to the predicted value turns it back into original units. it is useful after any log transformation. alternatively, `np.exp` undoes natural logs."},{"sid":"D5.20","title":"Fitting a model with a formula","cmd":"model = smf.ols('y ~ x', data=df).fit()","terms":["smf.ols()","statsmodels"],"tags":["regression","statsmodels","non-examinable"],"task":["statsmodels formula","ols","fit with a formula","y ~ x"],"examinable":false,"search":"fitting a model with a formula model = smf.ols('y ~ x', data=df).fit() regression statsmodels non-examinable smf.ols() statsmodels statsmodels formula ols fit with a formula y ~ x the `smf.ols(\"y ~ x\", data=df).fit()` function allows us to fit a regression using a formula. it is useful for fuller output (confidence intervals, tests) and for several predictors. alternatively, `stats.linregress` fits a line with one predictor and no formula."},{"sid":"D5.21","title":"The full model summary","cmd":"model.summary()","terms":["model.summary()"],"tags":["regression","statsmodels","non-examinable"],"task":["model summary","full regression table","summary output","coefficients table"],"examinable":false,"search":"the full model summary model.summary() regression statsmodels non-examinable model.summary() model summary full regression table summary output coefficients table the `model.summary()` method allows us to see coefficients, standard errors, p-values, r² and diagnostics in one table. it is useful for a complete report of a fit. alternatively, pick individual numbers from `model.params` and friends."},{"sid":"D5.22","title":"Extracting single results from a model","cmd":"model.params, model.rsquared, model.pvalues","terms":["model.params","model.rsquared"],"tags":[],"task":["get the slope from a model","extract R squared","p values from statsmodels","model parameters"],"examinable":false,"search":"extracting single results from a model model.params, model.rsquared, model.pvalues  model.params model.rsquared get the slope from a model extract r squared p values from statsmodels model parameters attributes such as `.params`, `.rsquared` and `.pvalues` allow us to extract single numbers from a fitted model. they are useful for quoting results in text. alternatively, `model.summary().tables` gives the tables."},{"sid":"D5.23","title":"Fitted values and residuals","cmd":"model.fittedvalues, model.resid, sm.qqplot(model.resid, line='r')","terms":["model.resid","sm.qqplot()"],"tags":[],"task":["fitted values and residuals from statsmodels","QQ plot from a model","model residuals"],"examinable":false,"search":"fitted values and residuals model.fittedvalues, model.resid, sm.qqplot(model.resid, line='r')  model.resid sm.qqplot() fitted values and residuals from statsmodels qq plot from a model model residuals the `.fittedvalues` and `.resid` attributes allow us to get predictions and residuals, and `sm.qqplot` draws their qq plot. they are useful for assumption checks on a fitted model. alternatively, calculate them from the predictions as shown earlier."},{"sid":"D5.24","title":"The Durbin-Watson statistic","cmd":"durbin_watson(res)","terms":["durbin_watson()"],"tags":["regression","statsmodels","residuals","non-examinable"],"task":["Durbin Watson","autocorrelation of residuals","independence of errors","test for serial correlation"],"examinable":false,"search":"the durbin-watson statistic durbin_watson(res) regression statsmodels residuals non-examinable durbin_watson() durbin watson autocorrelation of residuals independence of errors test for serial correlation the `durbin_watson()` function allows us to test whether residuals are independent of one another. it is useful for ordered data (time series). values near 2 suggest independence. alternatively, calculate it from `np.diff` of the residuals."},{"sid":"D5.25","title":"Confidence intervals and predictions","cmd":"model.conf_int(), model.predict(df_new)","terms":["conf_int()","predict()"],"tags":["regression","statsmodels","non-examinable"],"task":["confidence interval for the slope","predict new values","prediction interval","conf_int"],"examinable":false,"search":"confidence intervals and predictions model.conf_int(), model.predict(df_new) regression statsmodels non-examinable conf_int() predict() confidence interval for the slope predict new values prediction interval conf_int the `model.conf_int()` and `model.predict()` methods allow us to get intervals for the coefficients and predictions for new data. they are useful for reporting uncertainty. alternatively, build intervals from `stderr` and `stats.t.ppf`."},{"sid":"D5.26","title":"Multiple regression","cmd":"smf.ols('y ~ x1 + x2', data=df).fit()","terms":["multiple regression"],"tags":["regression","statsmodels","non-examinable"],"task":["multiple regression","several predictors","two explanatory variables","y ~ x1 + x2"],"examinable":false,"search":"multiple regression smf.ols('y ~ x1 + x2', data=df).fit() regression statsmodels non-examinable multiple regression multiple regression several predictors two explanatory variables y ~ x1 + x2 adding more terms to the formula (`y ~ x1 + x2`) allows us to model several predictors at once. it is useful when one variable alone does not explain the outcome. alternatively, fit separate simple regressions and compare."},{"sid":"D5.27","title":"A separate line per group","cmd":"sns.lmplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', hue='group')","terms":["sns.lmplot()"],"tags":["regression","seaborn","hue","non-examinable"],"task":["a line for each group","regression by group","lmplot","separate fits per category"],"examinable":false,"search":"a separate line per group sns.lmplot(data=df, x='col_1', y='col_2', hue='group') regression seaborn hue non-examinable sns.lmplot() a line for each group regression by group lmplot separate fits per category the `sns.lmplot(hue=...)` function allows us to fit a separate regression line for each group. it is useful for seeing whether groups follow different relationships. alternatively, fit `smf.ols` with an interaction term."},{"sid":"D6.01","title":"Some principles for scientific data analysis","cmd":"","terms":["good analysis","good practice","reproducible analysis"],"tags":["pandas","workflow","non-examinable"],"task":["good practice in data analysis","avoid mistakes in analysis","reliable results","reproducible analysis","quality of an analysis"],"examinable":false,"search":"some principles for scientific data analysis  pandas workflow non-examinable good analysis good practice reproducible analysis good practice in data analysis avoid mistakes in analysis reliable results reproducible analysis quality of an analysis a good analysis gives the right answer, shows how it was reached, and gives the same numbers when someone else runs it. these principles are a short list to think about while working and before results are reported."},{"sid":"D6.02","title":"Looking at the data before analysing it","cmd":"","terms":["look at the data first","exploring before testing"],"tags":["pandas","workflow","non-examinable"],"task":["look at data first","explore before analysing","check data before statistics","first look at a dataset"],"examinable":false,"search":"looking at the data before analysing it  pandas workflow non-examinable look at the data first exploring before testing look at data first explore before analysing check data before statistics first look at a dataset a statistic computed straight away can hide missing values, mixed groups and odd values; a first look at the size, the missing values, the groups and a plot shows what the statistic will be computed from."},{"sid":"D6.03","title":"Checking each step against what is expected","cmd":"assert condition, \"message\"","terms":["checking each step","assert","sanity checks"],"tags":["pandas","workflow","checking","non-examinable"],"task":["check each step","sanity check","assert in analysis","filter returned nothing","catch mistakes early"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking each step against what is expected assert condition, \"message\" pandas workflow checking non-examinable checking each step assert sanity checks check each step sanity check assert in analysis filter returned nothing catch mistakes early a step that runs without an error can still be wrong, for example a filter that matches no rows; checking the result of each step against what is expected (a row count, a set of values, a hand calculation) finds the mistake where it happens."},{"sid":"D6.04","title":"Keeping the raw data unchanged","cmd":"df_clean = df_raw.dropna()","terms":["raw data","inplace","cleaning a copy"],"tags":[],"task":["keep raw data","do not overwrite data","inplace changes original","clean a copy of the data"],"examinable":false,"search":"keeping the raw data unchanged df_clean = df_raw.dropna()  raw data inplace cleaning a copy keep raw data do not overwrite data inplace changes original clean a copy of the data cleaning done in place changes the data that every later step, and every rerun, starts from; keeping the raw table as it was read and cleaning into a new table means each step can be checked against the original."},{"sid":"D6.05","title":"Making an analysis repeatable","cmd":"df.sample(n, random_state=seed)   Kernel > Restart and Run All","terms":["reproducible results","random_state","restart and run all"],"tags":["pandas","numpy","workflow","non-examinable"],"task":["same results every time","reproducible analysis","random sample changes","set a seed","restart and run all"],"examinable":false,"search":"making an analysis repeatable df.sample(n, random_state=seed)   kernel > restart and run all pandas numpy workflow non-examinable reproducible results random_state restart and run all same results every time reproducible analysis random sample changes set a seed restart and run all an analysis is repeatable when running it again gives the same numbers: every random step has a seed, and the notebook gives its results when it is run from the top, not only in the order the cells were written."},{"sid":"D6.06","title":"Deciding the test before seeing the result","cmd":"","terms":["choosing the test in advance","p-hacking"],"tags":["scipy","hypothesis-testing","t-test","non-examinable"],"task":["choose the test in advance","p-hacking","trying several tests","one-sided after looking","decide alpha first"],"examinable":false,"search":"deciding the test before seeing the result  scipy hypothesis-testing t-test non-examinable choosing the test in advance p-hacking choose the test in advance p-hacking trying several tests one-sided after looking decide alpha first the test, the alternative hypothesis (one or two sides) and the significance level are chosen from the question before the result is seen; choosing them after looking, or trying tests until one gives p < 0.05, makes a false positive far more likely than the stated 5%."},{"sid":"D6.07","title":"Reporting the size of an effect, not only p","cmd":"result.confidence_interval(0.95)","terms":["effect size","confidence interval","reporting results"],"tags":["scipy","t-test","reporting","non-examinable"],"task":["report effect size","p value is not enough","how big is the difference","confidence interval for difference"],"examinable":false,"search":"reporting the size of an effect, not only p result.confidence_interval(0.95) scipy t-test reporting non-examinable effect size confidence interval reporting results report effect size p value is not enough how big is the difference confidence interval for difference a p-value says whether a difference is likely to be more than chance, not how large it is, and with enough data a tiny difference gives a small p; a report gives the size of the effect in its units, with a confidence interval for its uncertainty."},{"sid":"D6.08","title":"Computing numbers instead of typing them","cmd":"f\"... {value:.1f} ...\"","terms":["hard-coded numbers","f-strings in reports"],"tags":["pandas","workflow","reporting","non-examinable"],"task":["typed numbers in report","hard-coded results","numbers out of date","put results in text"],"examinable":false,"search":"computing numbers instead of typing them f\"... {value:.1f} ...\" pandas workflow reporting non-examinable hard-coded numbers f-strings in reports typed numbers in report hard-coded results numbers out of date put results in text a number typed into the text stays the same when the data or the code change, and can be copied wrongly; a number computed and put into the sentence with an f-string is always the one the code produced."},{"sid":"D6.10","title":"Reporting values to a sensible precision","cmd":"f\"{mean:.0f} g (SE {se:.0f} g)\"","terms":["significant figures","decimal places in a report","precision of results"],"tags":["pandas","reporting","f-strings","non-examinable"],"task":["how many significant figures","how many decimal places","round results for a report","precision of a mean","too many decimals"],"examinable":false,"search":"reporting values to a sensible precision f\"{mean:.0f} g (se {se:.0f} g)\" pandas reporting f-strings non-examinable significant figures decimal places in a report precision of results how many significant figures how many decimal places round results for a report precision of a mean too many decimals a value in a report is rounded to what the data can support: the uncertainty (a standard error or a confidence interval) is rounded to one or two significant figures, and the estimate to the same decimal place. while working, the full value is printed, so nothing is hidden; the rounding is for the sentence that reports it."},{"sid":"D6.11","title":"Reporting a p-value","cmd":"def format_p(p): ...","terms":["format_p","p < 0.001","reporting p-values"],"tags":["scipy","reporting","f-strings","non-examinable"],"task":["report p value","p less than 0.001","format a p value","how many decimals for p","very small p value"],"examinable":false,"search":"reporting a p-value def format_p(p): ... scipy reporting f-strings non-examinable format_p p < 0.001 reporting p-values report p value p less than 0.001 format a p value how many decimals for p very small p value a common way to report a p-value is to two significant figures, and as \"p < 0.001\" when it is smaller, because the digits of a very small p carry no extra meaning. it is useful in the sentence of a report; in tutorial code and while working, the exact value is printed so that the result of the test can be seen. alternatively, `:.2g` alone gives scientific notation for small values (6e-08), which is exact but hard to read in a report."},{"sid":"D6.09","title":"Checking an analysis before handing it in","cmd":"Kernel > Restart and Run All","terms":["analysis checklist","before submitting"],"tags":["pandas","workflow","checking","non-examinable"],"task":["checklist for my analysis","check before submitting","review my analysis","before handing in a notebook"],"examinable":false,"search":"checking an analysis before handing it in kernel > restart and run all pandas workflow checking non-examinable analysis checklist before submitting checklist for my analysis check before submitting review my analysis before handing in a notebook a checklist of questions, one for each principle of this sheet, to read through before an analysis is handed in or shared, with a last cell that checks the facts the report relies on."}]};
